/* In-course Quiz Centre bank: the same quizzes as the QTI packages (generated by tools/build_qti.js) */
window.COURSE_QUIZBANK = [{"pkg":"End-of-Module Tests","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_47","title":"Module 1: Module 1 Test","desc":"<p>Module 1: Visual and Interactive Design. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>King Street Fabrics asks Island Pulse Media for a promotional video. Which pre-production activity should Zara's team complete FIRST?</p>","options":["Defining the problem, purpose and goal of the video","Booking the camera, tripod and lighting kit","Choosing transitions and text overlays in WeVideo","Drawing the storyboard for each scene"],"correct":0,"why":"Problem definition comes first because every later decision (audience, message, shots, budget) depends on knowing what the product must achieve. The storyboard is tempting because it is an early planning tool, but you cannot plan scenes until you know the purpose and target audience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>Which planning tool shows each production task as a bar against a time scale, with milestones such as 'storyboard approved'?</p>","options":["A mood board","A storyboard","A Gantt chart","A site map"],"correct":2,"why":"A Gantt chart plots tasks against time, showing durations, overlaps and milestones, so a team can manage a production schedule. The storyboard is tempting because it is also a sequence, but it shows the order of shots in the product, not the timing of the work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>A document that fixes the logo placement, fonts, hex colour codes and spacing rules so every designer on a Carnival campaign produces consistent work is a</p>","options":["style guide","rough sketch","mood board","storyboard"],"correct":0,"why":"A style guide sets fixed rules that keep every item in a project consistent. A mood board is tempting because it also deals with colours and fonts, but it is a loose collection of inspiration that sets a feeling; it does not give exact rules.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>Which of the following is NOT one of the elements of visual design?</p>","options":["Texture","Proximity","Shape","Value"],"correct":1,"why":"The elements are line, shape, form, space, texture, value and colour. Proximity is a principle of composition (how elements are arranged), not a basic element. Value is tempting because it sounds abstract, but it is the lightness or darkness of a colour and is a core element.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>The logo of the Frigate Bay Grill uses orange and blue, which sit directly opposite each other on the colour wheel. This colour scheme is</p>","options":["triadic","split-complementary","complementary","analogous"],"correct":2,"why":"Complementary colours are directly opposite each other on the wheel and create strong contrast. Split-complementary is tempting because it is based on the complement, but it uses a base colour with the two colours either side of its complement, so it has three colours, not two.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A photographer frames the Castries courthouse so that its cupola sits where two lines of a three-by-three grid cross. This applies</p>","options":["symmetrical balance","the golden ratio","repetition","the rule of thirds"],"correct":3,"why":"The rule of thirds divides the frame into a three-by-three grid and places the subject on a line or intersection. The golden ratio is tempting because it is also a composition rule, but it uses a proportion of about 1 to 1.618 and a spiral, not equal thirds.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>On the ANU Ferry booking app, the 'Pay' button turns green and a message reads 'Booking confirmed' as soon as it is tapped. Which principle of interactive design is shown?</p>","options":["Learnability","Predictability","Feedback","Consistency"],"correct":2,"why":"Feedback means the system responds visibly to every user action, confirming that it worked. Predictability is tempting, but it is about users being able to expect what will happen BEFORE they act, not the response afterwards.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>Malik is designing a villain for a Caribbean animated short and wants colour alone to signal danger and menace. Which palette is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Pastel pink and baby blue tints","Bright yellow and sky blue","Pale analogous greens","Black, deep red and dark purple shades"],"correct":3,"why":"Dark shades of red and purple with black are widely read as threatening, powerful and dangerous, which suits a villain. Bright yellow and sky blue is tempting because it is high contrast, but those colours suggest cheerfulness and energy, which fit a hero or a comic character better.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>Which statement BEST describes an indigenous digital product?</p>","options":["Any game that is sold in a Caribbean store","Any app that can be downloaded in the Caribbean","Digital content created by local creators that reflects their own culture, identity and stories","A foreign film dubbed into a Caribbean language"],"correct":2,"why":"Indigenous products are made by people from the culture and express that culture from the inside. A dubbed foreign film is tempting because it is adapted for local audiences, but it is still imported content: the stories and creative decisions come from elsewhere.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A Trinidadian studio produces an animated series featuring folklore characters such as Papa Bois and the soucouyant. What is the MAIN cultural motivation for this product?</p>","options":["To avoid paying any production costs","To avoid copyright law because folklore is old","To copy the style of imported cartoons exactly","To preserve and share Caribbean heritage with local and global audiences"],"correct":3,"why":"Using local folklore keeps stories alive, builds cultural pride and introduces Caribbean heritage to the world. Avoiding copyright is tempting because folklore is traditional, but the studio's own scripts, characters and animation are still protected, and avoiding the law is not a cultural motivation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>Which of the following is an example of POLICY support for local digital creators?</p>","options":["A creator buying followers on Instagram","A government film fund offering grants to Caribbean filmmakers","A viewer streaming a Hollywood film","An imported game renaming characters with Caribbean names"],"correct":1,"why":"A government fund is a policy decision that directly finances local creative work. The renamed imported game is tempting because it looks local, but it is surface localisation of foreign content and does not support local creators.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>Wikipedia is BEST classified as which type of digital media platform?</p>","options":["Marketplace","Knowledge platform","Application store","Infrastructure platform"],"correct":1,"why":"A knowledge platform exists to collect and share information, which is exactly Wikipedia's purpose. Infrastructure platform is tempting because Wikipedia is large and widely used, but infrastructure platforms provide underlying services (such as cloud hosting) for other products to be built on.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>Maya needs the Island Pulse Media logo on a website with a transparent background, sharp edges and no compression artefacts. Which format is BEST?</p>","options":["GIF","PNG","JPEG","MP3"],"correct":1,"why":"PNG is lossless and supports full transparency, so edges stay crisp. GIF is tempting because it also supports transparency, but it is limited to 256 colours and only on-or-off transparency, so smooth edges and gradients look rough.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>Maya places a YouTube video of the Dominica Carnival parade inside a web page so that it plays there, without copying the video file to her own server. This is called</p>","options":["importing","compressing","exporting","embedding"],"correct":3,"why":"Embedding displays content hosted elsewhere inside your page using code such as an iframe. Importing is tempting, but importing brings a copy of the file into your own software or project.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>Malik needs a full-quality master of a voice-over to edit in Audacity before the final export. Which format should he record and save in?</p>","options":["OGG","WAV","MP3","MP4"],"correct":1,"why":"WAV is uncompressed, so no quality is lost while editing; you export to MP3 or OGG only at the end. OGG is tempting because it is an open format, but like MP3 it is lossy, and repeated saves in a lossy format degrade the audio.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>In three-point lighting, which light is placed behind the subject to separate it from the background?</p>","options":["Practical light","Fill light","Back light","Key light"],"correct":2,"why":"The back light (rim or hair light) shines from behind to outline the subject. The fill light is tempting because it is also a supporting light, but it sits on the opposite side of the key light to soften shadows on the face.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>Keisha is recording an interview on a windy beach at Montego Bay. Which action BEST reduces unwanted noise?</p>","options":["Turn the input gain up as high as possible","Add loud background music to cover the wind","Use a lavalier microphone with a furry windscreen and shield it from the wind","Use the camera's built-in microphone from five metres away"],"correct":2,"why":"A close lavalier with a windscreen captures the voice strongly while blocking wind. Turning the gain up is tempting because it makes the voice louder, but it also amplifies the wind noise and can cause clipping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>Which export setting is MOST suitable for a 30-second reel to be posted on Instagram?</p>","options":["MP4 (H.264), 1080 x 1920, 9:16 vertical","PDF, A4","WAV, 48 kHz","GIF, 4:3, 256 colours"],"correct":0,"why":"Instagram Reels expect vertical 9:16 video, and MP4 with H.264 gives good quality at a small file size. GIF is tempting because it can animate, but it has no sound, poor colour and a very large file size for video-length content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>When a bouncing ball flattens as it hits the ground and lengthens as it rises, the animator is applying</p>","options":["anticipation","squash and stretch","follow-through","staging"],"correct":1,"why":"Squash and stretch deforms the object to show weight, impact and speed while keeping its volume. Anticipation is tempting because it also involves a change of shape, but it is a preparatory movement before an action, such as crouching before a jump.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>In Adobe Animate, Malik sets the ball's position on frame 1 and frame 24, and the software creates all the frames in between. This process is called</p>","options":["interpolation (tweening)","rendering","rigging","onion skinning"],"correct":0,"why":"Interpolation, or tweening, is the automatic generation of in-between frames between keyframes. Onion skinning is tempting because it is also used when animating, but it only shows faint previous and next frames as a guide; it does not create frames.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>In Blender, the flat surface enclosed by three or more edges of a mesh is called a</p>","options":["face","keyframe","modifier","vertex"],"correct":0,"why":"A face is the flat surface bounded by edges; faces together form the visible surface of the model. A vertex is tempting because it is also part of a mesh, but it is a single point where edges meet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>In a basic walk cycle, which key pose shows the legs furthest apart with both feet touching the ground?</p>","options":["Passing","Down","Contact","Up"],"correct":2,"why":"The contact pose has the front heel and back toe both on the ground with the legs at full stride. The passing pose is tempting because it is also a key pose, but it shows one leg passing the other under the body, with the legs close together.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>Why is version history in Google Docs an ADVANTAGE for a group writing an SBA proposal?</p>","options":["It closes the digital divide between group members","The group can see who changed what and restore an earlier version if needed","It stops group members from editing the same document","It removes the need for an internet connection"],"correct":1,"why":"Version control records every change and lets the team roll back mistakes, which protects shared work. Working without internet is tempting, but Google Docs offline mode is limited and needs syncing; version history itself has nothing to do with connectivity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>Which of the following is a DISADVANTAGE of digital collaboration for a design team with members in Dominica and Guyana?</p>","options":["Real-time feedback on shared files","Unreliable connectivity during storms and power cuts","Access to a wider pool of talent","Automatic saving to the cloud"],"correct":1,"why":"Connectivity problems and the digital divide can stop members from joining calls or uploading work. Access to a wider pool of talent is tempting because it involves distance, but it is an advantage (global reach), not a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>Spice Lane food court paints giant footprints along the St. George’s boardwalk leading from the cruise pier to its stalls. This is an example of</p>","options":["cross-platform marketing","guerrilla marketing","influencer marketing","email marketing"],"correct":1,"why":"Guerrilla marketing uses unconventional, low-cost, surprising tactics in public spaces. Cross-platform marketing is tempting because the idea could be shared online too, but cross-platform means keeping one consistent message across several channels, not one physical stunt.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>Which KPI BEST shows whether a Mailchimp email campaign persuaded readers to visit a tour booking page?</p>","options":["Open rate","Click-through rate","Total number of subscribers","Number of emails sent"],"correct":1,"why":"Click-through rate measures the percentage of readers who clicked the link to the booking page, which is the action the campaign wanted. Open rate is tempting, but it only shows that people opened the email, not that they acted on it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>Smartphones, smart TVs and connected screens are now found in almost every home, school and workplace in Barbados, so designers must plan for them. Which driving factor does this BEST illustrate?</p>","options":["Pervasiveness of technology","Commoditisation","Empowerment","Affordability"],"correct":0,"why":"Pervasiveness means technology is present everywhere in daily life. Commoditisation is tempting because cheap devices help spread technology, but commoditisation describes technology becoming standardised and cheap, not how widespread it is.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>A user interface uses frosted, semi-transparent panels floating over a blurred, colourful background. This trend is known as</p>","options":["glassmorphism","flat design","neumorphism","skeuomorphism"],"correct":0,"why":"Glassmorphism imitates frosted glass with transparency and background blur. Neumorphism is tempting because it is also a recent soft UI trend, but it uses solid, same-colour surfaces with soft shadows so elements look pushed out of or into the background.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>A St. Lucian youth group raises money for an animated film by collecting small donations from hundreds of supporters on an online platform. This source of funding is</p>","options":["an angel investor","sponsorship","crowdfunding","a government grant"],"correct":2,"why":"Crowdfunding raises many small contributions from the public online. Sponsorship is tempting because supporters give money, but sponsorship comes from one business or organisation in return for publicity, not from many individuals.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>Which cost belongs under 'personnel' in an itemised budget for a 30-second ad?</p>","options":["A new tripod","The fee for a freelance voice actor","Website hosting for one year","A monthly Adobe Creative Cloud subscription"],"correct":1,"why":"Personnel costs are payments to people for their time and skills. The Adobe subscription is tempting because it is a recurring cost, but it belongs under software, and the tripod belongs under equipment.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_8e","title":"Module 2: Module 2 Test","desc":"<p>Module 2: Web and Digital Publishing. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Which statement BEST describes the ePub format?</p>","options":["A video format for online courses","A content management system for blogs","An open e-book format with reflowable text that adapts to the reader's screen","A fixed image of printed pages that cannot change size"],"correct":2,"why":"ePub is an open standard whose text reflows to fit phones, tablets and e-readers. A fixed image of printed pages is tempting because some e-books look like print, but that describes a PDF or a fixed-layout scan, not reflowable ePub.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>A Jamaican author publishes the same novel as a paperback and as an ePub e-book. What is the main purpose of giving each format its own ISBN?</p>","options":["To uniquely identify each edition and format for sellers, libraries and distributors","To protect the novel automatically under copyright law","To improve the book's search engine ranking","To compress the e-book file"],"correct":0,"why":"An ISBN is an identifier that lets the book trade track one specific edition and format. Copyright protection is tempting because both relate to books, but copyright exists automatically when the work is created; an ISBN gives no legal protection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>The network funded by the US Department of Defense in the late 1960s, which became the forerunner of the Internet, was</p>","options":["Web 2.0","ARPANET","Ethernet","the World Wide Web"],"correct":1,"why":"ARPANET linked research computers from 1969 and introduced packet switching that the Internet still uses. The World Wide Web is tempting, but it came about twenty years later (Tim Berners-Lee, around 1989 to 1991) and runs on top of the Internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>An online game in which thousands of players from Barbados, Guyana and around the world share one persistent virtual world at the same time is a</p>","options":["PWA","CMS","ePub","MMOG"],"correct":3,"why":"MMOG means massively multiplayer online game: a very large, shared, persistent world. A PWA is tempting because games can run in browsers, but a PWA is simply a web app that installs like a native app; it says nothing about large numbers of shared players.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>A Barbadian photographer licenses her photos under Creative Commons BY-NC. A hotel wants to use one in a paid advertisement. What should the hotel do?</p>","options":["Use it without credit because it is free online","Use it freely as long as it credits the photographer","Ask the photographer for permission, because NC does not allow commercial use","Edit the photo so that it becomes a new work"],"correct":2,"why":"NC (non-commercial) forbids commercial use without separate permission, and an advert is commercial. Crediting the photographer is tempting because BY does require attribution, but attribution alone does not cancel the non-commercial condition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A student copies three paragraphs from a national newspaper article into her blog post and publishes it under her own name with no credit. This is</p>","options":["parody","plagiarism","fair use","attribution"],"correct":1,"why":"Presenting another writer's words as your own is plagiarism, and it may also infringe copyright. Fair use is tempting because the passage is short, but fair use requires a limited, transformative purpose such as criticism; copying without credit and claiming authorship is never fair use.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>Which writing technique is MOST suitable for a podcast script about the history of Anguilla's salt ponds?</p>","options":["Long paragraphs with footnotes and references","Hyperlinks placed inside every sentence","Conversational language, short sentences and spoken signposts such as 'next, we will hear'","Tables and bullet points for each date"],"correct":2,"why":"Podcast scripts are written for the ear, so they must sound natural and guide the listener. Tables and bullet points are tempting because they organise facts clearly, but listeners cannot see them; they suit web pages, not audio.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>Maya sketches the layout of a tour company's home page on paper and asks five users where they would tap to book. This is a</p>","options":["hierarchy map","low-fidelity prototype","high-fidelity prototype","style guide"],"correct":1,"why":"Paper sketches are low-fidelity prototypes: fast, cheap and ideal for early testing of layout and flow. A high-fidelity prototype is tempting because it is also tested with users, but it is a detailed digital model, often clickable, that looks close to the finished product.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>A diagram that lists every page of the Tamarind Heritage website and shows how the pages link together is a</p>","options":["site map","storyboard","concept map","mood board"],"correct":0,"why":"A site map shows the full set of pages and their links, planning site structure. A concept map is tempting because it also shows relationships, but it links ideas and topics, not the actual pages of a website.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>Which of the following goes AGAINST the criteria for web-friendly content?</p>","options":["Long, dense paragraphs filled with technical jargon","Addressing the reader directly as 'you'","Plain language and clear calls to action","Short paragraphs and scannable headings"],"correct":0,"why":"Web-friendly content uses short paragraphs and direct address, and avoids jargon and needless repetition. Addressing the reader as 'you' is tempting because it feels informal, but direct address is specifically recommended for web writing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>Keisha adds a descriptive title tag, a meta description and relevant keywords to each page of a Grenada spice tour website. This MAINLY improves</p>","options":["the loading speed of images","the security of the booking form","the site's search engine optimisation and machine indexability","the site's colour contrast"],"correct":2,"why":"Titles, meta descriptions and keywords help search engines understand and rank the page. Loading speed is tempting because it also affects SEO, but metadata does not make images load faster; compressing and resizing images does.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>What is the MAIN purpose of adding alt text to images on a web page?</p>","options":["To add a caption below the image for all visitors","To describe the image for screen reader users and search engines","To make the image file smaller","To stop people from copying the image"],"correct":1,"why":"Alt text is read aloud by screen readers and indexed by search engines, supporting accessibility and SEO. A visible caption is tempting, but alt text is normally hidden and only shown if the image fails to load; captions are separate elements.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>A photographer shooting johnny cakes for a café website wants the food sharp and the background softly blurred. Which technique should she use?</p>","options":["A shallow depth of field with a wide aperture","The rule of thirds with a fisheye lens","A high-angle shot with flash","A deep depth of field with a small aperture"],"correct":0,"why":"A shallow depth of field (wide aperture such as f/1.8, or portrait mode on a phone) keeps the subject sharp and blurs the background. Deep depth of field is tempting because it keeps things sharp, but it keeps the background sharp too, which makes it distracting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>A podcast recorded in a Castries classroom has a constant air-conditioner hum. In Audacity, the BEST way to remove it is to</p>","options":["increase the volume of the whole track","select a few seconds of hum only, capture the noise profile, then apply Noise Reduction to the whole track","convert the file to MP3","add a fade-in to the start of the recording"],"correct":1,"why":"Noise Reduction learns the hum from a sample (the noise profile) and removes it across the track. Converting to MP3 is tempting because it changes the file, but MP3 compression does not remove noise and actually loses some audio quality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>On a tour website, the line 'Home > Tours > Booby Island' at the top of a page is an example of</p>","options":["a footer","a hamburger menu","an anchor link","a breadcrumb trail"],"correct":3,"why":"Breadcrumbs show users their location in the site hierarchy and let them step back up a level. An anchor link is tempting because it also helps navigation, but it jumps to a section within the same page.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>Which HTML code opens an external booking site in a new browser tab?</p>","options":["&lt;link href=\"https://book.example.com\"&gt;Book&lt;/link&gt;","&lt;a href=\"https://book.example.com\" new&gt;Book&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;a href=\"https://book.example.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"&gt;Book&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;a src=\"https://book.example.com\" tab=\"new\"&gt;Book&lt;/a&gt;"],"correct":2,"why":"The a element uses href for the address, and target=\"_blank\" opens a new tab (rel=\"noopener\" improves security). The link element is tempting because of its name, but it belongs in the head to connect files such as stylesheets; it does not create clickable links.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>In the CSS box model, what is the correct order of layers from the inside of an element outwards?</p>","options":["Margin, border, padding, content","Padding, content, margin, border","Content, border, padding, margin","Content, padding, border, margin"],"correct":3,"why":"Content sits inside padding, which is surrounded by the border, with margin outside the border separating the box from others. Content, border, padding, margin is tempting because it starts correctly, but padding is always inside the border.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>Which elements does the CSS rule <code>.price { color: #b00020; }</code> style?</p>","options":["Only elements named &lt;price&gt;","All elements with class=\"price\"","The single element with id=\"price\"","Every paragraph on the page"],"correct":1,"why":"A full stop before a name is a class selector, which can apply to many elements. The id option is tempting, but id selectors use a hash (#price) and should match only one element.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>A Grenada tourism page uses light grey text (#bbbbbb) on a white background. Which design principle does this MOST clearly break?</p>","options":["The rule of thirds","Responsive layout","Accessibility, because the colour contrast is too low (WCAG)","Consistency of navigation"],"correct":2,"why":"WCAG requires sufficient contrast between text and background; pale grey on white is hard to read, especially outdoors or for users with low vision. Responsive layout is tempting because it is also a UI principle, but it concerns adapting to screen sizes, not colour.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>A student researching hurricane preparedness checks who wrote a blog post and whether the writer works for a national disaster management office. Which evaluation criterion is she applying?</p>","options":["Authority","Usability","Satisfaction","Accessibility"],"correct":0,"why":"Authority asks who created the content and whether they are qualified and credible. Usability is tempting because it is a common evaluation criterion, but it measures how easy the content is to use and navigate, not who wrote it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>A Dominican heritage group wants commuters to learn Caribbean history in 20-minute episodes while driving. Which form of digital publishing is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["An infographic","A podcast","An interactive e-book","A photo blog"],"correct":1,"why":"Podcasts are audio and can be enjoyed safely while driving. An interactive e-book is tempting because it can include audio, but it is designed to be read and tapped, which is unsafe and impractical for drivers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>A Grenadian bakery built its whole audience on one social platform. After an algorithm change, its posts now reach far fewer followers. This illustrates which limitation of online platforms?</p>","options":["Monetisation tools","Analytics tools","Platform dependency","Community features"],"correct":2,"why":"Platform dependency means you do not control the rules or algorithms of the platform your audience lives on. Analytics tools are tempting because the bakery would notice the drop through analytics, but analytics are a benefit, not the cause of the problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>Which editorial standard is REQUIRED when writing a Wikipedia article about the Battle of Fort George?</p>","options":["A neutral point of view supported by reliable, verifiable sources","A persuasive tone that promotes Belize tourism","The author's name and photograph at the top","Original research from the writer's own interviews"],"correct":0,"why":"Wikipedia requires neutrality and verifiability from published reliable sources. Original research is tempting because interviews sound rigorous, but Wikipedia does not allow unpublished original research; it must cite existing sources.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>Compared with free hosted WordPress.com, a self-hosted WordPress site gives Frigate Bay Surf School</p>","options":["full control over plugins, design and data, but responsibility for hosting, backups and security","no need to pay for hosting or a domain","a guaranteed higher Google ranking","automatic security updates handled by the platform"],"correct":0,"why":"Self-hosting gives maximum control and ownership but the owner must pay for hosting and maintain security and backups. Automatic updates are tempting because many hosts offer them, but on a self-hosted site keeping the system updated is ultimately the owner's responsibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>A news organisation stores articles once and sends them through an API to its website, mobile app and smartwatch alerts, each with its own design. This is a</p>","options":["enterprise resource system","document CMS","headless CMS","static HTML site"],"correct":2,"why":"A headless CMS separates content (the 'body') from presentation (the 'head'), delivering content to many channels. A document CMS is tempting because it stores documents, but it manages files such as PDFs and contracts inside an organisation, not multi-channel publishing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>In a content workflow, which stage comes immediately after an editor reviews a draft blog post?</p>","options":["Publishing","Approval","Creation","Archiving"],"correct":1,"why":"The workflow runs creation, review, approval, publishing, archiving. Publishing is tempting because it seems the natural next step, but content must be approved by someone with authority before it goes live.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>Which of the following is a proprietary, cloud-hosted CMS?</p>","options":["Joomla","Wix","WordPress.org software","Drupal"],"correct":1,"why":"Wix is owned by a company and runs on its own servers. WordPress.org software is tempting because WordPress is very well known, but the WordPress.org software is open source and can be self-hosted.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>Which factor influencing trends in web publishing covers government regulation, censorship and data laws?</p>","options":["Affordability","Commoditisation","Accessibility","Politics"],"correct":3,"why":"Politics covers government regulation, censorship and data laws, and sits alongside commoditisation, pervasiveness, affordability, accessibility, adoption and empowerment. Accessibility is tempting because it is very topical, but it is about whether people can reach and use content, not about government action.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>More people now ask smart speakers questions such as 'Where can I buy sorrel drink in Port of Spain?'. How should web publishers respond to this trend?</p>","options":["Use longer pages full of repeated keywords","Remove all text and use only images","Publish only PDF documents","Write clear, conversational question-and-answer content with local details"],"correct":3,"why":"Voice search favours natural-language questions and direct answers, such as FAQ sections with local information. Repeating keywords is tempting because it once helped SEO, but keyword stuffing is now penalised and does not match how people speak.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>Which BEST describes a progressive web app (PWA)?</p>","options":["An app that is only available on the Apple App Store","A website that can be installed on the home screen and can work offline like an app","A printed brochure converted to PDF","A social media page for a business"],"correct":1,"why":"A PWA is a website with app-like features such as installation, offline access and notifications. An App Store app is tempting because PWAs feel like apps, but PWAs are delivered through the web and do not need an app store.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_cl","title":"Module 3: Module 3 Test","desc":"<p>Module 3: Mobile App Development. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Which of the following is part of the INFRASTRUCTURE component of the mobile ecosystem?</p>","options":["Cell towers and submarine fibre-optic cables","A smartwatch","A news app from a national newspaper","A teenager streaming music in Port of Spain"],"correct":0,"why":"Infrastructure is the physical and technical backbone that carries data, such as towers, spectrum and cables. A smartwatch is tempting because it is hardware, but it is a device, a separate component of the ecosystem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>A national regulator sets rules for spectrum licences and personal data protection that all mobile operators and app developers must follow. In the mobile ecosystem, the regulator is part of the</p>","options":["content layer","consumer base","device layer","enabling environment"],"correct":3,"why":"The enabling environment includes policy frameworks, regulators and laws that shape how the ecosystem works. The content layer is tempting because data protection affects apps, but content refers to the apps, media and services themselves, not the rules governing them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>After a hurricane damages towers across Grenada, residents cannot use a mobile banking app even though the bank's servers are working. This BEST illustrates</p>","options":["interdependence within the mobile ecosystem","app store optimisation","freemium pricing","fragmentation"],"correct":0,"why":"Each part of the ecosystem relies on the others: without carrier infrastructure, content and devices cannot connect. Fragmentation is tempting because it is a mobile problem, but it describes the variety of Android devices and versions, not network failure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>Which feature BEST describes the Android operating system?</p>","options":["Closed source and available only on Apple devices","Open source, used by many manufacturers, with a wide range of devices and versions","Unable to run apps from Google Play","Used only on smartwatches"],"correct":1,"why":"Android is open source and licensed to many manufacturers, which brings variety but also fragmentation. Closed source on Apple devices describes iOS, which is the most tempting confusion between the two platforms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>Jerome wants to build a native iPhone app. Which development environment and language should he use?</p>","options":["Android Studio and Kotlin","VS Code and HTML only","Xcode and Swift","MIT App Inventor and blocks"],"correct":2,"why":"Apple's Xcode with the Swift language and the iOS SDK is the official route to native iOS apps. Android Studio and Kotlin is tempting because it is also an official native toolset, but it builds Android apps, not iOS apps.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A farmer in St. Kitts places soil-moisture sensors in a field that send readings over the network to an app on her phone. This is an example of</p>","options":["a super app","the Internet of Things","white-label licensing","a progressive web app"],"correct":1,"why":"The Internet of Things connects everyday objects with sensors so they can collect and share data. A progressive web app is tempting because the farmer views data on her phone, but a PWA is a type of web app; the sensors connected to the network are what make this IoT.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>A start-up in Jamaica has a small budget and wants to reach as many local users as possible quickly. Most of its target users have mid-range Android phones. Which platform decision is MOST sensible?</p>","options":["Build for Android first, or use a cross-platform tool such as Flutter","Build separate native apps for Android, iOS and HarmonyOS at once","Build for iOS first because Apple users spend more","Build for smartwatches only"],"correct":0,"why":"Matching the platform to the target audience, budget and time-to-market points to Android first, or one cross-platform code base. iOS first is tempting because iOS users often spend more on apps, but it ignores where this start-up's actual users are and adds cost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>Many Caribbean users spend hours on international streaming and social apps, but relatively few locally made apps and channels exist. This is described as</p>","options":["edge computing","the imbalance between content consumption and content production","the digital divide between islands","platform fragmentation"],"correct":1,"why":"The syllabus highlights that the Caribbean consumes far more digital content than it produces. The digital divide is tempting because it is also an inequality, but it refers to unequal ACCESS to technology, not the gap between consuming and creating content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>What is the MAIN role of submarine fibre-optic cables in the Caribbean mobile ecosystem?</p>","options":["They provide GPS signals to phones","They replace the need for local cell towers","They carry international Internet traffic between islands and the rest of the world","They charge mobile phone batteries"],"correct":2,"why":"Most international data travels through undersea cables that link island networks to the global Internet. Replacing cell towers is tempting because cables carry data, but towers are still needed to connect phones wirelessly to the network on land.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A Guyanese family living in Toronto sends money home and video-calls relatives every week using mobile apps. This BEST illustrates</p>","options":["content production","the diaspora connection supported by mobile technology","e-government services","enterprise distribution"],"correct":1,"why":"Mobile apps bridge Caribbean communities across the world, supporting remittances and family ties. E-government is tempting because money transfers can be official, but e-government means government services delivered online, not personal family communication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>Which is the STRONGEST economic benefit of developing apps locally in Barbados rather than importing them?</p>","options":["Local apps cannot be used by tourists","Local development creates jobs and builds skills, and successful apps can be exported to other islands","Local apps never need updating","Imported apps are always illegal"],"correct":1,"why":"Local app development keeps money and skills in the economy and can grow into regional exports. 'Local apps never need updating' is tempting because local developers are close by, but every app needs ongoing updates for OS changes and security.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>KIN Heritage Trail app guides visitors to historic sites such as Fort Charles with audio stories told by local elders. Which benefit of local apps does this MOST directly demonstrate?</p>","options":["Hardware fragmentation","Reduced data usage","Cultural preservation and social linkage","Enterprise distribution"],"correct":2,"why":"Recording elders' stories preserves heritage and connects visitors and residents to local culture. Reduced data usage is tempting because good apps optimise data, but it is a UX factor, not a benefit of the app being local.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>An app for Dominica's disaster preparedness lets residents find shelters, receive alerts and mark themselves safe. This is BEST classified as a</p>","options":["social impact (civic) app","white-label shopping app","freemium game","subscription entertainment app"],"correct":0,"why":"Disaster preparedness apps serve public safety and community well-being, which makes them social impact or civic apps. A subscription app is tempting because alerts are ongoing, but charging people for life-saving alerts would conflict with the app's public purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>Older users of a health clinic app in Antigua keep tapping the wrong buttons. Which change MOST directly addresses this UX problem?</p>","options":["Use smaller text so more fits on screen","Add more features to the home screen","Add a loading animation","Increase touch target sizes and spacing between buttons"],"correct":3,"why":"Larger touch targets (about 44 to 48 points) with space between them reduce mistaken taps, especially for older users. Adding more features is tempting because it seems helpful, but it crowds the screen and makes mis-taps more likely.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>Many users of a Guyanese farming app live in rural areas with weak, expensive mobile data. Which design strategy is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Add a large animated splash screen","Compress images, cache data and provide an offline mode","Require users to stay online at all times","Use high-resolution autoplay videos on every screen"],"correct":1,"why":"Optimising for low bandwidth means small files, caching and offline features so the app still works with poor coverage. Autoplay video is tempting because it is engaging, but it consumes large amounts of data and fails on weak connections.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>A delivery app is used by drivers outdoors in bright Caribbean sunlight. Which factor of the mobile user experience is MOST relevant?</p>","options":["The physical environment","App store ranking","Processing power","Revenue model"],"correct":0,"why":"The environment (lighting, noise, movement) affects readability, so designers use high contrast, large text and possibly a high-visibility mode. Processing power is tempting because drivers need fast apps, but it does not explain the difficulty of reading a screen in sunlight.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>Which feature MOST improves accessibility for blind users of a mobile app?</p>","options":["Labels on every button and image that screen readers such as TalkBack and VoiceOver can read","A colourful splash screen","Gesture-only navigation with no buttons","Text placed inside images"],"correct":0,"why":"Screen readers depend on proper labels to describe buttons and images aloud. Gesture-only navigation is tempting because it removes visual clutter, but hidden gestures are hard to discover and are often unusable with a screen reader.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>What is the main DIFFERENCE between a wireframe and a clickable prototype?</p>","options":["A wireframe is the finished app; a prototype is a sketch","A wireframe shows static screen layouts; a clickable prototype links screens so users can test the flow","There is no difference","A wireframe is written in Kotlin; a prototype is written in Swift"],"correct":1,"why":"Wireframes plan layout and structure; prototypes add interactivity so user flows can be tested before coding. The first option is tempting because both are stages, but it reverses them: the wireframe comes first and the finished app comes last.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>A group of CAPE students with no coding experience wants a working Android prototype of a school canteen ordering app within two weeks. Which tool is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["A text editor and Java only","MIT App Inventor or Thunkable","Blender","Xcode"],"correct":1,"why":"No-code and low-code builders let beginners create working apps quickly with visual blocks. Xcode is tempting because it is a professional app tool, but it builds iOS apps and requires Swift coding, which does not fit beginners targeting Android.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>Before launch, Jerome gives the BusLink GND app to twenty commuters, who check whether it actually meets their needs. This is</p>","options":["debugging","app store optimisation","user acceptance testing (UAT)","unit testing"],"correct":2,"why":"UAT is performed by real target users to confirm the app meets their requirements. Unit testing is tempting because it is also testing, but developers do it on small pieces of code, not real users on the whole app.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>A Barbados bank releases an internal app only to its own staff, without listing it on Google Play or the App Store. This distribution model is</p>","options":["enterprise distribution","freemium","direct download for the public","a beta release on a public store"],"correct":0,"why":"Enterprise distribution delivers apps privately to an organisation's employees. Direct download is tempting because the app avoids public stores, but direct download usually offers the file to the general public from a website.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>Keisha rewrites an app's store title, adds the keywords 'St. Lucia bus' and 'Castries', uploads clear screenshots and encourages happy users to leave ratings. This is</p>","options":["enterprise distribution","App Store Optimisation (ASO)","a press release","influencer marketing"],"correct":1,"why":"ASO improves the title, keywords, description, screenshots and ratings so the app ranks higher in store searches. Influencer marketing is tempting because ratings involve other people, but influencer marketing pays or partners with content creators to promote the app outside the store.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>Which sequence shows the user journey for a mobile app in the correct order?</p>","options":["Retain, review, test, discover, download, purchase","Purchase, download, discover, test, review, retain","Discover, test, review, purchase, download, retain","Download, discover, review, retain, test, purchase"],"correct":2,"why":"Users first discover the app, try or test it, read reviews, purchase (if paid), download and finally are retained. Starting with purchase is tempting because buying feels like the key step, but users rarely buy before they have discovered and judged the app.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>A Trinidad language-learning app is free to download with basic lessons, but users pay to unlock advanced courses and offline downloads. This revenue model is</p>","options":["paid upfront","freemium","donation-based","white-label licensing"],"correct":1,"why":"Freemium offers a free core product with paid premium features. Paid upfront is tempting because users do pay, but paid upfront means paying before downloading at all.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>In a free mobile game, players can choose to watch a 30-second advert to earn extra lives. This is</p>","options":["a subscription","an interstitial ad","a rewarded video ad","a banner ad"],"correct":2,"why":"Rewarded video gives users a reward for choosing to watch an advert. An interstitial is tempting because it is also full screen, but interstitials appear automatically at breaks; the user does not choose to watch them for a reward.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>A St. Lucian developer builds one restaurant ordering app and licenses it to ten restaurants, each of which adds its own logo and colours. This revenue model is</p>","options":["white-label licensing","in-app advertising","crowdfunding","freemium"],"correct":0,"why":"White-label apps are built once and licensed to other businesses to rebrand as their own. Freemium is tempting because the developer may offer a basic version, but freemium earns from end users upgrading, not from businesses licensing the app.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>Which revenue approach is MOST appropriate for a hurricane-alert app run by a Caribbean charity?</p>","options":["Interstitial ads before every alert","Grants, sponsorship and donations, with the app free to all users","A monthly subscription for alerts","Charging per alert received"],"correct":1,"why":"Civic and social good apps are usually funded by grants, sponsors and donations so that everyone can access safety information. A subscription is tempting because it gives steady income, but it would exclude people who cannot pay from life-saving alerts, which is unethical for this purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>One app that combines messaging, taxi booking, food delivery and payments, as WeChat and Grab do, is known as a</p>","options":["headless app","white-label app","wearable app","super app"],"correct":3,"why":"Super apps consolidate many services into one platform. White-label app is tempting because it is also a business model term, but it describes one app rebranded for many businesses, not many services in one app.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>A Caribbean health app developer must explain what personal data is collected, obtain consent and allow users to delete their data. These requirements are MOST influenced by</p>","options":["App Store Optimisation","foldable devices","5G rollout","data protection regulation such as the GDPR and regional data protection laws"],"correct":3,"why":"Privacy laws such as the GDPR and Caribbean data protection acts require transparency, consent and the right to deletion. 5G is tempting because it is a major trend, but it concerns network speed, not how personal data must be handled.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>Which tool is designed MAINLY for tracking app downloads, rankings and market trends?</p>","options":["Audacity","Sensor Tower","Blender","Balsamiq"],"correct":1,"why":"Sensor Tower (like data.ai, formerly App Annie, and Statista) tracks app market data and trends. Balsamiq is tempting because it is used in app development, but it is a wireframing tool, not a research tool.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Module 1","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_iw","title":"Module 1 · Unit 1 quiz: Pre-production principles: creating a digital media product","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 1: Pre-production principles: creating a digital media product. 42 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Put the creative design process in order","q":"<p>The Gouyave Heritage Club in Grenada wants a short digital tour of its fishing town. Put the seven stages of the creative process in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Acceptance: the team commits to the task and identifies the time, resources and responsibility needed.","Analysis: the team researches the idea and its market and asks residents and visitors for their opinion.","Definition: the team restates the problem as a clear goal and sets the scope, timeline and milestones.","Ideation and brainstorming: the team logs every idea, such as an audio walk, a map and a video series.","Selection: ideas are compared on feasibility and risk, and the one that best meets the objectives is chosen.","Implementation: the team takes the plunge, sequences the tasks in a Gantt chart and produces the tour.","Evaluation: visitors and the club give feedback, and the team decides what to improve or eliminate."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Match each stage to its description","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is labelling the project wall for a breadfruit festival campaign in Kingstown. Match each stage of the creative process to its description.</p>","left":["Acceptance","Analysis","Definition","Ideation and brainstorming","Selection","Implementation","Evaluation"],"right":["Compare options on feasibility and risk; choose the one that best meets the objectives","Restate the problem as a clear goal; set the scope, timelines and milestones","Research the idea and its market; speak to the people the product will be useful to","Generate ideas and keep a log of all of them, even those that seem irrelevant now","Commit to the task; identify the time, energy, resources and responsibility needed","Take the plunge: sequence the tasks in a Gantt chart and use resources to execute them","Gather feedback from the audience and clients, often with a rubric, to see what to improve"],"correct":[4,2,1,3,0,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which creative process model?","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is designing a booking website for the English Harbour Kayak Tours. After analysis and definition she develops three different homepage ideas at the same time, brings them together to select one, and after implementation the evaluation sends her back to re-analyse. Which creative process model is she using?</p>","options":["Stepped with immediate feedback loops","Cyclic model","Linear model","Branched network"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["This model feeds back to the previous step as you go. The key feature here is the split into several ideas that converge.","A cyclic model repeats the stages in a circle, but it does not split into several parallel ideas.","A linear model follows one line, completing each step before the next. Maya explored three paths at once.","Correct. The process splits into several ideas after analysis and definition, converges at selection, and evaluation leads to re-analysis."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which core element of pre-production? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is filling in the pre-production checklist for a Sugar Mas promo video in Basseterre. Sort each task under its core element.</p><p>Which core element does this task belong to?</p><p><b>Set a budget of US$1,500 for crew, gear hire and editing.</b></p>","options":["Clearances","Codes, regulations, health and safety","Resources","Copyright and IP"],"correct":2,"why":"Money (budget) is a resource: it decides scale, equipment, crew and quality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which core element of pre-production? (2)","q":"<p>Which core element does this task belong to?</p><p><b>Book an editor and a sound engineer for two days.</b></p>","options":["Clearances","Copyright and IP","Resources","Codes, regulations, health and safety"],"correct":2,"why":"Personnel are a resource.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which core element of pre-production? (3)","q":"<p>Which core element does this task belong to?</p><p><b>Get a permit to film in a public area during the parade.</b></p>","options":["Codes, regulations, health and safety","Resources","Clearances","Copyright and IP"],"correct":2,"why":"Permits for public or regulated spaces are clearances.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which core element of pre-production? (4)","q":"<p>Which core element does this task belong to?</p><p><b>Collect signed consent forms from the troupe members filmed.</b></p>","options":["Resources","Codes, regulations, health and safety","Clearances","Copyright and IP"],"correct":2,"why":"Releases (consent forms) from talent and interviewees are clearances.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which core element of pre-production? (5)","q":"<p>Which core element does this task belong to?</p><p><b>License the calypso track used as background music.</b></p>","options":["Copyright and IP","Codes, regulations, health and safety","Clearances","Resources"],"correct":0,"why":"Properly licensing third-party music protects against copyright infringement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which core element of pre-production? (6)","q":"<p>Which core element does this task belong to?</p><p><b>Check that every photo in the end titles is original or cleared.</b></p>","options":["Clearances","Codes, regulations, health and safety","Resources","Copyright and IP"],"correct":3,"why":"All material used must be legal and cleared: copyright and IP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which core element of pre-production? (7)","q":"<p>Which core element does this task belong to?</p><p><b>Make sure the sponsor's claims in the advert are truthful.</b></p>","options":["Codes, regulations, health and safety","Resources","Copyright and IP","Clearances"],"correct":0,"why":"Truthful advertising is legal and ethical compliance under codes of practice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which core element of pre-production? (8)","q":"<p>Which core element does this task belong to?</p><p><b>Do a risk assessment for filming on a moving float at night.</b></p>","options":["Resources","Copyright and IP","Codes, regulations, health and safety","Clearances"],"correct":2,"why":"Risk assessments for filming conditions are health and safety.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the strongest problem statement","q":"<p>Grande Riviere Turtle Watch group in Trinidad is worried that beachgoers disturb nesting turtles. Which is the STRONGEST problem statement for a digital media product?</p>","options":["We need to make a TikTok video about turtles.","Beachgoers at Grande Riviere and Matura Beach need a quick way to learn how to behave near turtle nests because many disturb nests without realising it; goal: reduce reported disturbances by half during the next nesting season.","The group wants a beautiful website with lots of turtle photos.","Turtles are important to the Caribbean."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This names a solution before the problem is understood, and it has no user or measurable goal.","Correct. It names the users, the need and the insight, does not lock in a solution, and has a measurable, time-bound goal.","This describes a product and its style, not the user problem. A beautiful website might not change anyone's behaviour.","This is a true background fact, but it does not state who has the problem, what they need or what success looks like."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is building a persona for Fish Fry Hop food-tour app in Providenciales. Sort each research finding.</p><p>What kind of audience information is this?</p><p><b>Most users are aged 25 to 44.</b></p>","options":["Behaviour","Psychographic","Demographic"],"correct":2,"why":"Age is a measurable fact about people, so it is demographic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour? (2)","q":"<p>What kind of audience information is this?</p><p><b>Users value authentic local food over chain restaurants.</b></p>","options":["Psychographic","Behaviour","Demographic"],"correct":0,"why":"Values and attitudes are psychographic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour? (3)","q":"<p>What kind of audience information is this?</p><p><b>Users book tours on their phones in the evening.</b></p>","options":["Behaviour","Demographic","Psychographic"],"correct":0,"why":"This describes what people actually do, so it is a behaviour.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour? (4)","q":"<p>What kind of audience information is this?</p><p><b>Many visitors come from the United States and Canada.</b></p>","options":["Psychographic","Demographic","Behaviour"],"correct":1,"why":"Location or country of origin is demographic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour? (5)","q":"<p>What kind of audience information is this?</p><p><b>Users see themselves as adventurous foodies.</b></p>","options":["Psychographic","Demographic","Behaviour"],"correct":0,"why":"Self-image and lifestyle are psychographic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour? (6)","q":"<p>What kind of audience information is this?</p><p><b>Users read reviews on TripAdvisor before choosing.</b></p>","options":["Demographic","Psychographic","Behaviour"],"correct":2,"why":"Reading reviews before deciding is a behaviour.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour? (7)","q":"<p>What kind of audience information is this?</p><p><b>Most users have a household income above the regional average.</b></p>","options":["Psychographic","Behaviour","Demographic"],"correct":2,"why":"Income is a measurable demographic factor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour? (8)","q":"<p>What kind of audience information is this?</p><p><b>Users share food photos on Instagram during the meal.</b></p>","options":["Psychographic","Demographic","Behaviour"],"correct":2,"why":"Sharing photos is an action they take, so it is a behaviour.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the SCAMPER prompt","q":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is using SCAMPER to improve the Port of Spain Public Library's old storytime videos. Match each SCAMPER prompt to his idea.</p>","left":["Substitute","Combine","Adapt","Modify","Put to another use","Eliminate","Reverse / Rearrange"],"right":["Remove the long title sequence at the start","Replace the live-action narrator with an animated pelican character","Merge the storytime video with a short quiz at the end","Borrow the \"choose your path\" idea from interactive video games","Use the recorded audio as a podcast for car journeys","Start with the story's exciting ending, then go back to the beginning","Shorten each 20-minute video into 3-minute episodes"],"correct":[1,2,3,6,4,0,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide? (1)","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is planning a website for the Punda Market Collective in Willemstad. Sort each planning item.</p><p>Which planning document does this belong in?</p><p><b>A quick pencil drawing showing the menu bar at the top and three product boxes below.</b></p>","options":["Style guide","Rough sketch","Mood board"],"correct":1,"why":"A loose layout drawing that explores structure is a rough sketch.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide? (2)","q":"<p>Which planning document does this belong in?</p><p><b>Photos of spice stalls, woven baskets and sunset colours pinned together.</b></p>","options":["Rough sketch","Style guide","Mood board"],"correct":2,"why":"A collage of inspiring images that captures a feeling is a mood board.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide? (3)","q":"<p>Which planning document does this belong in?</p><p><b>Heading font: Montserrat Bold, 32 px; body: Open Sans, 16 px.</b></p>","options":["Rough sketch","Mood board","Style guide"],"correct":2,"why":"Exact typography rules belong in the style guide.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide? (4)","q":"<p>Which planning document does this belong in?</p><p><b>Primary colour: #E07A1F; never place the logo on a busy photo.</b></p>","options":["Style guide","Rough sketch","Mood board"],"correct":0,"why":"Exact colour values and logo rules are style guide content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide? (5)","q":"<p>Which planning document does this belong in?</p><p><b>Words like \"fresh\", \"bilingual\" and \"market morning\" beside fabric textures.</b></p>","options":["Style guide","Rough sketch","Mood board"],"correct":2,"why":"Mood words and textures set the direction, so this is a mood board.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide? (6)","q":"<p>Which planning document does this belong in?</p><p><b>Eight tiny thumbnail layouts drawn in eight minutes.</b></p>","options":["Style guide","Rough sketch","Mood board"],"correct":1,"why":"Thumbnails are small rough sketches for exploring many layouts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide? (7)","q":"<p>Which planning document does this belong in?</p><p><b>All buttons have rounded corners and 12 px padding.</b></p>","options":["Style guide","Rough sketch","Mood board"],"correct":0,"why":"Rules for UI components keep the site consistent, so they belong in the style guide.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide? (8)","q":"<p>Which planning document does this belong in?</p><p><b>A loose drawing of the homepage hero area with notes saying \"photo here\".</b></p>","options":["Style guide","Mood board","Rough sketch"],"correct":2,"why":"An annotated, unfinished layout is a rough sketch.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"What belongs in a storyboard frame?","q":"<p>Malik is checking a student's storyboard for a 30-second Emancipation Day video for the Guyana Heritage Collective. Pick the details that a well-annotated storyboard frame should include.</p><p>Select ALL the details that belong in a storyboard frame annotation.</p>","options":["Scene 2, 0:06 to 0:12","Narration: \"We remember, we rise.\"","The client's invoice and payment terms","The total budget for the whole project","Transition: slow dissolve to Scene 3","Close-up of hands beating a drum, low angle","On-screen text: \"1 July: Emancipation Day\"","A list of every team member's phone number"],"correct":[0, 1, 4, 5, 6],"why":"Correct. A storyboard frame shows number and timing, the visual with shot type and angle, audio, on-screen text and the transition. Budgets, contacts and invoices belong in other planning documents."},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the production plan (1)","q":"<p>Jerome, Malik and Keisha at the Island Pulse Media are planning a promotional video for the English Harbour Reef Dive Centre. Answer each question about their planning.</p><p>On their Gantt chart, \"Client approves storyboard\" is shown as a diamond with no length. This is a...</p>","options":["dependency","milestone","work plan"],"correct":1,"why":"A milestone is a key checkpoint with no duration, often drawn as a diamond.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the production plan (2)","q":"<p>Editing cannot begin until underwater filming is complete. This relationship is called a...</p>","options":["mood board","buffer","dependency"],"correct":2,"why":"When one task must wait for another, the tasks are dependent.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the production plan (3)","q":"<p>The document that lists each task, who is responsible and the deadline is the...</p>","options":["style guide","work plan","storyboard"],"correct":1,"why":"A work plan assigns tasks, people, resources and deadlines.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the production plan (4)","q":"<p>Filming is booked for 7 a.m. Saturday at the reef, with a back-up date the next weekend. Where is this recorded?</p>","options":["the mood board","the production schedule","the problem statement"],"correct":1,"why":"The production schedule gives the detailed when, where and who of production.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the production plan (5)","q":"<p>The divers who appear in the video must sign a form allowing their image to be used. This is a...</p>","options":["safety briefing","shot list","model release form"],"correct":2,"why":"A model release form gives legal permission to use a person's image, a key legal consideration in pre-production.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the production plan (6)","q":"<p>Two extra days are added before the deadline in case of rough seas. This is...</p>","options":["the critical path","buffer time","a milestone"],"correct":1,"why":"Buffer time protects the deadline from delays such as bad weather.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Factors affecting creation and the virtuous cycle","q":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing problems from a shoot for the Holetown Food Festival. Match each problem to its factor, then complete the virtuous cycle links.</p>","left":["Technical factors","Creative direction","Budget constraints","Time constraints","Legal and ethical factors","External conditions","Product and Optimise","Strategy and Biz Dev"],"right":["Support media in the virtuous cycle","Diners' faces could not be shown without image rights cleared","The old microphone picked up wind noise, and the slow connection delayed the upload","A tropical storm warning moved the outdoor shoot","The client wanted a bright, upbeat tone with the festival's brand colours","Enhance technology in the virtuous cycle","There was no money to hire a professional chef as presenter","A two-day deadline rushed the planning and lowered the quality"],"correct":[2,4,6,7,1,3,5,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pre-production for a Carnival app (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a junior designer at Island Pulse Media. Carnival Village Committee wants a digital product to help people enjoy Trinidad and Tobago Carnival. Zara asks you to lead pre-production. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The committee chair says: \"Just build us an app with everything on it, fast.\" What do you do first?</p>","options":["Hold a short meeting to agree the brief: the problem, the users, the goal, the budget and the deadline.","Open Android Studio and start coding an app straight away."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Acceptance and definition come first; a clear brief prevents wasted work.","Jumping to production skips analysis and definition, so you may build the wrong product."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pre-production for a Carnival app (step 2)","q":"<p>You survey 60 residents and visitors. Most want to know when and where bands and jump-ups pass, and many have limited data. Which problem statement do you write?</p>","options":["\"The committee needs a beautiful app with a 3D Carnival City.\"","\"Carnival-goers need a quick, low-data way to know where and when events happen because they miss events and waste data searching social media.\""],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This names a solution and ignores the users' data limits found in your research.","Strong. It names users, need and insight, and keeps the solution open."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pre-production for a Carnival app (step 3)","q":"<p>In ideation your team suggests a full app, a WhatsApp alert channel, a printable QR map and a live TikTok feed. You select a low-data web map with WhatsApp alerts. What next?</p>","options":["Create rough sketches, a mood board and a style guide, a storyboard for the 30-second promo, and a Gantt chart with milestones and buffer time.","Skip planning: Carnival is only six weeks away."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Planning designs and timelines before production keeps the team aligned and on time.","A tight deadline is exactly why you need a schedule. Without one, tasks clash and deadlines slip."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pre-production for a Carnival app (step 4)","q":"<p>Maya has a clickable Figma prototype of the map. Before full development, what do you do?</p>","options":["Ask the team if they like it, then launch.","Test it with eight real Carnival-goers on their own phones, observe them, collect feedback and fix problems."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["The team are not the target audience. Only real users can validate the idea.","Perfect. Validating with target users catches problems while they are still cheap to fix."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Why validate a prototype?","q":"<p>The Bridgetown Fit Club in Barbados has a paper prototype of a workout app. The owner wants to skip testing and go straight to full development. Which is the BEST justification for validating the prototype first?</p>","options":["Testing makes the app look more professional.","Testing with real members reveals usability problems and confirms demand while changes are still cheap, reducing the risk of building an app nobody uses.","Testing is required by app stores before any app is published.","Testing means the team will not need a Gantt chart."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Validation is about whether the idea works for users, not about appearance.","Correct. This links validation to cost, risk and user needs, which is what \"justify\" requires.","App stores review apps for policy and security, but they do not require prototype testing with users.","Validation and scheduling are separate pre-production tasks. You still need a schedule."]}]},{"id":"assess_mw","title":"Module 1 · Unit 2 quiz: Elements of visual design and colour theory","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 2: Elements of visual design and colour theory. 28 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the element of design","q":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing designs for the Grace Bay Beach Festival. Match each element to the design decision that uses it.</p>","left":["Line","Shape","Form","Space","Texture","Value","Colour"],"right":["Diagonal stripes that lead the eye towards the ticket button","A flat circle and triangle combined into a simple sun-and-sail logo","A shaded, rotating 3D steel pan in the opening animation","Wide empty margins around the headline so it can breathe","A photo-realistic sand pattern behind the schedule","A very dark background so the white date stands out","Hot pink and turquoise chosen to feel fun and tropical"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour wheel quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Students at the Sol Studio in Georgetown are mixing paint for a mural of Kaieteur Falls. Answer each question using the traditional RYB colour wheel.</p><p>Mixing yellow and blue in equal amounts gives...</p>","options":["orange","violet","green"],"correct":2,"why":"Yellow + blue = green, a secondary colour.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour wheel quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>Which of these is a tertiary colour?</p>","options":["blue-green","red","orange"],"correct":0,"why":"Blue-green is a primary (blue) mixed with a neighbouring secondary (green).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour wheel quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>How many tertiary colours are on a standard 12-part wheel?</p>","options":["twelve","three","six"],"correct":2,"why":"There are six tertiaries, one between each primary and secondary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour wheel quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>Which group contains only warm colours?</p>","options":["red, blue, green","red, orange, yellow","blue, green, violet"],"correct":1,"why":"Reds, oranges and yellows are warm; they feel energetic and seem to advance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour wheel quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>A vivid turquoise is made duller and greyer. Which property has changed?</p>","options":["temperature","hue","saturation"],"correct":2,"why":"Saturation is intensity. The hue stays turquoise, but it becomes less pure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour wheel quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Red, yellow and blue are primary on the RYB wheel because...</p>","options":["they cannot be made by mixing other colours","they are always used in logos","they are the brightest colours"],"correct":0,"why":"Primary colours are the starting colours that cannot be mixed from others in that model.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Name that harmony rule","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is choosing palettes for five client websites. Match each harmony rule to its palette description.</p>","left":["Complementary","Analogous","Triadic","Split-complementary","Tetradic","Monochromatic"],"right":["Navy, blue and sky blue only, for a calm insurance company page","Two opposite pairs, red and green plus orange and blue, for a busy Carnival band site","Red, yellow and blue, evenly spaced, for a children's reading app","Violet with yellow-orange and yellow-green for a jazz festival poster","Yellow, yellow-orange and orange, side by side, for a sunset cruise site","Blue and orange, directly opposite on the wheel, for a dive shop's \"Book a dive\" button"],"correct":[5,4,2,3,1,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tint, shade or tone? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is building a colour palette for the Sorrel Express delivery app, starting from one rich sorrel red. Sort each variation.</p><p>How was this variation of the base red created?</p><p><b>A soft pink used for the app's background cards.</b></p>","options":["Tone (added grey)","Shade (added black)","Tint (added white)"],"correct":2,"why":"Pink is red lightened with white, so it is a tint.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tint, shade or tone? (2)","q":"<p>How was this variation of the base red created?</p><p><b>A deep wine colour for the header bar.</b></p>","options":["Tone (added grey)","Shade (added black)","Tint (added white)"],"correct":1,"why":"Darkening red with black gives a shade such as wine or maroon.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tint, shade or tone? (3)","q":"<p>How was this variation of the base red created?</p><p><b>A dusty, muted rose for disabled buttons.</b></p>","options":["Shade (added black)","Tone (added grey)","Tint (added white)"],"correct":1,"why":"Muted and greyish means grey was added, so it is a tone.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tint, shade or tone? (4)","q":"<p>How was this variation of the base red created?</p><p><b>A very pale blush for the splash-screen glow.</b></p>","options":["Tone (added grey)","Shade (added black)","Tint (added white)"],"correct":2,"why":"Very light versions of a hue are tints.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tint, shade or tone? (5)","q":"<p>How was this variation of the base red created?</p><p><b>Near-black maroon for text that must be dark but still warm.</b></p>","options":["Tint (added white)","Tone (added grey)","Shade (added black)"],"correct":2,"why":"Lots of black added to red produces a deep shade.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tint, shade or tone? (6)","q":"<p>How was this variation of the base red created?</p><p><b>A greyish red that feels calm and sophisticated.</b></p>","options":["Tint (added white)","Shade (added black)","Tone (added grey)"],"correct":2,"why":"Grey reduces saturation without just lightening or darkening, which creates a tone.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tint, shade or tone? (7)","q":"<p>How was this variation of the base red created?</p><p><b>Strawberry milk pink for a \"special offer\" sticker.</b></p>","options":["Tone (added grey)","Shade (added black)","Tint (added white)"],"correct":2,"why":"Red plus white makes a light pink tint.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tint, shade or tone? (8)","q":"<p>How was this variation of the base red created?</p><p><b>A dark burgundy shadow beneath the logo.</b></p>","options":["Shade (added black)","Tone (added grey)","Tint (added white)"],"correct":0,"why":"Burgundy is a dark shade of red.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Screen or print?","q":"<p>Regent Street Bookshop in Georgetown has a logo with the hex colour #00A86B. The owner wants the logo printed on 500 paper bags and also shown on the shop's Instagram page. Which statement is MOST accurate?</p>","options":["The same hex code works perfectly on screens and paper because colours are universal.","The printer will use red, yellow and blue ink to match the hex code exactly.","The hex code is an RGB screen value, so it is right for Instagram, but the logo should be converted to a CMYK value and proof-checked before printing because ink mixes subtractively.","The logo should be designed in CMYK for Instagram because CMYK has more colours."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Screens emit light (RGB) and paper reflects light through ink (CMYK), so colours can shift between them.","Commercial printing uses cyan, magenta, yellow and black (CMYK), not RYB paint primaries.","Correct. Hex codes describe additive RGB light for screens. Printing uses subtractive CMYK ink, so the colour must be converted and checked.","Instagram is viewed on screens, which use RGB. RGB generally has a wider gamut of bright colours than CMYK."]},{"t":"match","title":"Create and save a new colour in GIMP","q":"<p>In a Paper 02 practical, you must create a custom colour sampled from a photo of a Montserrat sunset and save it for reuse. Put the GIMP steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Open the sunset photo in GIMP (File > Open).","Select the Color Picker tool (O) and click the orange area of the sky to sample it.","Click the foreground colour swatch to open the Change Foreground Color dialog.","Fine-tune the colour with the HSV or RGB sliders and note the hex value in the HTML notation box.","Click OK, then add the colour to a new palette in the Palettes dialog (Windows > Dockable Dialogs > Palettes).","Apply the colour to your design and export as PNG with a clear file name, such as Candidate123_Sunset_Palette.png."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour psychology for clients (1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has five new clients across the Caribbean. Choose the most suitable main colour for each brief.</p><p>Calm Waters counselling app for teenagers in Barbados wants users to feel safe and relaxed.</p>","options":["bright red","soft blue and green","black and neon yellow"],"correct":1,"why":"Soft cool colours are associated with calm and trust; red and neon would feel urgent.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour psychology for clients (2)","q":"<p>Flash Sale Friday campaign for a Spanish Town electronics shop needs urgency.</p>","options":["beige","pastel lavender","bold red with yellow accents"],"correct":2,"why":"Red signals urgency and energy, and yellow grabs attention.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour psychology for clients (3)","q":"<p>The Nature Isle Treks company in Dominica promotes rainforest hikes.</p>","options":["hot pink","greens and earthy browns","silver and black"],"correct":1,"why":"Greens and earth tones connect directly with nature and the rainforest.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour psychology for clients (4)","q":"<p>Velvet Nights luxury spa in Rodney Bay, St. Lucia, wants a premium, elegant feel.</p>","options":["deep purple, black and gold","primary red, yellow and blue","lime green and orange"],"correct":0,"why":"Purple, black and gold suggest luxury and sophistication; primary triads feel playful.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour psychology for clients (5)","q":"<p>A designer assumes white will mean \"pure\" to every visitor of a global wedding site. What is the risk?</p>","options":["white is linked to mourning in some cultures","white cannot be shown on screens","none: colours mean the same everywhere"],"correct":0,"why":"Colour meanings vary by culture, so a global audience needs testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the colour problems","q":"<p>Maya is auditing the Port Side Eats food-ordering website before launch. Several colour decisions could cause problems on screen.</p><p>Select ALL the colour decisions that will cause legibility or accessibility problems.</p>","options":["Error messages in red with a warning icon and the word \"Error\".","Navy body text on a very pale cream background.","An orange accent button on an otherwise calm blue page.","White \"Order now\" text on a dark teal button.","Pale yellow menu text on a white background.","Order status shown only as a red or green dot, with no words.","Bright red headings on a bright green banner of similar lightness.","Light grey prices on a white background to look minimal."],"correct":[4, 5, 6, 7],"why":"Correct. Low contrast (yellow or light grey on white), colour-only meaning and vibrating complements all hurt legibility and accessibility. Dark-on-light text, a contrasting accent and colour plus icon plus words all work well."},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour a hero and a villain (step 1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media is making a mobile game, <i>Guardians of the Salt Pond</i>, set in Bonaire. Zara asks you to choose the colours for the hero, a young pelican named Pip, and the villain, a pollution monster called Smog. Make the best decisions.</p><p>First, Pip the hero. Players are aged 9 to 14 and should trust and cheer for Pip instantly. Which palette do you choose?</p>","options":["Warm golden-yellow and soft sea blue with white highlights; light values and rounded shapes.","Dark grey and black with red eyes to look cool."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Light values, hopeful yellows and trustworthy blues read clearly as a hero.","Dark values and red eyes are villain signals. Players would distrust Pip."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour a hero and a villain (step 2)","q":"<p>Now Smog the villain. Smog must look threatening and must stand out clearly from Pip in every scene. Which palette?</p>","options":["Deep purple and charcoal shades with a sickly yellow-green glow, sharp jagged shapes.","The same golden-yellow and sea blue as Pip, but slightly darker."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Dark shades, tense accents and spiky shapes read as danger and contrast strongly with Pip.","If hero and villain share a palette, players cannot tell them apart quickly in action."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour a hero and a villain (step 3)","q":"<p>The game background is a bright blue sky and turquoise salt pond. Malik worries Pip's blue may blend in. What do you do?</p>","options":["Run a greyscale and contrast check, then give Pip a darker outline and more yellow so Pip stands out from the background.","Ignore it; players will find Pip eventually."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Smart. Checking value contrast against the background keeps the hero readable.","Poor contrast with the background makes the hero hard to follow, especially on small phone screens."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour a hero and a villain (step 4)","q":"<p>Keisha notes that some players may have red-green colour vision deficiency. Smog's attacks are shown only by turning red. What do you change?</p>","options":["Add a shape change and an icon to each attack, so it does not rely on colour alone, and record all hex codes in the style guide.","Make the red brighter."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Perfect. Colour plus shape and icons makes the game accessible, and the style guide keeps it consistent.","Brighter red still relies on colour alone, which some players cannot distinguish."]}]},{"id":"assess_t9","title":"Module 1 · Unit 3 quiz: Composition and principles of interactive design","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 3: Composition and principles of interactive design. 41 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Thirds or golden? Quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is framing shots for a tourism reel about English Harbour in Antigua. Answer each question.</p><p>Malik places the yacht exactly where the top-right grid lines cross in a 3 by 3 grid. He is using...</p>","options":["radial balance","a golden spiral","the rule of thirds"],"correct":2,"why":"The intersections of a 3 by 3 grid are the power points of the rule of thirds.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Thirds or golden? Quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>The golden ratio is approximately...</p>","options":["1 : 1.618","1 : 1.777","1 : 1.333"],"correct":0,"why":"Phi is about 1.618. 1.333 is the 4:3 aspect ratio and 1.777 is 16:9, which are traps.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Thirds or golden? Quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>A point about 61.8% along a line, dividing it in the golden ratio, is called the...</p>","options":["vanishing point","golden section","golden rectangle"],"correct":1,"why":"The golden section is a point (or line) that divides a length in the golden ratio.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Thirds or golden? Quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>He wants the sea horizon to emphasise a dramatic sunset sky. Where should the horizon go?</p>","options":["exactly in the centre","on the upper third line","on the lower third line"],"correct":2,"why":"A low horizon gives about two thirds of the frame to the sky, which is the part he wants to emphasise.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Thirds or golden? Quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>A rectangle that leaves another rectangle of the same proportions when a square is removed is a...</p>","options":["safe frame","thirds grid","golden rectangle"],"correct":2,"why":"That self-repeating property defines the golden rectangle and produces the golden spiral.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Thirds or golden? Quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>A jet-ski rider is moving left to right. Where should Malik leave the most space?</p>","options":["behind the rider, on the left","in front of the rider, on the right","above the rider"],"correct":1,"why":"Lead room in front of a moving or looking subject gives it somewhere to go and feels natural.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Thirds or golden? Quick-fire (7)","q":"<p>On a 24 in tall poster, where does the golden section put the headline, measured from the top?</p>","options":["about 8 in","about 12 in","about 9.2 in"],"correct":2,"why":"24 divided by 1.618 is about 14.8 in; 24 minus 14.8 leaves about 9.2 in from the top. 8 in is the thirds line and 12 in is the dead centre.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the prominence techniques","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media wrote these notes about a Carnival poster for Trinidad and Tobago. Some describe techniques that make the main dancer more prominent; others weaken her prominence.</p><p>Select every note that INCREASES the prominence of the dancer.</p>","options":["Every element on the poster is lit equally brightly.","A line of street lights along the parade route leads towards her.","Her costume is bright gold against a deep blue night background.","The background crowd is blurred with a shallow depth of field.","The dancer is placed on the left power point of the thirds grid.","Six sponsor logos of the same size as the dancer are placed around her.","The event title, the dancer and a large map compete in the centre.","She fills about half of the frame height, much larger than anything else."],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 4, 7],"why":"Well spotted. Placement, colour contrast, focus, leading lines and scale all push the eye to one clear point of interest."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the technique to its effect","q":"<p>Jerome and Malik are planning shots for a short film set in Castries and Soufrière. Match each technique to the effect it creates.</p>","left":["Low angle","High angle","Dutch angle","Bird's-eye view","Low-key lighting","Golden hour","One-point perspective","Back light (rim light)"],"right":["A drone shows the pattern of a whole Carnival band","The hurricane scene feels unstable and tense","The headmistress looks powerful and in charge","Warm, soft light for a romantic beach scene","Jeremie Street stretches towards a single vanishing point","The actor is separated from a dark background by a bright edge","A night scene full of mystery and deep shadow","A lost child in the market looks small and vulnerable"],"correct":[2,7,1,0,6,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Match each principle to its quick test","q":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media pins the seven quick tests above her desk. Match each layout principle to the question that checks it.</p>","left":["Repetition","Proximity","White space","Balance","Contrast","Alignment","Hierarchy"],"right":["Can you name the most important element within three seconds?","Do the elements share a common edge or centre line?","Can you read the body text easily, with at least 4.5 : 1 contrast?","Does one side of the page feel heavier than the other?","Does each element have room to breathe?","Can you see which items form groups before reading the labels?","Do the same elements look the same wherever they appear?"],"correct":[6,5,4,3,2,1,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which layout principle? (1)","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is redesigning the website of a Grand Anse dive shop. Sort each change by the principle it mainly applies.</p><p>Which principle does this change mainly apply?</p><p><b>Every page uses the same coral-orange buttons and the same heading font.</b></p>","options":["Alignment","Proximity","Hierarchy","Repetition"],"correct":3,"why":"Reusing colours and fonts across pages is repetition, which builds unity and brand identity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which layout principle? (2)","q":"<p>Which principle does this change mainly apply?</p><p><b>The price, duration and depth of each dive are grouped tightly under its photo.</b></p>","options":["Proximity","Hierarchy","Repetition","Alignment"],"correct":0,"why":"Placing related information close together is proximity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which layout principle? (3)","q":"<p>Which principle does this change mainly apply?</p><p><b>All text blocks and images now line up on the same left margin.</b></p>","options":["Hierarchy","Alignment","Repetition","Proximity"],"correct":1,"why":"Lining elements up on a common edge is alignment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which layout principle? (4)","q":"<p>Which principle does this change mainly apply?</p><p><b>The headline \"Dive the Wrecks of Gros Islet\" is now three times larger than the body text.</b></p>","options":["Proximity","Hierarchy","Alignment","Repetition"],"correct":1,"why":"Using size to show what matters most is hierarchy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which layout principle? (5)","q":"<p>Which principle does this change mainly apply?</p><p><b>The \"Book a dive\" button is bold, bright and placed above the details.</b></p>","options":["Hierarchy","Proximity","Alignment","Repetition"],"correct":0,"why":"Weight, colour and position signal importance, so this is hierarchy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which layout principle? (6)","q":"<p>Which principle does this change mainly apply?</p><p><b>Each section uses the same wave-shaped divider.</b></p>","options":["Alignment","Repetition","Hierarchy","Proximity"],"correct":1,"why":"A repeated visual motif is repetition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which layout principle? (7)","q":"<p>Which principle does this change mainly apply?</p><p><b>The contact phone, email and address are moved together into one box.</b></p>","options":["Repetition","Hierarchy","Alignment","Proximity"],"correct":3,"why":"Grouping related contact details is proximity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which layout principle? (8)","q":"<p>Which principle does this change mainly apply?</p><p><b>Captions now start at the exact left edge of their photos.</b></p>","options":["Repetition","Hierarchy","Proximity","Alignment"],"correct":3,"why":"Matching edges is alignment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a type of balance","q":"<p>The Montserrat Heritage Circle wants a formal invitation for an Emancipation Day ceremony. The design must feel dignified, calm and orderly, with the foundation's crest at the top centre and the text mirrored around a central axis. Which type of balance BEST meets this brief?</p>","options":["Symmetrical balance","Radial balance","Mosaic balance","Asymmetrical balance"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Mirroring elements around a central axis gives formal, calm, dignified balance, which suits a solemn ceremony.","Radial balance radiates from a centre point. The brief describes elements mirrored around a vertical axis, not radiating outward.","Mosaic balance spreads many equal elements with no focal point. The brief has a clear focal point: the crest.","Asymmetrical balance uses different weights on each side and feels dynamic and informal, not the dignified order the brief asks for."]},{"t":"match","title":"Build the environment frame in order","q":"<p>Malik is drawing the opening environment frame for an animated short set at the Port of Spain cruise port. Put his steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Read the brief and note audience, scene and mood","Draw the frame at the correct aspect ratio with a safe area","Draw the horizon on a third line and set the vanishing point","Add the thirds grid or golden section guides","Sketch foreground, middle ground and background on separate layers","Add leading lines and light the point of interest","Write frame notes (number, shot, move, duration, sound, transition) and export a PNG"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"What users see or what users do? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing a Carnival events app. Sort each note: is it about composition (what users see) or interaction (what users do)?</p><p>Is this note mainly about composition or interaction?</p><p><b>The J'ouvert headline is the largest element on the home screen.</b></p>","options":["Interaction (what users do)","Composition (what users see)"],"correct":1,"why":"Size and hierarchy decide what the eye sees first: composition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What users see or what users do? (2)","q":"<p>Is this note mainly about composition or interaction?</p><p><b>The band photo is placed on a power point with a blurred crowd behind.</b></p>","options":["Composition (what users see)","Interaction (what users do)"],"correct":0,"why":"Placement and depth of field are composition tools.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What users see or what users do? (3)","q":"<p>Is this note mainly about composition or interaction?</p><p><b>The Buy button turns into a spinner, then a green tick, after it is tapped.</b></p>","options":["Interaction (what users do)","Composition (what users see)"],"correct":0,"why":"A response to the user's action is feedback: interaction.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What users see or what users do? (4)","q":"<p>Is this note mainly about composition or interaction?</p><p><b>The Back arrow is in the same top-left corner on every screen.</b></p>","options":["Interaction (what users do)","Composition (what users see)"],"correct":0,"why":"Controls that work the same way everywhere are about consistency of interaction.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What users see or what users do? (5)","q":"<p>Is this note mainly about composition or interaction?</p><p><b>The date, time and venue sit together in one block.</b></p>","options":["Composition (what users see)","Interaction (what users do)"],"correct":0,"why":"Grouping by proximity organises what users see.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What users see or what users do? (6)","q":"<p>Is this note mainly about composition or interaction?</p><p><b>Gold text on a navy background passes the 4.5 : 1 contrast check.</b></p>","options":["Interaction (what users do)","Composition (what users see)"],"correct":1,"why":"Contrast for readability is a layout (composition) principle.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What users see or what users do? (7)","q":"<p>Is this note mainly about composition or interaction?</p><p><b>Tapping \"Pay XCD 45.00\" does exactly what the label says.</b></p>","options":["Interaction (what users do)","Composition (what users see)"],"correct":0,"why":"An outcome label lets users predict what an action will do: interaction.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What users see or what users do? (8)","q":"<p>Is this note mainly about composition or interaction?</p><p><b>First-time users get three short onboarding tips before they start.</b></p>","options":["Composition (what users see)","Interaction (what users do)"],"correct":1,"why":"Onboarding helps users learn what to do: learnability, an interaction principle.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match each principle to its silent question","q":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media tapes the five silent questions beside his screen while he tests apps. Match each interactive design principle to the question users silently ask, then to one of its rules.</p>","left":["Consistency","Perceivability","Learnability","Predictability","Feedback","Touch targets of 44 x 44 pt or 48 x 48 dp","A button labelled \"Pay XCD 45.00\""],"right":["Will this work the way it did last time?","What happens if I press this?","Can I see it, and can I tap it?","A perceivability rule: big enough to see and tap","How do I do this the first time?","A predictability rule: label buttons with their outcome","Did that work?"],"correct":[0,2,4,1,6,3,5]},{"t":"ma","title":"Good or poor example?","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media has collected examples from apps and websites for a client workshop. Some are good interactive design; some are poor.</p><p>Select every GOOD example of interactive design.</p>","options":["Directions appear as grey text that looks like an ordinary link.","A screen of 40 unlabelled icons that users must memorise.","A bookmark icon quietly shares your list publicly.","WhatsApp uses the same message bubbles and green send button throughout.","A school portal shows Submit as a green button on one page and as a grey link on another.","Google Maps shows a large blue Start button.","Gmail offers Undo right after you send an email.","Duolingo teaches through short guided lessons with clear labels.","Heritage Collective ticket site charged customers three times with no confirmation."],"correct":[3, 5, 6, 7],"why":"Spot on. WhatsApp (consistency), Google Maps (perceivability), Duolingo (learnability) and Gmail Undo (predictability) are the good examples; the Heritage Collective site is a lesson in missing feedback."},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interactive design principle (1)","q":"<p>Jerome is testing a beta of the \"Doubles Locator\" app, which shows doubles vendors in Port of Spain and San Fernando. Sort each observation by the principle it mainly shows.</p><p>Which principle does this observation mainly show?</p><p><b>The orange \"Order\" button looks and works the same on every stall page.</b></p>","options":["Learnability","Consistency","Predictability","Perceivability"],"correct":1,"why":"Same look and behaviour across screens is consistency.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interactive design principle (2)","q":"<p>Which principle does this observation mainly show?</p><p><b>Tappable items have a shadow and a clear border, so users can tell they are buttons.</b></p>","options":["Consistency","Learnability","Predictability","Perceivability"],"correct":3,"why":"Making interactive elements visibly interactive is perceivability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interactive design principle (3)","q":"<p>Which principle does this observation mainly show?</p><p><b>A first-time visitor finds and orders a plate of ribs in under a minute with no help.</b></p>","options":["Predictability","Consistency","Perceivability","Learnability"],"correct":3,"why":"Being easy to pick up quickly is learnability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interactive design principle (4)","q":"<p>Which principle does this observation mainly show?</p><p><b>Users correctly guess that the map pin icon will open directions.</b></p>","options":["Consistency","Learnability","Perceivability","Predictability"],"correct":3,"why":"Users knowing in advance what an action will do is predictability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interactive design principle (5)","q":"<p>Which principle does this observation mainly show?</p><p><b>The swipe gestures match those in other common apps users already know.</b></p>","options":["Predictability","Learnability","Consistency","Perceivability"],"correct":2,"why":"Following platform conventions is external consistency.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interactive design principle (6)","q":"<p>Which principle does this observation mainly show?</p><p><b>Icons are labelled with words as well as pictures, and the text passes a contrast check.</b></p>","options":["Perceivability","Learnability","Predictability","Consistency"],"correct":0,"why":"Making information easy to see and understand is perceivability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interactive design principle (7)","q":"<p>Which principle does this observation mainly show?</p><p><b>Three short tips on first launch explain the main screens, and users still remember them a week later.</b></p>","options":["Consistency","Learnability","Predictability","Perceivability"],"correct":1,"why":"Quick to learn and easy to remember is learnability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interactive design principle (8)","q":"<p>Which principle does this observation mainly show?</p><p><b>\"Cancel\" always closes a pop-up without saving, exactly as users expect.</b></p>","options":["Predictability","Perceivability","Consistency","Learnability"],"correct":0,"why":"Behaving as users expect is predictability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fixing the ferry booking app (step 1)","q":"<p>A ferry company runs trips between Kingstown, Bequia and Union Island. Its booking app has poor reviews. Zara asks you, the Island Pulse Media designer, to fix it using the principles of interactive design.</p><p>Reviews say: \"I tapped Pay and nothing happened, so I tapped again and was charged twice.\" What is your first fix?</p>","options":["Make the Pay button bigger and redder.","Disable the Pay button after one tap, show a spinner, then a clear confirmation screen and email receipt."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Size helps perceivability, but users still would not know whether the payment worked.","Good. The core problem is missing feedback; immediate, clear feedback stops repeat taps."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fixing the ferry booking app (step 2)","q":"<p>Next complaint: \"On the booking page Back means previous step, but on the payment page Back logs you out.\" What do you do?</p>","options":["Add a warning pop-up on the payment page explaining that Back logs you out.","Make Back always return to the previous step on every screen, and place it in the same position each time."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A warning patches the symptom. The control still behaves inconsistently and unpredictably.","Correct. This fixes consistency, which also restores predictability."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fixing the ferry booking app (step 3)","q":"<p>Older passengers say the seat map is confusing: available and taken seats are both pale grey, and the icons have no labels. Your fix?</p>","options":["Keep the minimalist look; younger users like it, and older users will learn eventually.","Change taken seats to red and available to green, with no other change.","Use strongly contrasting colours plus a symbol for taken seats, add text labels and a short legend, and test with older users."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Style over usability excludes users. Perceivability and learnability matter for everyone.","Better, but colour alone fails users with red-green colour blindness, and unlabelled icons still hurt learnability.","Excellent. You improved perceivability (contrast, not colour alone) and learnability (labels, legend), then validated with real users."]}]},{"id":"assess_y1","title":"Module 1 · Unit 4 quiz: Impact and motivations for indigenous digital products","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 4: Impact and motivations for indigenous digital products. 36 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Where does culture show up? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is checking a draft Sint Maarten web video. For each detail, choose where culture shows up.</p><p>The narrator speaks in a natural Sint Maarten English accent, using local expressions.</p>","options":["Details of the country","Language","Appearance"],"correct":1,"why":"Accent and local expressions show culture through language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does culture show up? (2)","q":"<p>The video is filmed along the Philipsburg boardwalk with Great Bay behind.</p>","options":["The way events are celebrated","Content in the story","Environment (screen)"],"correct":2,"why":"The setting we see on screen is the environment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does culture show up? (3)","q":"<p>Dancers wear sequinned Carnival costumes and headpieces.</p>","options":["Language","Appearance","Details of the country"],"correct":1,"why":"How people look and dress shows culture through appearance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does culture show up? (4)","q":"<p>The plot follows a family rebuilding their home after Hurricane Irma.</p>","options":["Content in the story","Environment (screen)","Appearance"],"correct":0,"why":"The plot and its themes are the content in the story.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does culture show up? (5)","q":"<p>A segment celebrates a Sint Maarten athlete's national achievement.</p>","options":["Language","Appearance","Details of the country"],"correct":2,"why":"The achievement of the country belongs under details of the country.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does culture show up? (6)","q":"<p>The final scene shows a J'ouvert jump-up at dawn during Carnival.</p>","options":["The way events are celebrated","Environment (screen)","Content in the story"],"correct":0,"why":"How an event is celebrated in the country is its own place where culture shows up.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Technology shaping culture or culture shaping technology? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media collected these examples for a talk at a youth forum. Sort each one.</p><p>Which direction does this example mainly show?</p><p><b>Carnival parades in Port of Spain are now livestreamed to the diaspora.</b></p>","options":["Technology changing culture","Culture shaping digital creation"],"correct":0,"why":"A technology (livestreaming) has changed how a cultural event is experienced.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Technology shaping culture or culture shaping technology? (2)","q":"<p>Which direction does this example mainly show?</p><p><b>A game designer bases her villain on the Caribbean folklore figure of the soucouyant.</b></p>","options":["Culture shaping digital creation","Technology changing culture"],"correct":0,"why":"Folklore (culture) is shaping the content of a digital product.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Technology shaping culture or culture shaping technology? (3)","q":"<p>Which direction does this example mainly show?</p><p><b>Teenagers pick up American slang from streaming services more than from local radio.</b></p>","options":["Culture shaping digital creation","Technology changing culture"],"correct":1,"why":"Imported content delivered by technology is changing language: cultural homogenisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Technology shaping culture or culture shaping technology? (4)","q":"<p>Which direction does this example mainly show?</p><p><b>An animator writes all dialogue in Bajan with local expressions.</b></p>","options":["Technology changing culture","Culture shaping digital creation"],"correct":1,"why":"Local language and voice are shaping how the product is made.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Technology shaping culture or culture shaping technology? (5)","q":"<p>Which direction does this example mainly show?</p><p><b>A TikTok challenge makes an older soca song popular with a new generation.</b></p>","options":["Technology changing culture","Culture shaping digital creation"],"correct":0,"why":"A platform has transformed how and by whom a cultural product is enjoyed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Technology shaping culture or culture shaping technology? (6)","q":"<p>Which direction does this example mainly show?</p><p><b>A web designer picks a colour palette inspired by painted chattel houses.</b></p>","options":["Technology changing culture","Culture shaping digital creation"],"correct":1,"why":"Local aesthetics are driving design choices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Technology shaping culture or culture shaping technology? (7)","q":"<p>Which direction does this example mainly show?</p><p><b>Elders' stories are recorded and stored in an online heritage archive.</b></p>","options":["Culture shaping digital creation","Technology changing culture"],"correct":1,"why":"Digitisation is preserving culture: technology acting on culture.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Technology shaping culture or culture shaping technology? (8)","q":"<p>Which direction does this example mainly show?</p><p><b>An editor cuts a music video to the rhythm of steel pan.</b></p>","options":["Culture shaping digital creation","Technology changing culture"],"correct":0,"why":"A cultural rhythm shapes the editing pace of the product.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Run a screening and critique","q":"<p>Your class is critiquing a Caribbean animated short. Put the steps of the screening and critique method in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Record title, creator, country, year, format and platform","Research the intended audience and purpose","Watch once as an audience member and note first reactions","Watch again, pausing to take timed notes and screenshots","Apply each criterion: audience, authenticity, technical quality, storytelling, impact, motivation","Weigh strengths against weaknesses","Write a justified overall judgement supported by evidence"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the screening technique","q":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media screened a Caribbean short and wrote these notes. Match each screening technique to the note that uses it.</p>","left":["Details and background","Dialogue/speech","Sound effects","Camera angles","Target audience/market","Purpose"],"right":["\"Behind the actors you can see a painted chattel house and a snow-cone stall.\"","\"The characters speak natural Trinidadian Creole, and the jokes depend on it.\"","\"The car horn and minibus music suit the street scene; nothing feels out of place.\"","\"A low-angle shot makes the pastor look powerful, which makes his fall funnier.\"","\"It is aimed at adults in Trinidad and the diaspora who know the humour.\"","\"The main aim is to entertain, while gently mocking hypocrisy.\""],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the motivation","q":"<p>Creators make indigenous digital products for different reasons. Match each Caribbean creator's statement to the main motivation behind it.</p>","left":["Cultural identity","Preservation","Economic gain","Education","Social change","Tourism and nation branding","Exposure and career building"],"right":["\"Our music video challenges violence against women.\"","\"The app teaches students about emancipation in an interactive way.\"","\"If the game sells well on Steam, I can hire three local artists.\"","\"The digital campaign shows visitors the real Grenada beyond the beach.\"","\"Our grandparents' Anansi stories will be lost unless we record them now.\"","\"I want Guyanese children to hear their own accent in a cartoon.\"","\"Winning at a festival will get my work noticed by studios abroad.\""],"correct":[5,4,2,1,0,3,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Case study quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Test your recall of the case studies in this unit.</p><p>Cabbie Chronicles is an example of...</p>","options":["a Barbadian mobile app","a Trinidadian puppet series","Jamaican 2D animation"],"correct":2,"why":"Cabbie Chronicles is a Jamaican animated short by Allison and Anieph Latchman.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Case study quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>Santana spread mainly through...</p>","options":["a mobile game store","national TV networks","online sharing across the Caribbean and diaspora"],"correct":2,"why":"The puppet shorts went viral online, showing the power of direct online distribution.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Case study quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>Which film helped take reggae music to a world audience in the 1970s?</p>","options":["The Harder They Come","I'm Santana!","Sprinter"],"correct":0,"why":"The Harder They Come (1972) and its soundtrack exported reggae globally.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Case study quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>Duppy Detective Tashia draws its setting and characters mainly from...</p>","options":["American superhero comics","Caribbean folklore","European fairy tales"],"correct":1,"why":"It is rooted in Caribbean folklore, including Anansi, which makes it an indigenous product.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Case study quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>A developer builds a Carnival app because imported apps do not list local events. The main motivation is...</p>","options":["copying a foreign trend","avoiding the local market","solving a local problem"],"correct":2,"why":"Many indigenous apps exist to fill gaps that imported products ignore.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Case study quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Which is the BEST way to describe impact when you do not have reliable figures?</p>","options":["Invent a realistic number","Leave impact out","Say it was widely shared and suggest checking current figures"],"correct":2,"why":"Never invent statistics. Describe impact honestly and point to where figures can be checked.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid? (1)","q":"<p>Zara is building a playlist for a Dominica youth media workshop. Sort each product.</p><p>Is this product indigenous, imported or hybrid?</p><p><b>A Hollywood superhero film streamed on a global platform.</b></p>","options":["Hybrid","Indigenous","Imported"],"correct":2,"why":"Produced abroad for a global audience, with no local creative input: imported.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid? (2)","q":"<p>Is this product indigenous, imported or hybrid?</p><p><b>Cabbie Chronicles, made in Jamaica by Jamaican animators.</b></p>","options":["Hybrid","Indigenous","Imported"],"correct":1,"why":"Conceived and made locally from local culture: indigenous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid? (3)","q":"<p>Is this product indigenous, imported or hybrid?</p><p><b>A Roseau artist's bouyon track with K-pop-style choreography in the video.</b></p>","options":["Indigenous","Hybrid","Imported"],"correct":1,"why":"Local creator and genre combined with an imported style: hybrid.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid? (4)","q":"<p>Is this product indigenous, imported or hybrid?</p><p><b>A European studio's cartoon set on a Caribbean island, with no Caribbean writers.</b></p>","options":["Indigenous","Hybrid","Imported"],"correct":2,"why":"A Caribbean setting does not make it indigenous. It was created from outside: imported.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid? (5)","q":"<p>Is this product indigenous, imported or hybrid?</p><p><b>A St. Lucian app that teaches Kwéyòl, built by a local developer.</b></p>","options":["Imported","Hybrid","Indigenous"],"correct":2,"why":"Local language, local creator, local need: indigenous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid? (6)","q":"<p>Is this product indigenous, imported or hybrid?</p><p><b>A Japanese anime series dubbed into English.</b></p>","options":["Indigenous","Hybrid","Imported"],"correct":2,"why":"Produced in Japan; dubbing does not change who created it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid? (7)","q":"<p>Is this product indigenous, imported or hybrid?</p><p><b>A Trinidadian comic about Papa Bois drawn in a manga style.</b></p>","options":["Imported","Hybrid","Indigenous"],"correct":1,"why":"Local folklore and creator using an imported art style: hybrid.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid? (8)","q":"<p>Is this product indigenous, imported or hybrid?</p><p><b>A Dominica documentary on Hurricane Maria recovery by local filmmakers.</b></p>","options":["Imported","Hybrid","Indigenous"],"correct":2,"why":"Local creators telling a local story: indigenous.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the authenticity problems","q":"<p>Malik is reviewing the script notes for a foreign-made tourism cartoon about St. Lucia.</p><p>Select every note that shows a problem with authenticity or representation.</p>","options":["The voice actors are from St. Lucia and speak in natural local English and Kwéyòl.","The writers consulted local historians about Emancipation Day.","A Kalinago sacred symbol is used as decoration on a hotel sign without permission.","The island is shown only as beaches and hotels; no schools, homes or workplaces appear.","All the heroes are tourists; locals only help them or get in their way.","Every local character is a waiter, a bartender or a comic taxi driver.","Carnival is shown as random people dancing, with no reference to its history or its meaning.","Real locations such as Castries Market and the Pitons are drawn accurately."],"correct":[2, 3, 4, 5, 6],"why":"Well spotted. Limited roles, the tourist gaze, shallow use of tradition, sidelined local heroes and misuse of sacred symbols all damage authenticity."},{"t":"mc","title":"The best support for a new creator","q":"<p>A 17-year-old animator from Holetown, Barbados, has finished a five-minute 2D short about a Barbadian folk tale. She has no budget, no distributor and no industry contacts. Which step would BEST give her exposure, feedback and industry connections in the region?</p>","options":["Wait until she can afford to buy television airtime.","Keep the film private until it is as polished as a Hollywood feature.","Re-dub it with American accents so it appeals to imported-content audiences.","Submit the short to a regional festival such as Animae Caribe and share it online with a festival screening announcement."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Buying airtime is expensive and gives no industry feedback or networking. It ignores low-cost routes that exist now.","Waiting for big-studio polish delays her career. Festivals value authentic stories and emerging talent.","This removes the authenticity that makes the work distinctive, and does not give her exposure or contacts.","Correct. Festivals offer screenings, awards, workshops and contact with professionals, and festival selection gives credibility when she shares the film online."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pitching an indigenous product (step 1)","q":"<p>Island Pulse Media wants to create an animated web series based on Grenadian folklore and history. Zara asks you to lead the planning.</p><p>A foreign investor offers money on condition that the series is set on a generic \"tropical island\" with American-accented characters, to reach a bigger market. What do you do?</p>","options":["Explain that authenticity is the selling point, and look for funding that lets the team keep Grenadian voices and settings.","Accept the money and the conditions; a bigger audience matters more."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Authentic, specific stories stand out in a crowded global market and serve local audiences.","This turns an indigenous product into imported-style content and loses its purpose."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pitching an indigenous product (step 2)","q":"<p>You need to write the scripts. How do you research the folk tales and history?</p>","options":["Interview elders and local historians, consult cultural organisations, and hire local writers and voice actors.","Copy a few stories from a foreign website and let the team guess the accents."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Community consultation and local talent build authenticity and trust.","Second-hand sources and guessed accents lead to errors and stereotypes."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pitching an indigenous product (step 3)","q":"<p>The pilot episode is ready. How do you fund the full series and build an audience?</p>","options":["Sell all rights to the first buyer for a small fee.","Apply for regional creative-industry funding, approach local sponsors, enter the pilot in Animae Caribe and CaribbeanTales, and release it on YouTube with a community screening in St. George's.","Post it once on social media and hope it goes viral."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Giving away all rights means the team loses control and future income from its own culture.","Strong plan. You combined policy and funding routes, festivals and community support.","Going viral is not a strategy. Sustained support needs funding, festivals and community."]}]},{"id":"assess_133","title":"Module 1 · Unit 5 quiz: Digital media platforms and formats","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 5: Digital media platforms and formats. 36 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or format? (1)","q":"<p>The platform carries the message. The format is the form the message takes. Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is listing everything in a client's campaign. Sort each item.</p><p>Is this a platform or a format?</p><p><b>YouTube</b></p>","options":["Platform","Format"],"correct":0,"why":"YouTube carries video to its audience. It is a platform; the video file uploaded to it has a format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or format? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a platform or a format?</p><p><b>An MP4 promo video of Eagle Beach in Aruba</b></p>","options":["Format","Platform"],"correct":0,"why":"MP4 is the form the video takes. It needs a platform such as YouTube or Instagram to reach viewers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or format? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a platform or a format?</p><p><b>Instagram</b></p>","options":["Format","Platform"],"correct":1,"why":"Instagram is a social platform that carries photos and videos to followers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or format? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a platform or a format?</p><p><b>A PNG logo with a transparent background</b></p>","options":["Format","Platform"],"correct":0,"why":"PNG is an image format. The logo could appear on many platforms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or format? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a platform or a format?</p><p><b>Google Classroom</b></p>","options":["Platform","Format"],"correct":0,"why":"Google Classroom is an eLearning environment: a platform that carries lessons, files and assignments to students.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or format? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a platform or a format?</p><p><b>A PDF menu for a fish fry stall in Oistins</b></p>","options":["Format","Platform"],"correct":0,"why":"PDF is a document format. It could be shared on a website, WhatsApp or Facebook.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or format? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a platform or a format?</p><p><b>The Apple App Store</b></p>","options":["Platform","Format"],"correct":0,"why":"An app store is a platform that carries software to users.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or format? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a platform or a format?</p><p><b>An MP3 radio jingle</b></p>","options":["Platform","Format"],"correct":1,"why":"MP3 is an audio format. Spotify or a station's website would be the platform.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the platform type","q":"<p>Keisha is mapping every platform the Island Pulse Media agency uses for its clients. Match each platform type to the example that best shows its main purpose.</p>","left":["Search engine","Social platform","Knowledge platform","Application store","Marketplace","Media platform","Crowdsourcing platform","Infrastructure platform"],"right":["A visitor types \"best beach bar Negril\" and gets a ranked list of links","Maya has cloud servers and storage allocated automatically to host a client's website","A Kingston boutique posts outfit photos and replies to comments from followers","Volunteers write and correct an encyclopedia article about Port Royal","A guesthouse in Treasure Beach lists rooms and pays a fee on each booking","Jerome uploads a new version of a ferry timetable app for users to download","A Barbadian band streams its new album to listeners around the world","Drivers across Jamaica report a blocked road so others can avoid it"],"correct":[0,2,3,5,4,6,7,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"How platforms work: quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Use the details from the notes to answer each question about platforms used across the Caribbean.</p><p>A Bahamian student finds a website claiming the island has no hurricane season. What is the best advice about search engines?</p>","options":["Trust it if it is the first result","Check the source before you rely on it","Search engines only return verified facts"],"correct":1,"why":"A search result only points you toward information. The top result is not automatically reliable, so check who published it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How platforms work: quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>On a social platform, who mainly keeps the conversation moving?</p>","options":["The search engine","The platform's own staff","The users who comment, react and share"],"correct":2,"why":"The platform provides the space; its users keep the conversation moving through post, respond, share.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How platforms work: quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>A seller in Antigua lists a used laptop on a classified-listing site. How is payment usually arranged?</p>","options":["Buyer and seller usually arrange payment between themselves","Payment is made through an app store","The site always processes the payment"],"correct":0,"why":"Classified-listing platforms such as Craigslist often leave payment to buyers and sellers. Transaction-based marketplaces manage it through the site.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How platforms work: quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>Which is an example of an app store beyond mobile devices?</p>","options":["A cloud storage server","A search engine results page","A browser's catalogue of extensions and add-ons"],"correct":2,"why":"Browsers such as Chrome and Firefox offer stores or catalogues for extensions and add-ons.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How platforms work: quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>Malik posts a Blender tutorial on YouTube and viewers ask questions underneath. Which idea from the notes does this show?</p>","options":["Classified listings","Infrastructure allocation","A social layer surrounds the media"],"correct":2,"why":"Profiles and comments connect the video to its creator and audience, so a tutorial is both something to watch and a place to ask questions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How platforms work: quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Which pair work \"behind the scenes\"?</p>","options":["Search engines and marketplaces","Social and media platforms","Crowdsourcing and infrastructure platforms"],"correct":2,"why":"Crowdsourcing coordinates contributions from many people; infrastructure supplies servers, storage and networking.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Six types of digital platform","q":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is planning where a Bahamas heritage project will appear. Match each of the six platform types to the best example.</p>","left":["Apps","Websites","Dynamic platforms","eLearning environments","Curated content sources","Social media"],"right":["A Google Classroom where a CAPE class gets lessons and submits assignments","A YouTube channel where new Carnival videos and comments appear every day","Posting election updates on X for followers to reply to and repost","A guesthouse on Harbour Island with its own pages at a web address","A Pinterest board collecting the best costume designs for one theme","Google Earth installed on a tablet to explore Fort Fincastle from above"],"correct":[5,3,1,0,4,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Resolution, depth and compression quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is preparing files for a Spicemas promotion in Grenada. Answer each question.</p><p>Malik saves a poster as JPEG, opens it, edits it and saves again five times. The text edges look blotchy. Why?</p>","options":["JPEG converts the image to 256 colours","JPEG is lossy, so each re-save discards more data","JPEG reduces the resolution each time"],"correct":1,"why":"This is generation loss. JPEG re-compresses and discards data on every save. Keep a lossless master (XCF, PSD or PNG) and export a JPEG once at the end.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Resolution, depth and compression quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>An image uses 8 bits per pixel. How many colours can each pixel show?</p>","options":["256","8","about 16.7 million"],"correct":0,"why":"2 to the power of 8 is 256. That is why GIF and PNG-8 images are limited to 256 colours.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Resolution, depth and compression quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>Which change would REDUCE file size the most for a web banner photo?</p>","options":["Changing it from 24-bit to 32-bit","Scaling it from 4000 px wide to 1600 px wide and exporting as JPEG","Saving it as an uncompressed TIFF"],"correct":1,"why":"Fewer pixels plus lossy compression gives a much smaller file. Adding an alpha channel or saving uncompressed makes files bigger.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Resolution, depth and compression quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>A talking-head interview and a fast Carnival parade clip, both 1080p and one minute long, are exported at the same quality. Which is likely to be larger?</p>","options":["The parade clip","The interview","They will be exactly the same size"],"correct":0,"why":"Inter-frame compression stores only the changes between frames. Fast movement means many changes, so more data is needed to keep the same quality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Resolution, depth and compression quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>Roughly how big is a raw 1920 by 1080 image at 24-bit colour?</p>","options":["About 60 KB","About 6 MB","About 600 MB"],"correct":1,"why":"1920 x 1080 = 2,073,600 pixels; x 3 bytes = about 6.2 MB before compression.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Resolution, depth and compression quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Which pair are BOTH lossless?</p>","options":["PNG and ZIP","MP4 and GIF","JPEG and MP3"],"correct":0,"why":"PNG and ZIP rebuild the original data exactly. JPEG, MP3 and most MP4 video are lossy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which image or document format? (1)","q":"<p>Zara, creative director at Island Pulse Media, needs files for several Caribbean clients. Sort each job into the best format.</p><p>Which format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A photo of sunset at Seven Mile Beach in Negril for a hotel's website gallery</b></p>","options":["PNG","GIF","JPEG","PDF"],"correct":2,"why":"A continuous-tone photograph compresses well as JPEG with little visible loss, keeping the page fast.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which image or document format? (2)","q":"<p>Which format suits this job best?</p><p><b>The Bayside Water Sports logo to place over a coloured website header</b></p>","options":["PNG","PDF","JPEG","GIF"],"correct":0,"why":"The logo needs a transparent background and crisp edges. PNG supports an alpha channel; JPEG does not.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which image or document format? (3)","q":"<p>Which format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A three-frame waving flag loop to embed in an Emancipation Day email newsletter</b></p>","options":["JPEG","GIF","PNG","PDF"],"correct":1,"why":"Most email programs do not play video, but they do play GIF animation. Three simple frames suit the 256-colour palette.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which image or document format? (4)","q":"<p>Which format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A restaurant menu in Oranjestad that must print on A4 exactly as designed</b></p>","options":["JPEG","PDF","PNG","GIF"],"correct":1,"why":"PDF fixes the layout and embeds fonts, so it prints the same on any computer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which image or document format? (5)","q":"<p>Which format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A screenshot of an app interface with small text, for a tutorial</b></p>","options":["PDF","JPEG","GIF","PNG"],"correct":3,"why":"Screenshots have sharp text and flat colour. PNG keeps them crisp; JPEG adds blur and artefacts around text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which image or document format? (6)","q":"<p>Which format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A product photo of a bottle of local pepper sauce for an Instagram post</b></p>","options":["PDF","GIF","PNG","JPEG"],"correct":3,"why":"Social platforms re-compress uploads anyway; a high-quality JPEG is the efficient choice for a photo.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which image or document format? (7)","q":"<p>Which format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A 10-page SBA proposal with images and headings, to be submitted to the teacher</b></p>","options":["PNG","PDF","GIF","JPEG"],"correct":1,"why":"A PDF keeps headings, fonts and images in place whatever device the teacher uses to open it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which image or document format? (8)","q":"<p>Which format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A simple animated \"Book now\" button for a ferry company website banner</b></p>","options":["PNG","GIF","JPEG","PDF"],"correct":1,"why":"A short, flat-colour loop is a classic GIF use. It plays without a video player.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"The right format for the audience","q":"<p>The Lagoon Kayak Tours company in Antigua wants to send past guests a short animated \"We miss you!\" greeting inside a marketing email. Many guests read email on older phones. Which format is MOST appropriate for the animation?</p>","options":["PDF","PNG","GIF","MP4"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["A PDF is a fixed-layout document. It cannot play as an animation inside the body of an email.","PNG gives crisp, lossless images, but a standard PNG is a still image, so the greeting would not animate.","Correct. Most email programs display animated GIFs inline, while video is usually blocked or shown only as a link. A short, flat-colour loop suits GIF's 256-colour palette.","MP4 is the best video format for social platforms, but most email programs will not play embedded video, so many guests would see nothing."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Keisha's Carnival campaign files (step 1)","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is preparing digital assets for the Jouvert Junction band's Vincy Mas launch. Help her make the best format and platform decisions.</p><p>Malik has sent a 2 GB master video of the costume launch. Keisha needs to post a 30-second teaser on Instagram Reels and TikTok tonight. What should she export?</p>","options":["A vertical 1080 by 1920 MP4 (H.264 video, AAC audio) with captions.","Upload the 2 GB 16:9 master file as it is; the apps will sort it out."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Vertical MP4 matches both platforms' specifications and captions help viewers who scroll with sound off.","The platforms will crop or letterbox a landscape video and may reject or heavily compress a huge file."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Keisha's Carnival campaign files (step 2)","q":"<p>The band leader wants the costume price list available to members. It includes photos and a table. Members will read it on phones and some will print it. Which format?</p>","options":["A photo of the printed price list, posted as a JPEG.","A text-based PDF with compressed images, shared through a view-only Google Drive link.","An editable Word file, shared with \"anyone can edit\" access."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A photographed list is hard to read, cannot be searched and is inaccessible to screen readers.","Strong choice. PDF keeps the table layout for printing, compressed images keep it small, and view-only sharing protects the original.","Anyone could change the prices, and layout may break on phones without Word."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Keisha's Carnival campaign files (step 3)","q":"<p>Maya is building the band's registration page. Keisha wants the teaser video on it too. What should Maya do?</p>","options":["Put the 2 GB master file directly on the web server and link to it.","Upload the teaser to YouTube and embed it with the Share > Embed code."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Visitors would have to download a huge file and the band's hosting bandwidth would be used up quickly.","Excellent. YouTube streams the video efficiently to every device and the band's small web host is not overloaded."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the compatibility problems","q":"<p>Jerome sent files to the Jacmel Market Collective, a group of craft vendors in Haiti. Several vendors say the files will not open or look wrong.</p><p>Select every item that is likely to cause a cross-platform compatibility problem.</p>","options":["The price flyer was sent as a PDF with fonts embedded.","The website banner was designed only at 1920 px wide with small text.","A 1080 by 1920 MP4 was prepared for Instagram Reels.","The vendor photos were sent straight from an iPhone in HEIC format.","The promo video was exported as H.265 inside a .mov file.","The logo was sent only as a GIMP .xcf project file.","The jingle was exported as a 192 kbps MP3.","The website images were named \"Stall Photo 1.JPG\" with spaces and capitals."],"correct":[1, 3, 4, 5, 7],"why":"Well spotted. HEIC, H.265 .mov, project-only files, messy file names and desktop-only layouts are classic compatibility traps."},{"t":"match","title":"Convert a scanned sketch for Paper 02","q":"<p>A Paper 02 task asks you to scan a pencil sketch of a Willemstad storefront, clean it up and submit it as a 1200 px wide JPEG. Put the GIMP steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Scan or photograph the sketch and open the file in GIMP (File > Open).","Crop away the edges of the paper and the scanner bed.","Use Colours > Levels to make the paper white and the lines darker.","Use Image > Scale Image to set the width to 1200 px with the chain linked.","Save the editable master as an .xcf file.","Use File > Export As, type the required file name ending in .jpg and set quality about 85.","Open the exported JPEG and check its dimensions and appearance."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]}]},{"id":"assess_1bl","title":"Module 1 · Unit 6 quiz: Audio and video production principles","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 6: Audio and video production principles. 58 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the image tool to the job","q":"<p>Zara is retouching photos for the Blue Cove Marina brochure in Tortola. Match each GIMP or Photoshop tool to the job it does best.</p>","left":["Fuzzy Select (Magic Wand)","Layer mask","Levels","Heal tool","Clone tool","Feather","Hue-Saturation","Export As"],"right":["Brighten a hazy, grey-looking harbour photo by setting black and white points","Select a plain blue sky in one click so it can be replaced","Soften the edge of a cut-out so it blends into the new scene","Hide the old background behind a sailor without deleting any pixels","Make the turquoise water a little richer without changing brightness","Remove a small stain on a boat's sail and blend it into the cloth texture","Create the final JPEG for the brochure website from the .xcf project","Copy a section of water exactly to cover an unwanted floating bottle"],"correct":[1,3,0,5,7,2,4,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Build a composite in GIMP","q":"<p>Malik needs a poster that places a tamarind bottle on a Holetown beach photo. Put the GIMP steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Open the beach photo as the background (File > Open).","Bring in the bottle photo with File > Open as Layers.","Select the bottle and feather the selection by 2 px.","Add a layer mask from the selection to hide the bottle's old background.","Scale and move the bottle so its size and perspective fit the beach.","Use Curves on the bottle layer to match the beach lighting, then add a soft shadow layer.","Save the .xcf master, then Export As a JPEG for the poster."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audacity quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is recording a 30-second radio-style ad for Johnny Cake Corner in Portsmouth. Help her solve each problem.</p><p>Her waveform is flattened at the top and the meter flashes red. What has happened?</p>","options":["The noise profile is missing","Clipping: the input was too loud","The sample rate is wrong"],"correct":1,"why":"Clipping happens when the signal is louder than the maximum. The flattened peaks are distortion that cannot be repaired, so she must lower the input level and record again.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audacity quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>There is a constant air-conditioner hum behind her voice. What should she do first?</p>","options":["Select a few seconds of hum only, Get Noise Profile, then apply Noise Reduction","Add a Fade In","Normalise the track"],"correct":0,"why":"Noise Reduction learns the noise from a profile of room tone and removes it from the whole track. Normalising first would make the hum louder.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audacity quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>She wants the soca music to dip automatically whenever she speaks.</p>","options":["Change Tempo","Clone tool","Auto Duck"],"correct":2,"why":"Auto Duck lowers one track (the music) when another track (the voice) has sound. The Envelope tool does the same thing by hand.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audacity quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>Her voice is clean but quiet. Which effect brings the loudest peak up to -1 dB?</p>","options":["Fade Out","Normalize","Noise Reduction"],"correct":1,"why":"Normalize raises (or lowers) the whole track so the loudest peak reaches the target, without clipping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audacity quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>How should the music end so it does not stop abruptly?</p>","options":["Export it as OGG","Select the last 2 seconds and apply Fade Out","Delete the last 2 seconds"],"correct":1,"why":"A fade out reduces the volume smoothly to silence, which sounds professional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audacity quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>She needs to edit again next week. What should she save?</p>","options":["Nothing; Audacity remembers automatically","The .aup3 project file as the master, plus the exported MP3","Only the MP3"],"correct":1,"why":"The .aup3 project keeps the separate tracks and edits. An MP3 is a mixed, lossy file that is hard to re-edit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MP3, WAV or OGG? (1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has several audio jobs this week. Sort each into the most suitable export format.</p><p>Which audio format suits this job best?</p><p><b>The master voice-over for the SBA ad, to be imported into the video editor</b></p>","options":["OGG","MP3","WAV"],"correct":2,"why":"Uncompressed WAV keeps full quality, so nothing is lost before the video editor compresses the final export.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MP3, WAV or OGG? (2)","q":"<p>Which audio format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A weekly podcast episode about Barbados history for listeners on mobile data</b></p>","options":["OGG","WAV","MP3"],"correct":2,"why":"MP3 is small and plays on every podcast app and phone, which keeps data costs down for listeners.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MP3, WAV or OGG? (3)","q":"<p>Which audio format suits this job best?</p><p><b>Sound effects for Jerome's Android game built in Godot</b></p>","options":["OGG","WAV","MP3"],"correct":0,"why":"OGG is open, royalty-free and well supported by game engines and Android.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MP3, WAV or OGG? (4)","q":"<p>Which audio format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A jingle a client will send to friends on WhatsApp</b></p>","options":["MP3","WAV","OGG"],"correct":0,"why":"MP3 is compact and opens on almost any phone.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MP3, WAV or OGG? (5)","q":"<p>Which audio format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A studio recording of a steel pan band to be archived at full quality</b></p>","options":["MP3","OGG","WAV"],"correct":2,"why":"Archiving needs every sample kept. WAV is uncompressed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MP3, WAV or OGG? (6)","q":"<p>Which audio format suits this job best?</p><p><b>Background music for an open-source web project that must avoid any patented format</b></p>","options":["MP3","OGG","WAV"],"correct":1,"why":"Ogg Vorbis is an open, royalty-free format designed for exactly this kind of project.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MP3, WAV or OGG? (7)","q":"<p>Which audio format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A radio ad delivered to a local FM station that asked for uncompressed audio</b></p>","options":["MP3","WAV","OGG"],"correct":1,"why":"If the client specifies uncompressed audio, deliver WAV.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MP3, WAV or OGG? (8)","q":"<p>Which audio format suits this job best?</p><p><b>A music track for a Barbados restaurant's website that must play on iPhones and every browser</b></p>","options":["MP3","OGG","WAV"],"correct":0,"why":"MP3 has the widest compatibility, including Apple devices, where OGG support is weaker.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Composition and shot types","q":"<p>Malik is planning shots for a St. Kitts tourism Reel using the correct terms. Match each term to the shot or decision it describes.</p>","left":["Rule of thirds","Leading lines","Depth and layering","Negative space","Wide / establishing shot","Medium shot","Close-up"],"right":["Only the guide's smiling eyes as she spots a turtle","Placing the sunset horizon on the lower grid line and the boat on an intersection","Using the runway markings at Robert L. Bradshaw International Airport to guide the eye towards a landing jet","The tour guide from the waist up, so we see her gestures and her face","Opening on all of Basseterre and its bay so viewers know where they are","A lone kayaker with wide, empty turquoise water around her","Sea grapes in the foreground, a fisherman in the middle and Nevis on the horizon"],"correct":[1,2,6,5,4,3,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Two-column (A/V) script or screenplay? (1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has several productions in pre-production. Sort each feature or project into the script format it belongs to.</p><p>Which script format is this?</p><p><b>Video descriptions on the left, narration and sound effects on the right</b></p>","options":["Screenplay format","Two-column (A/V) script"],"correct":1,"why":"Splitting picture (A for audio, V for video) into two columns is the defining feature of the A/V script.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Two-column (A/V) script or screenplay? (2)","q":"<p>Which script format is this?</p><p><b>Scene headings such as INT. KITCHEN, NIGHT</b></p>","options":["Screenplay format","Two-column (A/V) script"],"correct":0,"why":"INT. and EXT. scene headings are standard screenplay format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Two-column (A/V) script or screenplay? (3)","q":"<p>Which script format is this?</p><p><b>Character names and dialogue centred on the page</b></p>","options":["Two-column (A/V) script","Screenplay format"],"correct":1,"why":"Centred dialogue under the character's name is how screenplays present speech.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Two-column (A/V) script or screenplay? (4)","q":"<p>Which script format is this?</p><p><b>A documentary on the history of Anguilla's salt ponds with narration and interviews</b></p>","options":["Screenplay format","Two-column (A/V) script"],"correct":1,"why":"Documentary and informational content usually uses the two-column format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Two-column (A/V) script or screenplay? (5)","q":"<p>Which script format is this?</p><p><b>A 10-minute drama about a family preparing for a hurricane</b></p>","options":["Screenplay format","Two-column (A/V) script"],"correct":0,"why":"Narrative and dramatic content with characters and scenes uses screenplay format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Two-column (A/V) script or screenplay? (6)","q":"<p>Which script format is this?</p><p><b>A corporate video for a Jolly Harbour marina showing its services</b></p>","options":["Screenplay format","Two-column (A/V) script"],"correct":1,"why":"Corporate and informational videos pair shots with voice-over, which the A/V layout shows clearly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Two-column (A/V) script or screenplay? (7)","q":"<p>Which script format is this?</p><p><b>Action lines describing what a character does</b></p>","options":["Two-column (A/V) script","Screenplay format"],"correct":1,"why":"Action lines are a screenplay element that describe movement and setting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Two-column (A/V) script or screenplay? (8)","q":"<p>Which script format is this?</p><p><b>The SBA 30-second recycling ad with a voice-over and on-screen text</b></p>","options":["Two-column (A/V) script","Screenplay format"],"correct":0,"why":"An ad with narration matched to shots is best planned in two columns.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Exposure triangle quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is learning manual camera settings before a shoot for the Bay Breeze Boutique on Young Street. Choose the best setting for each situation.</p><p>She wants the shop owner sharp and the busy street behind her softly blurred.</p>","options":["A very high ISO","A wide aperture such as f/1.8","A narrow aperture such as f/11"],"correct":1,"why":"A wide aperture gives a shallow depth of field, so the background blurs. This is perfect for interviews.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Exposure triangle quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>She is filming at 24 fps. Which shutter speed follows the 180-degree rule?</p>","options":["1/500","1/12","1/50"],"correct":2,"why":"The 180-degree rule doubles the frame rate: 1/48 is the ideal, so 1/50 is the nearest standard setting. It gives natural-looking motion blur.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Exposure triangle quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>Her footage inside a dim Gros Islet restaurant is grainy. What is the best fix?</p>","options":["Raise the ISO further","Use a faster shutter speed","Add light, such as an LED panel, and lower the ISO"],"correct":2,"why":"Grain comes from high ISO. Use the lowest ISO that works and add light rather than cranking ISO.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Exposure triangle quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>Under warm tungsten bulbs, faces look orange on camera.</p>","options":["Switch to 60 fps","Use a narrower aperture","Set the white balance manually to tungsten"],"correct":2,"why":"White balance makes colours accurate for the light source. Matching it to tungsten removes the orange cast.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Exposure triangle quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>She wants slow-motion shots of a jet landing at Lynden Pindling International Airport.</p>","options":["Record at 120 fps and play back at 24 or 30 fps","Record at 24 fps","Record at 720p"],"correct":0,"why":"High frame rates played back at a normal rate stretch time, giving smooth slow motion.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Exposure triangle quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>For a wide shot of Montego Bay from Fort Charles, everything from the rocks to the cruise ships must be sharp.</p>","options":["f/1.8","ISO 6400","f/11"],"correct":2,"why":"A narrow aperture gives a deep depth of field, which is ideal for landscapes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Exposure triangle quick-fire (7)","q":"<p>The client may want a vertical crop of a landscape shot later in the edit.</p>","options":["Use a slower shutter speed","Shoot in 4K so there is room to reframe","Shoot in 720p to save space"],"correct":1,"why":"4K allows reframing in post-production without losing too much detail.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a capture setup","q":"<p>Malik must film a smooth walking tour along the Hip Strip in Montego Bay for a tourism Reel, using his smartphone. The footage he shot handheld yesterday was shaky. Which setup is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["A fixed tripod set up at one end of the Hip Strip","Handheld again, but with 4K resolution to make it sharper","A gimbal stabiliser, with the phone set to vertical 9:16 at 1080p, focus and exposure locked","A tablet held at arm's length to see the frame better"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["A tripod is excellent for still shots, but it cannot move with Malik for a walking tour.","Higher resolution does not remove shake. The footage would still be unsteady, and the files would be much larger.","Correct. A gimbal keeps a moving camera level and smooth, the vertical framing matches Reels, and locked exposure stops brightness jumping between sun and shade.","A larger screen helps framing, but a tablet is heavier and harder to hold steady while walking."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The Maho Beach interview (step 1)","q":"<p>You are helping Malik at Island Pulse Media film a 2-minute interview with the owner of the Sunset Snorkel Tours on Maho Beach. Make the best production decisions.</p><p>It is windy, planes land every few minutes and the owner wants the beach behind her. Which microphone setup do you choose?</p>","options":["A lavalier mic clipped to her shirt with a furry windscreen, plus the phone's built-in mic as backup.","The phone's built-in mic from 3 metres away; it is the simplest option."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. The lavalier sits close to her voice, the windscreen blocks wind and the backup protects you if something fails.","An omnidirectional mic far away records mostly wind, waves and planes."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The Portsmouth Beach interview (step 2)","q":"<p>A plane is approaching during her best answer. What do you do?</p>","options":["Pause, let the plane pass, then ask her to repeat the answer.","Keep recording and plan to remove the plane in Audacity later."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Uncontrolled noise is best handled by waiting. Noise reduction cannot remove a jet engine from speech.","Noise Reduction works on steady hum, not on loud, changing sounds that overlap the voice."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The Grand Anse Beach interview (step 3)","q":"<p>It is 12 noon and the sun is directly overhead, causing dark shadows under her eyes. What lighting approach do you use?</p>","options":["Move her into open shade under a palm with the bright beach behind, and bounce light onto her face with a reflector.","Turn on the phone's built-in flash.","Shoot her facing straight into the midday sun so her face is bright."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong choice. Open shade softens harsh light and the reflector acts as a fill light.","A tiny flash cannot balance midday sun and flattens the image.","Facing the sun makes people squint and creates harsh shadows and burnt-out highlights."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The Holetown Beach interview (step 4)","q":"<p>Back at the studio, how should Malik prepare the audio and video for the edit?</p>","options":["Import the video and the lavalier WAV into the editor, sync them using the clap at the start, and reduce the wind noise gently.","Use only the built-in mic audio because syncing takes too long."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Syncing a separate, high-quality track is standard professional practice.","The backup track is far noisier. Throwing away the best audio wastes the planning you did."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the lighting mistakes","q":"<p>Jerome set up lights for a vlog about his new app in a small Chaguanas office. Zara reviews his notes.</p><p>Select every statement that describes a lighting MISTAKE.</p>","options":["The ceiling tubes are cool and the desk lamp is warm, and white balance is left on auto, giving his face mixed colour casts.","The fill light is brighter than the key light, so there are no shadows at all and the face looks flat.","White balance is set to match the LED panels at 5600 K.","The key light is placed at about 45 degrees to the side of the camera.","A white reflector bounces some of the key light into the shadow side of his face.","There is no back light, and his dark shirt merges into the dark wall.","He sits with a bright window directly behind him, so his face is in silhouette.","A softbox is used on the key light to soften shadows."],"correct":[0, 1, 5, 6],"why":"Well spotted. A fill brighter than the key, no back light, backlighting from a window and mixed colour temperatures are classic lighting faults."},{"t":"match","title":"Edit the SBA 30-second ad","q":"<p>Your SBA group is editing the 30-second audio-visual ad for a Mahoe Grove High School recycling drive in OpenShot. Put the steps of the edit workflow in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Ingest: copy the footage into the project folder, choose the 1080p 30 fps profile and import the clips, voice-over WAV and music.","Rough cut: drag the clips onto the timeline in storyboard order.","Fine cut: trim each edit with the Razor tool, cut on action, and bring the ad to exactly 30 seconds.","Polish the fine cut: hard cuts or a few dissolves, colour correction then a consistent grade, and text with the call to action in the title-safe area.","Sound mix: clean the voice-over, balance it above the music, layer the effects and master the output below 0 dB.","Export as an H.264 MP4 with AAC audio at 48 kHz.","Watch the whole exported file on a phone and check its length and settings."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Cutting principles and transitions","q":"<p>Zara is reviewing Malik's edit of a documentary about a heritage museum in Willemstad, Curaçao. Match each term to what she sees on the timeline.</p>","left":["Cut on action","Cut on motivation","J-cut","L-cut","Fast pacing","Dissolve","Fade to black","Colour correction"],"right":["The edit happens as the curator lifts an old map, so the cut is invisible","The screen darkens between the two chapters of the film","He cuts to the doorway only when a visitor's footsteps are heard","Fixing white balance so every interview clip looks neutral and consistent","The interviewee's voice starts before we see her face","Short, quick shots of Carnival costumes to build excitement","Her voice continues over a shot of the harbour after her face has gone","A gentle blend from a 1950s photo of the Handelskade to the waterfront today, showing time passing"],"correct":[0,2,4,6,5,7,1,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which platform are these export settings for? (1)","q":"<p>Malik has exported several versions of a promo for the Mango Festival in Roseau. Sort each export into the platform it suits best.</p><p>Which platform suits this export best?</p><p><b>1920 by 1080, 16:9, H.264, about 8 Mbps, 48 kHz AAC audio</b></p>","options":["Website","Instagram","YouTube"],"correct":2,"why":"Full HD landscape at a high bitrate matches YouTube's upload guidance for standard videos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which platform are these export settings for? (2)","q":"<p>Which platform suits this export best?</p><p><b>1080 by 1920, 9:16 vertical, 30 fps, text kept away from the bottom edge</b></p>","options":["YouTube","Website","Instagram"],"correct":2,"why":"Vertical 9:16 is the Reels format, and the bottom of the screen is covered by app buttons and captions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which platform are these export settings for? (3)","q":"<p>Which platform suits this export best?</p><p><b>1280 by 720, muted, 10-second loop at about 2 Mbps for a homepage background</b></p>","options":["YouTube","Instagram","Website"],"correct":2,"why":"A short, muted, low-bitrate loop loads quickly on a web page without overwhelming mobile data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which platform are these export settings for? (4)","q":"<p>Which platform suits this export best?</p><p><b>1080 by 1080 square post for the festival's feed</b></p>","options":["YouTube","Website","Instagram"],"correct":2,"why":"The 1:1 square is a classic Instagram feed format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which platform are these export settings for? (5)","q":"<p>Which platform suits this export best?</p><p><b>3840 by 2160 4K landscape documentary of the festival history</b></p>","options":["Website","YouTube","Instagram"],"correct":1,"why":"Long-form 4K landscape video is best hosted on YouTube, which streams it at a quality matched to each viewer's connection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which platform are these export settings for? (6)","q":"<p>Which platform suits this export best?</p><p><b>1080 by 1350, 4:5 portrait, to fill more of the phone screen in the feed</b></p>","options":["Instagram","YouTube","Website"],"correct":0,"why":"4:5 portrait is an Instagram feed format that takes up more screen than square.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which platform are these export settings for? (7)","q":"<p>Which platform suits this export best?</p><p><b>A short H.264 MP4 with \"fast start\" enabled so it begins playing before fully downloading on the festival site</b></p>","options":["YouTube","Website","Instagram"],"correct":1,"why":"Fast start (web optimisation) moves the file information to the start so browsers can begin playing immediately.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the good publishing habits","q":"<p>Jerome is finishing a short documentary about the Hillside Farm in Belize's Cayo District for his portfolio. Zara checks his notes against good practice.</p><p>Select every statement that shows GOOD practice.</p>","options":["His portfolio has every video he has ever made, including tests.","His project has folders called Footage, Audio, Graphics, Exports and Project Files.","He used a Free Music Archive track and credited the artist as the licence requires.","He named a clip \"final final NEW (2).mp4\".","He named a clip SentryHill_Scene04_Take02_2026-10-12.mp4.","He keeps the footage on his laptop, on an external drive and in cloud storage.","His YouTube title leads with the main keyword and the description lists his role and the tools used.","He used a friend's drone shots without asking or crediting her.","He used a popular commercial soca song because it was only 10 seconds long."],"correct":[1, 2, 4, 5, 6],"why":"Well spotted. Clear folders, consistent names, the 3-2-1 backup rule, licensed and credited music, and keyword-led titles are the good habits."},{"t":"match","title":"Cloud tool features","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media team uses cloud tools every day. Match each tool or term to its description.</p>","left":["Figma components","Figma prototype mode","Google Docs","Prezi","Canva","WeVideo","Version history","Local backup"],"right":["Keeping copies of raw footage on an external drive in case the Internet goes down","A zoomable, non-linear presentation for a tourism pitch to a cruise port team","Reusable elements that update everywhere when the main version changes","Restoring yesterday's copy after a teammate deletes a scene by mistake","Linking frames with clicks so a client can test an app idea before coding","Drag-and-drop templates for a quick Emancipation Day Instagram post","Zara and Keisha edit the same script at once, with comments and revision history","A browser-based timeline editor for cutting clips, adding text and exporting video"],"correct":[2,4,6,1,5,7,3,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is building a toolkit list for the Island Pulse Media. Sort each tool into its family.</p><p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Google Docs: co-writing a Carnival press release with comments.</b></p>","options":["Design and presentation tool","Cloud platform (documents and files)","E-learning authoring suite"],"correct":1,"why":"Google Docs is a cloud document platform built for real-time collaboration and revision history.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (2)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Microsoft 365 web apps: editing a budget in Excel in the browser.</b></p>","options":["E-learning authoring suite","Design and presentation tool","Cloud platform (documents and files)"],"correct":2,"why":"The Microsoft 365 web apps are browser versions of Word, Excel and PowerPoint.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (3)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Adobe Creative Cloud: syncing Photoshop files between the office and home.</b></p>","options":["Design and presentation tool","Cloud platform (documents and files)","E-learning authoring suite"],"correct":1,"why":"Creative Cloud is a cloud platform for professional tools with cross-device syncing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (4)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Canva: a template for a mango festival flyer.</b></p>","options":["Design and presentation tool","E-learning authoring suite","Cloud platform (documents and files)"],"correct":0,"why":"Canva is a drag-and-drop graphic design tool with templates.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (5)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Prezi: a zoomable pitch for a national tourism board.</b></p>","options":["E-learning authoring suite","Cloud platform (documents and files)","Design and presentation tool"],"correct":2,"why":"Prezi makes non-linear, zoomable presentations.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (6)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Articulate 360: a branching customer-service course with Storyline.</b></p>","options":["Cloud platform (documents and files)","E-learning authoring suite","Design and presentation tool"],"correct":1,"why":"Articulate 360 (Storyline and Rise) builds interactive courses with branching and assessments.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (7)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>iSpring Suite: turning PowerPoint slides into a quiz-based course.</b></p>","options":["E-learning authoring suite","Design and presentation tool","Cloud platform (documents and files)"],"correct":0,"why":"iSpring converts PowerPoint into interactive e-learning.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (8)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>H5P: free interactive video questions for a Moodle class.</b></p>","options":["Cloud platform (documents and files)","E-learning authoring suite","Design and presentation tool"],"correct":1,"why":"H5P is an open-source framework for interactive learning content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (9)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Adobe Captivate: a software demo and simulation for new GPL billing staff.</b></p>","options":["E-learning authoring suite","Design and presentation tool","Cloud platform (documents and files)"],"correct":0,"why":"Captivate specialises in simulations and software demos for e-learning.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_1hl","title":"Module 1 · Unit 7 quiz: 2D and 3D animation concepts","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 7: 2D and 3D animation concepts. 41 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the animation term","q":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is animating a short Carnival J'ouvert promo. Match each term to what he is doing.</p>","left":["Keyframe","Inbetweens","Timing","Spacing","Hold","Frame rate","Animating on twos"],"right":["Keeping the final pose still for 12 frames so viewers can read it","Placing drawings close together at the top of the jump and far apart near the ground","Deciding the jump will last 18 frames","Setting the project to 24 frames per second","The frames that fill the gap between those two poses","Setting the masquerader's starting pose and the highest point of the jump","Showing each new drawing for two frames to save drawing time"],"correct":[5,4,2,1,0,3,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"2D or 3D animation? (1)","q":"<p>Using the space, process and look criteria, sort each feature or project into 2D or 3D animation.</p><p>Is this 2D or 3D animation?</p><p><b>Objects exist only in width and height</b></p>","options":["3D animation","2D animation"],"correct":1,"why":"Two dimensions, width and height, is the definition of 2D space.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"2D or 3D animation? (2)","q":"<p>Is this 2D or 3D animation?</p><p><b>Models have width, height and depth and can be rotated like real objects</b></p>","options":["3D animation","2D animation"],"correct":0,"why":"Depth and the ability to rotate a model to any angle are what make it 3D.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"2D or 3D animation? (3)","q":"<p>Is this 2D or 3D animation?</p><p><b>Each drawing differs slightly from the last, played at 24 fps</b></p>","options":["3D animation","2D animation"],"correct":1,"why":"Sequences of slightly different flat drawings are the traditional 2D process.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"2D or 3D animation? (4)","q":"<p>Is this 2D or 3D animation?</p><p><b>The figure starts as a polygon mesh</b></p>","options":["2D animation","3D animation"],"correct":1,"why":"3D figures are usually built from a polygon mesh of connected polygons.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"2D or 3D animation? (5)","q":"<p>Is this 2D or 3D animation?</p><p><b>Classic films such as Bambi and The Little Mermaid</b></p>","options":["3D animation","2D animation"],"correct":1,"why":"These Disney classics were hand-drawn 2D animation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"2D or 3D animation? (6)","q":"<p>Is this 2D or 3D animation?</p><p><b>The stages of modelling, layout and rendering</b></p>","options":["3D animation","2D animation"],"correct":0,"why":"These are the three main stages of 3D animation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"2D or 3D animation? (7)","q":"<p>Is this 2D or 3D animation?</p><p><b>A virtual reality tour of Fort George in St. George's that visitors can walk around</b></p>","options":["2D animation","3D animation"],"correct":1,"why":"VR needs models with depth so the viewer can move through the space.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"2D or 3D animation? (8)","q":"<p>Is this 2D or 3D animation?</p><p><b>Figures drawn as bitmap or vector graphics in Krita or Synfig</b></p>","options":["3D animation","2D animation"],"correct":1,"why":"Bitmap and vector drawings are the two ways to make 2D figures.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the principle (1)","q":"<p>Zara, creative director at Island Pulse Media, is reviewing animations for a Bahamas tourism campaign. Which principle is each animator using?</p><p>A cartoon pelican pulls its head back before diving into Nassau harbour.</p>","options":["follow through","appeal","anticipation"],"correct":2,"why":"The small backward move prepares the viewer for the dive. That is anticipation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the principle (2)","q":"<p>After the steel pan player stops, the long ribbons on his sleeves keep swinging, then settle.</p>","options":["staging","secondary action","follow through and overlapping action"],"correct":2,"why":"Loose parts continuing to move after the body stops is follow through.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the principle (3)","q":"<p>A ferry starts slowly, speeds up in the middle of the crossing, then slows before docking.</p>","options":["squash and stretch","slow in and slow out","exaggeration"],"correct":1,"why":"Gradual acceleration and deceleration is slow in and slow out (easing).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the principle (4)","q":"<p>A swinging arm is checked frame by frame to make sure the hand follows a curved path.</p>","options":["timing","solid drawing","arcs"],"correct":2,"why":"Natural movements follow curves. Checking the path of the hand is checking arcs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the principle (5)","q":"<p>A character's jaw drops to the floor when she sees the price of a cruise ticket.</p>","options":["anticipation","exaggeration","staging"],"correct":1,"why":"Pushing an expression beyond reality for clarity and humour is exaggeration.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the principle (6)","q":"<p>The camera is placed side-on with a plain sky behind the character so the jump is easy to read.</p>","options":["appeal","arcs","staging"],"correct":2,"why":"Choosing angle and background so one idea reads clearly is staging.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the principle (7)","q":"<p>While a fisherman hauls in his net (main action), he whistles and taps his foot.</p>","options":["timing","overlapping action","secondary action"],"correct":2,"why":"Small supporting actions that add personality are secondary action, as long as they do not steal focus.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a working method","q":"<p>The Grenada Spice Studios must deliver a 10-second animated advert in which a mascot nutmeg waves, points to a logo and smiles, timed exactly to a voice-over. Which approach is MOST suitable, and why?</p>","options":["Straight-ahead, because the computer generates all the inbetweens.","Straight-ahead, because it always produces the smoothest motion.","Neither: a 10-second advert does not need any planning of poses.","Pose-to-pose, because key poses can be planned to match the voice-over and fit the fixed 10-second length."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Software tweening between planned keys is a pose-to-pose workflow, not straight-ahead.","Straight-ahead can be fluid, but it is hard to control length and synchronisation. It suits fire, water and splashes more than timed acting.","Every professional animation is planned. A fixed duration and voice-over make planning essential.","Correct. Pose-to-pose gives control over timing and staging, which matters when an action must hit exact beats and a set duration."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the animation tool","q":"<p>Keisha is writing the comparison report and must list software that supports 2D, 3D or both. Match each tool to its description.</p>","left":["Blender","Krita","Synfig Studio","Pencil2D","OpenToonz","Adobe Animate","Toon Boom Harmony","Paper, pen and pencil"],"right":["Industry-standard paid 2D suite that replaced Toon Boom Studio","Free, open-source vector 2D with motion graphics and bones","Free painting program with frame-by-frame 2D animation tools","The traditional animator's toolkit","Simple flipbook-style tool for practising frame-by-frame fundamentals","The paid successor to Adobe Flash, still using .fla project files","Free 3D suite that also draws 2D animation with Grease Pencil","Free professional 2D production software with an Xsheet"],"correct":[6,2,1,4,7,5,0,3]},{"t":"match","title":"Order the 2D bouncing ball workflow","q":"<p>Keisha, the Island Pulse Media marketing lead, is learning to animate a bouncing guinep for an Instagram story. Put her steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Create a 24 fps document at the right size for the platform.","Sketch the path of the bounces, each one lower and shorter.","Draw the key positions: top of each arc and each contact with the ground.","Draw a timing chart with close spacing at the tops and wide spacing near the ground.","Add the inbetweens on the arc following the timing chart.","Add stretch before and after contact and squash on the contact frame, keeping volume.","Play back with onion skinning, fix bumps, then export."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"ma","title":"Flash then, Animate now","q":"<p>Jerome found an old Flash project for a Grenada spice festival and wants to update it. Zara checks his notes against today's software.</p><p>Select every CORRECT statement.</p>","options":["On the Timeline, at the same frame rate, more frames make the animation longer.","The .fla file is the editable project, so he can open it in Adobe Animate and keep working.","The Properties panel changes the characteristics of the object selected on the stage.","Animation in Flash could only be done by hand, never with code.","The Tools panel is used to select, draw and change items on the stage.","Adding frames at the same frame rate makes the animation shorter.","He should export HTML5 Canvas or MP4 so the animation plays on today's phones and browsers.","He should publish a SWF file, because every modern browser still plays Flash."],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 4, 6],"why":"Correct. You know the .fla, Tools, Properties and Timeline, and why HTML5 Canvas and MP4 replace SWF today."},{"t":"mc","title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened? (1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has a list of jobs. Decide whether each is best done by hand, frame by frame, or with software tweening.</p><p>Which technique suits this job best?</p><p><b>A telecom company's logo sliding in and scaling up smoothly for a 5-second bumper.</b></p>","options":["Tweened (software interpolates)","Frame-by-frame (drawn by hand)"],"correct":0,"why":"Simple changes in position and scale between two keys are perfect for a motion or classic tween.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened? (2)","q":"<p>Which technique suits this job best?</p><p><b>A crashing wave at Bathsheba with spray and foam changing shape every frame.</b></p>","options":["Frame-by-frame (drawn by hand)","Tweened (software interpolates)"],"correct":0,"why":"Chaotic, organic shapes need straight-ahead frame-by-frame drawing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened? (3)","q":"<p>Which technique suits this job best?</p><p><b>A circle morphing into a sun shape in a vector tool.</b></p>","options":["Tweened (software interpolates)","Frame-by-frame (drawn by hand)"],"correct":0,"why":"A shape tween in Animate (or Synfig) interpolates one vector shape into another.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened? (4)","q":"<p>Which technique suits this job best?</p><p><b>A hand-drawn character crying, with tears and quivering lips.</b></p>","options":["Tweened (software interpolates)","Frame-by-frame (drawn by hand)"],"correct":1,"why":"Expressive acting with changing drawings is classic frame-by-frame work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened? (5)","q":"<p>Which technique suits this job best?</p><p><b>An infographic bar chart growing to show tourist arrivals at the airport.</b></p>","options":["Tweened (software interpolates)","Frame-by-frame (drawn by hand)"],"correct":0,"why":"Bars growing from one value to another are easily tweened with easing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened? (6)","q":"<p>Which technique suits this job best?</p><p><b>A cut-out puppet of a mocko jumbie whose limbs rotate at joints in Synfig.</b></p>","options":["Frame-by-frame (drawn by hand)","Tweened (software interpolates)"],"correct":1,"why":"Cut-out animation with bones relies on interpolation between waypoints.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened? (7)","q":"<p>Which technique suits this job best?</p><p><b>Flames on a coal pot flickering under a pot of soup.</b></p>","options":["Frame-by-frame (drawn by hand)","Tweened (software interpolates)"],"correct":0,"why":"Fire changes shape unpredictably, so it is usually drawn straight ahead, frame by frame.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened? (8)","q":"<p>Which technique suits this job best?</p><p><b>A rough pencil sketch animation made in Pencil2D with onion skin.</b></p>","options":["Tweened (software interpolates)","Frame-by-frame (drawn by hand)"],"correct":1,"why":"Pencil2D is built mainly for drawing each frame by hand.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"From scene to finished image","q":"<p>Malik is making a 3D animation of a sea turtle swimming past the reef at the Tobago Cays for a marine park campaign. Put his production steps in order, using the 3D stages.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Storyboard the idea: plan the story and lay out the key shots as panels.","Modelling: build the turtle, the coral and the seabed as polygon meshes.","Add materials and textures to the turtle shell and the coral.","Layout: position the models in the scene and decide what the camera sees.","Animate: key the turtle's poses and movement over time, using arcs and slow in and slow out.","Rendering: turn the scene into finished images with lighting, surfaces and shadows.","Export the frames as an MP4 for the campaign."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the 3D term","q":"<p>Jerome, the Island Pulse Media mobile developer, is building a low-poly model of the old Court House in St. John's, Antigua, for an AR tour app. Match each term.</p>","left":["Vertex","Edge","Face","Mesh","Primitive","Quad","Texture","Low-poly"],"right":["A four-sided polygon that deforms and subdivides cleanly","The whole collection of points, lines and surfaces forming the courthouse","A flat surface enclosed by edges that is shaded when rendered","Keeping the face count small so the app runs on mid-range phones","A line joining two points","A single point with X, Y and Z coordinates","A photo of painted wood mapped onto the shutters","The cube he starts from before shaping the walls"],"correct":[5,4,2,1,7,0,6,3]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the correct Blender technique","q":"<p>An intern at the Island Pulse Media wrote these notes after a Blender bouncing ball session. Some are correct and some are wrong.</p><p>Select ALL the correct statements.</p>","options":["Pressing I in the 3D Viewport inserts a keyframe on the current frame.","The Dope Sheet is mainly used to change timing by moving keyframes left or right.","Each bounce should be the same height so the motion looks consistent.","For squash, scale Z down and X and Y up so the volume stays roughly the same.","Bezier interpolation at the contact frames makes the ball hit the floor with a sharp, fast impact.","The origin should be at the centre of the ball so it squashes against the floor.","Setting the contact keys to Vector handles in the Graph Editor gives a sharp change of direction.","Shift+A opens the Add menu, where you choose Mesh then UV Sphere."],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 6, 7],"why":"Correct. You know which panel does what, how to sharpen contacts in the Graph Editor, and how to keep volume when squashing."},{"t":"mc","title":"Graph Editor and walk cycle quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Answer these questions from the Island Pulse Media animation team.</p><p>A wind turbine on a hill in Curaçao must spin at a perfectly steady speed. Which interpolation?</p>","options":["Linear","Constant","Bezier"],"correct":0,"why":"Linear gives a constant rate of change, so the speed never eases in or out.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Graph Editor and walk cycle quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>On an F-curve, the steepest section shows where the object is...</p>","options":["moving fastest","holding still","at its keyframe"],"correct":0,"why":"A steep slope means a large change in value per frame, which is high speed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Graph Editor and walk cycle quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>Malik wants to check his key poses with no inbetweens first. He sets interpolation to...</p>","options":["Elastic","Constant (stepped)","Bezier"],"correct":1,"why":"Stepped keys show only the poses, which is how animators block pose-to-pose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Graph Editor and walk cycle quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>In a walk cycle, the pose where the body is at its LOWEST point is the...</p>","options":["up pose","passing pose","down pose"],"correct":2,"why":"Just after contact the front leg bends to take the weight: the down pose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Graph Editor and walk cycle quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>In a walk cycle, both feet are on the ground and the legs are at their widest in the...</p>","options":["up pose","passing pose","contact pose"],"correct":2,"why":"The contact pose has the heel striking and the back toe on the ground.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Graph Editor and walk cycle quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>To make a 24-frame walk loop forever without adding more keys, he uses...</p>","options":["Shade Smooth","Smart UV Project","Make Cyclic (a Cycles modifier)"],"correct":2,"why":"Cyclic extrapolation repeats the curve indefinitely.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deadline for the Emancipation Day animation (step 1)","q":"<p>You are an intern at the Island Pulse Media. Zara needs a 15-second animated sting for an Emancipation Day community event post, due at 9 a.m. tomorrow. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The sting has a 3D conch shell that bounces onto the screen and reveals the event title. How do you begin?</p>","options":["Plan key poses and timing on paper or in a quick storyboard, then block them in Blender with stepped keys.","Start animating straight ahead from frame 1 and see where it ends up."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Pose-to-pose planning lets you hit the exact 15-second duration.","Straight-ahead makes it hard to control length and staging for a timed brand piece."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deadline for the Emancipation Day animation (step 2)","q":"<p>Your blocked keys look good. When you switch to Bezier, the shell floats and drifts into the floor between bounces. What do you do?</p>","options":["Add more and more keyframes on every frame until it looks right.","Open the Graph Editor, set the contact keys to Vector handles and add squash on the contact frames.","Switch everything to Linear so it moves evenly."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Keying every frame defeats interpolation and makes changes very slow. The curves are the real problem.","Correct. Sharp contacts and squash make the impact read clearly.","Linear removes all easing, so the shell would move like a machine with no hang time."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deadline for the Emancipation Day animation (step 3)","q":"<p>It is 10 p.m. A full Cycles render would take about 8 hours on the office computer. How do you render?</p>","options":["Render straight to an animated GIF in Cycles at full quality.","Render in Eevee at 1080 by 1920 (it will be posted as a vertical story), 30 fps, as a PNG sequence, then convert to MP4 H.264."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["GIF is limited to 256 colours and gives a large, poor-quality file, and Cycles may miss the deadline.","Smart. Eevee is fast, the PNG sequence protects you from crashes, and MP4 suits Instagram."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deadline for the Emancipation Day animation (step 4)","q":"<p>The render is done. How do you hand it over?</p>","options":["Name it IPM_EmancipationSting_v1_1080x1920.mp4, upload it with the .blend file to the shared Drive folder and message Zara with the settings used.","Email only the MP4 called final.mp4 and delete the project files to save space."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Professional. Clear naming, source files kept and a short handover note.","Without the source file no one can fix a typo in the title, and \"final.mp4\" is impossible to track."]}]},{"id":"assess_1mn","title":"Module 1 · Unit 8 quiz: Interactive digital media for collaboration","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 8: Interactive digital media for collaboration. 45 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Synchronous or asynchronous? (1)","q":"<p>A group of Coralstone College students is planning their DME2 SBA. Sort each activity.</p><p>Is this collaboration synchronous (same time) or asynchronous (different times)?</p><p><b>A live Microsoft Teams call to agree on the product idea.</b></p>","options":["Asynchronous","Synchronous"],"correct":1,"why":"Everyone is present at the same time, so it is synchronous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Synchronous or asynchronous? (2)","q":"<p>Is this collaboration synchronous (same time) or asynchronous (different times)?</p><p><b>Leaving comments on a Google Doc for a group member to read later.</b></p>","options":["Asynchronous","Synchronous"],"correct":0,"why":"The reply can come hours later, so it is asynchronous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Synchronous or asynchronous? (3)","q":"<p>Is this collaboration synchronous (same time) or asynchronous (different times)?</p><p><b>Three members editing the same Figma frame while talking on a call.</b></p>","options":["Synchronous","Asynchronous"],"correct":0,"why":"Live co-editing in real time is synchronous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Synchronous or asynchronous? (4)","q":"<p>Is this collaboration synchronous (same time) or asynchronous (different times)?</p><p><b>Moving a Trello card to \"Done\" overnight for others to see in the morning.</b></p>","options":["Asynchronous","Synchronous"],"correct":0,"why":"The update waits for others, so it is asynchronous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Synchronous or asynchronous? (5)","q":"<p>Is this collaboration synchronous (same time) or asynchronous (different times)?</p><p><b>Posting mood-board images on a shared Padlet wall over the weekend.</b></p>","options":["Synchronous","Asynchronous"],"correct":1,"why":"Contributions build up at different times: asynchronous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Synchronous or asynchronous? (6)","q":"<p>Is this collaboration synchronous (same time) or asynchronous (different times)?</p><p><b>A live brainstorm on a Miro board during class with a timer running.</b></p>","options":["Asynchronous","Synchronous"],"correct":1,"why":"Everyone contributes together, in real time: synchronous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Synchronous or asynchronous? (7)","q":"<p>Is this collaboration synchronous (same time) or asynchronous (different times)?</p><p><b>A reply to a question on a Discord forum channel two days later.</b></p>","options":["Synchronous","Asynchronous"],"correct":1,"why":"Forum threads are classic asynchronous communication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Synchronous or asynchronous? (8)","q":"<p>Is this collaboration synchronous (same time) or asynchronous (different times)?</p><p><b>A WhatsApp video call to review the 30-second ad draft together.</b></p>","options":["Asynchronous","Synchronous"],"correct":1,"why":"A live call is synchronous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which job does this tool do? (1)","q":"<p>Use the three headings. A DME2 group at Hummingbird Ridge High School is preparing a class presentation on Emancipation Day. Sort each use of a tool.</p><p>Which job is the tool doing here?</p><p><b>A Slack message asking which photo the group should use on the title slide.</b></p>","options":["Make task progress visible","Keep the conversation going","Create the work together"],"correct":1,"why":"Quick messaging keeps the team conversation together: job 1.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which job does this tool do? (2)","q":"<p>Which job is the tool doing here?</p><p><b>A Webex call to rehearse the presentation the night before.</b></p>","options":["Create the work together","Keep the conversation going","Make task progress visible"],"correct":1,"why":"Talking things through in an online call is a video meeting: job 1.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which job does this tool do? (3)","q":"<p>Which job is the tool doing here?</p><p><b>A Trello card called \"Record voice-over\" moved from To do to Done.</b></p>","options":["Make task progress visible","Keep the conversation going","Create the work together"],"correct":0,"why":"Moving a task from \"to do\" to \"done\" makes progress visible: job 2.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which job does this tool do? (4)","q":"<p>Which job is the tool doing here?</p><p><b>An Asana project where each slide has one named owner and a due date.</b></p>","options":["Make task progress visible","Keep the conversation going","Create the work together"],"correct":0,"why":"Assigning owners is the first step of Assign, Update, Review: job 2.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which job does this tool do? (5)","q":"<p>Which job is the tool doing here?</p><p><b>A monday.com board (once called DaPulse) showing which tasks are in progress.</b></p>","options":["Make task progress visible","Keep the conversation going","Create the work together"],"correct":0,"why":"Showing what is in progress is the Update step of project management: job 2.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which job does this tool do? (6)","q":"<p>Which job is the tool doing here?</p><p><b>Four students editing the same Google Docs script at the same time.</b></p>","options":["Create the work together","Keep the conversation going","Make task progress visible"],"correct":0,"why":"One shared space instead of separate copies: job 3.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which job does this tool do? (7)","q":"<p>Which job is the tool doing here?</p><p><b>Two students coding the group website together in one GitHub repository.</b></p>","options":["Create the work together","Keep the conversation going","Make task progress visible"],"correct":0,"why":"Coding together in one shared space is creating the work together: job 3.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which job does this tool do? (8)","q":"<p>Which job is the tool doing here?</p><p><b>An Igloo space that holds the project guidance and team knowledge.</b></p>","options":["Create the work together","Make task progress visible","Keep the conversation going"],"correct":0,"why":"Sharing team knowledge and guidance in one place supports creating together: job 3.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the tool to the job","q":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is setting up a new project for a Grand Anse marina. Match each need to the best tool.</p>","left":["Track tasks on cards across To Do, Doing and Done","Co-design the marina app screens in the browser","Run a sticky-note brainstorm and vote on ideas","Write the proposal together with version history","Organise team chat into topic channels with threads","Collect mood-board photos from the client on a simple wall","Hold a recorded video meeting with the client in a team channel"],"right":["Padlet","Microsoft Teams","Miro","Figma","Slack","Trello","Google Docs (Google Workspace)"],"correct":[5,3,2,6,4,0,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which electronic community? (1)","q":"<p>Students at Sandbox Tree College want to get help and share their work online. Choose the best type of community for each need.</p><p>A searchable answer to a Blender error message that someone solved three years ago.</p>","options":["a live video call","a Padlet wall","a forum"],"correct":2,"why":"Forums keep threads organised and searchable for years.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which electronic community? (2)","q":"<p>A class guide to the history of St. John’s that every student can edit, with a record of each change.</p>","options":["a Trello board","a WhatsApp group","a wiki"],"correct":2,"why":"Wikis are built for collaborative editing with page history.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which electronic community? (3)","q":"<p>Live voice chat plus a #critique channel where animators post work for feedback.</p>","options":["an email newsletter","a Discord server","a printed magazine"],"correct":1,"why":"Discord combines text channels, voice rooms and roles for creative communities.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which electronic community? (4)","q":"<p>Local entrepreneurs sharing news, offers and advice in a Dominica business group.</p>","options":["a Kanban board","a wiki","an online group (e.g. Facebook or WhatsApp)"],"correct":2,"why":"Social online groups suit news and discussion among local members.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which electronic community? (5)","q":"<p>A portfolio site where illustrators get comments from professionals worldwide.</p>","options":["Google Sheets","Behance or ArtStation","Microsoft Planner"],"correct":1,"why":"Portfolio communities are built for showcasing work and receiving feedback.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (1)","q":"<p>Dominica Eco-Trails group is building a hiking app with members in Roseau, Trinidad and St. Kitts. Sort each statement.</p><p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of digital collaboration?</p><p><b>A designer in Trinidad comments on the app mock-up minutes after it is uploaded.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":1,"why":"This is real-time feedback, which speeds up improvement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (2)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of digital collaboration?</p><p><b>One member in a rural village cannot join video calls because her data plan is too expensive.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Unequal access to affordable data is the digital divide.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (3)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of digital collaboration?</p><p><b>The group restores yesterday's version of the proposal after someone deletes a section by mistake.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Version control protects work from accidental loss.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (4)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of digital collaboration?</p><p><b>A sharing link set to \"Anyone with the link\" exposes members' phone numbers.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Poor sharing settings create privacy risks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (5)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of digital collaboration?</p><p><b>Hikers from the diaspora in London and Toronto give feedback on the trail descriptions.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Global reach brings in a wider audience and more perspectives.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (6)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of digital collaboration?</p><p><b>Work stops for two days when the only shared file is on a platform that goes down.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Over-reliance on one technology with no backup is a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (7)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of digital collaboration?</p><p><b>Trello shows exactly who completed each task, so marks can be shared fairly.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Transparency and accountability are advantages of task-tracking tools.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (8)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of digital collaboration?</p><p><b>A blunt one-line chat message is read as rude and causes an argument.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Text-based communication can lose tone and lead to miscommunication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk? (1)","q":"<p>POS Shutterbugs is an online photography community for young people across Trinidad and Tobago and the wider Caribbean. Sort each situation into the correct list.</p><p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of an electronic community?</p><p><b>A member in Grenada gives a Port of Spain student detailed tips on her Frederick Street night photo.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Different people and places: members exchange ideas across countries.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk? (2)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of an electronic community?</p><p><b>A moderator removes an abusive comment and reminds everyone of the group rules.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Some arenas are moderated, so discussion can be guided and abuse removed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk? (3)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of an electronic community?</p><p><b>A shy member uses a nickname and finally asks how to shoot in manual mode.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"A sense of anonymity can make asking a question feel easier.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk? (4)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of an electronic community?</p><p><b>The group arranges a supervised photo walk at Fort Rodney with parents present.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Interaction can lead to safe physical meetings.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk? (5)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of an electronic community?</p><p><b>Three frequent posters reply to everything, and newer members stop posting.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":1,"why":"A few can dominate discussion and crowd out quieter members.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk? (6)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of an electronic community?</p><p><b>A fake account uses a stolen name to send hurtful messages to a member.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Anonymity can also encourage deception and hurtful behaviour.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk? (7)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of an electronic community?</p><p><b>A student checks replies all evening and does not finish his homework.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Electronic communities take time, and following replies can interrupt homework.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk? (8)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of an electronic community?</p><p><b>A member receives prejudiced comments about where she comes from.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Discrimination is different online, but it is not absent.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Set up a collaborative SBA workspace","q":"<p>Three Calabash Point Secondary students in Grenada are starting their DME2 SBA: a website and short ad for a Gouyave fish-fry stall. Put their set-up steps in a sensible order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Meet and agree the project goal, team roles and a short team charter.","Choose the tools: one for chat, one for files, one for tasks, and agree what each is for.","Create a shared Google Drive folder with clear sub-folders and naming rules, shared only with the group and teacher.","Build a Trello board with tasks, owners and due dates from the production schedule.","Brainstorm ideas on a Miro board and record the decisions.","Work on tasks, leaving comments and using version history as drafts develop.","Hold weekly check-ins, export screenshots and minutes as evidence of collaboration."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the good netiquette","q":"<p>Here are messages from a group chat for a DME2 SBA team building an app for the Belize Reef Watch project.</p><p>Select ALL the messages that show good netiquette and professional conduct.</p>","options":["\"WHO DELETED MY SECTION?????\"","\"lol ur logo is trash, just let me do it\"","\"Here is a screenshot of what Jordan said about the teacher in our private chat.\"","\"I changed the whole report to my version so it sounds better.\"","\"Meeting notes and decisions from today are in the Drive folder: Minutes_Week3.\"","\"I added my ideas as comments in Suggesting mode so you can accept or reject them.\"","\"Sorry, I will miss Thursday's deadline for the survey. I can finish it by Saturday morning, is that OK?\"","\"Thanks for the wireframes, Ana. The menu is clear. Could we make the buttons bigger for older users?\""],"correct":[4, 5, 6, 7],"why":"Correct. Good netiquette is respectful, constructive, honest about problems and transparent about decisions."},{"t":"mc","title":"Rescue the group project (step 1)","q":"<p>You are the project manager for a 3-person DME2 SBA group at Bayleaf Hill Secondary. Your product is a website and 30-second ad for the Jamaican Turtle Watch volunteers. Make the best decisions.</p><p>Week 2: files are scattered across three phones and email attachments called \"final\", \"final2\" and \"finalREAL\". What do you do?</p>","options":["Set up one shared Google Drive folder with sub-folders and a naming rule, shared only with the group and teacher.","Ask everyone to keep emailing attachments but add the date to the name."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. One source of truth with version history prevents lost work.","Attachments still create many copies and no shared version history."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Rescue the group project (step 2)","q":"<p>Week 4: one member, Devon, has not completed his storyboard task and is not replying in the group chat. How do you respond?</p>","options":["Send Devon a polite private message asking if he is okay, and offer to agree a new deadline or adjust tasks; inform the teacher if it continues.","Quietly do his task yourself and say nothing.","Post in the group chat: \"DEVON IS LAZY. Do your work!!!\""],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Professional. It is respectful, private and solution-focused.","This hides the problem, overloads you and leaves no honest record of who did what.","Public shouting and insults break netiquette and damage teamwork."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Rescue the group project (step 3)","q":"<p>Devon explains his home Internet was cut off after a power problem. He can use the school library Wi-Fi only at lunch. How do you adapt?</p>","options":["Switch his tasks to asynchronous work: he downloads files for offline editing at lunch, uploads them the next day, and you record meetings for him.","Insist that all work happens in live evening video calls only."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Smart. You reduced the effect of connectivity issues and the digital divide.","This excludes a member with limited access, which is the digital divide in action."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Rescue the group project (step 4)","q":"<p>Two weeks before submission, how do you show evidence of collaboration?</p>","options":["Write one sentence: \"We all worked together well.\"","Include dated Trello screenshots, version history, comment threads and meeting minutes, with captions and private details removed."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A claim with no evidence earns little credit.","Excellent. This is strong, honest evidence of collaboration."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluating a collaboration tool","q":"<p>The Antigua Youth Film Collective has members on three islands, several of whom use only mid-range phones with prepaid data. They need to share storyboards and feedback over a month. Which choice is BEST justified?</p>","options":["A desktop-only professional editing suite, because it has the most features.","A Padlet board, because it is browser-based, works on phones, lets members post images and comments at any time, and needs little data compared with live video calls.","Emailing large image attachments to everyone, because email is universal.","Daily two-hour live video calls, because face-to-face discussion is always best."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["More features do not help if members cannot run the software on their phones.","Correct. It matches the task (visual feedback), the devices, the data constraint and the asynchronous schedule across islands.","Attachments create many copies, use data and give no single place for organised feedback.","Long live calls use a lot of data and exclude members with limited access: the digital divide."]}]},{"id":"assess_1s9","title":"Module 1 · Unit 9 quiz: Industry practices for promotional campaigns","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 9: Industry practices for promotional campaigns. 38 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the platform to the campaign","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media has seven briefs on her desk. Match each brief to the platform that fits it best.</p>","left":["LinkedIn","TikTok","Instagram","X (formerly Twitter)","Facebook Events","Instagram Stories","Facebook Groups"],"right":["Show off the plating of dishes at a Holetown restaurant to visitors","Recruit experienced accountants for a firm in Port of Spain","Post live, minute-by-minute updates during a storm watch","Get secondary school students excited about a youth coding camp","Invite parents and grandparents to a church fair in Linden","Build a community of St. Lucian home gardeners swapping tips","Share a 24-hour flash discount with followers who check in daily"],"correct":[1,3,0,2,4,6,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Blog, vlog, podcast or newsletter? (1)","q":"<p>The Grenada Spice Trail tour company is planning its content marketing. Sort each idea into the content type it describes.</p><p>Which content marketing format is this?</p><p><b>A 1,000-word written guide, \"How nutmeg is processed in Gouyave\", published on the company website.</b></p>","options":["Blog","Podcast","Email newsletter","Vlog"],"correct":0,"why":"Written posts on a website are blog content, and they help the site rank in search results.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Blog, vlog, podcast or newsletter? (2)","q":"<p>Which content marketing format is this?</p><p><b>A YouTube video of the guide walking through a cocoa estate, talking to camera.</b></p>","options":["Email newsletter","Podcast","Vlog","Blog"],"correct":2,"why":"A video diary or walkthrough with a presenter is a vlog.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Blog, vlog, podcast or newsletter? (3)","q":"<p>Which content marketing format is this?</p><p><b>A 30-minute audio interview with a spice farmer, released every two weeks on Spotify.</b></p>","options":["Email newsletter","Blog","Vlog","Podcast"],"correct":3,"why":"Episodic audio distributed to listening apps is a podcast.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Blog, vlog, podcast or newsletter? (4)","q":"<p>Which content marketing format is this?</p><p><b>A monthly email to past guests with new tour dates and a discount code.</b></p>","options":["Email newsletter","Vlog","Blog","Podcast"],"correct":0,"why":"A regular email to opted-in subscribers is a newsletter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Blog, vlog, podcast or newsletter? (5)","q":"<p>Which content marketing format is this?</p><p><b>A written post titled \"Ten things to pack for a rainforest hike\", with photos and links.</b></p>","options":["Email newsletter","Podcast","Vlog","Blog"],"correct":3,"why":"It is written, published on the web and updated regularly: a blog post.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Blog, vlog, podcast or newsletter? (6)","q":"<p>Which content marketing format is this?</p><p><b>A TikTok series called \"A day in the life of a tour guide\", filmed on a phone.</b></p>","options":["Podcast","Blog","Vlog","Email newsletter"],"correct":2,"why":"Short personal video diaries are vlogs, even on TikTok.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Blog, vlog, podcast or newsletter? (7)","q":"<p>Which content marketing format is this?</p><p><b>An audio series where listeners hear the history of Grenada's spice trade while driving.</b></p>","options":["Blog","Email newsletter","Vlog","Podcast"],"correct":3,"why":"Audio that people listen to while doing other things is the strength of a podcast.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Blog, vlog, podcast or newsletter? (8)","q":"<p>Which content marketing format is this?</p><p><b>A subscriber-only \"Spice of the Month\" email with a recipe and a booking link.</b></p>","options":["Vlog","Email newsletter","Blog","Podcast"],"correct":1,"why":"It goes to a list the company owns, by email: a newsletter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that practice (1)","q":"<p>Malik and Keisha at the Island Pulse Media are reviewing campaigns from around the region. Identify the industry practice in each.</p><p>A Trinidad snack brand leaves giant foam \"doubles\" on park benches in Port of Spain with a QR code on each one.</p>","options":["email marketing","guerrilla marketing","influencer marketing"],"correct":1,"why":"Unexpected physical objects in public spaces, cheap to produce and surprising: classic guerrilla marketing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that practice (2)","q":"<p>A Jamaican dancehall step challenge spreads because thousands of people film themselves copying it and tag friends.</p>","options":["cross-platform marketing","content curation","viral marketing"],"correct":2,"why":"The spread comes from people sharing and copying it, which is viral marketing with user-generated content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that practice (3)","q":"<p>A Barbados festival uses the same logo, colours, hashtag and slogan on its website, Instagram, radio ads and posters.</p>","options":["guerrilla marketing","SMS marketing","cross-platform marketing"],"correct":2,"why":"Consistent messaging across several channels is cross-platform marketing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that practice (4)","q":"<p>A gym in Bridgetown sends members a free monthly email with workouts and a renewal reminder.</p>","options":["guerrilla marketing","email marketing","viral marketing"],"correct":1,"why":"Regular emails to opted-in members are email marketing, combined with content marketing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that practice (5)","q":"<p>A Guyanese food creator with 40,000 engaged local followers is paid to review a new restaurant, and labels it #ad.</p>","options":["organic reach","influencer marketing","cross-platform marketing"],"correct":1,"why":"Partnering with a trusted creator who has a relevant audience is influencer marketing, and the #ad label is the honest disclosure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that practice (6)","q":"<p>A dentist in Mandeville texts patients a reminder the day before each appointment.</p>","options":["vlogging","text messaging (SMS)","viral marketing"],"correct":1,"why":"Short direct messages to a phone are SMS marketing, which works even without mobile data.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the guerilla tactics","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media has a list of ideas for launching an energy drink aimed at young people in Trinidad and Tobago.</p><p>Select ALL the ideas that are guerilla marketing.</p>","options":["Post the same weekly graphic on Facebook, Instagram and X.","Buy a full-page advert in a printed newspaper for six weeks.","Wrap the escalator at a shopping centre so the steps look like cans of the drink.","Send a welcome email to everyone who subscribes on the website.","Pay for a 30-second television advert across the region.","Dress a street lamp on the Brian Lara Promenade as a giant drink can with a QR code.","Turn a parking space on Frederick Street into a mini chill-out lounge for one Saturday, with permission.","Set up a surprise sampling team at a concert in Chaguanas to invite the crowd to take part (an event ambush)."],"correct":[2, 5, 6, 7],"why":"Correct. Guerilla tactics use surprise, a small budget and a particular public place: an escalator wrap, a parking space installation, an event ambush and a street lamp turned into a product."},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right influencer","q":"<p>Rodney Bay Smoothie Shack wants to increase visits from St. Lucia residents aged 18 to 30 and has a small budget. Which influencer partnership is the BEST choice?</p>","options":["An account with 80,000 followers that bought most of them and gets about 10 likes per post.","A popular gaming streamer in Europe with 50,000 followers who does not drink smoothies.","A St. Lucian fitness creator with 6,000 followers, mostly local young adults, who gets around 400 genuine comments and shares per post.","A US celebrity with 3 million followers, mostly in North America, who charges a very high fee per post."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Bought followers do not visit shops. The tiny engagement shows the audience is not real or not interested.","Wrong location, weak brand fit and no authenticity. Followers would not trust the recommendation.","Correct. The audience matches by location and age, engagement is high, and a nano-influencer fits a small budget.","Huge reach, but almost none of it is local, and the cost is far beyond a small budget. Reach without relevance wastes money."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Principle or tactic? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each statement for Emancipation Day concert campaign.</p><p>Is this one of the four principles of a unified strategy, or one of the four tactics?</p><p><b>Find out that 18 to 25 year olds on the island mostly use TikTok and Instagram.</b></p>","options":["Principle of a unified strategy","Tactic for a unified campaign"],"correct":0,"why":"Understanding your target audience, so resources go to the right platforms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Principle or tactic? (2)","q":"<p>Is this one of the four principles of a unified strategy, or one of the four tactics?</p><p><b>Use the same logo, colours and tone of voice on every post and poster.</b></p>","options":["Tactic for a unified campaign","Principle of a unified strategy"],"correct":1,"why":"Maintaining consistent branding keeps the brand recognisable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Principle or tactic? (3)","q":"<p>Is this one of the four principles of a unified strategy, or one of the four tactics?</p><p><b>Make a vertical video for TikTok and a square version for the Facebook feed.</b></p>","options":["Tactic for a unified campaign","Principle of a unified strategy"],"correct":1,"why":"Adapting content for each platform tailors formats to user behaviour.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Principle or tactic? (4)","q":"<p>Is this one of the four principles of a unified strategy, or one of the four tactics?</p><p><b>Reply to questions in the comments every evening and note common complaints.</b></p>","options":["Principle of a unified strategy","Tactic for a unified campaign"],"correct":0,"why":"Monitoring and engaging means listening and responding.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Principle or tactic? (5)","q":"<p>Is this one of the four principles of a unified strategy, or one of the four tactics?</p><p><b>Add keywords such as \"Georgetown concert tickets\" and make the page mobile-friendly.</b></p>","options":["Tactic for a unified campaign","Principle of a unified strategy"],"correct":0,"why":"This is search engine optimisation, one of the four tactics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Principle or tactic? (6)","q":"<p>Is this one of the four principles of a unified strategy, or one of the four tactics?</p><p><b>Run Facebook and Instagram ads aimed at 18 to 35 year olds in Guyana.</b></p>","options":["Principle of a unified strategy","Tactic for a unified campaign"],"correct":1,"why":"Cross-platform advertising lets you target specific demographics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Principle or tactic? (7)","q":"<p>Is this one of the four principles of a unified strategy, or one of the four tactics?</p><p><b>Partner with a local creator who posts on both TikTok and Instagram.</b></p>","options":["Principle of a unified strategy","Tactic for a unified campaign"],"correct":1,"why":"Influencer marketing across platforms taps diverse audiences and credibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Principle or tactic? (8)","q":"<p>Is this one of the four principles of a unified strategy, or one of the four tactics?</p><p><b>Send opted-in fans a compelling email with the line-up and a ticket button.</b></p>","options":["Tactic for a unified campaign","Principle of a unified strategy"],"correct":0,"why":"Email marketing reaches the audience directly.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Build an email campaign","q":"<p>Keisha is training a new intern to run the Bridgetown Book Fair email campaign in Mailchimp. Put the steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Collect subscribers with a sign-up form and QR code, so everyone on the list opted in.","Segment the audience with tags such as \"students\", \"teachers\" and \"parents\".","Create a Regular email campaign and write the subject line and preview text.","Design the email from a template: logo, one image with alt text, short copy and a call-to-action button.","Send a test email and check it on a phone and a desktop, clicking every link.","Schedule the send for the time the segment usually reads email.","Review the report: open rate, click-through rate, unsubscribes and bounces."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the email campaign type","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is setting up emails for the Punda Market online shop. Match each email to its type.</p>","left":["Welcome email","Newsletter","Promotional email","Special occasion email","Announcement email","Lead nurturing email","Transactional email","Abandoned cart email"],"right":["A three-part series building interest in a cooking class","\"Half price on all spices this Saturday only\"","A monthly round-up of new stalls, recipes and market news","\"You left two jars of hot sauce in your basket\"","A birthday greeting with a small discount code","\"We now deliver to Otrobanda and Westpunt\"","Sent the moment someone subscribes, introducing the shop","An order receipt with the delivery time"],"correct":[6,2,1,4,5,0,7,3]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the SMART objectives","q":"<p>A group's campaign plan for Belize Reef Clean-Up lists several \"objectives\". Only some are SMART.</p><p>Select every objective that is SMART (specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound).</p>","options":["Achieve a 25% open rate on the three volunteer emails sent in April.","Make the clean-up really popular.","Get everyone in Belize to care about the reef.","Post a lot of content on social media.","Collect 200 pledge sign-ups on the campaign landing page before Earth Day (22 April).","Grow the Instagram account from 800 to 1,200 followers during the six-week campaign.","Recruit 120 volunteers aged 15 to 25 through Instagram and WhatsApp by 30 April.","Be the best environmental campaign ever."],"correct":[0, 4, 5, 6],"why":"Correct. Each SMART objective names a number, an audience or channel, and a deadline, so success can be measured."},{"t":"mc","title":"Metrics workout (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is preparing a report for the Antigua Sailing Week Fan Zone campaign. Use the formulas to answer.</p><p>A post reached 5,000 people and received 400 engagements. What is the engagement rate (by reach)?</p>","options":["12.5%","4%","8%","80%"],"correct":2,"why":"400 ÷ 5,000 × 100 = 8%. Engagement rate compares interactions with the people reached.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Metrics workout (2)","q":"<p>An ad had 20,000 impressions and 300 clicks. What is the CTR?</p>","options":["0.15%","15%","6.7%","1.5%"],"correct":3,"why":"300 ÷ 20,000 × 100 = 1.5%. CTR divides clicks by impressions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Metrics workout (3)","q":"<p>300 people clicked through and 45 bought tickets. What is the conversion rate?</p>","options":["6.7%","30%","15%","45%"],"correct":2,"why":"45 ÷ 300 × 100 = 15%. Conversion rate divides goal actions by clicks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Metrics workout (4)","q":"<p>One person saw the same Story three times. How does this affect reach and impressions?</p>","options":["Reach 3, impressions 1","Reach 3, impressions 3","Reach 1, impressions 1","Reach 1, impressions 3"],"correct":3,"why":"Reach counts unique people (1); impressions count every display (3).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Metrics workout (5)","q":"<p>The objective is \"sell 500 fan zone passes by 20 April\". Which is the most important KPI?</p>","options":["passes sold (conversions)","follower count","impressions","number of likes"],"correct":0,"why":"The KPI must match the objective. Likes and followers are vanity metrics here; sales are the conversion that matters.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Metrics workout (6)","q":"<p>Keisha wants to know which of three influencers produced the most sales. The best tracking method is...</p>","options":["compare the influencers' profile photos","ask customers to guess","give each influencer a unique discount code or UTM link","count each influencer's followers"],"correct":2,"why":"Unique codes or tagged links tie each sale to the source that produced it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Running the Carnival app campaign (step 1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has built an Antigua Carnival companion app with the parade route, J'ouvert times and Carnival City show line-ups. Keisha must promote it with a small budget. Make the decisions.</p><p>The client says, \"Just tell everybody about the app.\" What does Keisha do first?</p>","options":["Start posting immediately on every platform she can think of.","Agree a SMART objective: 3,000 downloads from residents and visitors in the four weeks before the Grand Parade."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Without an objective or audience, there is no way to choose channels or judge success.","Good. A measurable objective with a deadline tells the team what success looks like."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Running the Carnival app campaign (step 2)","q":"<p>The main audience is 16 to 35 year old Carnival lovers on the island, plus visitors arriving through V. C. Bird International Airport. Which channel mix does she choose?</p>","options":["A LinkedIn article and a printed brochure only.","TikTok and Instagram Reels with a branded hashtag, WhatsApp Status graphics, a nano-influencer partnership, and QR code posters at Carnival City.","Buy a list of 10,000 email addresses and send them all a message."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["LinkedIn and brochures do not reach young Carnival fans, and nothing links them to a download.","Strong cross-platform mix. It reaches young locals where they are and links offline posters to downloads.","Bought lists break consent rules and damage the brand. Email must go to people who opted in."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Running the Carnival app campaign (step 3)","q":"<p>Two weeks in, the Reels have huge reach and lots of likes, but downloads are only 600. What does Keisha do?</p>","options":["Celebrate the likes and report that the campaign is a success.","Check analytics: CTR is low, so she adds a clear \"Download free\" call to action, a link sticker and a UTM link, and tests a new caption."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Likes are a vanity metric here. The objective is downloads, and it is far behind.","Excellent. She looked past vanity metrics to the conversion problem and changed one thing at a time."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Running the Carnival app campaign (step 4)","q":"<p>An influencer offers to post about the app without labelling it as paid, \"so it looks natural\". How does Keisha respond?</p>","options":["Insist on clear disclosure (#ad or the paid partnership label) in the written agreement.","Accept, because unlabelled posts get more engagement."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Disclosure is honest, protects the brand and is required in many jurisdictions.","Hiding a paid relationship misleads followers and can lead to complaints and loss of trust."]}]},{"id":"assess_1wt","title":"Module 1 · Unit 10 quiz: Trends and developments in visual and interactive design","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 10: Trends and developments in visual and interactive design. 35 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Pervasiveness of technology: the five points","q":"<p>There are five ways the pervasiveness of technology affects design. Match each point to the Caribbean example that shows it.</p>","left":["Used in every step","Designs for all devices","Supports accessibility and inclusion","Enables constant improvement","Always connected"],"right":["A contrast checker and scalable text are added to a Barbados government form","Analytics show users leave the checkout page, so the team updates it the same week","The Nevis ferry site must work on a phone at the dock, a tablet and a hotel laptop","Keisha plans in Canva, Zara designs in Figma and Malik finishes the video in Adobe Premiere Pro","Designers in Bridgetown and Kingston edit the same cloud file in real time"],"correct":[3,2,0,1,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which accessibility consideration? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing the Lagoon Kayak Tours app. Choose the accessibility consideration that each change shows.</p><p>Jerome raises the colour contrast of the grey text and enlarges the font for readability.</p>","options":["Design thinking","Inclusive design","Cross-platform compatibility"],"correct":1,"why":"Colour contrast, text readability and navigational structure are the inclusive design points in the handout.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which accessibility consideration? (2)","q":"<p>The booking screen is checked on a tablet, a laptop and two different phones.</p>","options":["Cross-platform compatibility","User-centric design","Inclusive design"],"correct":0,"why":"Making the design accessible across desktops, tablets and mobile devices is cross-platform compatibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which accessibility consideration? (3)","q":"<p>Before redesigning, the team interviews tour guides and visitors about what frustrates them.</p>","options":["Commoditisation","User-centric design","Cross-platform compatibility"],"correct":1,"why":"User research and testing to understand needs and preferences is user-centric design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which accessibility consideration? (4)","q":"<p>The tour company uses empathise, define, ideate, prototype and test to improve its whole booking process, not just the app.</p>","options":["Design thinking","Affordability","Inclusive design"],"correct":0,"why":"The handout notes that design thinking is now applied to business processes to foster innovation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which accessibility consideration? (5)","q":"<p>The owner asks why accessibility matters. Which reason is correct?</p>","options":["It removes the need for testing","It makes the app cheaper to host","It is often required by law, for example WCAG standards"],"correct":2,"why":"The three reasons are: more people can use it, it is inclusive and fair, and it is often required by law (WCAG).","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Which features empower users?","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing a draft of the Coronation Market Pay app in Kingston against the eight empowerment considerations.</p><p>Select every feature that EMPOWERS users.</p>","options":["Error messages say only \"Error 4031\".","Users can choose English, Dutch, French or Spanish.","The settings page is hidden three menus deep with no label.","The home screen shows twelve buttons, three banners and a pop-up advert at once.","A \"Saved\" message appears when a product listing is stored.","Users can undo a payment request within one minute.","Menu labels use plain words such as \"Pay\", \"Sell\" and \"Help\".","A progress bar shows \"Step 2 of 3\" during sign-up."],"correct":[1, 4, 5, 6, 7],"why":"Correct. Progress indicators, undo (user control), clear labels (clarity and intuitive navigation), language choice (inclusive language) and \"Saved\" feedback all empower users."},{"t":"mc","title":"Which factor is driving this? (1)","q":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media keeps a notebook of changes she sees in design across the Caribbean. Sort each note by the factor it best illustrates.</p><p>Which factor is the main driver?</p><p><b>Hundreds of near-identical restaurant menus in Barbados use the same free template.</b></p>","options":["Affordability","Commoditisation","Accessibility","Pervasiveness of technology"],"correct":1,"why":"Standardised, interchangeable design products are a sign of commoditisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which factor is driving this? (2)","q":"<p>Which factor is the main driver?</p><p><b>The same airline booking flow must work on a phone, a tablet, a laptop and an airport kiosk.</b></p>","options":["Accessibility","Commoditisation","Pervasiveness of technology","Affordability"],"correct":2,"why":"Technology on every kind of screen drives responsive, multi-device design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which factor is driving this? (3)","q":"<p>Which factor is the main driver?</p><p><b>A Belize student makes a professional animation with Blender, which costs nothing.</b></p>","options":["Affordability","Commoditisation","Pervasiveness of technology","Accessibility"],"correct":0,"why":"Free software lowers cost, so more people can create.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which factor is driving this? (4)","q":"<p>Which factor is the main driver?</p><p><b>A government website adds captions, alt text and a high-contrast mode for visually impaired users.</b></p>","options":["Pervasiveness of technology","Commoditisation","Affordability","Accessibility"],"correct":3,"why":"Designing for people with disabilities is accessibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which factor is driving this? (5)","q":"<p>Which factor is the main driver?</p><p><b>Basic website themes now sell for a few dollars and buyers choose mainly on price.</b></p>","options":["Affordability","Commoditisation","Accessibility","Pervasiveness of technology"],"correct":1,"why":"When buyers see little difference and choose on price, the product has been commoditised.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which factor is driving this? (6)","q":"<p>Which factor is the main driver?</p><p><b>Smartwatches, smart TVs and car dashboards all need interfaces readable at a glance.</b></p>","options":["Pervasiveness of technology","Accessibility","Affordability","Commoditisation"],"correct":0,"why":"Screens everywhere in daily life illustrate pervasiveness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which factor is driving this? (7)","q":"<p>Which factor is the main driver?</p><p><b>Cheaper mid-range phones with good cameras lead to more vertical, phone-shot ads.</b></p>","options":["Affordability","Pervasiveness of technology","Accessibility","Commoditisation"],"correct":0,"why":"Lower device cost (affordability) changed the style of video content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which factor is driving this? (8)","q":"<p>Which factor is the main driver?</p><p><b>Free public Wi-Fi in a town square lets more residents use an online heritage guide.</b></p>","options":["Commoditisation","Accessibility","Affordability","Pervasiveness of technology"],"correct":1,"why":"Improving access to technology widens who can use digital content, which a strong answer adds to the accessibility definition.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the six factors","q":"<p>Match each factor to the Caribbean example that best illustrates it.</p>","left":["Commoditisation","Pervasiveness of technology","Affordability","Accessibility","Adoption","Empowerment"],"right":["Logo templates are so common that a Kingston agency competes on original brand strategy instead","Flight information, payments and hotel check-in at the airport all happen on screens","A Dominica bill-pay app shows clear steps, progress bars and an undo button so customers pay confidently on their own","A Guyanese teen edits music videos on a low-cost phone with a free app","Dark mode becomes expected in every app once major phone systems include it","A St. Lucia tourism site meets WCAG contrast rules and captions every video"],"correct":[0,1,3,5,4,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the trend (1)","q":"<p>Jerome is reviewing app designs for Caribbean Beats music app. Identify the trend or evaluate it.</p><p>A now-playing panel is semi-transparent with the album art blurred behind it and a thin white border.</p>","options":["glassmorphism","neumorphism","dark mode"],"correct":0,"why":"Translucency, background blur and a light border are the signs of glassmorphism.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the trend (2)","q":"<p>Buttons look softly raised from a pale grey background, with a light shadow top-left and a dark shadow bottom-right.</p>","options":["glassmorphism","neumorphism","flat design"],"correct":1,"why":"Two soft shadows on a same-colour background create the extruded look of neumorphism.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the trend (3)","q":"<p>Users can switch to light text on a dark grey background for late-night listening.</p>","options":["immersive UI","dark mode","motion design"],"correct":1,"why":"Light content on a dark background, chosen by the user, is dark mode.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the trend (4)","q":"<p>Testers with low vision cannot tell whether a neumorphic play button is pressed. What is the main problem?</p>","options":["file size","too many colours","low contrast and unclear state"],"correct":2,"why":"Neumorphism relies on subtle shadows, so contrast is low and state changes are hard to see.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the trend (5)","q":"<p>Which is the BEST practice for a dark mode palette?</p>","options":["dark grey background with softened colours and good contrast","pure black with fully saturated neon text","white text on mid-grey"],"correct":0,"why":"Dark grey reduces harsh contrast and smearing, while softened colours stay readable and pass contrast checks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which trend fits the brief?","q":"<p>The Dominica Waitukubuli Trail app needs to confirm instantly when a hiker taps \"Save trail for offline use\", so that users know the download has started without opening a new screen. Which design trend is the BEST fit?</p>","options":["Dark mode: switch the whole app to a dark theme.","Immersive UI: a full-screen 3D fly-through of the whole trail.","Neumorphism: make the save button look softly raised.","A micro-interaction: the save icon fills with a small progress animation and the phone gives a short vibration."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Dark mode changes the colour scheme; it does not tell the user that their action worked.","Impressive, but it does not confirm the save, and it would use a lot of data on a trail with weak signal.","A visual style does not provide feedback, and neumorphic buttons often make the pressed state harder to see.","Correct. A micro-interaction gives small, immediate feedback for a single action, exactly what the brief describes."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The AI artwork dilemma (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a junior designer at Island Pulse Media. The Jacmel Food Festival in Haiti wants a poster and social pack in three days. Make the right calls.</p><p>The client loves a Midjourney image a colleague made by typing \"in the style of\" a well-known living Caribbean painter. What do you do?</p>","options":["Explain the ethical and legal risk of imitating a living artist, and propose original concepts instead.","Use it; the client likes it and nobody will notice."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Copying a living artist's style can harm their livelihood and the client's reputation.","The artist and their fans are likely to notice, and the festival could face public criticism."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The AI artwork dilemma (step 2)","q":"<p>You decide to use AI for early ideas only. Which tool and workflow do you choose for concept images?</p>","options":["Generate the final poster entirely with AI and submit it as your own hand-made work.","Adobe Firefly for mood-board ideas, then design the final poster yourself in Illustrator and Photoshop."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Presenting AI output as fully your own is dishonest, and purely AI material may not be protected by copyright.","Sensible. AI speeds up ideation while the final work stays human-made and under your control."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The AI artwork dilemma (step 3)","q":"<p>An AI mood-board image shows festival-goers in clichéd, inaccurate \"tropical\" costume that does not reflect Haitian culture. What next?</p>","options":["Reject it, and use your own photographs from local events, with permission, as the visual reference.","Keep it; tourists expect that look."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Checking AI output for bias and using authentic local imagery respects the community.","Stereotyped imagery misrepresents the culture and can offend the local audience."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The AI artwork dilemma (step 4)","q":"<p>The client asks how AI was used. How do you respond?</p>","options":["Say no AI was used at all.","Disclose honestly: AI helped with early mood boards; all final artwork and photography is original."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A false statement could damage the relationship if the truth comes out later.","Transparency builds trust and protects both the agency and the client."]},{"t":"mc","title":"AR or VR? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media has received eight project ideas. Sort each one.</p><p>Is this augmented reality or virtual reality?</p><p><b>Point a phone at Brimstone Hill Fortress and see the original walls rebuilt on screen.</b></p>","options":["Augmented reality (AR)","Virtual reality (VR)"],"correct":0,"why":"Digital content is layered over the real ruins, so it is AR.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AR or VR? (2)","q":"<p>Is this augmented reality or virtual reality?</p><p><b>Wear a headset and explore a fully virtual coral reef without leaving the classroom.</b></p>","options":["Augmented reality (AR)","Virtual reality (VR)"],"correct":1,"why":"The real world is replaced by a virtual one: VR.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AR or VR? (3)","q":"<p>Is this augmented reality or virtual reality?</p><p><b>An Instagram filter that adds Carnival feathers to your selfie.</b></p>","options":["Virtual reality (VR)","Augmented reality (AR)"],"correct":1,"why":"Face filters add graphics on top of the live camera view: AR.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AR or VR? (4)","q":"<p>Is this augmented reality or virtual reality?</p><p><b>A fully simulated cockpit for training pilots at a flight school in Trinidad.</b></p>","options":["Virtual reality (VR)","Augmented reality (AR)"],"correct":0,"why":"An immersive simulated environment is VR.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AR or VR? (5)","q":"<p>Is this augmented reality or virtual reality?</p><p><b>See how a new sofa would look in your living room through the phone camera.</b></p>","options":["Augmented reality (AR)","Virtual reality (VR)"],"correct":0,"why":"A virtual object is placed in the real room: AR.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AR or VR? (6)","q":"<p>Is this augmented reality or virtual reality?</p><p><b>A 360-degree headset tour of a Barbados hotel suite before booking.</b></p>","options":["Virtual reality (VR)","Augmented reality (AR)"],"correct":0,"why":"The viewer is immersed in a recorded or virtual space, not their real surroundings: VR.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AR or VR? (7)","q":"<p>Is this augmented reality or virtual reality?</p><p><b>Arrows appear on the live street view to guide you to the Basseterre ferry.</b></p>","options":["Virtual reality (VR)","Augmented reality (AR)"],"correct":1,"why":"Directions overlaid on the real street are AR.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AR or VR? (8)","q":"<p>Is this augmented reality or virtual reality?</p><p><b>A virtual concert hall where fans with headsets watch a soca artist perform as avatars.</b></p>","options":["Augmented reality (AR)","Virtual reality (VR)"],"correct":1,"why":"Users are inside a fully virtual environment: VR.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Good trend research?","q":"<p>A CAPE group in Antigua is researching design trends for a school app. Here are statements from their notes.</p><p>Select every statement that shows GOOD trend research practice.</p>","options":["We saw one cool design on Dribbble, so it must be the biggest trend in the world.","Google Trends shows that exactly 50 people searched for neumorphism last week.","We copied a Behance designer's layout exactly and called it our own.","We checked whether a heavy immersive design would load on the low-cost phones most students use.","We collected examples from Dribbble, Behance and Awwwards and recorded the date and link for each.","We noted that dark mode grew after Apple and Google built it into their phone systems, an example of adoption.","We compared \"dark mode\" and \"glassmorphism\" in Google Trends over five years with the region set to the Caribbean countries available.","We tested our trend-based mock-up with ten students and recorded their feedback."],"correct":[3, 4, 5, 6, 7],"why":"Correct. Good trend research compares several sources, records evidence, links trends to factors, checks audience fit and tests with users."}]},{"id":"assess_22j","title":"Module 1 · Unit 11 quiz: Considerations for a project proposal","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 11: Considerations for a project proposal. 45 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the proposal section","q":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is checking a draft proposal for the Grenada Spice Island Recipes app. Match each extract to its section.</p>","left":["Executive summary","Problem statement","Objectives","Methodology","Timeline","Target audience","Budget"],"right":["We will follow the creative design process, filming in 4K and building the app in Thunkable","A Gantt chart: research weeks 1 to 2, filming weeks 3 to 6, build weeks 7 to 10, testing weeks 11 to 12","Grenadians aged 16 to 35 at home and in the diaspora who use smartphones daily","65% of surveyed young Grenadians said they rarely cook traditional dishes because recipes are not written down","Publish 40 video recipes and reach 2,000 app users by 31 August","A half-page overview of the problem, solution, cost of USD 9,500 and 12-week timeline","An itemised table of equipment, software, personnel, hosting and a 10% contingency"],"correct":[5,3,4,0,1,2,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audience and market check (1)","q":"<p>Jerome is reviewing a CAPE group's proposal for a St. Kitts bus-times app. Answer each question.</p><p>\"Most of our users use Android phones on prepaid data plans.\" This is an example of...</p>","options":["technographics","psychographics","demographics"],"correct":0,"why":"Devices and data plans describe technology use, which is technographics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audience and market check (2)","q":"<p>\"Our users value saving time and dislike waiting in the sun without information.\" This is...</p>","options":["a competitor analysis","psychographics and pain points","market size"],"correct":1,"why":"Values, attitudes and frustrations are psychographics and pain points.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audience and market check (3)","q":"<p>The group lists two existing bus apps from other islands and notes what each lacks. This is...</p>","options":["a SWOT strength","a problem statement","competitor analysis"],"correct":2,"why":"Studying existing products and their weaknesses is competitor analysis, which reveals the gap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audience and market check (4)","q":"<p>\"Heavy rain and hurricanes could stop buses and make the app's data wrong.\" In SWOT this is a...</p>","options":["threat","strength","weakness"],"correct":0,"why":"It is external and harmful, so it is a threat. Weaknesses are internal to the team or product.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audience and market check (5)","q":"<p>Which is the BEST data capture instrument for this proposal?</p>","options":["asking two friends what they think","a Google Forms survey of bus users at the Basseterre bus terminal, with 10 clear questions","copying statistics from an unrelated country"],"correct":1,"why":"A designed survey of real users gives relevant evidence and earns the SBA research marks.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Put the proposal in order","q":"<p>A CAPE group in Barbados has written its proposal sections on separate pages. Put them in the order a reader would expect to find them.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Title page","Executive summary","Background and problem statement","Proposed solution and SMART objectives","Target audience and market analysis","Methodology and timeline","Itemised budget","Evaluation plan and conclusion"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (1)","q":"<p>This is the \"Funding a Digital Media Project\" activity. Each card is a statement about one of seven funding categories: crowdfunding, grants, angel investors, loans, venture capital, sponsorships and partnerships, and bootstrapping. Is it a benefit or a disadvantage for the creator?</p><p>Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?</p><p><b>Crowdfunding: it can build a loyal audience early and prove demand before launch.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Benefit"],"correct":1,"why":"Backers are your first fans, and their pledges are evidence that people want the product: a benefit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?</p><p><b>Crowdfunding: it needs heavy promotion and frequent updates, and campaigns can fail to reach the goal.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Benefit"],"correct":0,"why":"A campaign is a lot of marketing work with no guarantee of success: a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?</p><p><b>Grants: they provide money without giving up ownership or taking on debt.</b></p>","options":["Benefit","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Non-repayable money that costs you no shares is a major benefit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?</p><p><b>Grants: they are competitive and demand detailed proposals, timelines and reporting.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Benefit"],"correct":0,"why":"Many applicants, long forms and strict reports take time and may still end in rejection: a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?</p><p><b>Angel investors: they often add mentoring and industry connections along with the money.</b></p>","options":["Benefit","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Advice and contacts from an experienced investor can be worth as much as the cash: a benefit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?</p><p><b>Loans: you keep full ownership and creative control of the project.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Benefit"],"correct":1,"why":"A lender wants repayment, not shares or a say in your content: a benefit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?</p><p><b>Loans: repayments add financial pressure and are due even if income is delayed.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Benefit"],"correct":0,"why":"The bank still expects its monthly payment if the series launches late: a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?</p><p><b>Venture capital: it typically requires significant equity and board or decision-making power.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Benefit"],"correct":0,"why":"Giving away a large share and control of direction is the main drawback of VC.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (9)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?</p><p><b>Sponsorships and partnerships: creative choices may need to follow sponsor guidelines.</b></p>","options":["Benefit","Disadvantage"],"correct":1,"why":"A sponsor protects its brand, which can limit what you say or show: a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (10)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?</p><p><b>Bootstrapping: it preserves complete ownership and creative freedom.</b></p>","options":["Benefit","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Using your own money means nobody else owns or directs the project: a benefit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Debt or equity? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is explaining funding offers to a new client, the Salt Pond Animation Studio in Sandy Ground, Anguilla. Sort each offer.</p><p>Is this debt or equity?</p><p><b>A local bank lends XCD 20,000 for animation computers, repaid monthly over three years at an agreed interest rate.</b></p>","options":["Equity","Debt"],"correct":1,"why":"Borrowed money repaid with interest is debt. The bank owns no part of the studio.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Debt or equity? (2)","q":"<p>Is this debt or equity?</p><p><b>A credit union lends to the studio, secured against its camera and lighting equipment.</b></p>","options":["Debt","Equity"],"correct":0,"why":"A secured loan uses assets as collateral, but it is still borrowing: debt.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Debt or equity? (3)","q":"<p>Is this debt or equity?</p><p><b>An angel investor puts in USD 30,000 in return for 10% of the studio's shares.</b></p>","options":["Equity","Debt"],"correct":0,"why":"Money in exchange for a share of ownership is equity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Debt or equity? (4)","q":"<p>Is this debt or equity?</p><p><b>A venture capital firm invests a large sum, takes 30% ownership and a seat on the board.</b></p>","options":["Debt","Equity"],"correct":1,"why":"VC is equity funding: a big stake plus influence over direction.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Debt or equity? (5)","q":"<p>Is this debt or equity?</p><p><b>300 people each lend a small amount through an online platform and are repaid with interest.</b></p>","options":["Equity","Debt"],"correct":1,"why":"This is debt crowdfunding (peer-to-peer lending): many small loans, still debt.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Debt or equity? (6)","q":"<p>Is this debt or equity?</p><p><b>Fans invest through an equity crowdfunding platform and each receive a small share of the company.</b></p>","options":["Equity","Debt"],"correct":0,"why":"Equity crowdfunding gives backers shares, so it is equity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Debt or equity? (7)","q":"<p>Is this debt or equity?</p><p><b>The owner's uncle lends XCD 5,000 under a written agreement to be repaid within a year.</b></p>","options":["Equity","Debt"],"correct":1,"why":"FFF money can be either type. This one must be repaid, so it is debt.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Debt or equity? (8)","q":"<p>Is this debt or equity?</p><p><b>The owner's aunt gives XCD 5,000 in exchange for a 5% share of the studio.</b></p>","options":["Equity","Debt"],"correct":0,"why":"FFF money in exchange for ownership is equity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Debt or equity? (9)","q":"<p>Is this debt or equity?</p><p><b>A short-term bridge loan pays the voice actors until a grant arrives, then is paid back.</b></p>","options":["Equity","Debt"],"correct":1,"why":"Bridge funding is temporary borrowing that is repaid: debt.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which funding source? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is advising clients on how to fund their projects. Sort each description.</p><p>Which source of funding is described?</p><p><b>A ministry awards XCD 15,000 for a youth culture documentary; it does not need to be repaid but receipts must be submitted.</b></p>","options":["Crowdfunding","Angel investor","Sponsorship","Government grant"],"correct":3,"why":"Public money for a project that meets public goals, with reporting rules: a government grant.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which funding source? (2)","q":"<p>Which source of funding is described?</p><p><b>420 people each pledge between USD 5 and USD 100 on Kickstarter to fund a Caribbean folklore animation.</b></p>","options":["Government grant","Angel investor","Sponsorship","Crowdfunding"],"correct":3,"why":"Many small online pledges from the public is crowdfunding.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which funding source? (3)","q":"<p>Which source of funding is described?</p><p><b>A beverage company pays for the stage screens at a festival in return for its logo on every video.</b></p>","options":["Crowdfunding","Angel investor","Government grant","Sponsorship"],"correct":3,"why":"Money in return for visibility is sponsorship.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which funding source? (4)","q":"<p>Which source of funding is described?</p><p><b>A retired businesswoman invests USD 50,000 in a student's app start-up for 15% of the company.</b></p>","options":["Government grant","Sponsorship","Angel investor","Crowdfunding"],"correct":2,"why":"Personal money for a share of ownership (equity) is angel investment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which funding source? (5)","q":"<p>Which source of funding is described?</p><p><b>Supporters receive rewards such as stickers, a credit in the film or an early download, depending on how much they pledge.</b></p>","options":["Angel investor","Government grant","Crowdfunding","Sponsorship"],"correct":2,"why":"Reward tiers are a typical feature of crowdfunding campaigns.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which funding source? (6)","q":"<p>Which source of funding is described?</p><p><b>A mobile network provides free data for viewers of a learning app, and its name appears on the splash screen.</b></p>","options":["Sponsorship","Government grant","Angel investor","Crowdfunding"],"correct":0,"why":"In-kind support in exchange for brand visibility is sponsorship.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which funding source? (7)","q":"<p>Which source of funding is described?</p><p><b>The applicant must complete a formal application form and meet a fixed deadline set by a public department.</b></p>","options":["Sponsorship","Angel investor","Government grant","Crowdfunding"],"correct":2,"why":"Formal applications to a public department are the typical route to a government grant.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which funding source? (8)","q":"<p>Which source of funding is described?</p><p><b>The investor also mentors the team and joins its board of directors.</b></p>","options":["Crowdfunding","Government grant","Angel investor","Sponsorship"],"correct":2,"why":"Angel investors often bring advice and contacts, and may want a say in decisions.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the funding term","q":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is preparing a glossary for a funding workshop for young creators in Basseterre. Match each term to its definition.</p>","left":["Debt financing","Equity financing","FFF","Angel investor","Venture capital","Restricted funding","Bridge funding","Bootstrapping"],"right":["Money raised by selling shares of ownership in the business","A wealthy individual who invests personal money in an early-stage business, usually for equity","Borrowed money repaid with interest on an agreed schedule","Temporary money to cover a short gap until expected funding arrives","A firm investing large sums in high-growth companies for a big stake and board influence","Early money from friends, family and supporters who back you on trust","Money that may only be spent on the purpose the funder specified","Funding a project from your own savings, income or pre-sales"],"correct":[2,0,5,1,4,6,3,7]},{"t":"mc","title":"Funding Salt Pond Tales (step 1)","q":"<p>Zara and Malik at the Island Pulse Media want to make a six-episode animated web series, Salt Pond Tales, retelling Caribbean folk stories for children. The budget is XCD 45,000. You are advising them on funding.</p><p>Zara asks for your first funding step. What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Apply to a CARICOM-region arts council or cultural grant programme with the full proposal, budget and timeline, and ask a local business to sponsor the launch.","Borrow the full XCD 45,000 as an unsecured, high-interest loan, even though no income is expected until the series is finished.","Plan an IPO on a regional stock exchange to raise the whole amount."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong. A non-repayable grant plus a sponsor starts a mixed funding plan without debt or loss of ownership.","Repayments would start long before the series earns anything.","An Initial Public Offering is for large, established companies, not a first project."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Funding Salt Pond Tales (step 2)","q":"<p>The grant panel awards XCD 25,000, restricted to animation and voice talent. Bay Street Bakehouse sponsors XCD 5,000 for its logo at the end of each episode. XCD 15,000 is still needed. Keisha suggests crowdfunding. Which campaign do you plan?</p>","options":["A rewards-based Kickstarter campaign with a realistic XCD 15,000 target, a 60-second trailer, reward tiers (name in the credits, sticker pack, early access) and weekly updates.","A Kickstarter campaign with an XCD 60,000 target \"to be safe\", promoted with one post."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. A realistic target, a strong video and clear rewards give an all-or-nothing campaign the best chance.","On an all-or-nothing platform, missing a target that high means collecting nothing."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Funding Salt Pond Tales (step 3)","q":"<p>The campaign succeeds, but the grant payment is delayed by two months and the voice actors must be paid now. What do you do?</p>","options":["Arrange short-term bridge funding, such as a small credit union loan, repay it when the grant arrives and add the interest to the budget.","Accept an angel investor's offer of XCD 10,000 for 40% of the rights to the series."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Bridge funding is designed for exactly this kind of short gap.","That gives away a large, permanent share to solve a two-month problem."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Funding Graeme Hall Swamp Tales (step 4)","q":"<p>The grant arrives. Malik suggests using some of it to pay the office rent and boost social media posts. How do you respond?</p>","options":["No: the grant is restricted to animation and voice talent. Pay rent and promotion from unrestricted money, such as the sponsor fee, and keep receipts for the grant report.","Yes: the funder will probably not check."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Exactly. Restricted funding must be spent only on its stated purpose, and reports must prove it.","Misusing restricted money breaks the agreement and damages your reputation with funders."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Audit the budget","q":"<p>A CAPE group in Jamaica is proposing a 30-video cooking channel. Maya at the Island Pulse Media is checking their budget lines.</p><p>Select every budget line that has a PROBLEM (missing detail, unrealistic figure, or a calculation error).</p>","options":["Microphone: 2 × USD 45 = USD 120","Food ingredients: lots of food = USD 100","Camera rental: 4 days × USD 60 = USD 240","Web hosting for the recipe site: 12 months × USD 8 = USD 96","Contingency (10% of subtotal) = USD 70","Editing software: DaVinci Resolve free version = USD 0","Presenter fee: 30 videos × USD 0.50 = USD 15","Miscellaneous stuff = USD 500"],"correct":[0, 1, 4, 6, 7],"why":"Good auditing. You spotted a vague quantity, a calculation error (2 × 45 = 90, not 120), an unrealistic fee, a contingency that is not 10% of the other lines, and an unexplained \"miscellaneous\" line."},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right deck slide","q":"<p>A group is pitching the Tobago Reef Watch app to a panel of local sponsors. They have 10 minutes. Which slide design is MOST effective for presenting their survey results?</p>","options":["Five different charts on one slide, each in different colours and fonts, to show how much work was done.","A slide with the full questionnaire and every response pasted in 12-point text.","One slide with a bold headline (\"82% of divers want live reef updates\"), one clear bar chart in brand colours, and the source of the data in small text.","A slide with a dramatic stock photo of the sea and no data at all."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Clutter and inconsistent design weaken the message. Choose the one chart that proves the point.","Too much text: the panel cannot read it from a distance and the key finding is lost. Put full data in an appendix.","Correct. One idea per slide, a visual chart, a headline that states the finding and a cited source make the evidence quick to grasp and credible.","Attractive, but it gives no evidence. Sponsors need proof that users want the app."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pitch day at Island Pulse Media (step 1)","q":"<p>Your CAPE group is pitching an app, Market Day KY, that lists local vendors at the George Town farmers' market, to a panel that includes a possible sponsor and an angel investor. Make the decisions.</p><p>You have 8 minutes. How do you open?</p>","options":["By reading your full proposal document aloud from the first page.","With a hook: a 15-second phone video of a vendor saying sales dropped because tourists cannot find her stall, plus one survey statistic."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Reading a document loses the audience. A pitch must be selective and engaging.","Strong. A human story and evidence grab attention and establish the problem."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pitch day at Island Pulse Media (step 2)","q":"<p>The sponsor asks, \"Why should we believe vendors and visitors will use this?\" How do you answer?</p>","options":["\"Everyone will love it, trust us.\"","Show your market analysis: survey of 60 visitors and 25 vendors, competitor gaps, and the persona slide."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Claims without evidence do not persuade funders.","Good. Evidence from your research answers the question convincingly."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pitch day at Island Pulse Media (step 3)","q":"<p>The angel investor asks about money. Which budget slide do you show?</p>","options":["A clear itemised summary by category (production, equipment, software, personnel, hosting, marketing) with a 10% contingency, total in KYD and USD, and your exact ask.","A single line: \"Total cost: about KYD 10,000, give or take.\""],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Clear categories, contingency and a specific ask show you have planned realistically.","A vague figure suggests poor planning; investors want to see what the money buys."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pitch day at Island Pulse Media (step 4)","q":"<p>The investor offers money in return for 40% of the app. You only wanted sponsorship. What do you say?</p>","options":["Thank them, explain the difference between equity and sponsorship, and ask for time to consider and compare options.","Accept on the spot to secure the money."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Wise. Understanding what each funding source costs you is part of a professional pitch.","Giving away a large share without thinking can cost you control of your own project."]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Module 2","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_27v","title":"Module 2 · Unit 1 quiz: Digital publishing and internet terminologies","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 1: Digital publishing and internet terminologies. 45 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Which key feature is this? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is explaining digital publishing platforms to a client. Name the key feature, from the list of eight, that each situation shows.</p><p>A Grenada author sees a dashboard showing how many readers finished chapter 3 and where they live.</p>","options":["Analytics","Self-publishing","Content creation"],"correct":0,"why":"Tracking engagement and audience data is analytics. Creators use it to improve content and marketing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which key feature is this? (2)","q":"<p>A Jamaican cookbook writer sets a price of US$4.99 and earns a royalty on every download.</p>","options":["Interactivity","Accessibility","Monetisation"],"correct":2,"why":"Earning money from content through sales, subscriptions or ads is monetisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which key feature is this? (3)","q":"<p>A Dominican student publishes her novel without being accepted by any publishing house.</p>","options":["Self-publishing","Distribution","Global reach"],"correct":0,"why":"Publishing independently, without a traditional publishing house, is self-publishing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which key feature is this? (4)","q":"<p>The same e-book opens on a Kindle, an Android phone, an iPad and a laptop.</p>","options":["Monetisation","Analytics","Accessibility"],"correct":2,"why":"Reading on many kinds of devices is the accessibility feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which key feature is this? (5)","q":"<p>A Barbados history e-book includes embedded video of Crop Over and links to museum archives.</p>","options":["Self-publishing","Global reach","Interactivity"],"correct":2,"why":"Multimedia and hyperlinks that go beyond print are interactivity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which key feature is this? (6)","q":"<p>A Belize travel guide is bought by readers in Japan, Germany and Canada on its first day, with no shipping.</p>","options":["Content creation","Analytics","Global reach"],"correct":2,"why":"Reaching a worldwide audience without physical distribution channels is global reach.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which key feature is this? (7)","q":"<p>A platform offers templates to lay out text, add images and build a quiz at the end of each chapter.</p>","options":["Monetisation","Content creation","Distribution"],"correct":1,"why":"Tools for creating and formatting content are the content creation feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the publishing platform","q":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is advising three local authors. Match each platform to its best description.</p>","left":["Amazon KDP","Draft2Digital","IngramSpark","Apple Books","Google Play Books","Kobo Writing Life","Barnes &amp; Noble Press","Wattpad"],"right":["Self-publishing portal for Rakuten Kobo e-readers and store","Story-sharing community whose Wattpad Books programme publishes selected stories","Self-publishing for Kindle e-books and print-on-demand paperbacks; assigns an ASIN","US bookseller service publishing to the Nook e-reader and app","An aggregator: upload once and the book goes to many stores","E-book store reached through the Partner Center, strong on Android devices","Store and reading app on iPhone, iPad and Mac with strong EPUB 3 support","Distributor that places books in bookshop and library catalogues"],"correct":[2,4,7,6,5,0,3,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or CMS? Make the call (1)","q":"<p>Clients are calling the Island Pulse Media. Choose the best option for each.</p><p>An Ocho Rios restaurant owner with no coding skills wants a beautiful menu site, hosting included, set up this week.</p>","options":["Wix or Squarespace","Self-hosted Joomla","IngramSpark"],"correct":0,"why":"Hosted drag-and-drop builders suit beginners who need speed and included hosting. Joomla needs technical skill, and IngramSpark is for books.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or CMS? Make the call (2)","q":"<p>A Guyanese historian wants her e-book in Apple Books, Kobo and Barnes &amp; Noble without uploading three times.</p>","options":["Amazon KDP only","Draft2Digital","Wix"],"correct":1,"why":"Draft2Digital is an aggregator: one upload, many stores. KDP reaches only Amazon.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or CMS? Make the call (3)","q":"<p>A Jamaican news start-up needs a flexible, open-source site with plugins and several editors with different roles.</p>","options":["WordPress (self-hosted)","Squarespace","Google Play Books"],"correct":0,"why":"Self-hosted WordPress is open-source, has a huge plugin library and supports user roles such as editor and author.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or CMS? Make the call (4)","q":"<p>A Barbadian poet wants her printed collection listed in bookshop and library catalogues.</p>","options":["Wix","IngramSpark","Kobo Writing Life"],"correct":1,"why":"IngramSpark distributes print-on-demand books through the network that supplies bookshops and libraries.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or CMS? Make the call (5)","q":"<p>A Trinidad author wants the biggest single e-book store and does not want to buy an ISBN for the Kindle edition.</p>","options":["Amazon KDP","Barnes &amp; Noble Press","Joomla"],"correct":0,"why":"KDP is the largest e-book store and assigns its own ASIN, so a Kindle e-book does not need a purchased ISBN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform or CMS? Make the call (6)","q":"<p>A Curaçao community group wants a multilingual site (Papiamentu, Dutch, English) and has a volunteer developer.</p>","options":["Draft2Digital","Joomla","Apple Books"],"correct":1,"why":"Joomla has strong multilingual features and suits a team with some technical skill.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflowable or fixed layout? (1)","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is converting a stack of client projects into e-books. Decide the best layout for each.</p><p>Should this be published as reflowable or fixed layout?</p><p><b>A 300-page novel about a family in San Fernando.</b></p>","options":["Fixed layout","Reflowable"],"correct":1,"why":"Text-heavy novels should reflow so readers can resize text on any screen.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflowable or fixed layout? (2)","q":"<p>Should this be published as reflowable or fixed layout?</p><p><b>A children's picture book where the text sits inside the illustrations.</b></p>","options":["Reflowable","Fixed layout"],"correct":1,"why":"Text placed on artwork must stay in position, so fixed layout is needed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflowable or fixed layout? (3)","q":"<p>Should this be published as reflowable or fixed layout?</p><p><b>A comic about a Carnival superhero, panel by panel.</b></p>","options":["Fixed layout","Reflowable"],"correct":0,"why":"Comic panels depend on exact placement: fixed layout.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflowable or fixed layout? (4)","q":"<p>Should this be published as reflowable or fixed layout?</p><p><b>A collection of short essays on Caribbean identity.</b></p>","options":["Reflowable","Fixed layout"],"correct":0,"why":"Plain text suits reflowable layout and screen readers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflowable or fixed layout? (5)","q":"<p>Should this be published as reflowable or fixed layout?</p><p><b>A recipe book for johnny cakes and coconut tarts with photos beside each step.</b></p>","options":["Reflowable","Fixed layout"],"correct":1,"why":"Designed spreads with photos beside steps keep their meaning only in fixed layout.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflowable or fixed layout? (6)","q":"<p>Should this be published as reflowable or fixed layout?</p><p><b>A poetry collection where readers with low vision need very large text.</b></p>","options":["Fixed layout","Reflowable"],"correct":1,"why":"Reflowable text can be enlarged without breaking the page, which supports accessibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflowable or fixed layout? (7)","q":"<p>Should this be published as reflowable or fixed layout?</p><p><b>A replica of a printed tourism magazine with full-page adverts.</b></p>","options":["Reflowable","Fixed layout"],"correct":1,"why":"A magazine replica must look like the print design, so it is fixed layout (or a PDF).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflowable or fixed layout? (8)","q":"<p>Should this be published as reflowable or fixed layout?</p><p><b>A guide to CAPE study skills that students read on phones.</b></p>","options":["Fixed layout","Reflowable"],"correct":1,"why":"Small phone screens need text that reflows.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is building a slide for a school library talk. Sort each statement about e-books.</p><p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books?</p><p><b>One e-reader can hold thousands of books for a long trip to Guyana.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Portability (one device, many books) is a key advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books? (2)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books?</p><p><b>The device battery dies during a power cut after a storm.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"E-books require a power source, a real problem during outages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books? (3)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books?</p><p><b>A history e-book embeds video of the 1848 Emancipation commemoration and audio interviews.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Digital convergence of text, video and audio is an advantage print cannot offer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books? (4)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books?</p><p><b>Reading on a bright screen for hours gives a student tired eyes.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Eye strain is one of the listed disadvantages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books? (5)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books?</p><p><b>A reader loses her whole library when she loses access to her account.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Some e-books are device or account specific, so access can be lost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books? (6)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books?</p><p><b>The author fixes an error and every reader gets the updated edition.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":1,"why":"E-books can be easily updated without reprinting costs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books? (7)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books?</p><p><b>A student with low vision makes the text larger and it reflows.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Rearranging the flow of text supports accessibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books? (8)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books?</p><p><b>A reader's account is hacked and her purchases are stolen.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"E-books and accounts can be hacked, a security disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books? (9)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books?</p><p><b>Some readers who love hard copies lose motivation to read on a screen.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"E-books are not suited to all types of readers.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the correct ISBN and metadata advice","q":"<p>Zara asked a new intern at the Island Pulse Media to write advice for a client publishing a book on Antiguan folk tales. Some of the intern's lines are wrong.</p><p>Select ALL the lines that give correct advice.</p>","options":["A vague title and no description will not affect sales.","A modern ISBN has 13 digits.","One ISBN covers every format of the same book forever.","The paperback and the EPUB edition each need their own ISBN.","Amazon gives Kindle e-books an ASIN, so a purchased ISBN is optional for that edition.","Metadata only matters for printed books in libraries.","Accessibility metadata tells readers whether the e-book works with screen readers.","Keywords such as \"Caribbean folklore\" and \"Antigua\" help readers find the book."],"correct":[1, 3, 4, 6, 7],"why":"Spot on. Each format needs its own ISBN, modern ISBNs have 13 digits, and accurate metadata (keywords, description, accessibility) drives discoverability."},{"t":"match","title":"Put the digital revolution in order","q":"<p>Jerome is building a \"then vs now\" timeline for an Island Pulse Media school workshop. Drag the milestones into the correct order, earliest first.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["ARPANET sends its first message between UCLA and Stanford Research Institute (1969)","Project Gutenberg begins publishing free e-books (1971)","ARPANET switches to TCP/IP (1983)","Tim Berners-Lee proposes the World Wide Web at CERN (1989)","The Mosaic graphical browser makes the Web popular (1993)","The term \"Web 2.0\" spreads as social, user-generated sites grow (2004)","Touchscreen smartphones and app stores bring the Web to pockets (2007 to 2008)"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web 1.0, 2.0 or 3.0? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is preparing a social media explainer for the Island Pulse Media. Sort each example into the correct stage of the Web.</p><p>Which stage of the Web does this example best represent?</p><p><b>A 1998 hotel website with one static page of text and a phone number.</b></p>","options":["Web 2.0","Web 1.0","Web 3.0"],"correct":1,"why":"Static, read-only pages with no user interaction are Web 1.0.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web 1.0, 2.0 or 3.0? (2)","q":"<p>Which stage of the Web does this example best represent?</p><p><b>Visitors post TikTok reviews of a Negril beach bar.</b></p>","options":["Web 2.0","Web 3.0","Web 1.0"],"correct":0,"why":"User-generated content and sharing are the heart of Web 2.0.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web 1.0, 2.0 or 3.0? (3)","q":"<p>Which stage of the Web does this example best represent?</p><p><b>A digital wallet lets a Belize artist sell and prove ownership of digital art on a blockchain.</b></p>","options":["Web 3.0","Web 1.0","Web 2.0"],"correct":0,"why":"Decentralised ownership on a blockchain is the Web3 idea of Web 3.0.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web 1.0, 2.0 or 3.0? (4)","q":"<p>Which stage of the Web does this example best represent?</p><p><b>Students edit a Wikipedia article about Emancipation Day.</b></p>","options":["Web 1.0","Web 3.0","Web 2.0"],"correct":2,"why":"Collaborative, read-write editing is Web 2.0.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web 1.0, 2.0 or 3.0? (5)","q":"<p>Which stage of the Web does this example best represent?</p><p><b>An early web directory where an editor lists links by hand.</b></p>","options":["Web 1.0","Web 3.0","Web 2.0"],"correct":0,"why":"Hand-built lists for reading only belong to Web 1.0.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web 1.0, 2.0 or 3.0? (6)","q":"<p>Which stage of the Web does this example best represent?</p><p><b>A search engine understands \"cheap ferry to Anguilla tomorrow\" and shows times and prices from tagged data.</b></p>","options":["Web 3.0","Web 1.0","Web 2.0"],"correct":0,"why":"Machines understanding meaning from structured data is the Semantic Web side of Web 3.0.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web 1.0, 2.0 or 3.0? (7)","q":"<p>Which stage of the Web does this example best represent?</p><p><b>A Trinidad blogger gets comments and shares on every post.</b></p>","options":["Web 2.0","Web 3.0","Web 1.0"],"correct":0,"why":"Two-way interaction through comments and shares is Web 2.0.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web 1.0, 2.0 or 3.0? (8)","q":"<p>Which stage of the Web does this example best represent?</p><p><b>An AI assistant personalises a news feed using linked data about the user's interests.</b></p>","options":["Web 1.0","Web 2.0","Web 3.0"],"correct":2,"why":"Intelligent, personalised and machine-readable services point to Web 3.0.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MMOGs and digital publishing","q":"<p>The Kingston studio Yaad Games releases a free online game in which thousands of players explore a shared Caribbean island at the same time. Every month the studio publishes a new story chapter, and players can build and share their own levels. Which statement BEST explains why this is an example of digital publishing?</p>","options":["Thousands of people play it, so it is a website.","Content is distributed online, published in ongoing updates and includes user-generated levels shared with other players.","It is free, so it must be a form of publishing.","It uses graphics, and anything with graphics is digital publishing."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["An MMOG is not the same as a website. Player numbers make it \"massively multiplayer\", but that does not explain the publishing link.","Correct. Digital distribution, continuous publishing of new content and user-generated content are the key links between MMOGs and digital publishing.","Price has nothing to do with whether something is published. Many paid products are publications too.","Graphics alone do not make a product a publication. The answer must link to how content is distributed and released."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Publishing a Caribbean e-book (step 1)","q":"<p>You are working with Zara at the Island Pulse Media. A retired teacher from Spanish Town has written an illustrated history of Emancipation Day in Jamaica and wants it read across the region and the diaspora. Make the best publishing decisions.</p><p>The book has about 80 pages of text and 20 photographs with captions. Most of her readers will use phones. Which format do you recommend first?</p>","options":["A scanned PDF of the printed pages.","A reflowable EPUB 3 with alt text for every photograph."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A scanned PDF has fixed pages that are hard to read on phones, and screen readers cannot read scanned text.","Good. Reflowable text suits phones, and alt text supports readers who use screen readers."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Publishing a Caribbean e-book (step 2)","q":"<p>She wants the book in Apple Books, Kobo, Google Play Books and Barnes &amp; Noble, but has little time. How should she distribute it?</p>","options":["Email the file to readers who ask for it.","Upload once through an aggregator such as Draft2Digital, and use KDP for Amazon.","Post the full book free on her Facebook page."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This gives up discoverability, sales tracking and protection. Readers cannot find the book in stores.","Efficient. The aggregator reaches several retailers with one upload, while KDP covers the largest store.","Social media is useful for promotion, but it is not a publishing platform with stores, metadata and sales."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Publishing a Caribbean e-book (step 3)","q":"<p>Before upload, she asks about ISBNs and metadata. What do you advise?</p>","options":["Use one ISBN for every format and leave the description blank so readers are curious.","Get separate ISBNs for the e-book and any print edition, and write a keyword-rich description with categories such as Caribbean history."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["One ISBN cannot cover several formats, and a blank description hides the book from search.","Excellent. One ISBN per format, and strong metadata makes the book discoverable."]}]},{"id":"assess_2ef","title":"Module 2 · Unit 2 quiz: Authoring digital content in a Caribbean context","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 2: Authoring digital content in a Caribbean context. 46 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the writing technique to the format","q":"<p>Malik and Keisha at the Island Pulse Media are training interns. Match each technique to the format it suits best.</p>","left":["Signposting phrases and show notes with timestamps","A two-column layout pairing narration with shots","Narration written to a sequence of still photographs","A searchable headline, subheadings and a closing question for comments","Concise, task-focused copy and descriptive link text on a Contact page","A short hook, few hashtags and alt text on an image post"],"right":["Audio slideshow","Website","Social media","Video script","Blog post","Podcast"],"correct":[5,3,0,4,1,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Podcast quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Jerome is quizzing interns at the Island Pulse Media before they start the <i>Caribbean Roots</i> podcast.</p><p>The word \"podcast\" blends which two words?</p>","options":["pod and cast list","iPod and broadcast","portable and broadcast","iPod and forecast"],"correct":1,"why":"Podcast blends \"iPod\" and \"broadcast\", from the era when episodes were loaded onto iPods.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Podcast quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>What lets podcast episodes download automatically?</p>","options":["A USB cable","RSS or Atom feeds","Email attachments","A QR code"],"correct":1,"why":"RSS (or Atom) feeds tell apps that a new episode exists so they can fetch it automatically.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Podcast quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>A video podcast is called a...</p>","options":["livestream","vodcast","webinar","vlog feed"],"correct":1,"why":"Video podcasts are also called vodcasts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Podcast quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>What software checks feeds for new episodes and downloads them?</p>","options":["An antivirus program","An aggregator (feed reader or podcast app)","A video editor","A word processor"],"correct":1,"why":"Aggregators, today usually podcast apps, check subscribed feeds for new episodes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Podcast quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>Weekly interviews with Grenadian cocoa farmers fit which use of a podcast?</p>","options":["Tutorial","Talk show with no guests","Interviews","Music show"],"correct":2,"why":"In-depth conversations with guests and experts are the interview use.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Podcast quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Where should a podcast preview the next episode?</p>","options":["Halfway through the main content","In the first line of the introduction","In the conclusion","Nowhere"],"correct":2,"why":"The conclusion summarises key points, gives a call to action and previews upcoming episodes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Podcast quick-fire (7)","q":"<p>What makes a podcast different from an ordinary audio file on a website?</p>","options":["It is always longer","It must be in video","It can be subscribed to and updates automatically","It cannot be streamed"],"correct":2,"why":"Automatic delivery through a subscription feed is the key distinction.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the video script tip","q":"<p>Zara is reviewing scripts for a Vincy Mas promo by the Island Pulse Media. Match each action to its tip.</p>","left":["Writing a brief: audience, keyword and YouTube as the host platform","Opening with \"In 30 seconds, here is everything you need for J'ouvert\"","Changing \"attendees are advised\" to \"you'll want to bring\"","Noting where the host stands and where captions fall","Ending with \"Watch our Carnival food guide next\"","Reading the script to Keisha and asking what sounded awkward","Rewording a line the host keeps tripping over on set"],"right":["Consider the visual elements","Write like you speak","Read it out loud and get feedback","Promote your other content","Accept changes during filming","Keep it short and catchy","Establish the purpose"],"correct":[6,5,1,0,3,2,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"On camera or audio slideshow? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha has a pile of production notes for two projects by Island Pulse Media: a filmed host video and an audio slideshow. Sort each note into the right project.</p><p>Which project does this note belong to?</p><p><b>Check the host's yellow top against the background at Fort James.</b></p>","options":["Audio slideshow","On-camera video"],"correct":1,"why":"Dressing and background balance matter when viewers see the presenter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"On camera or audio slideshow? (2)","q":"<p>Which project does this note belong to?</p><p><b>Arrange the 15 photos on a storyboard before writing narration.</b></p>","options":["Audio slideshow","On-camera video"],"correct":0,"why":"Visual sequencing of photos before writing is an audio slideshow technique.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"On camera or audio slideshow? (3)","q":"<p>Which project does this note belong to?</p><p><b>Clip the mic to the shirt so it does not block the face.</b></p>","options":["Audio slideshow","On-camera video"],"correct":1,"why":"Microphones must not block the speaker's face on camera.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"On camera or audio slideshow? (4)","q":"<p>Which project does this note belong to?</p><p><b>Slow the narration so each photo has time to register.</b></p>","options":["Audio slideshow","On-camera video"],"correct":0,"why":"Pacing control lets images and sound work together.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"On camera or audio slideshow? (5)","q":"<p>Which project does this note belong to?</p><p><b>Keep cue cards in order and slide each one away discreetly.</b></p>","options":["On-camera video","Audio slideshow"],"correct":0,"why":"Cue cards support an on-camera presenter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"On camera or audio slideshow? (6)","q":"<p>Which project does this note belong to?</p><p><b>Choose a calm voice tone for a hurricane remembrance story.</b></p>","options":["On-camera video","Audio slideshow"],"correct":1,"why":"Voice tone selection matches narration to the emotional tone of the images.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"On camera or audio slideshow? (7)","q":"<p>Which project does this note belong to?</p><p><b>Work on posture, eye contact and deportment.</b></p>","options":["On-camera video","Audio slideshow"],"correct":0,"why":"Deportment is visible only when the presenter is on screen.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"On camera or audio slideshow? (8)","q":"<p>Which project does this note belong to?</p><p><b>Record the voice first, then lay out the images in the editor.</b></p>","options":["Audio slideshow","On-camera video"],"correct":0,"why":"Voice-first is a flexible workflow for audio slideshows.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Order the creative planning process","q":"<p>The Grenada Chocolate Collective has asked Island Pulse Media for a podcast series. Put Zara's planning steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Read the brief and clarify the goal and target audience","Research listeners, competing podcasts and the history of Grenadian cocoa","Brainstorm and mind-map many possible episode ideas","Select the strongest concept against the brief and budget","Write the logline, episode outline and scripts","Pitch the concept to the client and revise from feedback","Begin recording and production"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which planning tool? (1)","q":"<p>Maya and Malik are planning several projects for the Island Pulse Media. Decide which tool each task needs.</p><p>Which planning tool fits this task best?</p><p><b>Sketching the eight shots of a 30-second ad for the Heineken Regatta, with camera angles.</b></p>","options":["Storyboard","Mock-up","Script","Wireframe"],"correct":0,"why":"Shot-by-shot visual planning with angles is a storyboard.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which planning tool? (2)","q":"<p>Which planning tool fits this task best?</p><p><b>Writing the host's words and interview questions for a podcast episode.</b></p>","options":["Wireframe","Mock-up","Script","Storyboard"],"correct":2,"why":"The words to be spoken are planned in a script.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which planning tool? (3)","q":"<p>Which planning tool fits this task best?</p><p><b>Grey boxes showing where the menu, hero image and booking button go on a hotel home page.</b></p>","options":["Mock-up","Script","Storyboard","Wireframe"],"correct":3,"why":"A low-fidelity layout of structure only is a wireframe.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which planning tool? (4)","q":"<p>Which planning tool fits this task best?</p><p><b>A full-colour design of the app home screen with the real logo and photos, for client approval.</b></p>","options":["Storyboard","Wireframe","Script","Mock-up"],"correct":3,"why":"A high-fidelity static design that shows the final look is a mock-up.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which planning tool? (5)","q":"<p>Which planning tool fits this task best?</p><p><b>Narration lines beside shot descriptions for a tourism video.</b></p>","options":["Wireframe","Storyboard","Script","Mock-up"],"correct":2,"why":"A two-column audio-visual script pairs narration with shots.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which planning tool? (6)","q":"<p>Which planning tool fits this task best?</p><p><b>Frames showing the order of photos in an audio slideshow about Emancipation Day.</b></p>","options":["Script","Mock-up","Storyboard","Wireframe"],"correct":2,"why":"Sequencing images visually is storyboarding.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which planning tool? (7)","q":"<p>Which planning tool fits this task best?</p><p><b>A quick black-and-white layout of a blog post page drawn in Balsamiq.</b></p>","options":["Mock-up","Wireframe","Storyboard","Script"],"correct":1,"why":"Balsamiq is designed for quick, low-fidelity wireframes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which planning tool? (8)","q":"<p>Which planning tool fits this task best?</p><p><b>Showing a client exactly how an Instagram post will look with brand colours and fonts.</b></p>","options":["Storyboard","Mock-up","Script","Wireframe"],"correct":1,"why":"Showing the final visual design is a mock-up.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS? (1)","q":"<p>Maya is helping several Trinidadian clients choose a web authoring tool. Sort each tool or description into its type.</p><p>Which type of web authoring tool is this?</p><p><b>Visual Studio Code: typing HTML and CSS for a San Fernando restaurant menu page.</b></p>","options":["WYSIWYG editor","Content management system (CMS)","Text editor"],"correct":2,"why":"VS Code is a text (code) editor: you write the HTML and CSS yourself.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS? (2)","q":"<p>Which type of web authoring tool is this?</p><p><b>Notepad++: fixing a broken link tag by hand.</b></p>","options":["WYSIWYG editor","Content management system (CMS)","Text editor"],"correct":2,"why":"Notepad++ is a basic text editor for editing code directly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS? (3)","q":"<p>Which type of web authoring tool is this?</p><p><b>Sublime Text: editing a JavaScript file for a photo gallery.</b></p>","options":["WYSIWYG editor","Content management system (CMS)","Text editor"],"correct":2,"why":"Sublime Text is a text editor for writing code line by line.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS? (4)","q":"<p>Which type of web authoring tool is this?</p><p><b>Adobe Dreamweaver design view: dragging an image into place and seeing the page update.</b></p>","options":["Content management system (CMS)","Text editor","WYSIWYG editor"],"correct":2,"why":"Dreamweaver offers a visual WYSIWYG view that writes the code for you.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS? (5)","q":"<p>Which type of web authoring tool is this?</p><p><b>WordPress Gutenberg: adding a heading block and an image block to a blog post.</b></p>","options":["Content management system (CMS)","Text editor","WYSIWYG editor"],"correct":2,"why":"Gutenberg is a visual block editor: what you see is what you get.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS? (6)","q":"<p>Which type of web authoring tool is this?</p><p><b>Joomla: a heritage foundation site with user accounts, menus and many articles.</b></p>","options":["WYSIWYG editor","Text editor","Content management system (CMS)"],"correct":2,"why":"Joomla is a CMS for managing a whole site's content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS? (7)","q":"<p>Which type of web authoring tool is this?</p><p><b>Drupal: a large, structured site for a Caribbean university with many editors.</b></p>","options":["WYSIWYG editor","Text editor","Content management system (CMS)"],"correct":2,"why":"Drupal is a powerful CMS suited to complex sites.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS? (8)","q":"<p>Which type of web authoring tool is this?</p><p><b>WordPress with a theme and plugins: a Bequia guesthouse posting weekly news.</b></p>","options":["WYSIWYG editor","Content management system (CMS)","Text editor"],"correct":1,"why":"WordPress as a whole is a CMS: themes, plugins and content management.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Authentic or stereotyped?","q":"<p>Interns at the Island Pulse Media pitched ideas for the Island Pulse Youth Tourism Challenge. Zara wants concepts that show an authentic Caribbean perspective.</p><p>Select ALL the ideas that show an authentic, specific Caribbean perspective.</p>","options":["A video showing only a beach, a cocktail and a reggae track downloaded from YouTube.","A blog series in which young chefs in Belize City rework their grandmothers' johnny cake recipes.","An ad where actors put on fake accents to sound \"Caribbean\".","An audio slideshow of salt-pond workers' descendants telling family stories, recorded with their consent.","A podcast in Papiamentu with English show notes, interviewing Carnival troupe leaders.","A TikTok series on how local fishermen in Grenville, Grenada, are adapting to sargassum seaweed.","An interactive map of Willemstad's heritage buildings with stories from a local museum (used with permission).","A stock photo collage of generic tropical islands with the caption \"Paradise\"."],"correct":[1, 3, 4, 5, 6],"why":"Excellent. Authentic content is specific, uses real voices with consent, reflects real issues and treats culture with respect."},{"t":"mc","title":"Copyright quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Jerome is building an app for Antigua Sailing Club and keeps asking copyright questions. Help him.</p><p>Jerome writes original code for the app tonight. When is it protected by copyright?</p>","options":["Only after he adds the © symbol","Only after he registers it with an IP office","Automatically, as soon as it is written and saved"],"correct":2,"why":"Copyright is automatic once an original work is fixed in a form, such as a saved file. Registration and © are optional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Copyright quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>He wants to name the app \"Sail Away\". Can copyright protect the name?</p>","options":["No, titles and short names are not protected by copyright; a trademark may protect them","Yes, all names are copyright","Yes, but only for 10 years"],"correct":0,"why":"Copyright does not protect titles or short phrases. Brand names are protected by trademark registration.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Copyright quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>Which international agreement means his app is protected in Canada and the UK without registering there?</p>","options":["The CARICOM Treaty","The Berne Convention","The Kyoto Protocol"],"correct":1,"why":"The Berne Convention gives automatic protection in all member countries.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Copyright quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>The Berne Convention minimum term of copyright for most works is...</p>","options":["until the author stops selling the work","life of the author plus 50 years","exactly 20 years from publication"],"correct":1,"why":"Berne sets a minimum of life plus 50 years. Many countries go further, such as life plus 70.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Copyright quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>He finds a 19th-century engraving of English Harbour whose artist died over 150 years ago. It is most likely...</p>","options":["still in copyright","a trademark","in the public domain"],"correct":2,"why":"With the creator long dead, copyright has expired and the work is in the public domain. A modern photograph of it could still carry its own copyright, so check the source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Copyright quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>The club wants a soca track in the app trailer. Who can usually license it?</p>","options":["Any YouTube user who uploaded it","Nobody: music cannot be licensed","The rights holder or a collecting society such as COTT"],"correct":2,"why":"Rights holders, often through collecting societies, license music. An uploader on YouTube rarely owns the rights.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Can I use it? Match the Creative Commons licence","q":"<p>Keisha is choosing media for client campaigns at the Island Pulse Media. Match each licence to what it allows.</p>","left":["CC BY","CC BY-SA","CC BY-NC","CC BY-ND","CC BY-NC-ND","CC0"],"right":["Edit and use commercially, but share your adaptation under the same licence","Edit and share with credit, but not for commercial purposes","Share unchanged with credit, even commercially, but no edits or remixes","Share unchanged with credit, non-commercially only: the most restrictive","Use, edit and use commercially, as long as you credit the creator","No conditions: the creator has placed the work in the public domain"],"correct":[4,0,1,2,3,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fair dealing or infringement?","q":"<p>A CAPE student in Port of Spain is producing a promotional video for the Maracas Bay food truck. She uses a full three-minute hit soca song as the soundtrack, credits the artist in the description and says it is \"fair use because I am a student\". Which statement BEST evaluates her position?</p>","options":["It is likely infringement: Trinidad and Tobago applies fair dealing for listed purposes, and using a whole song in a commercial promotion fits none of them, even with credit.","It is fair use because she credited the artist.","It is legal because the song is on YouTube, so it is public.","It is legal because students are exempt from copyright law."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Fair dealing covers purposes such as research, criticism, review and news reporting. A whole song in a promotional video for a business is a commercial use, and credit is not permission.","Attribution is good practice, but it does not give permission. Crediting the artist does not make the use legal.","Being publicly available online does not mean a work is in the public domain. The song is still protected by copyright.","There is no general student exemption. Educational purposes can help in some fair dealing cases, but this video promotes a business."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The Carnival campaign deadline (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a junior creator at Island Pulse Media. The Spicemas Youth Troupe in Grenada needs a 30-second promotional video and a blog post in three days. Make the right authoring decisions.</p><p>Zara asks how you will start. The client's brief says: \"Show young people that Carnival is about culture and creativity, not just partying.\" What do you do first?</p>","options":["Start filming random party footage at a Spicemas fete tonight and decide later.","Brainstorm concepts against the brief, then storyboard the 30-second video and outline the blog post."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Without a concept or storyboard, footage may not match the brief, which asks you to move away from a party image.","Good. Concept development and planning tools save time and keep the content on brief."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The Carnival campaign deadline (step 2)","q":"<p>Your concept shows troupe members designing costumes, practising steelpan and talking about their grandparents' Carnival memories. You need music. Malik suggests a famous international chart hit. What do you choose?</p>","options":["Ask the troupe's steelpan players to record an original piece, or license a local soca track from the rights holder.","Use the chart hit and credit the artist in the caption."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Original or licensed local music is legal and adds an authentic Caribbean voice.","Credit is not permission. The platform will likely mute or remove the video, and the client could face a claim."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The Carnival campaign deadline (step 3)","q":"<p>For the blog post you want to include a beautiful photograph of last year's parade that you found online. What do you do?</p>","options":["Check the licence. If it is CC BY, use it with full attribution; if not, contact the photographer for permission or use your own photo.","Use it because it is on Google Images, so it is free.","Copy it and write the caption as if you took it."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. You checked the licence, attributed properly and respected the creator's rights.","Search engines show images; they do not license them. Most images online are still protected by copyright.","That is both plagiarism (claiming it as yours) and copyright infringement (using it without permission)."]}]},{"id":"assess_2m0","title":"Module 2 · Unit 3 quiz: Prototypes using design principles","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 3: Prototypes using design principles. 59 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Low-fidelity or high-fidelity? (1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media is designing a website for the Mango Lane Café on Fort Street. Sort each item from the project folder.</p><p>Is this a low-fidelity or a high-fidelity prototype?</p><p><b>A pencil sketch of the home page on printer paper, with boxes marked \"photo\" and \"menu\".</b></p>","options":["High-fidelity","Low-fidelity"],"correct":1,"why":"Paper, pencil and placeholder boxes are the signs of a low-fidelity prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Low-fidelity or high-fidelity? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a low-fidelity or a high-fidelity prototype?</p><p><b>A Figma file where clicking \"Order now\" slides to a checkout screen with the café's real logo.</b></p>","options":["High-fidelity","Low-fidelity"],"correct":0,"why":"Real branding plus working interactions make this high-fidelity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Low-fidelity or high-fidelity? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a low-fidelity or a high-fidelity prototype?</p><p><b>Sticky notes on a whiteboard, each one a page, moved around during a team meeting.</b></p>","options":["Low-fidelity","High-fidelity"],"correct":0,"why":"Sticky notes are quick, cheap and rough, which is low-fidelity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Low-fidelity or high-fidelity? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a low-fidelity or a high-fidelity prototype?</p><p><b>A grey-box Balsamiq wireframe with hand-drawn style lines and no colour.</b></p>","options":["High-fidelity","Low-fidelity"],"correct":1,"why":"Balsamiq deliberately looks sketchy so people focus on structure, not style. That is low-fidelity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Low-fidelity or high-fidelity? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a low-fidelity or a high-fidelity prototype?</p><p><b>A clickable mobile prototype using the café's brand colours, photos of johnny cakes and the final fonts.</b></p>","options":["High-fidelity","Low-fidelity"],"correct":0,"why":"Final colours, images and fonts with clickable links are high-fidelity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Low-fidelity or high-fidelity? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a low-fidelity or a high-fidelity prototype?</p><p><b>Paper screens that Zara swaps by hand while a customer \"taps\" them with a finger.</b></p>","options":["Low-fidelity","High-fidelity"],"correct":0,"why":"This is paper prototyping. A person acts as the computer, which is still low-fidelity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Low-fidelity or high-fidelity? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a low-fidelity or a high-fidelity prototype?</p><p><b>A prototype with smart-animate transitions that the developer will inspect for exact spacing values.</b></p>","options":["High-fidelity","Low-fidelity"],"correct":0,"why":"Detailed spacing, transitions and developer handover belong to high-fidelity work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Low-fidelity or high-fidelity? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a low-fidelity or a high-fidelity prototype?</p><p><b>A black-marker layout drawn in a Paper 02 exam with labelled boxes.</b></p>","options":["High-fidelity","Low-fidelity"],"correct":1,"why":"Hand-drawn layouts with labels are low-fidelity, and they are exactly what Paper 02 often asks for.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype? (1)","q":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media has mixed up the files for the Holetown Market Online project. Sort each file using the definitions.</p><p>Is this a wireframe, a mockup or a prototype?</p><p><b>A greyscale layout labelled \"Low-Fidelity Wireframe\", with boxes for images and lines for text.</b></p>","options":["Prototype","Mockup","Wireframe"],"correct":2,"why":"Greyscale, placeholders and the label all mark a wireframe: the skeleton of the design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a wireframe, a mockup or a prototype?</p><p><b>A whiteboard sketch mapping where the search bar, product list and menu go.</b></p>","options":["Mockup","Prototype","Wireframe"],"correct":2,"why":"It shows only layout and structure, so it is a wireframe.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a wireframe, a mockup or a prototype?</p><p><b>A full-colour home page with the market's logo, fonts and product photos, but nothing can be clicked.</b></p>","options":["Prototype","Wireframe","Mockup"],"correct":2,"why":"High-fidelity but static: that is a mockup.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a wireframe, a mockup or a prototype?</p><p><b>Two versions of the home page, one in teal and one in coral, for the client to compare.</b></p>","options":["Prototype","Mockup","Wireframe"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiple variations of a key screen to choose a visual direction is the mockup \"strategic decision point\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a wireframe, a mockup or a prototype?</p><p><b>A Sketch file used to perfect button styles and spacing across every screen.</b></p>","options":["Mockup","Prototype","Wireframe"],"correct":0,"why":"Refining visual consistency is a reason to use mockups.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a wireframe, a mockup or a prototype?</p><p><b>A Figma file where tapping \"Sign up\" moves through account creation to first login.</b></p>","options":["Prototype","Mockup","Wireframe"],"correct":0,"why":"A clickable sign-up flow simulating real interactions is a prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a wireframe, a mockup or a prototype?</p><p><b>A clickable demo tested with five vendors to find confusing steps before coding.</b></p>","options":["Wireframe","Prototype","Mockup"],"correct":1,"why":"Validating user experience with real users is a purpose of prototypes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a wireframe, a mockup or a prototype?</p><p><b>An Axure RP file where a form shows different screens depending on the answer chosen.</b></p>","options":["Prototype","Wireframe","Mockup"],"correct":0,"why":"Conditional logic and dynamic content are advanced prototyping features.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of prototype? (1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media is working on several projects across the islands. Sort each prototype into one of the four types.</p><p>Which of the four types of prototype is this?</p><p><b>Jerome writes a few lines of code to see whether a new route-finding algorithm for the ANU Bus Pass app is fast enough on older phones.</b></p>","options":["Live-data","High-fidelity user","Low-fidelity user","Feasibility"],"correct":3,"why":"An engineer testing new technology and performance with just enough code is a feasibility prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of prototype? (2)","q":"<p>Which of the four types of prototype is this?</p><p><b>A rough test checking whether a photo-recognition feature can identify Caribbean reef fish quickly enough to be useful for Belize Reef Divers.</b></p>","options":["Live-data","Low-fidelity user","High-fidelity user","Feasibility"],"correct":3,"why":"It tests technical risk before any design work, which is feasibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of prototype? (3)","q":"<p>Which of the four types of prototype is this?</p><p><b>Linked grey boxes in Balsamiq that let testers tap from \"Menu\" to \"Order\" for a fish-fry stand at Oistins.</b></p>","options":["Low-fidelity user","Feasibility","High-fidelity user","Live-data"],"correct":0,"why":"An interactive wireframe that does not look real, used to test the workflow, is a low-fidelity user prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of prototype? (4)","q":"<p>Which of the four types of prototype is this?</p><p><b>Zara watches three students try to register for a Carnival event using clickable sketches, noting where they hesitate.</b></p>","options":["Low-fidelity user","High-fidelity user","Live-data","Feasibility"],"correct":0,"why":"Finding usability issues early in the workflow with rough screens is the job of a low-fidelity user prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of prototype? (5)","q":"<p>Which of the four types of prototype is this?</p><p><b>A polished Figma simulation in full brand colours that Keisha presents to the board of an Antigua hotel to win approval.</b></p>","options":["Feasibility","Low-fidelity user","Live-data","High-fidelity user"],"correct":3,"why":"Realistic-looking and used to communicate a proposed product to stakeholders: high-fidelity user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of prototype? (6)","q":"<p>Which of the four types of prototype is this?</p><p><b>A realistic, working simulation of a Curaçao art trail app, with real photos and smooth transitions, shown to investors.</b></p>","options":["Low-fidelity user","High-fidelity user","Feasibility","Live-data"],"correct":1,"why":"It looks real and works as a simulation, but it does not use live data. That makes it high-fidelity user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of prototype? (7)","q":"<p>Which of the four types of prototype is this?</p><p><b>Developers release a very limited version of a market-price app to 30 vendors in Castries, pulling real prices each morning.</b></p>","options":["Live-data","Feasibility","High-fidelity user","Low-fidelity user"],"correct":0,"why":"A very limited implementation with real data and live traffic is a live-data prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of prototype? (8)","q":"<p>Which of the four types of prototype is this?</p><p><b>A bare-bones booking page goes online for one weekend to see whether real customers actually book Montego Bay kayak tours through it.</b></p>","options":["Live-data","Low-fidelity user","High-fidelity user","Feasibility"],"correct":0,"why":"Real traffic proving that it works in the world is the mark of a live-data prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the planning tool","q":"<p>Malik and Maya are planning a website for the Morne Diablotin Hiking Club in Dominica. Match each planning tool to the job it does.</p>","left":["Concept map","Navigational chart","Site map","Hierarchy map","User pathway","Wireframe","Mock-up"],"right":["A static, full-colour image of the final page, not yet clickable","A grey-box layout of one page showing where the logo, menu and photos go","A table of contents of every page, for both users and search engines","A flowchart that organises how visitors will be guided from Home to Trails to a trail page and back","Traces the exact steps a visitor takes to complete one task, such as joining a guided hike","Brainstorming with a purpose: links ideas such as \"trails\", \"safety\" and \"membership\" with arrows and linking words","A tree-like diagram with Home at the top, breaking into categories and then sub-categories"],"correct":[5,3,2,6,4,1,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML sitemap or XML sitemap? (1)","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is finishing the website for the Seaglass &amp; Silk boutique on Frederick Street. Sort each item.</p><p>Does this describe an HTML sitemap or an XML sitemap?</p><p><b>A simple bulleted outline of every page, with links that use page titles as the anchor text.</b></p>","options":["HTML sitemap","XML sitemap"],"correct":0,"why":"A bulleted outline of linked page titles is exactly what an HTML sitemap is.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML sitemap or XML sitemap? (2)","q":"<p>Does this describe an HTML sitemap or an XML sitemap?</p><p><b>A file at /sitemap.xml full of url and loc tags.</b></p>","options":["HTML sitemap","XML sitemap"],"correct":1,"why":"A list of URLs marked up with tags is an XML sitemap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML sitemap or XML sitemap? (3)","q":"<p>Does this describe an HTML sitemap or an XML sitemap?</p><p><b>Built exclusively for search engine bots, to guide them to every page you want indexed.</b></p>","options":["XML sitemap","HTML sitemap"],"correct":0,"why":"XML sitemaps are for bots, not people.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML sitemap or XML sitemap? (4)","q":"<p>Does this describe an HTML sitemap or an XML sitemap?</p><p><b>A shopper who cannot find the Returns policy clicks \"Sitemap\" in the footer and finds it in the list.</b></p>","options":["XML sitemap","HTML sitemap"],"correct":1,"why":"Helping visitors navigate by following links is the job of an HTML sitemap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML sitemap or XML sitemap? (5)","q":"<p>Does this describe an HTML sitemap or an XML sitemap?</p><p><b>Includes a lastmod tag so Googlebot knows the Sale page changed yesterday.</b></p>","options":["XML sitemap","HTML sitemap"],"correct":0,"why":"Last-updated information in tags makes the site more readable for Googlebot: XML.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML sitemap or XML sitemap? (6)","q":"<p>Does this describe an HTML sitemap or an XML sitemap?</p><p><b>Maya submits it in Google Search Console so the new Carnival Collection pages are crawled.</b></p>","options":["HTML sitemap","XML sitemap"],"correct":1,"why":"Submitting to a search engine so pages are crawled and indexed is what an XML sitemap is for.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML sitemap or XML sitemap? (7)","q":"<p>Does this describe an HTML sitemap or an XML sitemap?</p><p><b>Designed so users can find all the information they are looking for on the website.</b></p>","options":["HTML sitemap","XML sitemap"],"correct":0,"why":"Guiding visitors to everything on the site is the purpose of an HTML sitemap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML sitemap or XML sitemap? (8)","q":"<p>Does this describe an HTML sitemap or an XML sitemap?</p><p><b>Like the sitemap page on Apple.com, styled to match the rest of the site.</b></p>","options":["HTML sitemap","XML sitemap"],"correct":0,"why":"The sitemap on Apple.com is a classic HTML sitemap: a readable page for visitors.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Which navigation type?","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has five website jobs on Zara's board. Match each type of website navigation to the job it suits best.</p>","left":["Horizontal navigation bar","Dropdown menu","Hamburger menu","Vertical sidebar menu","Footer menu"],"right":["A creative agency wants an eye-catching menu with long link labels and many top-level options","The Seaglass & Silk boutique on Market Street has five main pages and wants them side by side in the header","An ANU Visitor Guide has 40 pages grouped under Beaches, Food, Shopping and Events","Opening hours, Privacy policy, Careers and a WhatsApp link at the bottom of every page, expanding on the header links","On phones, the Lagoon Grill in English Harbour must hide its menu links behind a three-line icon to save space"],"correct":[1,2,4,0,3]},{"t":"match","title":"Put the user pathway in order","q":"<p>A visitor from Antigua wants to buy a ticket to an Emancipation Day concert on the Barbados Live Events website. Put the pathway in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Finds the site through a Google search for \"Emancipation Day concert Barbados\".","Lands on the Home page and sees the featured event banner.","Taps the banner to open the Event Details page (date, venue, line-up).","Taps \"Buy tickets\" and chooses the number of tickets.","Enters name and payment details on the Checkout page.","Sees the Confirmation page with an e-ticket and QR code."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design principle? (1)","q":"<p>Zara is reviewing student interns' layouts for the Portsmouth Seafood Festival. Identify the principle each comment is about.</p><p>\"Your headline, photo and button are all stacked in the exact centre. Try putting the chef's face on one of the grid intersections.\"</p>","options":["contrast","rule of thirds","spacing"],"correct":1,"why":"Placing the focal point on a grid intersection, instead of the centre, is the rule of thirds.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design principle? (2)","q":"<p>\"The left column has three big photos and the right is empty. The page feels as if it will tip over.\"</p>","options":["balance","alignment","hierarchy"],"correct":0,"why":"Uneven visual weight is a balance problem. Add weight to the right or redistribute the photos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design principle? (3)","q":"<p>\"Your captions start at slightly different points from the edges of the images.\"</p>","options":["visual flow","contrast","alignment"],"correct":2,"why":"Elements that do not share a common edge are misaligned. Snap them to a grid.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design principle? (4)","q":"<p>\"Pale yellow text on a white background is almost impossible to read in the sun.\"</p>","options":["composition","contrast","balance"],"correct":1,"why":"Readability depends on contrast between text and background, especially on phones outdoors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design principle? (5)","q":"<p>\"The ticket price is the same size as the small print. Make the most important information biggest.\"</p>","options":["balance","rule of thirds","hierarchy"],"correct":2,"why":"Visual hierarchy uses size, weight and position to show what matters most.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design principle? (6)","q":"<p>\"Everything is jammed together. Give each section some breathing room.\"</p>","options":["contrast","spacing","alignment"],"correct":1,"why":"White space (spacing) separates groups and makes a page easier to scan.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the layout problems","q":"<p>Jerome drafted a home page wireframe for the Holetown Market Crafts shop. Read his design notes.</p><p>Select every note that BREAKS a design principle.</p>","options":["There is no space between the product grid, the reviews and the footer.","All product cards snap to a 4-column grid with equal 24 px gaps.","The logo, headline, photo, prices and button are all centred and the same size.","Body text is light grey (#CCCCCC) on a white background.","The \"Shop now\" button is placed where the eye finishes its Z-pattern scan.","The headline is the largest text on the page.","The hero photo of a craft stall is placed so the vendor's face sits on the upper-right thirds intersection.","Captions are left-aligned on some cards, centred on others and right-aligned on the rest."],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 7],"why":"Well spotted. Same-size centred items break hierarchy, pale grey on white breaks contrast, no gaps break spacing, and mixed text alignment breaks alignment."},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose a cloud tool for each map (1)","q":"<p>This activity asks you to list cloud-based tools for a concept map, a navigational chart, a site map and a hierarchy map. Help the Island Pulse Media team pick the best tool for each job.</p><p>Keisha wants to build a concept map for a Carnival website, with linking words on every arrow, starting from a ready-made concept map template.</p>","options":["XML-Sitemaps.com generator","Octopus.do","Lucidchart"],"correct":2,"why":"Lucidchart has concept map templates and lets you type linking words on each line, as in the video in this unit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose a cloud tool for each map (2)","q":"<p>Jerome needs a free flowchart of the checkout navigation for the Lagoon Grill in Frigate Bay, on a school computer where he cannot create an account.</p>","options":["MindMeister","Slickplan","draw.io (diagrams.net)"],"correct":2,"why":"draw.io is free, has flowchart shapes and arrows, and can be used without signing up.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose a cloud tool for each map (3)","q":"<p>Maya wants to lay out every page of the Seaglass & Silk boutique as a visual site map of page blocks before any design starts.</p>","options":["Octopus.do","Coggle","CapCut"],"correct":0,"why":"Octopus.do is a visual sitemap builder made for planning pages as blocks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose a cloud tool for each map (4)","q":"<p>The boutique site is live and Maya now needs an XML sitemap to submit to Google Search Console.</p>","options":["Canva whiteboards","XML-Sitemaps.com generator","FigJam"],"correct":1,"why":"The XML-Sitemaps.com generator crawls a live site and produces the XML file for search engines.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose a cloud tool for each map (5)","q":"<p>The team is already designing in Figma and wants a shared whiteboard to drag sticky notes into a hierarchy map, Home at the top.</p>","options":["XML-Sitemaps.com generator","Audacity","FigJam"],"correct":2,"why":"FigJam is Figma's whiteboard, so the hierarchy map sits right beside the design files.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose a cloud tool for each map (6)","q":"<p>A CAPE group already makes its SBA posters in Canva and wants a free infinite board to brainstorm ideas with a purpose.</p>","options":["Canva whiteboards","Google Search Console","Slickplan"],"correct":0,"why":"Canva whiteboards suit brainstorming and concept maps, and the group already knows Canva.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a prototyping tool","q":"<p>A group of three CAPE students at Seagrape Bay Secondary School must produce a clickable mobile app prototype for their SBA. They work from different homes, want to edit the same file at the same time, and need reusable buttons that update everywhere. Which tool BEST meets all of these needs?</p>","options":["Figma","Paper and black markers","Balsamiq","CapCut"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Figma offers real-time multi-user editing in the cloud, components that update every instance, and prototype links for clickable testing.","Paper prototypes are great for early ideas but cannot be edited by three people in different homes at once.","Balsamiq is excellent for quick low-fidelity wireframes, but the group needs a detailed, clickable prototype with reusable components.","CapCut is a video editor. It is not designed for building clickable app screens with reusable components."]},{"t":"match","title":"Order the Figma workflow","q":"<p>Jerome is building a wireframe for the Dominica Trail Guide app. Put his Figma steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Create a new design file and name it clearly.","Add phone-sized frames for each screen and name them.","Block out grey boxes and text for the layout.","Apply auto layout to rows and set gap and padding.","Turn repeated rows and buttons into components.","Add prototype links between frames in the Prototype tab.","Present the prototype and click through to test it.","Share the link with testers and collect comments."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"mc","title":"When to use each design artifact (1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has several jobs this month. Choose the best artifact for each stage or need.</p><p>The Curaçao Art Trail team has just agreed the idea and needs to settle which pages exist and how they connect.</p>","options":["Prototypes","Wireframes","Mockups"],"correct":1,"why":"Early-stage concept validation and structural alignment is when to use wireframes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"When to use each design artifact (2)","q":"<p>A hotel client must approve the brand colours and fonts before the developer receives handoff specifications.</p>","options":["Wireframes","Prototypes","Mockups"],"correct":2,"why":"Visual design refinement, stakeholder approval and developer handoff specifications are mockup jobs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"When to use each design artifact (3)","q":"<p>An investor wants to tap through a ferry-booking app on a phone before funding it.</p>","options":["Prototypes","Mockups","Wireframes"],"correct":0,"why":"Investor demos and interactive testing are what prototypes are best for.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"When to use each design artifact (4)","q":"<p>The team wants to explore five different layout ideas in one afternoon.</p>","options":["Mockups","Wireframes","Prototypes"],"correct":1,"why":"Exploring multiple concepts rapidly is a wireframe strength: they are quick and cheap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"When to use each design artifact (5)","q":"<p>A small restaurant only wants a fresh look for its existing website. Following \"choose tools strategically\", the team should mainly produce...</p>","options":["Nothing until coding starts","A full interactive prototype","Mockups"],"correct":2,"why":"A simple refresh might only need mockups. A complex app benefits from full prototyping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"When to use each design artifact (6)","q":"<p>Which principle says you should add detail only after confirming you are solving the right problem?</p>","options":["Start simple, iterate fast","Choose tools strategically","Engage stakeholders deeply"],"correct":0,"why":"Start simple with low-fidelity wireframes and iterate fast before adding detail.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping for a real client (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a junior designer at Island Pulse Media. Belize Reef Divers company wants a booking website. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The owner sends a long list of ideas: dive packages, a blog, photo galleries, safety rules, staff bios, prices and a booking form. Zara asks you where to begin.</p>","options":["Open Figma straight away and design a beautiful full-colour home page.","Make a concept map of the ideas, then group them into a site map with clear levels."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Jumping to hi-fi before agreeing on structure wastes time when the content changes.","Good. You organise ideas first, then structure the pages, before any visual design."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping for a real client (step 2)","q":"<p>The site map is approved. Next you must show the owner the layout of the home and booking pages. What do you produce?</p>","options":["Quick low-fidelity wireframes on paper or in Balsamiq, then test them with two real customers.","Skip testing; the owner already likes the site map.","A final-colour mock-up of every page so the owner can pick colours first."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Yes. Cheap lo-fi testing catches layout problems early.","A site map is not a layout. Users may still get lost.","Colour decisions distract from structure. Fix layout and flow first."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping for a real client (step 3)","q":"<p>Testers could not find the booking button on the home page. You move to a high-fidelity Figma prototype. How do you fix the problem?</p>","options":["Give the \"Book a dive\" button strong contrast, place it where the Z-pattern ends, and add it to every package page as a component.","Make the button the same colour as the background so the design looks calm."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Contrast, visual flow and components solve the problem consistently.","Low contrast hides the main call to action. Calm is fine, invisible is not."]}]},{"id":"assess_2sc","title":"Module 2 · Unit 4 quiz: Content development for online publishing","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 4: Content development for online publishing. 46 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Useful, usable or purposeful? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is reviewing early notes for the Taste of Castries guide. Each note describes one quality of the content. Sort it into the test it helps the content pass.</p><p>Which quality does this feature mainly show?</p><p><b>Each vendor entry lists prices in US dollars and opening hours.</b></p>","options":["Usable","Purposeful","Useful"],"correct":2,"why":"Prices and hours answer the real questions a visitor has, so this makes the content useful.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Useful, usable or purposeful? (2)","q":"<p>Which quality does this feature mainly show?</p><p><b>The page loads in under three seconds on a phone with roaming data.</b></p>","options":["Usable","Useful","Purposeful"],"correct":0,"why":"Speed is about how easy the content is to use, so this is usability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Useful, usable or purposeful? (3)","q":"<p>Which quality does this feature mainly show?</p><p><b>Every entry ends with a \"Get directions\" button.</b></p>","options":["Usable","Purposeful","Useful"],"correct":1,"why":"A clear button moves the reader to act, so this is purposeful.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Useful, usable or purposeful? (4)","q":"<p>Which quality does this feature mainly show?</p><p><b>Walking times from the cruise pier are given for every stop.</b></p>","options":["Purposeful","Useful","Usable"],"correct":1,"why":"Visitors need to know they can get there and back in time. That is a real need answered.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Useful, usable or purposeful? (5)","q":"<p>Which quality does this feature mainly show?</p><p><b>Descriptive headings let readers jump straight to the vendor they want.</b></p>","options":["Usable","Purposeful","Useful"],"correct":0,"why":"Headings make content easy to scan and navigate, which is usability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Useful, usable or purposeful? (6)","q":"<p>Which quality does this feature mainly show?</p><p><b>A short line invites readers to share the trail with friends on the ship.</b></p>","options":["Useful","Purposeful","Usable"],"correct":1,"why":"It prompts an action that spreads the content, so it is purposeful.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Useful, usable or purposeful? (7)","q":"<p>Which quality does this feature mainly show?</p><p><b>The guide appears when someone searches \"street food Castries\".</b></p>","options":["Purposeful","Useful","Usable"],"correct":2,"why":"Being easy to find is part of usability. Content that cannot be found cannot be used.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Useful, usable or purposeful? (8)","q":"<p>Which quality does this feature mainly show?</p><p><b>A note explains which vendors take cards and which take cash only.</b></p>","options":["Purposeful","Usable","Useful"],"correct":2,"why":"Payment is a real need for visitors, so this detail is useful.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Order the seven-stage content cycle","q":"<p>Malik has the Taste of Castries task cards in a muddle. Put the seven stages in the correct order, starting with the first.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Plan: agree the Taste of Castries scope and goals with the vendors","Research: study cruise visitors and the keywords they search for","Create: write the guide, shoot the photos and the three clips","Edit and review: check facts, names and fit with the brief","Publish: put the guide live by 1 December","Promote: share the clips and flyers so visitors see the guide","Measure and maintain: track analytics and update prices"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the scope element","q":"<p>Zara is reading the Taste of Castries scope statement aloud. Match each scope element to the team's decision.</p>","left":["Topic","Purpose","Audience","Depth","Format","Channels","Constraints"],"right":["Six street-food vendors near the Castries cruise port","About 800 words, one short section per vendor","A blog guide plus three 30-second clips","Cruise visitors on phones, plus local residents","The agency blog, Instagram, TikTok and QR flyers","Bring cruise visitors and residents to the vendors","Live by 1 December, no paid stock photos, mobile-first"],"correct":[0,5,3,1,2,4,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Credible or questionable? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is checking sources before the Taste of Castries guide goes live. Sort each source.</p><p>Is this source credible enough to use, or questionable?</p><p><b>A recorded interview with a johnny cake vendor about her recipe and prices this season.</b></p>","options":["Credible","Questionable"],"correct":0,"why":"Primary research from the person who knows best is credible, as long as it is quoted accurately with permission.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Credible or questionable? (2)","q":"<p>Is this source credible enough to use, or questionable?</p><p><b>An anonymous TikTok claiming accra was \"first made in 2010\".</b></p>","options":["Credible","Questionable"],"correct":1,"why":"No authority, no evidence and it cannot be confirmed. Questionable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Credible or questionable? (3)","q":"<p>Is this source credible enough to use, or questionable?</p><p><b>The official St. Lucia tourism website listing this year's cruise season events.</b></p>","options":["Credible","Questionable"],"correct":0,"why":"Official, current and relevant. Still double-check dates close to publishing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Credible or questionable? (4)","q":"<p>Is this source credible enough to use, or questionable?</p><p><b>A 2014 blog post giving vendor opening hours.</b></p>","options":["Questionable","Credible"],"correct":0,"why":"It fails currency. Opening hours change; confirm directly with the vendor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Credible or questionable? (5)","q":"<p>Is this source credible enough to use, or questionable?</p><p><b>A national newspaper report quoting named food vendors.</b></p>","options":["Credible","Questionable"],"correct":0,"why":"An established news outlet with named sources has authority and accountability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Credible or questionable? (6)","q":"<p>Is this source credible enough to use, or questionable?</p><p><b>A chatbot answer giving a precise number of food trucks on the island, with no source.</b></p>","options":["Questionable","Credible"],"correct":0,"why":"Such tools can invent figures. Without a verifiable source, do not publish it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Credible or questionable? (7)","q":"<p>Is this source credible enough to use, or questionable?</p><p><b>A vendor's own flyer saying it has \"the best ribs on the island\".</b></p>","options":["Questionable","Credible"],"correct":0,"why":"The purpose is to sell, so it is biased. You may quote it as their claim, not as fact.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Credible or questionable? (8)","q":"<p>Is this source credible enough to use, or questionable?</p><p><b>A university research paper on Caribbean food heritage with references.</b></p>","options":["Credible","Questionable"],"correct":0,"why":"Academic, referenced work has strong authority and accuracy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tone of voice and target group (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is checking the Taste of Castries brief and some briefs for other clients. Choose the best answer each time.</p><p>Denise has five hours ashore and roaming data. Which feature matters MOST for her?</p>","options":["walking times from the pier and fast-loading pages","a long history of Castries","a gallery of 60 full-size photos"],"correct":0,"why":"The persona tells you her real constraints: time and data. The content must serve those first.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tone of voice and target group (2)","q":"<p>Which line is \"friendly, not childish\"?</p>","options":["\"Patrons are advised that johnny cakes are available.\"","\"You'll love the crispy johnny cakes at this stall.\"","\"OMG these are SOOO yummy!!!\""],"correct":1,"why":"It is warm and addresses the reader, without childish slang or cold formality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tone of voice and target group (3)","q":"<p>Which line is \"confident, not boastful\"?</p>","options":["\"We think it might be quite nice.\"","\"A Jeremie Street favourite with locals for over ten years.\"","\"The greatest food on Earth.\""],"correct":1,"why":"Confidence comes from specific, believable evidence, not empty superlatives or weak hedging.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tone of voice and target group (4)","q":"<p>A brief for a storm shelter update in St. Lucia. The best tone is...</p>","options":["calm, clear and authoritative","formal legal language","playful, with emojis"],"correct":0,"why":"In emergencies people need calm, clear, trustworthy instructions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tone of voice and target group (5)","q":"<p>\"Real St. Lucian street food is a short walk from your ship\" is the brief's...</p>","options":["deliverables checklist","key message","measurable objective"],"correct":1,"why":"The key message is the one sentence readers must remember.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tone of voice and target group (6)","q":"<p>\"150 QR code scans from the pier flyers in December\" is a...</p>","options":["persona","tone of voice","measurable objective"],"correct":2,"why":"It has a number and a time, so success can be measured.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the weak copy","q":"<p>Malik's first draft of the Taste of Castries guide has some strong lines and some weak ones. Keisha needs your help with the edit.</p><p>Select every line that is WEAK web copy and needs an edit.</p>","options":["See prices and opening hours for every vendor.","The vendors are open daily. Every day of the week, the vendors are open.","Our curated gastronomic proposition leverages authentic culinary touchpoints.","Try crispy johnny cakes with saltfish for under 8 US dollars.","Click here for more information.","Back on board by 4 p.m.? Start at stop 1 and work your way along Jeremie Street.","Six local street-food stops, all within a 10-minute walk of the cruise pier.","At this point in time, we would like to take the opportunity to inform each and every visitor that the vendors are open."],"correct":[1, 2, 4, 7],"why":"Well spotted. Filler phrases, vague \"click here\" links, jargon and needless repetition all slow scanners down. The strong lines are front-loaded, specific and direct."},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the Creative Commons conditions (1)","q":"<p>Maya is teaching an intern the four CC conditions. Each card describes a rule or a use. Sort it under the condition it describes.</p><p>Which CC condition does this describe?</p><p><b>You must credit the photographer by name.</b></p>","options":["SA","BY","NC","ND"],"correct":1,"why":"BY means attribution: always credit the creator.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the Creative Commons conditions (2)","q":"<p>Which CC condition does this describe?</p><p><b>You remix a CC song, so your remix must be shared under the same licence.</b></p>","options":["ND","NC","SA","BY"],"correct":2,"why":"SA (share-alike) means new work must carry the same licence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the Creative Commons conditions (3)","q":"<p>Which CC condition does this describe?</p><p><b>You cannot use the photo on a page that promotes paying vendors.</b></p>","options":["ND","BY","SA","NC"],"correct":3,"why":"NC (non-commercial) forbids money-making uses, including promoting businesses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the Creative Commons conditions (4)","q":"<p>Which CC condition does this describe?</p><p><b>You may share the image, but you cannot crop it or add text.</b></p>","options":["ND","BY","SA","NC"],"correct":0,"why":"ND (no derivatives) means no changes at all.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the Creative Commons conditions (5)","q":"<p>Which CC condition does this describe?</p><p><b>The credit line follows TASL: title, author, source, licence.</b></p>","options":["SA","BY","NC","ND"],"correct":1,"why":"TASL is how you meet the BY (attribution) condition properly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the Creative Commons conditions (6)","q":"<p>Which CC condition does this describe?</p><p><b>A student uses the photo free in a school project, but a hotel cannot use it in an advert.</b></p>","options":["SA","BY","ND","NC"],"correct":3,"why":"Non-commercial use is allowed; commercial use is not. That is NC.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the Creative Commons conditions (7)","q":"<p>Which CC condition does this describe?</p><p><b>You translate a CC article into Papiamentu and must release it under the same CC licence.</b></p>","options":["ND","BY","SA","NC"],"correct":2,"why":"A translation is a new work; SA requires the same licence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the Creative Commons conditions (8)","q":"<p>Which CC condition does this describe?</p><p><b>You must use the video clip exactly as it is, with no edits.</b></p>","options":["SA","NC","BY","ND"],"correct":3,"why":"ND means you may not change the work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Maya's photo decision (step 1)","q":"<p>You are Maya, responsible for images on the Taste of Castries guide. The deadline is close and the brief says \"no paid stock photos\". Make the best decisions.</p><p>You find a stunning photo of Vigie Beach on Flickr. The licence label says <b>CC BY-NC 2.0</b>. What do you do first?</p>","options":["Use it with a credit line; credit is all CC asks for.","Read what each condition means for this project before deciding."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["BY asks for credit, but this licence also has NC. You must meet every condition.","Good. Check the licence terms against your use before you publish anything."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Maya's photo decision (step 2)","q":"<p>BY means credit the creator. NC means non-commercial. Is the Taste of Castries guide a commercial use?</p>","options":["No. It is a free blog post, so it is non-commercial.","Yes. The vendors paid for it and it promotes their businesses, so it is commercial."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Free to read does not mean non-commercial. The purpose is to earn money for the vendors.","Correct. Promoting paying businesses is commercial use, even on a blog."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Maya's photo decision (step 3)","q":"<p>So the CC BY-NC photo is ruled out, and paid stock is not allowed. What now?</p>","options":["Take a similar photo from a Google image search; it is online, so it is free.","Shoot your own photos of the vendors and their food."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Online does not mean free. Most images are proprietary by default.","Yes. Your own photos solve the licence problem and show the real vendors."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Maya's photo decision (step 4)","q":"<p>Your photos show vendors and some customers whose faces are clear. The images will promote businesses. What do you do before publishing?</p>","options":["Publish them; the photos were taken in a public street.","Get signed releases from the vendors and anyone recognisable, and log them with the photos."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["For commercial promotion, the professional standard is written permission from recognisable people.","Excellent. Releases give written permission to use people's images commercially."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right licence","q":"<p>The Antigua Sailing Week Fan Club runs a commercial website that sells T-shirts. Keisha wants to use a photo from Wikimedia Commons on the shop page and crop it into a banner. Which licence on the photo would allow this use, as long as she credits the photographer?</p>","options":["CC BY-ND 4.0","CC BY-NC 4.0","CC BY 4.0","All rights reserved"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["ND means no derivatives. Cropping the photo into a banner is a change, which ND does not allow.","NC means non-commercial. A website that sells T-shirts is a commercial use, so this licence does not allow it.","Correct. CC BY allows any use, including commercial use and changes such as cropping, provided the creator is credited.","All rights reserved means she would need direct permission or a paid licence from the owner."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the SEO element to its rule","q":"<p>Jerome is checking the Taste of Castries page before launch. Match each SEO element to its limit or rule.</p>","left":["Title tag","Meta description","URL slug","H1","Alt text","Internal links","Long-tail keyword","Keyword stuffing"],"right":["Only one per page: the main heading","Never: repeating a phrase unnaturally, which search engines penalise","A short description of an image for screen readers and crawlers","Links to related pages on the same site, so crawlers find them","50 to 60 characters, keyword near the front","150 to 160 characters that persuade people to click","\"where to eat near cruise port St Lucia\"","Short, lower case, words joined by hyphens"],"correct":[4,5,7,0,2,3,6,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format and size choices (1)","q":"<p>Maya is preparing assets for the Taste of Castries page. Choose the best option each time.</p><p>A colour photo of a plate of saltfish and johnny cakes.</p>","options":["BMP","WebP or JPEG","SVG"],"correct":1,"why":"Photos with many colours compress best as WebP or JPEG.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format and size choices (2)","q":"<p>The Taste of Castries logo, which must stay sharp at any size.</p>","options":["SVG","GIF","JPEG"],"correct":0,"why":"SVG is vector, so logos stay crisp at every size and the files are tiny.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format and size choices (3)","q":"<p>A trail map graphic with text labels and flat colours.</p>","options":["JPEG at 40% quality","PNG","MP4"],"correct":1,"why":"PNG keeps text and sharp edges clean. Low-quality JPEG would blur the labels.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format and size choices (4)","q":"<p>For most images on the page, aim to keep each file...</p>","options":["at 300 ppi","around 5 MB","under 200 KB"],"correct":2,"why":"Under 200 KB keeps pages fast on mobile data. Ppi is a print setting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format and size choices (5)","q":"<p>The best file name for a photo of bakes at the Castries Market is...</p>","options":["bakes-castries-market.webp","Bakes Photo FINAL (2).webp","IMG_7781.JPG"],"correct":0,"why":"Lower case, hyphens and descriptive words help both SEO and organisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format and size choices (6)","q":"<p>A 6000 px wide camera photo will be shown in an 800 px column. You should...</p>","options":["resize it to display size first","save it as PNG","upload it as it is"],"correct":0,"why":"Resize to display size. The extra pixels are invisible but cost data and time.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Keisha's final review","q":"<p>It is the day before launch. Keisha is running the review checklist on the Taste of Castries page. Here are her notes.</p><p>Select every note that shows a problem that must be fixed before publishing.</p>","options":["All six vendor photos shot by the team; signed releases on file.","Body text 16 px; contrast ratio 7:1.","Hero photo: 3.4 MB PNG straight from the camera.","Only one H1 on the page; H2s for each vendor.","Alt text on the bakes photo: \"image1\".","One vendor is spelled \"Miss Lyn's\" in one section and \"Ms. Lynn's\" in another.","Meta description: 212 characters long.","The last line says \"Click here\".","Title tag: \"Taste of Castries: Street Food Near Castries Cruise Port\" (56 characters)."],"correct":[2, 4, 5, 6, 7],"why":"Well reviewed. The meta description is over 160 characters, the hero photo is the wrong format and far too large, \"image1\" is useless alt text, the vendor name is inconsistent, and \"Click here\" is vague link text."}]},{"id":"assess_2xn","title":"Module 2 · Unit 5 quiz: Tools and techniques for digital content development","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 5: Tools and techniques for digital content development. 33 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the capture device (1)","q":"<p>Malik and Keisha are planning shoots for clients across the region. Choose the best option each time.</p><p>A 40-minute Instagram Live interview with a Trinidad soca artist. Which set-up?</p>","options":["handheld phone, no stand","tablet held by a friend","phone on a tripod with a lavalier mic"],"correct":2,"why":"Long recordings need steady, hands-free framing and clear voice. A tripod and clip-on mic solve both.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the capture device (2)","q":"<p>Close-up product photos of handmade jewellery for an online shop in St. John's, with a blurred background.</p>","options":["drone","mirrorless camera with a wide-aperture lens","webcam"],"correct":1,"why":"A wide aperture gives shallow depth of field, blurring the background so the product stands out.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the capture device (3)","q":"<p>A smooth walking tour of Redcliffe Quay filmed while moving.</p>","options":["tablet held at arm's length","phone on a gimbal","fixed webcam"],"correct":1,"why":"A gimbal stabilises movement so walking shots do not shake.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the capture device (4)","q":"<p>The client wants a quick TikTok from a Dickenson Bay beach fete tonight.</p>","options":["mobile phone shooting vertical 9:16","DSLR on a heavy tripod","mounted CCTV camera"],"correct":0,"why":"Phones are fast, light and already set up for vertical short video and instant upload.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the capture device (5)","q":"<p>Recording a podcast voice track at home. The biggest quality gain comes from...</p>","options":["a USB or lavalier microphone in a quiet room","a 4K camera","a brighter light"],"correct":0,"why":"For audio-only content, the microphone and the room matter most.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the technique to the feeling","q":"<p>Malik is shooting Antigua Sailing Week and Zara is shooting dishes at Chez Corail in English Harbour. Match each technique to the feeling or effect it creates.</p>","left":["Low angle","High angle","Eye level","Shallow focus at f/1.8","Deep focus at f/11","Golden hour light","Rule of thirds"],"right":["A small dinghy in choppy water looks vulnerable","The plate is sharp and the dining room melts into bokeh","Soft, warm light on the sails just before sunset","The winning crew looks powerful as they lift the trophy","The whole fleet off English Harbour stays sharp in an establishing shot","The chef's interview feels honest and relatable","The skipper sits on the left grid line, with space on the right for text"],"correct":[3,0,5,1,4,2,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Shooting the Heineken Regatta (step 1)","q":"<p>You are assisting Malik from the Island Pulse Media at Antigua Sailing Week in English Harbour. The brief: three hero photos for a website gallery and Instagram. Make the best camera decisions.</p><p>Shot 1 is a portrait of a young local skipper on the dock. Behind her are crowds, ropes and parked cars. Which setting do you choose?</p>","options":["Shallow focus: about f/1.8, step closer and zoom in a little so the background turns to bokeh.","Deep focus at f/11 so every rope and car is sharp."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Right. A wide aperture, a closer subject and a longer lens isolate her from the cluttered dock.","Deep focus keeps the clutter sharp, so it competes with the skipper."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Shooting the Heineken Regatta (step 2)","q":"<p>Shot 2 is an establishing shot of the whole fleet crossing the bay from Shirley Heights above English Harbour. What do you choose?</p>","options":["f/1.8 so one boat is sharp and the rest are blurred.","Deep focus around f/11 so the boats and the coastline are all sharp.","Portrait mode on the phone."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["An establishing shot must show the whole scene clearly; shallow focus hides it.","Yes. A narrow aperture keeps more of a landscape or establishing shot in focus.","Portrait mode blurs backgrounds, which is the opposite of what a wide fleet shot needs."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Shooting the Heineken Regatta (step 3)","q":"<p>Shot 3 celebrates the winning crew. It is 12 noon with harsh, overhead sun. The prize-giving is at 5.30 p.m. What do you do?</p>","options":["Wait for the prize-giving in the golden hour and shoot from a low angle as they lift the trophy.","Shoot now from a high angle, looking down at the crew."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Golden hour gives soft light and a low angle makes the crew look powerful.","Midday sun is harsh, and a high angle makes winners look small and vulnerable."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome is listing the software on the Island Pulse Media intern laptops. Sort each tool.</p><p>Which category does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>GIMP</b></p>","options":["Industry-standard","Cloud-based","Open-source"],"correct":2,"why":"GIMP is free, open-source raster image software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (2)","q":"<p>Which category does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Adobe Photoshop</b></p>","options":["Industry-standard","Cloud-based","Open-source"],"correct":0,"why":"Photoshop is the proprietary industry standard for photo editing, sold by subscription.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (3)","q":"<p>Which category does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Canva</b></p>","options":["Industry-standard","Open-source","Cloud-based"],"correct":2,"why":"Canva runs in the browser and stores designs online.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (4)","q":"<p>Which category does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Audacity</b></p>","options":["Open-source","Industry-standard","Cloud-based"],"correct":0,"why":"Audacity is a free, open-source audio editor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (5)","q":"<p>Which category does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>WeVideo</b></p>","options":["Cloud-based","Open-source","Industry-standard"],"correct":0,"why":"WeVideo is a browser-based video editor with cloud storage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (6)","q":"<p>Which category does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Kdenlive</b></p>","options":["Cloud-based","Industry-standard","Open-source"],"correct":2,"why":"Kdenlive is a free, open-source non-linear video editor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (7)","q":"<p>Which category does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>DaVinci Resolve</b></p>","options":["Industry-standard","Open-source","Cloud-based"],"correct":0,"why":"DaVinci Resolve is a professional editing and colour grading suite used in film and TV studios.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (8)","q":"<p>Which category does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>CapCut</b></p>","options":["Cloud-based","Industry-standard","Open-source"],"correct":0,"why":"CapCut is a cloud-connected editor popular for short vertical video.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (9)","q":"<p>Which category does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>OBS</b></p>","options":["Industry-standard","Open-source","Cloud-based"],"correct":1,"why":"OBS Studio is free and open-source software for screen recording and livestreaming.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (10)","q":"<p>Which category does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>Final Cut Pro</b></p>","options":["Industry-standard","Cloud-based","Open-source"],"correct":0,"why":"Final Cut Pro is Apple's professional, paid video editor.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Change the background in GIMP","q":"<p>Keisha needs a product photo of a bottle of pepper sauce for the Antigua Pepper Co. web shop, placed on a clean white background. Put her GIMP steps in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Open the photo and duplicate the layer, hiding the original.","Add an alpha channel to the duplicate layer.","Select the bottle with the Paths or Foreground Select tool.","Invert the selection and feather it by 1 to 2 px.","Delete the background or hide it with a layer mask.","Add a white layer underneath and check the edges at 200% zoom.","Save the working .xcf file, then export as WebP for the web shop."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fixing a noisy recording","q":"<p>Malik recorded an interview with a Dominica cocoa farmer for Caribbean Roots podcast. There is a steady air-conditioner hum throughout. Following good practice, he recorded ten seconds of room tone at the start. What should he do FIRST in Audacity?</p>","options":["Apply Noise Reduction at the maximum setting to the whole track.","Select the ten seconds of room tone and use Noise Reduction to Get Noise Profile.","Add intro music on a new track to cover the hum.","Normalize the track to make it louder."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Without a noise profile, Audacity does not know what to remove, and maximum settings make voices sound robotic.","Correct. Audacity needs a sample of the noise alone before it can remove that noise from the whole track.","Music would only hide the hum during the intro. The interview itself would still be noisy.","Normalising raises the hum along with the voice. Remove the noise first."]},{"t":"match","title":"Produce a podcast episode","q":"<p>Keisha is producing episode 1 of the <i>Island Eats</i> podcast about street food from the fish fry in Oistins to doubles in Trinidad. Put her workflow in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Plan the scope: audience, format, length and schedule.","Write the script and guest questions.","Record in a quiet room with a test of levels and room tone.","Edit in Audacity: trim, reduce noise and balance tracks.","Add licensed intro and outro music with fades.","Export as a named MP3 with metadata.","Upload to Spotify for Creators with cover art and show notes.","Share the RSS-distributed episode and promote it on social media."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"match","title":"Build an audio slideshow, storyboard first","q":"<p>Keisha is producing an Emancipation Day audio slideshow for the Palmetto Point Secondary Heritage Club using the storyboard-first workflow. Put her steps in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Select and crop the photos, with permission and credits.","Sequence the photos on a numbered storyboard.","Write one or two lines of narration under each frame.","Record the narration in a calm, respectful voice tone.","Edit the voice in Audacity and export a WAV.","Place images on V1 and the voice on A1 in storyboard order.","Adjust each image's duration so the pace matches the voice.","Add captions and export an MP4."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"match","title":"Order the video workflow","q":"<p>Malik is editing the Regatta promo for Island Pulse Media. Put the video workflow in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Ingest: copy and back up the clips, then import them.","Rough cut: place the best takes in story order.","Fine cut: trim each clip tightly and add J-cuts and L-cuts.","Audio: balance the voice, duck the music, add fades.","Colour: correct white balance and exposure so clips match.","Graphics: add captions, titles and the call to action.","Export: MP4, H.264, AAC at 1080 x 1920."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the export settings (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is publishing the Chez Corail promo and the Regatta highlights for the Island Pulse Media. Choose the correct setting each time.</p><p>Which video container is the web standard?</p>","options":["AVI","AUP3","MP4","XCF"],"correct":2,"why":"MP4 plays in almost every browser and app. XCF and AUP3 are project files, not videos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the export settings (2)","q":"<p>Which video codec should go inside it?</p>","options":["AAC","WAV","H.264","PNG"],"correct":2,"why":"H.264 compresses the picture efficiently and is supported almost everywhere. AAC is for audio.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the export settings (3)","q":"<p>Which audio codec pairs with H.264 in an MP4?</p>","options":["H.264","AAC","SVG"],"correct":1,"why":"AAC is the standard compressed audio codec in web MP4 files.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the export settings (4)","q":"<p>The Regatta promo is for Instagram Reels. Which size?</p>","options":["1080 x 1920","1920 x 1080","1080 x 1080"],"correct":0,"why":"Reels are vertical 9:16, which is 1080 x 1920.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the export settings (5)","q":"<p>The Chez Corail video is for the restaurant website banner. Which size?</p>","options":["1920 x 1080","1080 x 1920","400 x 400"],"correct":0,"why":"A website banner is landscape 16:9, which is 1920 x 1080.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the export settings (6)","q":"<p>The video has fast fireworks cuts. What is the safe limit?</p>","options":["Ten flashes per second","Any number if there are captions","No more than three flashes per second"],"correct":2,"why":"More than three flashes per second can trigger seizures; captions do not change that.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Finishing a web video for a client (step 1)","q":"<p>You are working with Malik on a 30-second web promo for Curaçao Jazz Nights. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The edit is finished. The client wants it on their website and on Instagram Reels. How do you export?</p>","options":["One uncompressed master file of several gigabytes for both.","Two MP4 (H.264/AAC) versions: 1920 by 1080 for the website and 1080 by 1920 for Reels."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Uncompressed files are far too large for web pages and many platforms reject them.","Good. MP4 H.264 plays almost everywhere, and each platform gets the right aspect ratio."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Finishing a web video for a client (step 2)","q":"<p>The client says, \"Most people watch Reels with the sound off.\" What do you add?</p>","options":["Unchecked auto-captions.","Proofread captions: burned-in open captions for Reels and an SRT file for the website player.","Nothing. The music is the best part."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Auto-captions often get names and places wrong. Always proofread.","Yes. Captions serve muted viewers and Deaf or hard-of-hearing viewers.","Muted viewers miss the key message, and the video is less accessible."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Finishing a web video for a client (step 3)","q":"<p>The video will sit on the page <i>Home &gt; Events &gt; Jazz Nights</i>. Visitors arrive from Google straight onto that page. How do you help them find other events?</p>","options":["Add a breadcrumb trail at the top so they can click back to Events or Home.","Hope they use the browser back button."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Breadcrumbs show location and give one-click routes back up the hierarchy.","The back button takes them to Google, not to your other events."]}]},{"id":"assess_35v","title":"Module 2 · Unit 6 quiz: Website building with HTML and CSS","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 6: Website building with HTML and CSS. 61 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (1)","q":"<p>Maya is planning a page for the Sea Breeze Guesthouse in Gros Islet. Using the house analogy, decide which language handles each job: the frame and walls, the paint and landscaping, or the electricity.</p><p>Which language does this job?</p><p><b>A main heading that says \"Sea Breeze Guesthouse\"</b></p>","options":["JavaScript (behaviour)","HTML (structure)","CSS (presentation)"],"correct":1,"why":"The heading itself is content and structure: part of the frame and walls.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (2)","q":"<p>Which language does this job?</p><p><b>Making that heading purple and centred</b></p>","options":["JavaScript (behaviour)","HTML (structure)","CSS (presentation)"],"correct":2,"why":"Colour and alignment are presentation: the paint.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (3)","q":"<p>Which language does this job?</p><p><b>Placing a photo of the beach on the page</b></p>","options":["CSS (presentation)","JavaScript (behaviour)","HTML (structure)"],"correct":2,"why":"The img element adds the image to the structure. CSS may size it later, but HTML puts it there.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (4)","q":"<p>Which language does this job?</p><p><b>A thank-you message that pops up when a guest presses \"Book\"</b></p>","options":["JavaScript (behaviour)","CSS (presentation)","HTML (structure)"],"correct":0,"why":"Responding to a click is behaviour: the electricity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (5)","q":"<p>Which language does this job?</p><p><b>Using the same font on every page of the site</b></p>","options":["HTML (structure)","JavaScript (behaviour)","CSS (presentation)"],"correct":2,"why":"Fonts are presentation, best set once in an external style sheet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (6)","q":"<p>Which language does this job?</p><p><b>A list of three menu links: Home, Rooms, Contact</b></p>","options":["HTML (structure)","JavaScript (behaviour)","CSS (presentation)"],"correct":0,"why":"Lists and links are structure, built with ul, li and a.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (7)","q":"<p>Which language does this job?</p><p><b>A slideshow that changes photo every five seconds</b></p>","options":["CSS (presentation)","JavaScript (behaviour)","HTML (structure)"],"correct":1,"why":"Changing the page over time is behaviour, handled by JavaScript.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (8)","q":"<p>Which language does this job?</p><p><b>Stacking the room boxes in one column on a phone</b></p>","options":["CSS (presentation)","JavaScript (behaviour)","HTML (structure)"],"correct":0,"why":"Layout is presentation. CSS with a media query rearranges the same HTML.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (9)","q":"<p>Which language does this job?</p><p><b>A form that warns \"Check your phone number\" as soon as a guest types one digit too few</b></p>","options":["CSS (presentation)","HTML (structure)","JavaScript (behaviour)"],"correct":2,"why":"Checking input and responding instantly is an action and response: JavaScript.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Build the page skeleton","q":"<p>Maya at Island Pulse Media (a Castries agency) is starting the home page for the Cocoa Grove guest house. Put the lines of her HTML skeleton in the correct order, top to bottom.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;","&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;","&lt;head&gt;","&lt;title&gt;Cocoa Grove | Home&lt;/title&gt;","&lt;/head&gt;","&lt;body&gt;","&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to Cocoa Grove&lt;/h1&gt;","&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the nesting error","q":"<p>Malik (video and animation, Island Pulse Media) typed some lines for the Sea Breeze Guesthouse page while Maya was at a client meeting. Use the nested-boxes rule: the last box opened gets its lid first, and empty tags have no lid at all.</p><p>Select every line with a nesting or closing-tag error.</p>","options":["&lt;h1&gt;Sea Breeze &lt;strong&gt;Guesthouse&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;","&lt;p&gt;Book &lt;em&gt;today&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/em&gt;","&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Our rooms&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;footer&gt;&lt;p&gt;Grand Anse, Grenada&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;","&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"contact.html\"&gt;Contact&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;img src=\"pool.jpg\" alt=\"Pool at the guesthouse\"&gt;&lt;/img&gt;","&lt;p&gt;Call us&lt;br&gt;+1 473 555 0123&lt;/p&gt;","&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Sea view&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Free Wi-Fi&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;","&lt;p&gt;Stay in &lt;strong&gt;Grand Anse&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;"],"correct":[0, 1, 4, 5],"why":"Sharp eyes. Three lines had crossed lids (em, strong and a closed after their outer element) and one gave the empty img tag a closing tag it must not have. Tidy nesting means closing tags in reverse order."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the tag to its job","q":"<p>Maya is marking up the Pommecythere Grove home page. Match each HTML element to the content it should hold.</p>","left":["&lt;nav&gt;","&lt;footer&gt;","&lt;h1&gt;","&lt;ol&gt;","&lt;table&gt;","&lt;section&gt;","&lt;main&gt;","&lt;title&gt;"],"right":["The links Home, Rooms, Gallery and Contact","Phone number, address in San Fernando and copyright line","The unique content of this page, used once","Room types and their nightly rates","Text shown on the browser tab and in search results","A themed block headed \"Guest reviews\"","The single main title: Pommecythere Grove Guest House","Numbered driving directions from Piarco Airport"],"correct":[0,1,6,7,3,5,2,4]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the link and image errors","q":"<p>Malik tried to help Maya by adding links and images to the Tamarind Grove pages, but some lines contain errors. All the files are in the same folder except images, which are in an <b>images</b> subfolder.</p><p>Select every line that contains an error.</p>","options":["&lt;a href=\"gallery.html\"&gt;Gallery","&lt;a href=\"https://www.youtube.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"&gt;Our video&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;img src=\"images/beach.jpg\"&gt;","&lt;a href=\"www.google.com/maps\" target=\"_blank\"&gt;Map&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;a href=\"#contact\"&gt;Contact us&lt;/a&gt; ... &lt;section id=\"contact\"&gt;","&lt;img src=\"images/pool.jpg\" alt=\"Pool surrounded by palm trees\"&gt;","&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\"&gt;Our rooms&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;img src=\"Images/Garden Room.JPG\" alt=\"Garden room\"&gt;"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 7],"why":"Sharp eyes. You found the missing closing tag, the missing alt text, the external link without https:// or rel=\"noopener\", and the file path with capitals and a space."},{"t":"mc","title":"External, internal or inline? (1)","q":"<p>Maya is tidying the CSS for the Sea Breeze Guesthouse site before Zara reviews it. Decide where each piece of CSS lives, or which method each description fits.</p><p>Is this external, internal or inline CSS?</p><p><b>&lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\"&gt; in the head</b></p>","options":["Inline","External","Internal"],"correct":1,"why":"The link tag connects the page to a separate .css file: external CSS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"External, internal or inline? (2)","q":"<p>Is this external, internal or inline CSS?</p><p><b>A &lt;style&gt; element inside the head of one page</b></p>","options":["Inline","External","Internal"],"correct":2,"why":"CSS written in a style element in the head is internal (embedded) CSS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"External, internal or inline? (3)","q":"<p>Is this external, internal or inline CSS?</p><p><b>&lt;p style=\"color:purple;\"&gt;</b></p>","options":["Inline","External","Internal"],"correct":0,"why":"A style attribute on one element is inline CSS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"External, internal or inline? (4)","q":"<p>Is this external, internal or inline CSS?</p><p><b>One file called style.css shared by all five pages</b></p>","options":["Internal","External","Inline"],"correct":1,"why":"A shared separate file is the whole point of external CSS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"External, internal or inline? (5)","q":"<p>Is this external, internal or inline CSS?</p><p><b>Best for consistent styling across the whole site</b></p>","options":["Internal","External","Inline"],"correct":1,"why":"Change one file and every linked page updates.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"External, internal or inline? (6)","q":"<p>Is this external, internal or inline CSS?</p><p><b>Useful for one unique page, such as a one-off Carnival countdown page</b></p>","options":["Internal","Inline","External"],"correct":0,"why":"Internal CSS suits a single page that needs its own styles.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"External, internal or inline? (7)","q":"<p>Is this external, internal or inline CSS?</p><p><b>Changes just that one element</b></p>","options":["External","Internal","Inline"],"correct":2,"why":"Inline CSS affects only the element it is written on.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"External, internal or inline? (8)","q":"<p>Is this external, internal or inline CSS?</p><p><b>&lt;h1 style=\"text-align:center;\"&gt;Sea Breeze&lt;/h1&gt;</b></p>","options":["Internal","External","Inline"],"correct":2,"why":"Another style attribute inside an opening tag: inline.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"External, internal or inline? (9)","q":"<p>Is this external, internal or inline CSS?</p><p><b>&lt;style&gt; body { font-family: Arial; } &lt;/style&gt;</b></p>","options":["Inline","External","Internal"],"correct":2,"why":"Rules inside a style element are internal CSS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which rule wins the cascade?","q":"<p>The Sea Breeze Guesthouse page links style.css, which contains p { color: blue; }. Further down the head, after the link, an internal style element contains p { color: green; }. One paragraph is written as &lt;p style=\"color:red;\"&gt;. No rule uses !important. What colours appear?</p>","options":["That paragraph is red; every other paragraph is blue, because external beats internal.","Every paragraph is blue, because the external style sheet always wins.","Every paragraph is green, because internal CSS beats inline CSS.","That paragraph is red; every other paragraph is green."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Half right. Neither external nor internal wins by type; with equal specificity, the rule that comes last wins, and the internal rule comes after the link.","External CSS does not automatically win. It is the easiest to maintain, not the strongest in the cascade.","Inline styles beat internal and external rules for normal declarations, so the styled paragraph stays red.","Correct. Inline beats the others for normal declarations, so the styled paragraph is red. The external and internal rules have equal priority and specificity (both select p), so the last one in the code, the internal green rule, wins."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element, class or id selector? (1)","q":"<p>Maya is writing the style sheet for Pineapple Grove. Decide which kind of selector each rule uses.</p><p>Which type of selector is this?</p><p><b>p { line-height: 1.6; }</b></p>","options":["Id selector","Element selector","Class selector"],"correct":1,"why":"No dot or hash: it targets every p element.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element, class or id selector? (2)","q":"<p>Which type of selector is this?</p><p><b>.room-card { border: 1px solid #ccc; }</b></p>","options":["Class selector","Element selector","Id selector"],"correct":0,"why":"The dot shows a class. Every room card can share it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element, class or id selector? (3)","q":"<p>Which type of selector is this?</p><p><b>#booking-form { background: #fff7e6; }</b></p>","options":["Element selector","Id selector","Class selector"],"correct":1,"why":"The hash shows an id. There is only one booking form on the page.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element, class or id selector? (4)","q":"<p>Which type of selector is this?</p><p><b>footer { text-align: center; }</b></p>","options":["Class selector","Element selector","Id selector"],"correct":1,"why":"footer is an HTML element name, so this is an element selector.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element, class or id selector? (5)","q":"<p>Which type of selector is this?</p><p><b>.btn { padding: 12px 24px; }</b></p>","options":["Element selector","Id selector","Class selector"],"correct":2,"why":"A reusable button style is a class: many buttons can use it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element, class or id selector? (6)","q":"<p>Which type of selector is this?</p><p><b>#hero { height: 60vh; }</b></p>","options":["Id selector","Element selector","Class selector"],"correct":0,"why":"The single large banner at the top of the home page is unique, so an id fits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element, class or id selector? (7)","q":"<p>Which type of selector is this?</p><p><b>h2 { color: #7a1f3d; }</b></p>","options":["Id selector","Element selector","Class selector"],"correct":1,"why":"All h2 headings are styled; this is an element selector.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element, class or id selector? (8)","q":"<p>Which type of selector is this?</p><p><b>.highlight { color: #b8860b; }</b></p>","options":["Element selector","Class selector","Id selector"],"correct":1,"why":"A class you can add to any text you want to highlight.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CSS quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Keisha (marketing, Island Pulse Media) keeps asking Maya for small changes to the Cocoa Grove site. Choose the CSS that does each job.</p><p>Keisha wants more space between the text of a room card and its border, inside the card.</p>","options":["padding","border-spacing","margin"],"correct":0,"why":"Padding is the space inside the border. Margin would push the whole card away from its neighbours.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CSS quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>She wants the cards spaced further apart from each other.</p>","options":["line-height","margin","padding"],"correct":1,"why":"Margin sits outside the border and separates one box from the next.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CSS quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>The brand red is rgb(122, 31, 61). Which hex code is the same colour?</p>","options":["#3d1f7a","#122313","#7a1f3d"],"correct":2,"why":"122 is 7a, 31 is 1f and 61 is 3d in hexadecimal, in the order red, green, blue.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CSS quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>Body text feels cramped. Which property adds space between lines?</p>","options":["line-height","letter-spacing","font-weight"],"correct":0,"why":"line-height controls the vertical space between lines; 1.5 to 1.7 suits body text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CSS quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>Which declaration gives the booking button rounded corners?</p>","options":["border-style: round;","border-radius: 8px;","corner: 8px;"],"correct":1,"why":"border-radius rounds the corners of the box. The other two are not valid CSS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CSS quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Which rule makes every link inside the nav white?</p>","options":["#nav.a { colour: #ffffff; }","nav a { color: #ffffff; }","a nav { color: #ffffff; }"],"correct":1,"why":"nav a is a descendant selector: links inside nav. Note that CSS uses the American spelling color.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flexbox or grid?","q":"<p>Maya needs the Guavaberry Grove photo gallery to show twelve photos in neat rows of four columns on a laptop, with equal gaps between rows and columns. Which CSS is the MOST suitable starting point?</p>","options":[".gallery img { margin: 16px; padding: 16px; }","&lt;table&gt; with four &lt;td&gt; cells in each row",".gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 16px; }",".gallery { display: flex; justify-content: center; }"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Spacing alone does not create columns. The images would simply flow one after another.","Tables are for data, not layout. They are hard to make responsive and confuse screen readers.","Correct. The gallery is two-dimensional (rows and columns), so grid is the right tool, and repeat(4, 1fr) creates four equal columns.","Flexbox works in one direction. Without wrapping and set widths, the twelve photos would squeeze into one long row."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the multimedia mistakes","q":"<p>Jerome (mobile developer at Island Pulse Media) drafted the media section for the Mango Grove \"Experience\" page. Media files are in a <b>media</b> folder.</p><p>Select every line that is wrong or poor practice.</p>","options":["&lt;audio controls&gt;&lt;source src=\"media/steelpan.mp3\" type=\"audio/mpeg\"&gt;&lt;/audio&gt;","&lt;img src=\"media/sunset-6000px.jpg\" alt=\"Sunset over Frigate Bay lagoon\"&gt;","&lt;iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/abc123\"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;","&lt;video controls&gt;&lt;source src=\"media/tour.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"&gt;&lt;/video&gt;","&lt;audio src=\"media/welcome.mp3\"&gt;&lt;/audio&gt;","&lt;track src=\"media/tour-en.vtt\" kind=\"captions\" srclang=\"en\" label=\"English\"&gt;","&lt;iframe src=\"https://www.google.com/maps/embed?pb=...\" title=\"Map to the guest house\" loading=\"lazy\"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;","&lt;video autoplay src=\"media/tour.mov\"&gt;&lt;/video&gt;"],"correct":[1, 2, 4, 7],"why":"Well spotted. You caught autoplay without controls in a poorly supported MOV file, an iframe with no title, an oversized 6000 px image, and audio with no controls so it cannot be played."},{"t":"mc","title":"Print or screen? (1)","q":"<p>Pommecythere Grove needs both a printed brochure for the Piarco Airport tourism desk and a website. Sort each setting by where it belongs.</p><p>Does this belong to print production or screen (web) production?</p><p><b>Images set to 300 dpi</b></p>","options":["Screen","Print"],"correct":1,"why":"300 dpi is the usual standard for sharp printed material.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print or screen? (2)","q":"<p>Does this belong to print production or screen (web) production?</p><p><b>CMYK colour model</b></p>","options":["Print","Screen"],"correct":0,"why":"Printers mix cyan, magenta, yellow and black inks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print or screen? (3)","q":"<p>Does this belong to print production or screen (web) production?</p><p><b>Hero photo exported at 1920 px wide and compressed to under 300 KB</b></p>","options":["Screen","Print"],"correct":0,"why":"Web images are measured in pixels and kept small so pages load quickly on mobile data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print or screen? (4)","q":"<p>Does this belong to print production or screen (web) production?</p><p><b>Colours written as hex codes such as #7a1f3d</b></p>","options":["Print","Screen"],"correct":1,"why":"Hex codes describe RGB light colours used on screens and in CSS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print or screen? (5)","q":"<p>Does this belong to print production or screen (web) production?</p><p><b>Font sizes in rem so text scales when the user zooms</b></p>","options":["Screen","Print"],"correct":0,"why":"rem is a CSS unit that respects the user's browser text settings.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print or screen? (6)","q":"<p>Does this belong to print production or screen (web) production?</p><p><b>A 3 mm bleed added around the page edge</b></p>","options":["Screen","Print"],"correct":1,"why":"Bleed stops white edges after a printed page is trimmed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print or screen? (7)","q":"<p>Does this belong to print production or screen (web) production?</p><p><b>Media queries for phones and tablets</b></p>","options":["Screen","Print"],"correct":0,"why":"Media queries adapt a web layout to different screen sizes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print or screen? (8)","q":"<p>Does this belong to print production or screen (web) production?</p><p><b>Saved as a high-quality PDF for the print shop</b></p>","options":["Print","Screen"],"correct":0,"why":"Print shops usually want a print-ready PDF.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the responsive tools","q":"<p>Keisha is checking her Carnival schedule page against the responsive toolkit. Match each tool or term to what it does.</p>","left":["Viewport meta tag","Media query","Breakpoint","display: flex","flex-direction: column","max-width: 100%","Mobile-first","F12 device toolbar"],"right":["Previews the page at phone, tablet and desktop widths","Lets an image shrink to fit its container without overflowing","Stops a phone shrinking the page to a tiny desktop-sized view","Writing the phone layout first, then adding rules for wider screens","Applies CSS only when a condition, such as min-width:768px, is true","The screen width where the layout changes, such as 768 px","Turns a parent box into a flexible row or column of child boxes","Stacks the three events one above another on a phone"],"correct":[2,4,5,6,7,1,3,0]},{"t":"match","title":"From brief to live multi-page site","q":"<p>Put Maya's workflow for the Guavaberry Grove site in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Meet the owner and agree the purpose, audience and pages in a brief","Draw a site map showing Home, Rooms, Gallery, Experience and Contact","Sketch grey-box wireframes for each page layout","Create a colour mock-up in Figma and get the owner's approval","Set up folders (css, images, media) and code index.html with the shared header, nav and footer","Copy the template to the other pages, add content and link the external style sheet","Test every link, image and media file on a phone and a laptop, and check accessibility","Upload the site to a web host and share the link"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"mc","title":"Maya builds the Guavaberry Grove site (step 1)","q":"<p>Guavaberry Grove is a six-room guest house in Sea Cows Bay, Tortola, in the British Virgin Islands. The owner, Mrs. Richardson, wants a website that brings in direct bookings instead of relying only on travel sites. Help Maya make the right decisions.</p><p>Mrs. Richardson is eager and says: \"Just start coding today. I want it to look like a luxury resort site I saw.\" What should Maya do first?</p>","options":["Agree a short brief (audience, purpose, pages), then draw a site map and wireframes for approval.","Start coding straight away and copy the luxury site's design and photos."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Planning first prevents costly rewrites and keeps the site focused on direct bookings.","Copying another site's design and photos risks copyright infringement, and skipping planning leads to rework."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Maya builds the Nutmeg Grove site (step 2)","q":"<p>The wireframes are approved. Maya now sets up the code. How should she handle the styling for five pages?</p>","options":["A data table to position the header, menu and content.","One external style sheet (css/style.css) linked from every page, with semantic header, nav, main and footer.","Inline style attributes on each element of each page."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Tables are for data. Table layouts are not responsive and confuse screen readers.","Correct. One external file keeps all pages consistent and easy to update.","Inline CSS must be repeated everywhere, so one change means editing hundreds of lines."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Maya builds the Guavaberry Grove site (step 3)","q":"<p>Testing shows that on a phone, the three room cards are squashed side by side and the 5 MB hero photo takes a long time to load on mobile data. What does Maya fix?</p>","options":["Add a mobile-first media query (one column on phones, three from 768 px), set img max-width: 100%, and export the hero at 1920 px compressed to about 250 KB.","Tell visitors in the footer to use a laptop for the best experience."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. That fixes both responsiveness and page speed for mobile visitors.","Most Caribbean visitors and tourists browse on phones. Asking them to switch devices loses bookings."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Maya builds the Sorrel Grove site (step 4)","q":"<p>Keisha wants a pale yellow booking button with white text, a YouTube tour that opens in a new tab, and a Google Map. What does Maya do?</p>","options":["Use the deep sorrel red with white text for strong contrast, embed the YouTube tour and map with titled iframes, and give any new-tab external link rel=\"noopener\".","Use the pale yellow button because it matches the brand, and leave out iframe titles to keep the code short."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong choices for accessibility, usability and security.","White on pale yellow fails WCAG contrast, and untitled iframes are unclear to screen-reader users."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accessible or a barrier? (1)","q":"<p>Zara is reviewing Cocoa Grove and two other Caribbean sites against WCAG basics. Sort each feature.</p><p>Does this feature support accessibility and usability, or create a barrier?</p><p><b>Every photo has meaningful alt text</b></p>","options":["Creates a barrier","Supports accessibility"],"correct":1,"why":"Alt text makes images perceivable for screen-reader users.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accessible or a barrier? (2)","q":"<p>Does this feature support accessibility and usability, or create a barrier?</p><p><b>Light grey text on a white background</b></p>","options":["Creates a barrier","Supports accessibility"],"correct":0,"why":"Low contrast fails WCAG and is hard to read in bright Caribbean sunlight.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accessible or a barrier? (3)","q":"<p>Does this feature support accessibility and usability, or create a barrier?</p><p><b>The video tour has captions</b></p>","options":["Supports accessibility","Creates a barrier"],"correct":0,"why":"Captions help deaf visitors and anyone watching with the sound off.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accessible or a barrier? (4)","q":"<p>Does this feature support accessibility and usability, or create a barrier?</p><p><b>Links are shown only by a slightly different colour, with no underline or hover change</b></p>","options":["Creates a barrier","Supports accessibility"],"correct":0,"why":"Users, especially those with colour blindness, may not see that the text is a link.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accessible or a barrier? (5)","q":"<p>Does this feature support accessibility and usability, or create a barrier?</p><p><b>The booking form can be completed using only the keyboard</b></p>","options":["Creates a barrier","Supports accessibility"],"correct":1,"why":"Keyboard access makes the form operable for people who cannot use a mouse.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accessible or a barrier? (6)","q":"<p>Does this feature support accessibility and usability, or create a barrier?</p><p><b>Music autoplays loudly when the page opens</b></p>","options":["Supports accessibility","Creates a barrier"],"correct":1,"why":"Autoplay audio interferes with screen readers and startles users.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accessible or a barrier? (7)","q":"<p>Does this feature support accessibility and usability, or create a barrier?</p><p><b>Headings follow h1, h2, h3 in order</b></p>","options":["Creates a barrier","Supports accessibility"],"correct":1,"why":"A logical heading structure lets screen-reader users navigate the page.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accessible or a barrier? (8)","q":"<p>Does this feature support accessibility and usability, or create a barrier?</p><p><b>Text is set in px and cannot grow when the user zooms</b></p>","options":["Creates a barrier","Supports accessibility"],"correct":0,"why":"Users who need larger text are blocked. rem units and zoom-friendly layouts help.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_38z","title":"Module 2 · Unit 7 quiz: Website infrastructure, data handling and security","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 7: Website infrastructure, data handling and security. 22 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"What happens when you type a URL?","q":"<p>A visitor at the Beef Island airport types guavaberrygrove.vg into her phone. Put the steps in the order they happen.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The visitor types guavaberrygrove.vg into the browser and presses Go","The browser asks a DNS resolver for the IP address of that domain","DNS servers look up the domain's records and return the IP address of the web host","The browser connects to the server at that IP address and sets up a secure HTTPS connection","The server sends back index.html, the CSS file and the images","The browser reads the HTML and CSS and displays the home page"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Infrastructure terms","q":"<p>Maya at Island Pulse Media (a regional agency) is explaining website infrastructure to the owner of the Guavaberry Grove guest house. Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Domain name","DNS","Registrar","ccTLD","Web host","A record","MX record"],"right":["A two-letter country ending such as .jm or .tt","A company that rents out domain names, usually yearly","A company whose servers store the site files and send them to visitors","The system that translates domain names into IP addresses","A DNS entry that points a domain to a server's IP address","The human-friendly address, such as guavaberrygrove.vg","A DNS entry that tells the Internet where to deliver email for the domain"],"correct":[5,3,1,0,2,4,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right hosting (1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has five clients across the Caribbean. Choose the most suitable hosting for each.</p><p>A CAPE student wants to publish her hand-coded HTML and CSS e-portfolio for free with HTTPS.</p>","options":["Dedicated server","VPS","Netlify or GitHub Pages"],"correct":2,"why":"A static site needs no server-side code, and free static hosts provide HTTPS at no cost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right hosting (2)","q":"<p>The Grenada Spice Tours needs a small WordPress site and a few email addresses on a tight budget.</p>","options":["GitHub Pages","Cloud cluster","Shared hosting"],"correct":2,"why":"Shared hosting is cheap, managed by the host and usually includes email and one-click WordPress. GitHub Pages cannot run WordPress.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right hosting (3)","q":"<p>The Port of Spain Carnival Tix site gets a huge traffic spike for two weeks each year.</p>","options":["Cloud hosting","Shared hosting","A laptop at the office"],"correct":0,"why":"Cloud hosting can scale up resources during the spike and scale down afterwards.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right hosting (4)","q":"<p>A growing online craft shop in Kingston needs custom software and more guaranteed resources, with a technician to manage it.</p>","options":["Shared hosting","Free static host","VPS"],"correct":2,"why":"A VPS gives guaranteed resources and control to install software, at a moderate cost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right hosting (5)","q":"<p>Which host type would NOT be able to process a booking form and save it to a database without extra services?</p>","options":["A free static host","A VPS","Shared hosting with PHP and MySQL"],"correct":0,"why":"Static hosts serve files only. They need a third-party form service or other add-ons to collect data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Static or dynamic?","q":"<p>Guavaberry Grove wants guests to check room availability online, submit a booking request, and let the owner update prices herself each season without editing code. Which solution BEST meets all three needs?</p>","options":["The hand-coded HTML site on GitHub Pages with prices typed into a table","A CMS such as WordPress on shared hosting, with a booking plugin that stores requests in a database","Inline CSS on every page to highlight the prices","A PDF price list emailed to guests who ask"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A static site cannot check availability or save bookings, and the owner would need to edit HTML to change prices.","Correct. A CMS provides a back end and database for availability and bookings, plus a dashboard so the owner can edit prices without coding.","CSS only changes appearance. It cannot store data or process bookings.","This is not a website feature and gives no online availability or booking."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the data-handling problems","q":"<p>Keisha (marketing, Island Pulse Media) drafted a newsletter sign-up form and its settings for the Kralendijk Dive Shack. Some lines break privacy or security principles.</p><p>Select every line that is a privacy or security problem.</p>","options":["A link to the privacy policy sits next to the Submit button.","The form page loads over http:// with no SSL certificate.","Every staff member, including the summer intern, has an administrator account.","Old sign-ups are deleted after two years of no activity.","The form also asks for date of birth and passport number \"for our records\".","The marketing consent box is already ticked when the page loads.","Subscriber passwords are stored in the database as plain text.","The form collects name and email address only."],"correct":[1, 2, 4, 5, 6],"why":"Well spotted. You applied data minimisation, active consent, HTTPS, password hashing and least privilege."},{"t":"match","title":"Threat and defence","q":"<p>Zara is preparing a security checklist for Island Pulse Media's clients. Match each threat to the defence that best reduces it.</p>","left":["A fake \"electricity bill overdue\" email asking staff to log in on a lookalike page","Attackers trying thousands of common passwords on the admin login","A three-year-old WordPress plugin with a known security hole","Ransomware encrypts all the site files","Booking details read by someone on the same café Wi-Fi","A former employee still logging in to the dashboard"],"right":["Staff training to check sender and URL, plus 2FA on accounts","Long unique passwords, login limits and 2FA","HTTPS with a valid TLS certificate","Remove old accounts and apply least privilege","Update or remove plugins and keep the CMS current","Recent tested backups stored off-site"],"correct":[0,1,4,5,2,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Guavaberry Grove goes live (step 1)","q":"<p>Maya has finished the Guavaberry Grove site. The owner, Mrs. Richardson, now wants it online with a booking enquiry form, and later a WordPress blog. Make the infrastructure and security decisions.</p><p>Mrs. Richardson asks which domain to register. Most guests come from the USA, the Netherlands and the wider Caribbean, and she wants a clear local identity. What does Maya recommend?</p>","options":["Let Maya register the domain in her own personal name to save time.","Register guavaberrygrove.vg (and guavaberrygrove.com if available) in the business's name, with auto-renew and WHOIS privacy turned on."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["The business must own its domain. If the domain is in a contractor's name, the owner can lose control of it.","Good. The .sx shows local identity, owning the .com protects the brand, and auto-renew in the owner's name prevents losing the domain."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Guavaberry Grove goes live (step 2)","q":"<p>The site needs a booking enquiry form and a blog the owner can update herself. Which hosting does Maya choose?</p>","options":["Reputable shared hosting with free SSL, automatic backups and WordPress support.","A free static host only, with the form posting to nowhere."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. It is affordable, supports a CMS and database, and includes key security features.","Static hosting cannot run WordPress, and a form with no back end or form service loses every enquiry."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Guavaberry Grove goes live (step 3)","q":"<p>Setting up WordPress, Maya must choose the admin login. The receptionist will also need access to read enquiries. What does Maya do?</p>","options":["A unique username (not \"admin\"), a long passphrase stored in a password manager, 2FA, and an Editor-level account for the receptionist.","Username \"admin\", password \"guavaberry1\", shared by everyone so nobody gets locked out."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Strong authentication plus least privilege.","A common username, a guessable password and a shared account are an open door to attackers."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Guavaberry Grove goes live (step 4)","q":"<p>Six months later, WordPress shows updates for three plugins, and one plugin has not been used since launch. A storm warning is issued for the island. What does Maya advise?</p>","options":["Back up the site, update WordPress and the plugins, delete the unused plugin, and confirm an off-site backup copy exists.","Ignore the updates because the site is working fine."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong. Updates close known holes, removing unused plugins shrinks the attack surface, and off-site backups protect against storm damage.","Outdated plugins are one of the most common ways small sites are hacked."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Builder or hand-coded? (1)","q":"<p>After building Guavaberry Grove both ways, Maya lists what she noticed. Sort each observation by the approach it describes.</p><p>Is this an advantage of hand-coding, or an advantage of a website builder?</p><p><b>Every element can be designed exactly as the mock-up shows.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of hand-coded HTML and CSS","Advantage of a website builder"],"correct":0,"why":"Hand-coding gives full creative control, without template limits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Builder or hand-coded? (2)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage of hand-coding, or an advantage of a website builder?</p><p><b>The owner could add a new page herself in ten minutes with no training in code.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of a website builder","Advantage of hand-coded HTML and CSS"],"correct":0,"why":"Drag-and-drop editors let non-coders update content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Builder or hand-coded? (3)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage of hand-coding, or an advantage of a website builder?</p><p><b>The site files can be moved to any host at any time.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of a website builder","Advantage of hand-coded HTML and CSS"],"correct":1,"why":"Hand-coded files are portable; builders often lock you in.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Builder or hand-coded? (4)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage of hand-coding, or an advantage of a website builder?</p><p><b>Hosting, SSL and security updates are handled by the platform.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of a website builder","Advantage of hand-coded HTML and CSS"],"correct":0,"why":"Hosted builders manage the infrastructure for you.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Builder or hand-coded? (5)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage of hand-coding, or an advantage of a website builder?</p><p><b>Pages are lean and load quickly on mobile data.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of hand-coded HTML and CSS","Advantage of a website builder"],"correct":0,"why":"Hand-written code avoids extra platform scripts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Builder or hand-coded? (6)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage of hand-coding, or an advantage of a website builder?</p><p><b>A professional-looking template was ready in under an hour.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of a website builder","Advantage of hand-coded HTML and CSS"],"correct":0,"why":"Templates make builders very fast.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Builder or hand-coded? (7)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage of hand-coding, or an advantage of a website builder?</p><p><b>Building it proves coding skill for a portfolio or the SBA.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of hand-coded HTML and CSS","Advantage of a website builder"],"correct":0,"why":"Hand-coding demonstrates technical skill that examiners and employers value.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Builder or hand-coded? (8)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage of hand-coding, or an advantage of a website builder?</p><p><b>A map, a video and a contact form were added with a few clicks.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of a website builder","Advantage of hand-coded HTML and CSS"],"correct":0,"why":"Builders have ready-made widgets for common features.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_3d6","title":"Module 2 · Unit 8 quiz: Forms of digital publishing","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 8: Forms of digital publishing. 23 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the form to the Caribbean example","q":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is building a slide for a client workshop. Match each form of digital publishing to the example that best shows it.</p>","left":["Website","Blog","Social networking platform","Game","App","Podcast","E-book"],"right":["A Bay Street jeweller's pages: Home, Collections, About, Contact, all under one domain","Weekly dated posts by a Barbados surfer on the best breaks, with reader comments","A Negril beach bar shares Reels and Stories and replies to followers","A browser quiz-race where players collect guavaberries to unlock levels","A Jamaican taxi service installed from Google Play with live driver tracking","A Dominica hiking guide sold through Kindle Direct Publishing as an EPUB file","A series of audio episodes on Caribbean history, released every Friday through an RSS feed"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,6,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form fits? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is fielding calls from clients across the region. Choose the form of digital publishing each client is describing.</p><p>A Curaçao dive shop wants a regular email that lands in customers' inboxes every Monday with dive conditions and offers.</p>","options":["newsletter","blog","podcast"],"correct":0,"why":"A regular opt-in email pushed to subscribers is a newsletter. A blog waits for readers to visit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form fits? (2)","q":"<p>A Trinidad author wants readers to change the font size on their phones and have the text reflow.</p>","options":["PDF digital magazine","website","EPUB e-book"],"correct":2,"why":"EPUB is reflowable, so text adjusts to screen and font size. PDF keeps a fixed layout.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form fits? (3)","q":"<p>A Belize tourism body wants a quarterly, beautifully laid-out edition with photo spreads and embedded video.</p>","options":["social networking post","digital magazine","newsletter"],"correct":1,"why":"Issue-based, designed layout and a regular schedule are the marks of a digital magazine.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form fits? (4)","q":"<p>A St. Kitts historian wants listeners to subscribe and receive each new 30-minute audio episode automatically.</p>","options":["podcast","e-book","video"],"correct":0,"why":"Episodes delivered through an RSS feed that listeners subscribe to make a podcast.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form fits? (5)","q":"<p>A Speightstown bakery wants customers to order and pay from their phones, with push notifications when the bread is ready.</p>","options":["app","blog","digital magazine"],"correct":0,"why":"Ordering, payment and push notifications are app features. A blog or magazine cannot do this.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form fits? (6)","q":"<p>A Roseau artist wants to share short clips, build followers and reply to comments every day.</p>","options":["e-book","newsletter","social networking platform"],"correct":2,"why":"Profiles, followers, sharing and comments define social networking platforms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which basis of classification? (1)","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is grouping a client's publications in different ways. For each grouping, decide which basis she is using.</p><p>Which basis of classification is being used?</p><p><b>Putting an electricity outage alert and a national news site together because both inform people.</b></p>","options":["Content type","Purpose","Audience","Level of interactivity"],"correct":1,"why":"Grouping by why something was published (to inform) is classification by purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which basis of classification? (2)","q":"<p>Which basis of classification is being used?</p><p><b>Separating content for cruise visitors at the Willemstad cruise terminal from content for local residents.</b></p>","options":["Content type","Level of interactivity","Purpose","Audience"],"correct":3,"why":"Grouping by who the content is for is classification by audience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which basis of classification? (3)","q":"<p>Which basis of classification is being used?</p><p><b>Grouping a podcast and an audio walking tour together because both are audio.</b></p>","options":["Audience","Content type","Level of interactivity","Purpose"],"correct":1,"why":"Grouping by the media used (audio) is classification by content type.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which basis of classification? (4)","q":"<p>Which basis of classification is being used?</p><p><b>Placing a PDF brochure in \"read only\" and a quiz game in \"user choices change the outcome\".</b></p>","options":["Purpose","Content type","Level of interactivity","Audience"],"correct":2,"why":"Grouping by how much the user can do is classification by level of interactivity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which basis of classification? (5)","q":"<p>Which basis of classification is being used?</p><p><b>Grouping a brand Instagram account and an online store because both aim to sell.</b></p>","options":["Content type","Level of interactivity","Purpose","Audience"],"correct":2,"why":"Persuading and selling is a purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which basis of classification? (6)","q":"<p>Which basis of classification is being used?</p><p><b>Making a separate children's version of a history e-book for primary schools.</b></p>","options":["Content type","Level of interactivity","Audience","Purpose"],"correct":2,"why":"Children are a target audience, so this is classification by audience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which basis of classification? (7)","q":"<p>Which basis of classification is being used?</p><p><b>Listing a blog, an e-book and a newsletter together as text-based publications.</b></p>","options":["Purpose","Content type","Audience","Level of interactivity"],"correct":1,"why":"Text-based is a content type.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which basis of classification? (8)","q":"<p>Which basis of classification is being used?</p><p><b>Placing blog posts with comments in the \"medium\" group, between e-books and apps.</b></p>","options":["Content type","Audience","Purpose","Level of interactivity"],"correct":3,"why":"Comments and shares give medium interactivity, which is the interactivity basis.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the warning signs","q":"<p>A client forwards Keisha this post from an unknown blog and asks whether they should share it on their business page.</p><p>Which lines show problems with ACCURACY or AUTHORITY? Select all that apply.</p>","options":["BREAKING: soursop tea cures diabetes, doctors are hiding it!","Posted by \"IslandTruth247\". No author name, no About page.","No date is shown on the post.","The web address imitates a real health ministry but is spelt slightly differently.","The page has clear headings and loads quickly on a phone.","No study, doctor or source is named to support the claim.","Images have alt text describing each bottle.","The post links to a national tourism board website for ferry times."],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3, 5],"why":"Sharp eye. An anonymous author and a fake official-looking address (authority), plus a sensational claim with no date or sources (accuracy), mean the post should not be shared."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the evidence to the criterion","q":"<p>The Nevis Nature Trails app is being reviewed. Match each piece of evidence to the evaluation criterion it best supports.</p>","left":["Accuracy","Authority","Accessibility","Satisfaction","Purpose","Risk and security","Engagement (extra)","Privacy (extra)"],"right":["Trail lengths are checked against park ranger measurements and dated","Content is written by the island's national trust, with named authors","Audio descriptions and large-text mode for visually impaired hikers","The About screen states the app exists to promote safe, eco-friendly hiking, and sponsors are clearly labelled","Logins use two-factor authentication and data is sent over HTTPS","Location is used only while the trail map is open, as stated in the policy","An average 4.6-star rating with reviews saying \"found the waterfall easily\"","Users post 300 trail photos and comments in the first month"],"correct":[0,1,2,6,3,4,7,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluating a form of publishing","q":"<p>The Antigua Sailing Week Live page on a social networking platform has 40,000 followers and thousands of comments on every post. However, a survey shows many sailors could not find the race start times, and several said the comments were full of arguments. Which conclusion is the BEST evaluation?</p>","options":["The page has low accuracy because there are many comments.","The page has a privacy problem because sailors cannot find start times.","The page is excellent because 40,000 followers proves it is high quality.","High audience engagement but low user satisfaction and usability, so the page needs a clear schedule and better comment moderation."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Comments say nothing about whether the facts are correct. The problem is finding information and the tone of comments.","Finding information is a usability issue. Privacy is about personal data.","Follower numbers measure reach, not quality. The survey shows users' needs were not met.","Correct. You separated engagement (lots of interaction) from satisfaction and usability (people could not complete their task) and gave a justified improvement."]},{"t":"match","title":"Task 1: match each form to its definition","q":"<p>Complete task 1 the quick way. Match each of the eight forms of digital publishing to its definition.</p>","left":["Website","Blog","Social networking","Game","App","Podcast","Video","E-book"],"right":["Interactive entertainment with rules and goals, played on a browser, console or phone","Platforms where users create profiles, connect and share posts with others","A series of audio episodes delivered by an RSS feed that listeners subscribe to","Regularly updated dated posts, newest first, in a personal voice with comments","Software installed from an app store onto a phone or tablet to carry out a task","Linked web pages under one domain, built, uploaded and updated by its owner","Recorded or live moving images with sound, published online","A book in electronic form, read on a screen or e-reader, often as EPUB"],"correct":[5,3,1,0,4,2,6,7]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a form for a heritage project (step 1)","q":"<p>You are advising the Bonaire Heritage Youth Club, which wants to teach 13 to 18 year olds about the island's salt-pan history. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The club president says: \"Let's make a 200-page e-book. Everything in one place.\" Most of the audience use phones and spend their free time on TikTok and games. What do you advise first?</p>","options":["Analyse the purpose and audience first: what must they learn, and how do they already consume content?","Agree to the 200-page e-book; it is the most complete option."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. The form should follow the purpose and audience, not the other way round.","Completeness does not equal engagement. A long text form ignores how this audience consumes content."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a form for a heritage project (step 2)","q":"<p>Your research shows the teens prefer short video and interactive content, and many have limited mobile data. Which main form do you recommend?</p>","options":["A 4K two-hour documentary to stream on YouTube.","A short-form video series (60 to 90 seconds each) with captions, plus a lightweight quiz game on a mobile web page.","A custom native app with 3D graphics that must be installed."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Too long for the audience and too heavy on data. It fails usability and accessibility.","Strong. Short video matches their habits; captions help accessibility; a light web game adds interactivity without a large download.","High cost, large download and low likelihood of installation. A mobile web game meets the need more cheaply."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a form for a heritage project (step 3)","q":"<p>The game will ask players for a nickname. A parent asks about safety. What do you add?</p>","options":["Ask for full name, school and phone number so prizes can be sent.","Collect only a nickname (no email or location), state this in a simple privacy notice, use HTTPS and moderate any leaderboard names."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This collects far more personal data than needed from minors, creating privacy and safety risks.","Excellent. You minimised data, explained it, secured it and reduced the risk of abusive names."]},{"t":"match","title":"Steps to evaluate a form of digital publishing","q":"<p>Put Keisha's evaluation method into the correct order. She uses it for every Paper 02-style evaluation.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Identify the form and state its purpose and target audience.","Select the evaluation criteria that matter most for that purpose (for example accuracy and authority for news).","Gather evidence for each criterion: check sources, test on a phone, read the privacy policy, look at reviews and engagement.","Identify strengths and weaknesses, with a specific example for each.","Weigh the strengths against the weaknesses for this audience.","Reach a justified judgement and recommend improvements."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]}]},{"id":"assess_3gz","title":"Module 2 · Unit 9 quiz: Online publishing platforms","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 9: Online publishing platforms. 30 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Hosted free, hosted paid or self-hosted? (1)","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is sorting client notes. Decide which type of platform each note describes.</p><p>Which type of platform is described?</p><p><b>A student blog at grenadaspice.blogspot.com with no fees at all.</b></p>","options":["Hosted paid","Hosted free","Self-hosted"],"correct":1,"why":"Blogger is hosted by Google, free, and uses a blogspot subdomain.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hosted free, hosted paid or self-hosted? (2)","q":"<p>Which type of platform is described?</p><p><b>A Willemstad boutique pays a monthly Squarespace plan for templates and online sales.</b></p>","options":["Self-hosted","Hosted free","Hosted paid"],"correct":2,"why":"Squarespace is a hosted service that charges a subscription.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hosted free, hosted paid or self-hosted? (3)","q":"<p>Which type of platform is described?</p><p><b>The client installs WordPress.org software on rented hosting and must update plugins.</b></p>","options":["Self-hosted","Hosted free","Hosted paid"],"correct":0,"why":"Installing the software yourself on your own hosting is self-hosting, with full responsibility for updates.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hosted free, hosted paid or self-hosted? (4)","q":"<p>Which type of platform is described?</p><p><b>A Jamaican essayist publishes on Medium to reach its readers, paying nothing.</b></p>","options":["Self-hosted","Hosted paid","Hosted free"],"correct":2,"why":"Medium hosts the content; writing and publishing are free.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hosted free, hosted paid or self-hosted? (5)","q":"<p>Which type of platform is described?</p><p><b>A Wix premium plan removes Wix ads and connects a custom domain.</b></p>","options":["Hosted free","Self-hosted","Hosted paid"],"correct":2,"why":"Premium Wix plans are paid hosted services.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hosted free, hosted paid or self-hosted? (6)","q":"<p>Which type of platform is described?</p><p><b>The owner must arrange their own backups and security plugin after the server is hacked.</b></p>","options":["Self-hosted","Hosted free","Hosted paid"],"correct":0,"why":"On a self-hosted site, security and backups are the owner's responsibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hosted free, hosted paid or self-hosted? (7)","q":"<p>Which type of platform is described?</p><p><b>A free WordPress.com site that shows the platform's ads and a wordpress.com address.</b></p>","options":["Self-hosted","Hosted free","Hosted paid"],"correct":1,"why":"The free WordPress.com plan is hosted, free, and limited.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hosted free, hosted paid or self-hosted? (8)","q":"<p>Which type of platform is described?</p><p><b>Full freedom to edit the theme's PHP code and add any plugin.</b></p>","options":["Hosted free","Self-hosted","Hosted paid"],"correct":1,"why":"Full code and plugin control is the main benefit of self-hosting.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Blog post SEO check","q":"<p>A Barbados guesthouse owner sends Keisha the plan for her first blog post. The target keyword is <b>\"Bathsheba surfing guide\"</b>.</p><p>Which items are GOOD SEO practice? Select all that apply.</p>","options":["Images compressed so the page loads quickly on mobile data.","The keyword repeated 40 times in one short post.","Photos named IMG_0042.jpg with no alt text.","URL slug: /post-123456","Title: \"Bathsheba Surfing Guide: Waves, Seasons and Safety\"","Links to the guesthouse's other posts about the east coast.","A 155-character meta description that includes the keyword.","The keyword appears naturally in the first paragraph."],"correct":[0, 4, 5, 6, 7],"why":"Well spotted. Keyword in the title, opening and meta description, internal links and fast-loading images all help people find and stay on the post."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the Wikipedia standard","q":"<p>Your class is editing the Wikipedia article on a Caribbean festival. Match each editing problem to the Wikipedia standard it breaks or the feature it uses.</p>","left":["Neutral point of view","Verifiability","No original research","Notability","Conflict of interest","Talk page","View history"],"right":["Removing \"the most amazing party in the Caribbean\" from the opening sentence","Comparing versions and reverting vandalism from last night","Deleting a paragraph based on a student's own unpublished survey","Where editors discuss and reach consensus on a disputed date","A band manager declaring a connection before suggesting edits about the band","Adding a citation to a published newspaper article for the founding year","Deciding a one-off school party with no news coverage should not have its own article"],"correct":[0,5,2,6,4,3,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"YouTube upload decisions (1)","q":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is uploading a client's video about the Spice Island Emporium on Frederick Street in Port of Spain. Choose the best setting each time.</p><p>The client must approve the video before anyone else sees it, but needs to watch it without logging into Malik's account.</p>","options":["Unlisted","Public","Made for kids"],"correct":0,"why":"Unlisted lets anyone with the link watch it, but it does not appear in search or on the channel.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"YouTube upload decisions (2)","q":"<p>Which thumbnail size matches YouTube's recommended 16:9 format?</p>","options":["500 by 500","1280 by 720","1080 by 1920"],"correct":1,"why":"1280 by 720 is 16:9 landscape, YouTube's recommended thumbnail size. 1080 by 1920 is vertical 9:16.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"YouTube upload decisions (3)","q":"<p>Viewers want to jump straight to the tasting section. What should Malik add?</p>","options":["an end screen","more tags","chapters (timestamps in the description)"],"correct":2,"why":"Timestamps in the description create chapters, so viewers can jump to sections.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"YouTube upload decisions (4)","q":"<p>Auto-captions wrote \"shadow benny\" instead of \"chadon beni\". What should he do?</p>","options":["edit the captions","turn off captions","leave them; auto-captions are always fine"],"correct":0,"why":"Edited captions improve accuracy, accessibility and searchability. Turning them off excludes deaf viewers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"YouTube upload decisions (5)","q":"<p>The title, description, tags and thumbnail together are called the video's...</p>","options":["metadata","codec","bitrate"],"correct":0,"why":"Metadata is data about the video, used by search and recommendations.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"YouTube upload decisions (6)","q":"<p>The client is paid by a rum brand to feature its product. What must they do?</p>","options":["nothing","hide it in the tags","disclose the paid promotion"],"correct":2,"why":"Platform rules and advertising standards require paid promotions to be disclosed.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Setting up a live stream","q":"<p>Malik is preparing to live-stream the Gros Islet Friday night street party for a St. Lucia tourism client using OBS Studio. Put his steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Agree the purpose, platform and time with the client, and promote the stream in advance.","Test the upload speed and set up the camera on a tripod with an external microphone.","Build scenes in OBS Studio (camera, logo, captions) and paste the platform's stream key.","Run a private test stream and check audio, video and chat.","Go live, with Keisha moderating the chat and answering questions.","End with a call to action, save the replay and cut highlight clips.","Review the stream analytics (peak viewers, watch time) to plan the next one."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or limitation? (1)","q":"<p>Keisha is preparing a report for the Grande Riviere Turtle Watch in Trinidad on using social media platforms. Sort each point.</p><p>Is this a benefit or a limitation of using an online platform?</p><p><b>Free analytics show which turtle videos are watched longest.</b></p>","options":["Limitation","Benefit"],"correct":1,"why":"Built-in analytics are a key benefit of platforms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or limitation? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a limitation of using an online platform?</p><p><b>A feed algorithm change halves the reach of the group's posts overnight.</b></p>","options":["Limitation","Benefit"],"correct":0,"why":"Algorithm changes are outside your control: a limitation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or limitation? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a limitation of using an online platform?</p><p><b>A video is muted because it used a popular song without a licence.</b></p>","options":["Limitation","Benefit"],"correct":0,"why":"Copyright rules and claims limit what you can publish.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or limitation? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a limitation of using an online platform?</p><p><b>Volunteers can post from their phones in seconds.</b></p>","options":["Limitation","Benefit"],"correct":1,"why":"Ease of use is a benefit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or limitation? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a limitation of using an online platform?</p><p><b>The group cannot download a list of its followers' contact details.</b></p>","options":["Benefit","Limitation"],"correct":1,"why":"You do not own the audience on a rented platform.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or limitation? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a limitation of using an online platform?</p><p><b>A nesting-season post is shared by visitors in Canada and the Netherlands.</b></p>","options":["Limitation","Benefit"],"correct":1,"why":"Global audience reach is a major benefit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or limitation? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a limitation of using an online platform?</p><p><b>The account is hacked because two-factor authentication was not turned on.</b></p>","options":["Limitation","Benefit"],"correct":0,"why":"Security risks are a limitation that must be managed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit or limitation? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a limitation of using an online platform?</p><p><b>Live comments let residents ask questions during a beach clean-up stream.</b></p>","options":["Limitation","Benefit"],"correct":1,"why":"Community features are a benefit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform dependency","q":"<p>Speightstown Kitchen sells fish cakes and bakes and takes all its orders through one social media account. The account is suspended by mistake for a week. Which action would MOST reduce this risk in future?</p>","options":["Turn off comments on all posts.","Post more often on the same account.","Build an owned channel, such as a simple website with an order form and an email or WhatsApp list, and link to it from social media.","Buy followers to increase the account's size."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["This reduces community engagement and does nothing about dependency.","More posts do not protect against suspension; the business still depends on one platform.","Correct. Owned channels reduce platform dependency because the business controls them even if one platform suspends the account.","Bought followers breach platform rules and increase the risk of suspension."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a platform for a local start-up (step 1)","q":"<p>You are advising Georgetown Youth Media Collective, a group of four students who want to publish Caribbean news explainers, short videos and occasional live Q&amp;As. They have a small budget and plan to grow. Make the best decisions.</p><p>One member says: \"Let's just post everything on TikTok. It's free and everyone is there.\" What do you advise?</p>","options":["Agree: one free platform is enough.","Use TikTok and YouTube for reach, but also create an owned home such as a website or newsletter."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This creates platform dependency. One algorithm change or suspension could wipe out the audience.","Good. You get platform reach while reducing dependency on one platform."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a platform for a local start-up (step 2)","q":"<p>For the written explainers, which platform do you recommend to start with?</p>","options":["A hosted WordPress.com plan with a custom domain, upgrading to self-hosted WordPress when they need custom features.","Self-hosted WordPress with ten premium plugins straight away, though no one knows how to maintain it."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong. Easy and affordable now, with a clear path to more control later, and the content can be exported.","Self-hosting without maintenance skills creates security and cost risks."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a platform for a local start-up (step 3)","q":"<p>They plan a live Q&amp;A with a local politician on Facebook Live. What do you add to the plan?</p>","options":["Just go live from a phone in the hallway and see what happens.","A test stream, an external microphone, a chat moderator, clear comment rules and no copyrighted background music."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Without testing or moderation, poor audio and abusive comments could damage the collective's reputation.","Excellent. You covered technical quality, moderation and copyright."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a platform for a local start-up (step 4)","q":"<p>Six months later, how should they keep the platforms useful?</p>","options":["Set a maintenance schedule: update facts and plugins, check analytics, back up, and archive outdated explainers.","Stop updating old posts; only new content matters."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Yes. Maintenance keeps content accurate, secure and relevant.","Outdated explainers spread wrong information and damage authority."]}]},{"id":"assess_3o6","title":"Module 2 · Unit 10 quiz: Content management and trends in web publishing","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 10: Content management and trends in web publishing. 51 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Content management basics","q":"<p>The Bonaire Dive Log website runs on a CMS. Match each term to the task or description it shows.</p>","left":["Content Management Application (CMA)","Content Delivery Application (CDA)","Repository","Content reuse","Organising","Separating content from design"],"right":["The dashboard where the owner types a new reef post and uploads photos","Switching to a new theme without retyping any posts","Using the same reef description on the website, in the app and in the newsletter","The central store where each post and photo is kept only once","Putting every reef post into the category \"Dive sites\" with the tag \"beginner\"","The back-end process that combines the post with the template and shows it to visitors"],"correct":[0,5,3,2,4,1]},{"t":"match","title":"The five stages of ECM","q":"<p>The Leeward Islands Credit Union is handling a member's signed loan application. Put the five ECM stages in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Capture: the signed form is scanned at the St. John’s branch and entered into the system.","Manage: it is tagged with the member number and routed to a loans officer for approval.","Store: the approved form is given a home in the member records repository.","Preserve: it is archived and backed up off-island for the legally required number of years.","Deliver: when the member calls from English Harbour, the agent sees the correct version instantly."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of content management? (1)","q":"<p>Clients are asking the Island Pulse Media for advice. Choose the type of content management that best fits each need.</p><p>A Barbados law firm needs versioning, auditing and regulatory compliance for thousands of contracts.</p>","options":["DMS","WCM","MCM"],"correct":0,"why":"Controlling the document lifecycle and compliance is the main purpose of a DMS, the \"e-cabinet\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of content management? (2)","q":"<p>A Bridgetown restaurant wants to update its menu pages and blog through a browser.</p>","options":["DAM","WCM","ECM"],"correct":1,"why":"Content destined for the web is the job of WCM, such as WordPress.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of content management? (3)","q":"<p>A regional telecom company must capture, manage, store, preserve and deliver all its information across departments and islands.</p>","options":["ECM","MCM","DMS"],"correct":0,"why":"Those are the five ECM stages covering all of an organisation's information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of content management? (4)","q":"<p>A Carnival committee wants one set of event listings to feed its website, app, airport screens and a smart speaker.</p>","options":["Headless CMS","DMS","WCM"],"correct":0,"why":"Content delivered as data over an API to many heads is a headless CMS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of content management? (5)","q":"<p>A cruise line's sales staff need secure access to price sheets on their tablets, with remote wipe if one is lost.</p>","options":["WCM","ECM","MCM"],"correct":2,"why":"Secure access to corporate data on mobile devices, with identity management, is MCM.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of content management? (6)","q":"<p>A media house needs to tag 40,000 photos with photographer, location and licence expiry.</p>","options":["WCM","MCM","DAM"],"correct":2,"why":"Managing rich media files with rights metadata is Digital Asset Management.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Why go headless?","q":"<p>The St. Lucia Jazz and Arts Festival publishes its programme on a WordPress website. It now wants the same programme to appear in a new mobile app, on digital screens at the venue and in a smart speaker skill, without staff retyping it. Which solution BEST meets this need?</p>","options":["Document Management Software with versioning for the programme PDF.","Three separate websites, one for each channel.","A headless CMS that stores the programme once and delivers it through an API to each head.","A new WordPress theme with bigger fonts."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["A DMS controls document lifecycles, but a PDF does not adapt to an app, screens and a voice assistant.","This multiplies work and staff would have to retype content, which is the problem to solve.","Correct. Decoupling content from presentation lets one body feed many heads through an API: COPE.","A theme only changes how one website looks; it does not deliver content to other channels."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or headless? (1)","q":"<p>Malik is preparing a comparison for Windward Islands Weekly. Sort each statement using the comparison table.</p><p>Does this describe a traditional (coupled) CMS or a headless CMS?</p><p><b>Themes and templates are built in.</b></p>","options":["Headless CMS","Traditional (coupled) CMS"],"correct":1,"why":"A coupled CMS includes its own front end (the head) through themes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or headless? (2)","q":"<p>Does this describe a traditional (coupled) CMS or a headless CMS?</p><p><b>Developers build each front end separately.</b></p>","options":["Traditional (coupled) CMS","Headless CMS"],"correct":1,"why":"A headless CMS has no built-in front end, so each head is built by developers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or headless? (3)","q":"<p>Does this describe a traditional (coupled) CMS or a headless CMS?</p><p><b>Output is mainly one website.</b></p>","options":["Traditional (coupled) CMS","Headless CMS"],"correct":0,"why":"The body and one head are joined, so content is locked to one output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or headless? (4)","q":"<p>Does this describe a traditional (coupled) CMS or a headless CMS?</p><p><b>Content can go to any channel that can call the API.</b></p>","options":["Traditional (coupled) CMS","Headless CMS"],"correct":1,"why":"Delivering content as data over an API is what makes headless multi-channel.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or headless? (5)","q":"<p>Does this describe a traditional (coupled) CMS or a headless CMS?</p><p><b>Low skills needed; ideal for non-technical users.</b></p>","options":["Headless CMS","Traditional (coupled) CMS"],"correct":1,"why":"A traditional CMS lets non-technical users manage a site without developers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or headless? (6)","q":"<p>Does this describe a traditional (coupled) CMS or a headless CMS?</p><p><b>Very high flexibility: any design or framework.</b></p>","options":["Traditional (coupled) CMS","Headless CMS"],"correct":1,"why":"Because the front end is separate, developers can use any technology.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or headless? (7)","q":"<p>Does this describe a traditional (coupled) CMS or a headless CMS?</p><p><b>Wix, Squarespace and Joomla.</b></p>","options":["Headless CMS","Traditional (coupled) CMS"],"correct":1,"why":"These are coupled systems with built-in themes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or headless? (8)","q":"<p>Does this describe a traditional (coupled) CMS or a headless CMS?</p><p><b>Contentful, Sanity and Strapi.</b></p>","options":["Headless CMS","Traditional (coupled) CMS"],"correct":0,"why":"These are headless CMS products that deliver content via API.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome is preparing notes for a client in Antigua. Sort each statement into the three CMS classifications.</p><p>Which classification does this describe?</p><p><b>Drupal, which a developer can download and customise without special permission.</b></p>","options":["Open source CMS","SaaS CMS","Proprietary CMS"],"correct":0,"why":"Open source CMS can be used and customised by anyone without a licence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (2)","q":"<p>Which classification does this describe?</p><p><b>Microsoft SharePoint, used under a paid licence.</b></p>","options":["Open source CMS","SaaS CMS","Proprietary CMS"],"correct":2,"why":"SharePoint is owned by Microsoft and used under licence: proprietary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (3)","q":"<p>Which classification does this describe?</p><p><b>Wix: a monthly subscription that includes hosting and support.</b></p>","options":["Open source CMS","Proprietary CMS","SaaS CMS"],"correct":2,"why":"Software, hosting and support bundled in a cloud subscription is SaaS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (4)","q":"<p>Which classification does this describe?</p><p><b>Thousands of free plugins and themes written by a worldwide community.</b></p>","options":["Proprietary CMS","Open source CMS","SaaS CMS"],"correct":1,"why":"Because anyone can develop for open source CMS, the ecosystem is huge.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (5)","q":"<p>Which classification does this describe?</p><p><b>Even licence holders cannot change the code without a developer's licence.</b></p>","options":["Proprietary CMS","Open source CMS","SaaS CMS"],"correct":0,"why":"The owner controls a proprietary application.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (6)","q":"<p>Which classification does this describe?</p><p><b>Squarespace: the supplier handles maintenance and upgrades in real time.</b></p>","options":["Proprietary CMS","Open source CMS","SaaS CMS"],"correct":2,"why":"With SaaS, the supplier handles maintenance, upgrades and technical issues.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (7)","q":"<p>Which classification does this describe?</p><p><b>Joomla, which runs on PHP.</b></p>","options":["SaaS CMS","Proprietary CMS","Open source CMS"],"correct":2,"why":"The most popular open source CMS (WordPress, Drupal, Joomla) run on PHP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (8)","q":"<p>Which classification does this describe?</p><p><b>Sitecore, with professional vendor support for large enterprises.</b></p>","options":["SaaS CMS","Open source CMS","Proprietary CMS"],"correct":2,"why":"Vendor support and enterprise features are advantages of proprietary CMS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (9)","q":"<p>Which classification does this describe?</p><p><b>Webflow: priced per site, hosted in the cloud.</b></p>","options":["Proprietary CMS","SaaS CMS","Open source CMS"],"correct":1,"why":"Subscription priced per site or user, hosted in the cloud, is SaaS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Partial or fully cloud?","q":"<p>The Frigate Bay Yacht Services wants a cloud CMS installed on its own cloud web server so that its developer can alter the source code and add a custom berth-booking module. Which type of cloud CMS is this?</p>","options":["A headless CMS","A fully cloud CMS","Document Management Software","A partial cloud CMS"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Headless describes how content is delivered (over an API), not whether a cloud CMS is on your own server.","A fully cloud CMS comes as a package under the supplier's control, so custom code is not usually possible.","A DMS controls document lifecycles; it is not a type of cloud CMS for a booking site.","Correct. A partial cloud CMS sits on your own cloud web server and can be changed by altering the code or adding modules."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the CMS feature","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is training the Windward Islands Weekly staff. Match each problem to the CMS feature that solves it.</p>","left":["WYSIWYG / block editor","User roles and permissions","Version control and revisions","Scheduling","Multilingual support","SEO tools","Security, backups and updates"],"right":["An editor deleted a paragraph by mistake and needs yesterday's version","The Emancipation Day feature must go live at 6 a.m. on 1 July","Readers want stories in English, Dutch and Spanish","A trainee must be able to write stories but not publish them","The site must be recoverable after an attack or a storm","Reporters cannot write HTML but need to add headings and photos","Stories need better page titles and meta descriptions for search engines"],"correct":[5,3,0,1,2,6,4]},{"t":"match","title":"Put the content workflow in order","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media is producing a \"Hurricane season checklist\" post for an Antiguan client. Put the workflow steps in a sensible order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Create: Keisha drafts the checklist in structured fields and adds photos in the CMS editor.","Edit: Zara checks facts against official guidance, fixes style and adds alt text.","Organise and tag: placed under Safety > Antigua and tagged \"hurricane\", \"checklist\".","Approve: the client reviews and signs off the post.","Schedule: set to publish at 7 a.m. on 1 June, the start of the Atlantic hurricane season.","Publish: the post goes live on the website, app and newsletter at once.","Update: shelter details are revised when the government announces changes.","Archive: at the end of the season the post is archived and a new version is planned."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"ma","title":"Chapter review: true or false?","q":"<p>These statements come from Section B of the chapter review. Pick only the <b>TRUE</b> statements.</p><p>Which statements are TRUE?</p>","options":["ECM is a single piece of software.","A headless CMS cannot be used to build a website.","A content management system requires users to write all website code by hand.","Metadata helps users and search engines find content.","A DMS can be considered a sub-category of content management software.","SaaS CMS suppliers usually handle maintenance and upgrades.","Most proprietary CMS can be hosted on any web host.","The CDA stores content and makes it visible to visitors.","Open source CMS can be customised without special permission."],"correct":[3, 4, 5, 7, 8],"why":"Well done. You avoided the traps: a CMS needs no hand coding, ECM is a combination of strategies, methods and tools, a website is one of a headless CMS's heads, and most proprietary CMS run properly only on the owner's host."},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (1)","q":"<p>Zara is preparing a talk on why web publishing in the Caribbean has changed. Sort each example by the main factor it shows.</p><p>Which factor is MAINLY shown?</p><p><b>A Grenada spice farmer builds a professional site from a cheap template in an afternoon.</b></p>","options":["Politics and empowerment","Commoditisation and affordability","Accessibility","Pervasiveness and adoption"],"correct":1,"why":"Templates and site builders have become cheap standard products: commoditisation and affordability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (2)","q":"<p>Which factor is MAINLY shown?</p><p><b>Almost every customer at a Gros Islet café browses on a phone with 4G.</b></p>","options":["Commoditisation and affordability","Politics and empowerment","Pervasiveness and adoption","Accessibility"],"correct":2,"why":"Always-on mobile technology everywhere is pervasiveness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (3)","q":"<p>Which factor is MAINLY shown?</p><p><b>A news site adds captions and screen-reader-friendly headings for visually impaired readers.</b></p>","options":["Accessibility","Politics and empowerment","Commoditisation and affordability","Pervasiveness and adoption"],"correct":0,"why":"Making content usable by people with disabilities is accessibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (4)","q":"<p>Which factor is MAINLY shown?</p><p><b>A new data protection law requires sites to explain how they use visitors' data.</b></p>","options":["Pervasiveness and adoption","Accessibility","Commoditisation and affordability","Politics and empowerment"],"correct":3,"why":"Laws and regulation are the politics factor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (5)","q":"<p>Which factor is MAINLY shown?</p><p><b>A Jamaican teenager builds a large YouTube audience with no TV station behind her.</b></p>","options":["Commoditisation and affordability","Pervasiveness and adoption","Politics and empowerment","Accessibility"],"correct":2,"why":"Individuals publishing without gatekeepers is empowerment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (6)","q":"<p>Which factor is MAINLY shown?</p><p><b>Local businesses move their promotions to WhatsApp Channels after customers start using them daily.</b></p>","options":["Pervasiveness and adoption","Commoditisation and affordability","Accessibility","Politics and empowerment"],"correct":0,"why":"Following where users have moved is adoption.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (7)","q":"<p>Which factor is MAINLY shown?</p><p><b>Stock photos and fonts that once cost a lot are now free on Canva.</b></p>","options":["Accessibility","Commoditisation and affordability","Politics and empowerment","Pervasiveness and adoption"],"correct":1,"why":"Specialist resources becoming cheap and standard is commoditisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (8)","q":"<p>Which factor is MAINLY shown?</p><p><b>A government portal offers a low-data text version for users with weak connections.</b></p>","options":["Commoditisation and affordability","Politics and empowerment","Pervasiveness and adoption","Accessibility"],"correct":3,"why":"Making content reachable for people with weak connections is accessibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the emerging trend (1)","q":"<p>Clients of the Island Pulse Media describe what they have noticed. Name the trend each time.</p><p>A hotel's website can be added to a guest's home screen and still shows the map without Wi-Fi.</p>","options":["voice search","progressive web app","Web3"],"correct":1,"why":"Home-screen install and offline use through a service worker define a PWA.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the emerging trend (2)","q":"<p>Customers ask their phones, \"Where can I eat doubles near Maracas?\"</p>","options":["personalisation","newsletter","voice search"],"correct":2,"why":"Spoken, question-style searches are voice search.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the emerging trend (3)","q":"<p>A news site lets readers see three free articles a month, then asks them to subscribe.</p>","options":["hard paywall","voluntary membership","metered paywall"],"correct":2,"why":"A few free articles before payment is metered access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the emerging trend (4)","q":"<p>A writer moves her readers from social media to a paid email publication on Substack.</p>","options":["PWA","short-form video","newsletters and subscriptions"],"correct":2,"why":"Direct-to-subscriber email, sometimes paid, is the newsletter and subscription trend.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the emerging trend (5)","q":"<p>A newsroom uses a tool to transcribe interviews and draft alt text, then an editor checks everything.</p>","options":["Web3","AI in the publishing workflow","voice search"],"correct":1,"why":"AI assisting the workflow, with human checking, is the responsible approach.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the emerging trend (6)","q":"<p>A Trinidad podcast also publishes each episode as an audio article and a captioned vertical clip.</p>","options":["commoditisation","DMS","video, audio and visual storytelling"],"correct":2,"why":"Publishing in audio, video and visual formats alongside text is this trend.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"The zero-click challenge","q":"<p>Portsmouth Gazette notices that many readers now get answers such as \"Canefield market days\" straight from AI search summaries and never visit its website. Which response is MOST likely to protect its income?</p>","options":["Publish more short generic articles to appear in more search results.","Switch from WordPress to a proprietary CMS.","Build a direct relationship with readers through a newsletter and membership, and focus on local reporting AI cannot easily replace.","Remove the website and post only on one social platform."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Generic content is exactly what AI summaries answer without a click, so this does not solve the problem.","The CMS classification does not change how search engines show answers.","Correct. Direct relationships and unique local content are the main responses to zero-click search.","This increases platform dependency and gives up the publisher's own home and data."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the research tool","q":"<p>Match each research question to the tool that answers it best.</p>","left":["Google Trends","SEMrush","BuzzSumo","Feedly","Google Analytics","The Web Almanac"],"right":["Has interest in \"Dominica hurricane preparedness\" risen each June over five years?","Which keywords does a rival Curaçao tour site rank for on Google?","How can I read new posts from 20 design blogs in one place each morning?","Which articles about Caribbean travel were shared most last month?","What share of visitors to our own website use phones?","What does yearly data say about page weight and CMS use across the web?"],"correct":[0,1,3,2,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fixing the newsroom problem (step 1)","q":"<p>You are working with Zara's team at the Island Pulse Media on a case study: <b>Windward Islands Weekly</b> in Kingstown, St. Vincent. One developer pastes every story by hand, photos are on three laptops, and the app shows different headlines from the website. Make the best decisions.</p><p>Reporters email stories to the developer, and last week a story with a wrong date went live. What do you set up first?</p>","options":["Make every reporter an Administrator so they can fix mistakes faster.","A CMS with user roles and a workflow: reporters draft as Authors, an Editor checks, the editor-in-chief approves before scheduling."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["More admin rights increase security risk and remove checks; errors still reach the public.","Good. Roles and an approval workflow remove the single point of failure and catch errors."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fixing the newsroom problem (step 2)","q":"<p>The paper wants the same stories on its website, app, morning newsletter and a lobby screen, without retyping. What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Ask staff to copy and paste each story into the app, newsletter and screen software.","A headless set-up: the body stores each story once and the API sends it to all four heads."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Retyping wastes time and creates inconsistent versions, the original problem.","Strong. Decoupled content over an API gives COPE: create once, publish everywhere."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fixing the newsroom problem (step 3)","q":"<p>Jerome must sort 40,000 photos from three laptops. What does he use?</p>","options":["A DAM, tagging each photo with location, event, photographer and licence expiry.","An MCM system so staff can open photos on tablets.","One big shared folder named \"Photos\" with no tags."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Digital Asset Management with rights metadata makes photos findable and safe to reuse.","MCM secures access on mobile devices; it does not organise media with rights metadata.","Without metadata nobody can find images or check licences."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fixing the newsroom problem (step 4)","q":"<p>Search traffic is falling as AI summaries answer questions directly. How do you respond?</p>","options":["Copy whatever a large US site is doing this week.","Check analytics and Google Trends, then grow the newsletter and memberships and focus on local reporting."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Without your own data you may follow a trend that does not suit a Caribbean audience.","Excellent. Evidence first, then direct relationships and content AI cannot easily replace."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fixing the newsroom problem (step 5)","q":"<p>A manager suggests publishing 50 AI-written stories a day with no checks. What do you say?</p>","options":["Use AI only to assist with transcripts, alt text and drafts; a human checks every fact and AI use is disclosed under a clear AI policy.","Agree; more content always means more readers."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Yes. You gain speed while protecting accuracy and trust.","Unchecked AI content can contain errors that damage trust and the paper's reputation."]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Module 3","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_3ri","title":"Module 3 · Unit 1 quiz: The mobile ecosystem","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 1: The mobile ecosystem. 21 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Which component is it? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome, the mobile developer at the Island Pulse Media, is mapping the ecosystem for a new beach-safety app. Sort each item into the correct component.</p><p>Which ecosystem component does this belong to?</p><p><b>A live wave-height map inside the app.</b></p>","options":["Infrastructure","Content","Devices","Users"],"correct":1,"why":"It is what people view and use on the screen, so it is content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which component is it? (2)","q":"<p>Which ecosystem component does this belong to?</p><p><b>A cell tower on Crocus Hill carrying 4G signals.</b></p>","options":["Content","Users","Infrastructure","Devices"],"correct":2,"why":"Towers are part of the physical network that delivers data: infrastructure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which component is it? (3)","q":"<p>Which ecosystem component does this belong to?</p><p><b>A tourist from Toronto checking conditions at Shoal Bay, Anguilla.</b></p>","options":["Users","Content","Devices","Infrastructure"],"correct":0,"why":"A person using the service is a user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which component is it? (4)","q":"<p>Which ecosystem component does this belong to?</p><p><b>A mid-range Samsung phone running Android.</b></p>","options":["Infrastructure","Devices","Users","Content"],"correct":1,"why":"The handset and its operating system make up the device component.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which component is it? (5)","q":"<p>Which ecosystem component does this belong to?</p><p><b>The cloud server that stores the beach data.</b></p>","options":["Users","Devices","Infrastructure","Content"],"correct":2,"why":"Servers and data centres are back-end infrastructure, even if users never see them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which component is it? (6)","q":"<p>Which ecosystem component does this belong to?</p><p><b>A short safety video about rip currents.</b></p>","options":["Users","Infrastructure","Content","Devices"],"correct":2,"why":"Video shown to people is content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which component is it? (7)","q":"<p>Which ecosystem component does this belong to?</p><p><b>A lifeguard team updating flag colours each morning.</b></p>","options":["Content","Users","Infrastructure","Devices"],"correct":1,"why":"The lifeguards use the app to post updates, so they are users (and content contributors).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which component is it? (8)","q":"<p>Which ecosystem component does this belong to?</p><p><b>An Apple Watch that buzzes with a storm alert.</b></p>","options":["Devices","Content","Users","Infrastructure"],"correct":0,"why":"Wearables are devices in the ecosystem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which component is it? (9)","q":"<p>Which ecosystem component does this belong to?</p><p><b>The Google Play and Apple App Store distribution systems.</b></p>","options":["Users","Devices","Content","Infrastructure"],"correct":3,"why":"App stores are part of the distribution infrastructure that gets apps onto devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Carrier roles","q":"<p>Keisha is writing a social media explainer for a telco client. Match each carrier service or term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Roaming","Prepaid plan","Carrier billing","Zero-rating","Spectrum","eSIM","Mobile network operator"],"right":["A cruise passenger from Miami uses a US SIM on a Barbados network","A digital SIM downloaded to the phone with no plastic card","A student tops up credit before using data, with no monthly bill","A game purchase is charged to the phone account instead of a card","The company that owns and runs the towers and radio network","Using WhatsApp does not count against the data allowance","Radio frequencies licensed to an operator for its network"],"correct":[0,2,3,5,6,1,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading consumer behaviour (1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media is planning an app for the Oranjestad Night Market. Answer each question about mobile consumers.</p><p>Most target users are on prepaid data. Which design choice fits BEST?</p>","options":["A 300 MB download with 3D models","Autoplay HD video on the home screen","Compressed images and an offline menu"],"correct":2,"why":"Data-conscious prepaid users expect apps that use little data. Compression and offline content respect that.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading consumer behaviour (2)","q":"<p>Vendors already take orders through WhatsApp. What does this tell Jerome?</p>","options":["WhatsApp should be ignored","Users expect messaging-style ordering, so the app should link to or copy that flow","Vendors do not use phones"],"correct":1,"why":"Existing behaviour shows what users find natural. Good apps build on familiar patterns.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading consumer behaviour (3)","q":"<p>Traffic to the app will peak on Friday nights when cruise ships are in port. This is an example of...</p>","options":["a regulatory framework","a usage pattern","a device specification"],"correct":1,"why":"When and how often people use a service is a usage pattern.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading consumer behaviour (4)","q":"<p>A user deletes the app because it takes eight seconds to load. Which expectation was not met?</p>","options":["Speed and performance","Local relevance","Low price"],"correct":0,"why":"Mobile users expect near-instant loading. Slow performance is a top reason for uninstalling.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading consumer behaviour (5)","q":"<p>Visitors want to pay vendors by phone. Which consumer expectation is MOST important for payments?</p>","options":["Bright colours","Security and trust","Large fonts only"],"correct":1,"why":"Users will not enter payment details unless they trust the app is secure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Who regulates?","q":"<p>A mobile customer in Philipsburg has complained for months that her carrier keeps charging her for a data bundle she cancelled. The carrier has not resolved it. Which organisation is MOST appropriate for her to contact next?</p>","options":["Google Play customer support","A local app developer community","Bureau Telecommunications and Post (BTP), Sint Maarten","TATT"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Google Play handles app purchases, not carrier data bundles. The dispute is with the network operator.","Developer communities build content. They have no authority over carrier billing.","Correct. BTP is Sint Maarten's telecoms regulator, and consumer protection in the telecoms sector is part of a regulator's role.","TATT regulates telecoms in Trinidad and Tobago, not in Sint Maarten."]},{"t":"match","title":"Follow the voice note","q":"<p>Zara, creative director at Island Pulse Media, sends a WhatsApp voice note from Bridgetown to a client in Kingston, Jamaica. Put the journey through the ecosystem in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Zara records the voice note in the WhatsApp app (content) on her iPhone (device).","The phone compresses the audio and sends it by radio to the nearest carrier tower.","The carrier routes the data through its core network to its international gateway.","The data crosses a submarine cable to reach the wider Internet.","WhatsApp's servers (cloud infrastructure) receive and forward the message.","A Jamaican carrier delivers the data to the client's Android phone.","The client (user) plays the voice note and replies."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"The cable cut (step 1)","q":"<p>You are advising the Island Connect Nassau app, used by visitors to find restaurants, tours and taxis. One morning, a fault on an international link slows Internet across the island. Make the best decisions.</p><p>Users complain the app is not loading. The app's data is hosted on servers in the United States. What do you do first?</p>","options":["Find out which part of the ecosystem failed by checking with the carriers and the hosting provider.","Rewrite the app immediately; it must be badly coded."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. You identify the weak link before reacting, because the cause could be the network, the server or the app itself.","The app worked yesterday. Blaming the content ignores the infrastructure the app depends on."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The cable cut (step 2)","q":"<p>The carriers confirm reduced international capacity. Local traffic still works. How can the app keep serving visitors?</p>","options":["Add an offline mode with cached listings and keep a copy of key data on a local or regional server.","Tell visitors to wait until the cable is fixed.","Switch to sending HD promotional videos instead."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong. Caching and local hosting reduce dependence on a single international link.","Visitors cannot wait; restaurants and taxis lose business. Resilience is part of good design.","Heavy content makes things worse on a congested link."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The cable cut (step 3)","q":"<p>After the outage, the government asks how to reduce the risk in future. What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Support more cable routes and backup links, local data hosting, and clear rules from the regulator on outage reporting.","Nothing. Outages are just bad luck."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. You involved infrastructure, policy and the regulator: the whole enabling environment.","Policy and investment can reduce risk. Ignoring the enabling environment leaves the island exposed."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the challenges","q":"<p>Read Keisha's notes from a meeting with a Grenadian startup that wants to launch a farm-produce app.</p><p>Select every line that describes a CONNECTIVITY or INFRASTRUCTURE CHALLENGE in the Caribbean ecosystem.</p>","options":["Coverage is weak in the hilly farming areas of St. Andrew.","Many young people in Grenada are keen to learn app design.","Farmers already use WhatsApp groups to sell produce.","Most international traffic depends on a small number of submarine cables.","Visitors to the island want to buy local spices and nutmeg.","The app will be hosted on a server in the United States, adding delay.","After Hurricane Beryl, towers on Carriacou were damaged and service was lost for days.","Mobile data is expensive compared with larger markets."],"correct":[0, 3, 5, 6, 7],"why":"Well done. You separated connectivity challenges (coverage gaps, hazard damage, overseas hosting, data cost, cable dependence) from opportunities."}]},{"id":"assess_3ue","title":"Module 3 · Unit 2 quiz: Mobile operating systems, platforms and devices","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 2: Mobile operating systems, platforms and devices. 20 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Android, iOS or HarmonyOS? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is briefing new interns. Sort each statement to the operating system it describes.</p><p>Which operating system does this describe?</p><p><b>Built on the open-source AOSP and used by Samsung, Motorola and TCL.</b></p>","options":["iOS","Android","HarmonyOS"],"correct":1,"why":"Many manufacturers use Android's open-source core.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Android, iOS or HarmonyOS? (2)","q":"<p>Which operating system does this describe?</p><p><b>Runs only on iPhone, with Apple controlling hardware and software.</b></p>","options":["iOS","HarmonyOS","Android"],"correct":0,"why":"This closed, single-maker ecosystem is iOS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Android, iOS or HarmonyOS? (3)","q":"<p>Which operating system does this describe?</p><p><b>Developed by Huawei after it lost access to Google services.</b></p>","options":["HarmonyOS","iOS","Android"],"correct":0,"why":"HarmonyOS was Huawei's response to trade restrictions from 2019.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Android, iOS or HarmonyOS? (4)","q":"<p>Which operating system does this describe?</p><p><b>Developers struggle to test on thousands of different models and OS versions.</b></p>","options":["HarmonyOS","Android","iOS"],"correct":1,"why":"That is fragmentation, the classic Android challenge.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Android, iOS or HarmonyOS? (5)","q":"<p>Which operating system does this describe?</p><p><b>Apps must follow the Human Interface Guidelines and pass a strict review.</b></p>","options":["HarmonyOS","Android","iOS"],"correct":2,"why":"The HIG and App Review express Apple's design and security philosophy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Android, iOS or HarmonyOS? (6)","q":"<p>Which operating system does this describe?</p><p><b>Designed to share tasks across phones, watches, TVs and cars, with apps from AppGallery.</b></p>","options":["HarmonyOS","Android","iOS"],"correct":0,"why":"Distributed, multi-device design and AppGallery belong to HarmonyOS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Android, iOS or HarmonyOS? (7)","q":"<p>Which operating system does this describe?</p><p><b>Apps are distributed mainly through Google Play.</b></p>","options":["iOS","Android","HarmonyOS"],"correct":1,"why":"Google Play is Android's main store, though others exist.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Android, iOS or HarmonyOS? (8)","q":"<p>Which operating system does this describe?</p><p><b>Most devices receive updates at the same time, so fragmentation is low.</b></p>","options":["iOS","Android","HarmonyOS"],"correct":0,"why":"Apple pushes updates to most iPhones quickly.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the platform tool","q":"<p>Maya, the web developer at Island Pulse Media, is moving into mobile. Match each tool or term to its description.</p>","left":["Android Studio","Xcode","Kotlin","Swift","TestFlight","Sideloading","MIT App Inventor"],"right":["Installing an app from outside the official store","Official IDE for building Android apps, runs on Windows, macOS and Linux","Google's preferred language for Android apps","Apple's IDE, needed on a Mac to build and submit iOS apps","Apple service for sending beta versions to testers","Free, block-based tool for building simple Android apps","Apple's preferred language for iOS apps"],"correct":[1,3,2,6,4,0,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which device fits? (1)","q":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has five client briefs. Choose the most suitable device for each.</p><p>A Carnival road-march timer that shows only the next band and a countdown on the wrist.</p>","options":["Smart TV","Smart speaker","Smartwatch"],"correct":2,"why":"Glanceable, wrist-based information suits a smartwatch.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which device fits? (2)","q":"<p>A hands-free recipe guide for pommecythere liqueur while the user's hands are busy.</p>","options":["Smart speaker","Tablet stand with touch only","Fitness band"],"correct":0,"why":"Voice control lets the cook follow steps without touching a screen.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which device fits? (3)","q":"<p>A digital menu customers browse at the table in a San Fernando restaurant.</p>","options":["Tablet","Smart speaker","Smartwatch"],"correct":0,"why":"A tablet's larger screen shows photos and prices clearly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which device fits? (4)","q":"<p>A step-count challenge for a school health campaign.</p>","options":["Smart TV","Fitness band or smartwatch","Smart speaker"],"correct":1,"why":"Wearables with motion sensors count steps automatically.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which device fits? (5)","q":"<p>A tour app with maps and GPS for visitors walking around Port of Spain.</p>","options":["Smartphone","Smart TV","Smart speaker"],"correct":0,"why":"Smartphones are portable, have GPS and are what visitors already carry.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"How an IoT farm alert works","q":"<p>Blue Mountain Greens farm in Jamaica uses IoT to protect its crops. Put the steps of the system in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["A soil-moisture sensor in the field measures the water level in the soil.","An embedded microcontroller reads the sensor value every 15 minutes.","The device sends the reading over the mobile network to a cloud server.","Software on the server compares the reading with the safe level.","The farmer's mobile app shows a \"Soil too dry\" alert.","The farmer taps \"Water now\" and an actuator opens the irrigation valve."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a build approach","q":"<p>St. Lucia Jazz Pass startup has one developer who knows JavaScript, a small budget and only eight weeks before the festival. About half its audience uses iPhones and half uses Android. Which approach is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Build two separate native apps in Kotlin and Swift.","Build one app with React Native and publish to both stores.","Build an iOS-only app in Xcode.","Build an Android-only app in Android Studio."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Native gives top performance, but two codebases are too slow and costly for one developer in eight weeks.","Correct. React Native uses JavaScript, which the developer knows, and one codebase reaches both audiences within the budget and time.","This excludes Android users and needs Swift, which the developer does not know.","This would leave out about half the audience who use iPhones."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform decision for a ferry app (step 1)","q":"<p>You are advising Bay Hopper Ferries, which runs boats from Chaguanas and Port of Spain to Grenada and Venezuela. It wants an app for timetables, tickets and delay alerts. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The owner says, \"Everyone has an iPhone, so just build for iOS.\" What do you do first?</p>","options":["Agree and start an iOS-only app in Xcode.","Check the audience: survey passengers and look at web analytics and StatCounter data for the island."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["An assumption is not evidence. Local workers and many visitors may use Android.","Good. Decisions about platforms should rest on evidence about the target audience."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform decision for a ferry app (step 2)","q":"<p>Data shows a mix: many visitors use iPhones, while most local commuters use Android. The budget is modest and the app must be ready before the tourist season in eight weeks. Which build approach?</p>","options":["Two native apps built by two separate teams.","A cross-platform app (for example Flutter) for both stores, plus a simple web timetable."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This doubles cost and is unlikely to be ready in eight weeks.","Strong. One codebase reaches both groups within the budget and time."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Platform decision for a ferry app (step 3)","q":"<p>Passengers want delay alerts even when their phone has weak signal at sea. What feature do you prioritise?</p>","options":["A live HD camera stream from the boat.","Push notifications plus an offline timetable and saved tickets with QR codes."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This needs strong, constant connectivity and lots of data, which passengers will not have at sea.","Excellent. Offline tickets and push alerts work with the realities of connectivity at sea."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the accurate statements","q":"<p>An intern at the Island Pulse Media wrote these notes for a Paper 01 revision session. Some are wrong.</p><p>Select every statement that is ACCURATE.</p>","options":["Google Play charges developers a yearly fee of about US$99.","Fragmentation means many device models and OS versions are in use at once.","An embedded system is a small dedicated computer built into a device.","iOS apps can be built and submitted using Android Studio on Windows.","Cross-platform tools such as Flutter produce apps for both Android and iOS from one codebase.","A smart speaker is mainly controlled by touch.","HarmonyOS was developed by Huawei.","Android's core is open source and is used by many device manufacturers."],"correct":[1, 2, 4, 6, 7],"why":"Well done. You can spot accurate facts about operating systems, embedded systems and cross-platform tools, and avoid common traps."}]},{"id":"assess_3xi","title":"Module 3 · Unit 3 quiz: Caribbean context vs the international mobile ecosystem","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 3: Caribbean context vs the international mobile ecosystem. 20 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the statistics (1)","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is preparing an infographic comparing Caribbean and global mobile use. Help her interpret the data correctly.</p><p>Antigua and Barbuda shows more than 100 mobile subscriptions per 100 people. The BEST explanation is...</p>","options":["every resident owns exactly one phone","the data must be wrong","many people use two SIMs and visitors buy local SIMs"],"correct":2,"why":"Subscriptions count SIM cards, not people. Dual SIMs and tourists push the figure above 100.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the statistics (2)","q":"<p>Which organisation is the UN agency that publishes global ICT statistics?</p>","options":["GSMA","ITU","StatCounter"],"correct":1,"why":"The ITU is the United Nations agency for information and communication technologies.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the statistics (3)","q":"<p>Keisha wants remittances as a share of GDP for Jamaica. Which source fits BEST?</p>","options":["Google Play","World Bank development indicators","a social media poll"],"correct":1,"why":"The World Bank publishes remittance data by country and year.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the statistics (4)","q":"<p>Many people live within mobile broadband coverage but do not use the Internet. GSMA calls this...</p>","options":["the usage gap","the coverage gap","fragmentation"],"correct":0,"why":"The usage gap is people who are covered but not connected, often because of cost, skills or lack of relevant content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the statistics (5)","q":"<p>Which statement is written in the BEST exam style?</p>","options":["According to ITU (2025), roughly three quarters of people worldwide use the Internet.","Loads of people use phones now.","74.638% of everyone is online."],"correct":0,"why":"It names the source and year and uses an approximate, defensible figure.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the mobile service to its impact","q":"<p>Match each Caribbean mobile service to the impact it best shows.</p>","left":["Paying an electricity bill in a banking app","A digital wallet for people without a bank account","Sending money home from New York through an app","Video consultation with a specialist in Curaçao","Renewing a driving licence online","SMS reminders for clinic appointments","The Bahamas Sand Dollar"],"right":["Faster, cheaper remittances for families in Jamaica","Financial inclusion for the unbanked","E-government makes public services easier to reach","Fewer missed appointments and better health outcomes","Saves time and travel to a payment office in Castries","A central bank digital currency used through mobile wallets","Telemedicine reduces the need to travel for care"],"correct":[4,1,0,6,2,3,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which sector? (1)","q":"<p>Malik is filming a short documentary for the Island Pulse Media about mobile technology across the Caribbean. Sort each clip into the sector it shows.</p><p>Which sector does this mobile application serve?</p><p><b>A Dominica farmer receives an SMS warning of heavy rain before harvesting.</b></p>","options":["Tourism","Disaster management","Agriculture","Education"],"correct":2,"why":"Weather alerts to protect crops support agriculture.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which sector? (2)","q":"<p>Which sector does this mobile application serve?</p><p><b>A Breadfruit Valley Secondary student watches a CXC revision video on her phone on the bus.</b></p>","options":["Education","Agriculture","Tourism","Disaster management"],"correct":0,"why":"Mobile learning supports education.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which sector? (3)","q":"<p>Which sector does this mobile application serve?</p><p><b>A cruise passenger books a catamaran tour to the Tobago Cays by app.</b></p>","options":["Agriculture","Education","Disaster management","Tourism"],"correct":3,"why":"Booking visitor experiences is tourism.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which sector? (4)","q":"<p>Which sector does this mobile application serve?</p><p><b>Residents of the Turks and Caicos Islands check a hurricane tracker and shelter list.</b></p>","options":["Disaster management","Tourism","Agriculture","Education"],"correct":0,"why":"Preparing for storms is disaster management.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which sector? (5)","q":"<p>Which sector does this mobile application serve?</p><p><b>A Jamaican yam farmer posts produce in a WhatsApp group to find buyers.</b></p>","options":["Tourism","Agriculture","Education","Disaster management"],"correct":1,"why":"Selling produce by mobile supports agriculture.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which sector? (6)","q":"<p>Which sector does this mobile application serve?</p><p><b>A teacher posts homework in Google Classroom for students without laptops.</b></p>","options":["Education","Agriculture","Disaster management","Tourism"],"correct":0,"why":"Using mobile platforms for coursework is education.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which sector? (7)","q":"<p>Which sector does this mobile application serve?</p><p><b>A restaurant in Soufrière, St. Lucia, uses a QR-code menu in English and French.</b></p>","options":["Disaster management","Agriculture","Education","Tourism"],"correct":3,"why":"Serving visitors in their language supports tourism.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which sector? (8)","q":"<p>Which sector does this mobile application serve?</p><p><b>After a storm, a family abroad uses a social media group to locate relatives.</b></p>","options":["Disaster management","Tourism","Education","Agriculture"],"correct":0,"why":"Finding people after a hurricane is part of disaster response and recovery.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Explaining the imbalance","q":"<p>A report states that most of the apps used daily in Guyana are produced outside the Caribbean. Which statement BEST explains an ECONOMIC effect of this imbalance?</p>","options":["Young people may hear fewer local accents and stories.","Phones in Guyana have smaller screens.","Spending on subscriptions, in-app purchases and advertising flows mainly to foreign companies rather than local businesses.","The ITU publishes statistics every year."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["This is a real effect, but it is cultural and social, not economic.","Screen size has nothing to do with where content is produced.","Correct. This is an economic effect: money leaves the local economy instead of creating local jobs and income.","This is a fact about a source, not an effect of the imbalance."]},{"t":"match","title":"Rebuilding after the storm","q":"<p>Using lessons from Hurricane Irma, put these stages of restoring mobile service after a major hurricane in a sensible order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Before the season: carriers install backup generators at key towers and agree an emergency plan with government.","During the storm: towers and power lines are damaged and most mobile service is lost.","Immediately after: crews assess damage and restore power to the core network and main towers.","Temporary coverage is provided with portable cell sites and satellite links where cables or towers are down.","Residents use restored service to contact family, and agencies send updates by SMS and social media.","Longer term: towers are rebuilt stronger and backup cable routes and offline-capable apps are planned."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Launching a disaster-alert app (step 1)","q":"<p>You are advising the Antigua and Barbuda Ready app, which will send hurricane alerts and help residents check in as safe. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The developer wants to collect every user's contacts, live location at all times and photos of their ID. What do you advise?</p>","options":["Collect everything; it might be useful later.","Collect only what is needed, ask for clear consent and follow Antigua and Barbuda's data protection law."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Collecting unnecessary personal data breaks privacy principles and creates a security risk.","Good. Data minimisation and consent respect privacy law and build trust."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Launching a disaster-alert app (step 2)","q":"<p>Testing shows that after a storm, networks may be down for hours or days. How should the app handle this?</p>","options":["Rely only on a live online map that needs constant data.","Store shelter lists, emergency numbers and checklists offline, and send short SMS alerts as well as push notifications."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["When networks fail, an online-only app becomes useless exactly when people need it.","Strong. Offline content and SMS work when data networks are weak."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Launching a disaster-alert app (step 3)","q":"<p>During a storm, a fake message spreads on WhatsApp claiming a shelter has closed. What should the app do?</p>","options":["Ignore it; rumours are not the app's problem.","Post verified updates from official sources, clearly marked, and include a \"check before you share\" message."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["False information in a disaster can put lives at risk. Trusted apps must help correct it.","Excellent. You tackled misinformation ethically and kept users safe."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Caribbean or global?","q":"<p>Zara is checking a draft essay by an intern. She wants to highlight sentences that describe features that are MORE typical of the Caribbean context than of large global markets.</p><p>Select every sentence that describes a feature MORE typical of the Caribbean mobile ecosystem.</p>","options":["Huge domestic markets make it easy to fund nationwide 5G quickly.","Remittances sent by mobile from relatives abroad are a large share of some economies.","Most of the content people consume is produced outside the region.","Many international links depend on a small number of submarine cables.","Dozens of competing cable routes connect the country to the Internet.","Large domestic app industries produce most of the apps their citizens use.","Visitors and dual-SIM users push subscriptions above 100 per 100 people.","Hurricanes regularly damage towers and power, testing network resilience."],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 6, 7],"why":"Well done. You identified features that set the Caribbean apart: remittances, cable dependence, hurricanes, tourism and dual-SIM use, and the consumption imbalance."}]},{"id":"assess_40o","title":"Module 3 · Unit 4 quiz: Benefits of developing local mobile applications","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 4: Benefits of developing local mobile applications. 21 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Which local need does this app meet? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome, the mobile developer at the Island Pulse Media, has a whiteboard full of app ideas from clients. Sort each idea by the main local need it addresses.</p><p>Which local need does this app idea mainly address?</p><p><b>Live minibus locations between Castries and Gros Islet.</b></p>","options":["Agriculture and small business","Transport and tourism","Culture, heritage and education","Health and safety"],"correct":1,"why":"Helping people move around the island is a transport need.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which local need does this app meet? (2)","q":"<p>Which local need does this app idea mainly address?</p><p><b>A walking tour of Basseterre's Circus and Independence Square for cruise passengers with a map of shops.</b></p>","options":["Culture, heritage and education","Health and safety","Transport and tourism","Agriculture and small business"],"correct":2,"why":"Guiding visitors around attractions meets a tourism need.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which local need does this app meet? (3)","q":"<p>Which local need does this app idea mainly address?</p><p><b>Push alerts when a hurricane watch is issued, with a list of shelters.</b></p>","options":["Transport and tourism","Agriculture and small business","Health and safety","Culture, heritage and education"],"correct":2,"why":"Warning people and guiding them to safety is a safety and disaster preparedness need.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which local need does this app meet? (4)","q":"<p>Which local need does this app idea mainly address?</p><p><b>Reminders for a child's vaccinations at the local clinic.</b></p>","options":["Agriculture and small business","Culture, heritage and education","Health and safety","Transport and tourism"],"correct":2,"why":"Vaccination reminders support community health.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which local need does this app meet? (5)","q":"<p>Which local need does this app idea mainly address?</p><p><b>A Dominica farmer checks banana prices and pest alerts before harvest.</b></p>","options":["Culture, heritage and education","Agriculture and small business","Health and safety","Transport and tourism"],"correct":1,"why":"Market prices and pest alerts help farmers earn more: an agriculture need.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which local need does this app meet? (6)","q":"<p>Which local need does this app idea mainly address?</p><p><b>A WhatsApp-style ordering page for a nutmeg-products shop in Grenada.</b></p>","options":["Health and safety","Agriculture and small business","Culture, heritage and education","Transport and tourism"],"correct":1,"why":"Helping a small business take orders online meets a small business need.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which local need does this app meet? (7)","q":"<p>Which local need does this app idea mainly address?</p><p><b>Recorded stories from elders about Emancipation Day traditions.</b></p>","options":["Transport and tourism","Health and safety","Agriculture and small business","Culture, heritage and education"],"correct":3,"why":"Recording oral history preserves heritage and culture for future generations.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which local need does this app meet? (8)","q":"<p>Which local need does this app idea mainly address?</p><p><b>A game that teaches Papiamentu words to children in Curaçao.</b></p>","options":["Transport and tourism","Agriculture and small business","Health and safety","Culture, heritage and education"],"correct":3,"why":"A language learning game supports education and cultural identity.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Universal need, local twist","q":"<p>Match each universal app type to the local feature that would make it more useful in Trinidad and Tobago or the wider Caribbean.</p>","left":["Bus schedule app","Restaurant review app","Hotel ranking app","Popular destinations app","Event listings app","Weather app"],"right":["Hurricane watch and warning stages explained in plain language and several languages","Rider reports of where the San Fernando maxi taxi is right now","A weekly sargassum update for each beach","Photos, prices and opening hours for the doubles vendors and food trucks","Small family guesthouses in Tobago with notes on generators and shutters","Carnival road closures, J'ouvert routes and band schedules"],"correct":[1,3,4,2,5,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Social and cultural benefits quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Keisha, the marketing lead at Island Pulse Media, is preparing a pitch for a heritage app. Help her choose the best answers.</p><p>An app records elders in Sauteurs telling stories about traditional life. The main benefit is...</p>","options":["cultural preservation","export potential","lower data use"],"correct":0,"why":"Recording stories protects heritage and passes it on: cultural preservation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Social and cultural benefits quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>A Grenadian living in New York uses a local app to follow island news and order a gift from a St. George's shop. This shows...</p>","options":["a touch target","beta testing","a diaspora connection"],"correct":2,"why":"Mobile apps keep the diaspora linked to home, socially and economically.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Social and cultural benefits quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>A local news app sends an alert about a power outage in Grenville. This is mainly a...</p>","options":["news and information linkage","revenue model","cultural preservation linkage"],"correct":0,"why":"Timely local news keeps the community informed, a social benefit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Social and cultural benefits quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>Which is a SOCIAL benefit rather than an economic benefit?</p>","options":["The app is sold to clients in Belize","The developer hires two staff","Young people feel pride seeing their culture in an app"],"correct":2,"why":"Pride and identity are social benefits. Jobs and sales are economic benefits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Social and cultural benefits quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>Before publishing recordings of a Carnival band's music, the developer must...</p>","options":["compress the files to MP3 only","get permission and credit the band","publish them first and ask later"],"correct":1,"why":"Cultural content still belongs to its creators. Permission and credit are ethical and legal duties.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Social and cultural benefits quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>An audio tour plays the history of Fort George when the user arrives there. This links users to...</p>","options":["entertainment only","agriculture","history"],"correct":2,"why":"Location-based stories connect users to local history at the place it happened.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Identifying an economic benefit","q":"<p>St. Kitts start-up NevisCode builds a hurricane-preparedness app for its own island. Two years later it licenses the same app, rebranded, to disaster offices in Dominica and Grenada. Which economic benefit does this BEST illustrate?</p>","options":["Local job creation only","Export potential","Cultural preservation","Offline-first design"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["The business may hire staff, but the key event in the scenario is licensing the product to other countries.","Correct. Selling or licensing the app to other countries is a digital export that earns income from outside St. Kitts.","Cultural preservation is a social and cultural benefit about protecting heritage. The scenario is about selling the app abroad.","Offline-first is a user experience design strategy (Unit 3.5), not an economic benefit."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the economic benefits","q":"<p>Jerome drafted this pitch for the LoloGo app. Some lines describe economic benefits; others describe social benefits or design features.</p><p>Select every line that describes an ECONOMIC benefit.</p>","options":["Buttons will be at least 48 dp tall for easy tapping.","Lolo owners can take more orders on busy cruise ship days.","Menus will be available in English, Spanish and Dutch.","The app could be licensed to food stalls in Grenada and Antigua.","Elders share recipes for johnny cakes in short videos.","The app will create part-time delivery jobs for young people.","Payments stay with local businesses instead of overseas platforms.","Customers will see photos of traditional dishes and their history."],"correct":[1, 3, 5, 6],"why":"Correct. Sales, jobs, export licensing and keeping money in the local economy are economic benefits."},{"t":"mc","title":"Designing a storm-ready app (step 1)","q":"<p>The Turks and Caicos charity Rebuild Together has asked Island Pulse Media to plan a disaster preparedness app. You are advising Jerome. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The charity wants the app to load all its content live from the Internet so information is always fresh. What do you advise first?</p>","options":["Agree: live content is always best, and everyone has Internet.","Store the essential content (checklists, shelters, emergency numbers) on the phone so it works offline, and update it when there is a connection."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["After Irma, many people had no power or signal for days. A live-only app fails exactly when it is needed.","Good. After a hurricane, networks may be down. Essential content must work offline."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Designing a storm-ready app (step 2)","q":"<p>Good. Next, who should the content be written for?</p>","options":["Residents and visitors in English, Spanish, Dutch and French, in plain language, with official information from the Office of Disaster Management.","English only, using technical meteorological terms to sound professional."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Multiple languages and official sources make the app inclusive and accurate.","Many households speak other languages at home, and technical terms confuse people in a crisis."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Designing a storm-ready app (step 3)","q":"<p>The charity asks how to keep the app running for years, since it has only one grant. What do you suggest?</p>","options":["Spend the whole grant on launch and worry about updates later.","Plan sustainability: partner with the government and telecoms providers, seek sponsorship and add a donation feature for the diaspora."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["An outdated shelter list is dangerous. Social impact apps need a plan for long-term maintenance.","Strong. Sustainability planning means the app survives beyond the first grant."]},{"t":"match","title":"Build a BASE paragraph","q":"<p>Put the sentences in the best order to make one developed paragraph examining a benefit of the LoloGo app.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["One key benefit of a local food-ordering app is that it increases sales for small businesses.","For example, LoloGo lets customers order ribs and johnny cakes from Simpson Bay lolos before they arrive.","This helps lolo owners because they can serve more customers on busy cruise ship days without long queues.","It also keeps money in the local economy instead of paying high commissions to overseas delivery platforms.","However, this benefit depends on vendors keeping their menus updated and on customers trusting the app.","Overall, the app supports local entrepreneurship if it is supported with training for vendors."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]}]},{"id":"assess_43c","title":"Module 3 · Unit 5 quiz: Factors that impact the mobile user experience","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 5: Factors that impact the mobile user experience. 20 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Name the UX factor","q":"<p>Maya, the web developer at Island Pulse Media, collected complaints from testers of a new app. Match each complaint to the UX factor it reveals.</p>","left":["Screen size","Bandwidth","Portability","Processing power","Accessibility","Environment","User profile"],"right":["\"On Reduit Beach I cannot see anything on the screen in the sun.\"","\"My grandmother does not understand the icons, and she prefers Spanish.\"","\"My phone gets hot and the battery drops fast when I use it.\"","\"The text is tiny and I keep tapping the wrong button.\"","\"I was checking it on the bus and had to start again after a call came in.\"","\"My screen reader just says button, button, button.\"","\"The menu photos take forever to load on mobile data in Rodney Bay.\""],"correct":[3,6,4,2,5,0,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Screen and touch decisions (1)","q":"<p>Jerome, the mobile developer at Island Pulse Media, is reviewing the first screen of the LoloGo food-ordering app. Choose the best decision each time.</p><p>The \"Add to order\" button is 28 by 28 dp. Jerome should...</p>","options":["leave it; users can zoom in","make the text inside it smaller","increase it to at least 48 by 48 dp"],"correct":2,"why":"Material Design recommends touch targets of at least 48 by 48 dp so fingers can tap accurately.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Screen and touch decisions (2)","q":"<p>Two buttons, \"Cancel\" and \"Pay\", are touching each other. The risk is...</p>","options":["higher data usage","faster battery drain","accidental taps on the wrong action"],"correct":2,"why":"Targets need spacing so users do not hit the wrong one, especially for costly actions like paying.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Screen and touch decisions (3)","q":"<p>The logo looks blurry on a new high-density phone. The best fix is to...</p>","options":["reduce the screen brightness","use a vector (SVG) logo or higher-resolution versions","use a smaller font"],"correct":1,"why":"Vector graphics, or images supplied at @2x and @3x, stay sharp on high-density screens.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Screen and touch decisions (4)","q":"<p>Where should the main \"Checkout\" button go for comfortable one-handed use?</p>","options":["hidden in a menu","bottom of the screen, in the thumb zone","top left corner"],"correct":1,"why":"The lower half of the screen is easiest for the thumb to reach.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Screen and touch decisions (5)","q":"<p>Users must swipe left on a dish to delete it, but some do not discover it. Jerome should...</p>","options":["also provide a visible delete button","add more swipe gestures","remove the delete feature"],"correct":0,"why":"Hidden gestures need a visible alternative so every user can complete the task.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor for low connectivity? (1)","q":"<p>A Grenada nutmeg cooperative wants an app for farmers in the hills around Grenville, where the signal is weak. Sort each design choice.</p><p>Is this choice good or poor for users with weak connections and limited data?</p><p><b>Market prices are cached so farmers can see the last update offline.</b></p>","options":["Good for low connectivity","Poor for low connectivity"],"correct":0,"why":"Caching lets users see useful data even without a signal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor for low connectivity? (2)","q":"<p>Is this choice good or poor for users with weak connections and limited data?</p><p><b>Every screen autoplays a high-definition video introduction.</b></p>","options":["Good for low connectivity","Poor for low connectivity"],"correct":1,"why":"Autoplay HD video wastes data and loads slowly on weak signals.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor for low connectivity? (3)","q":"<p>Is this choice good or poor for users with weak connections and limited data?</p><p><b>Photos are compressed and resized to the size they display at.</b></p>","options":["Good for low connectivity","Poor for low connectivity"],"correct":0,"why":"Optimised images load faster and use less data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor for low connectivity? (4)","q":"<p>Is this choice good or poor for users with weak connections and limited data?</p><p><b>Forms are saved on the phone and sent when a signal returns.</b></p>","options":["Poor for low connectivity","Good for low connectivity"],"correct":1,"why":"This is offline-first: no work is lost when the connection drops.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor for low connectivity? (5)","q":"<p>Is this choice good or poor for users with weak connections and limited data?</p><p><b>The app shows a blank white screen when offline.</b></p>","options":["Poor for low connectivity","Good for low connectivity"],"correct":0,"why":"Users need clear feedback, not a blank screen, when there is no connection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor for low connectivity? (6)","q":"<p>Is this choice good or poor for users with weak connections and limited data?</p><p><b>A \"Wi-Fi only\" setting for downloading training videos.</b></p>","options":["Poor for low connectivity","Good for low connectivity"],"correct":1,"why":"Letting users control data use protects their prepaid data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor for low connectivity? (7)","q":"<p>Is this choice good or poor for users with weak connections and limited data?</p><p><b>The app downloads all 300 images at once when it opens.</b></p>","options":["Poor for low connectivity","Good for low connectivity"],"correct":0,"why":"Lazy loading would load only what the user needs right now.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor for low connectivity? (8)","q":"<p>Is this choice good or poor for users with weak connections and limited data?</p><p><b>The app is 400 MB because of unused graphics.</b></p>","options":["Good for low connectivity","Poor for low connectivity"],"correct":1,"why":"Large app size is costly to download and fills limited storage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Designing for interruptions","q":"<p>The Bajan bus app RouteMate asks users to fill in a five-step form to buy a ticket. Testers on real buses in Bridgetown often lose everything they typed when a call comes in or they reach their stop. Which design strategy BEST addresses this portability problem?</p>","options":["Require users to stay on the screen until the form is complete.","Increase the resolution of the images on the form.","Save the user's progress automatically so they can resume where they left off, and shorten the form.","Add a long tutorial video before the form."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Apps cannot stop calls or bus stops. Designers must expect interruptions, not forbid them.","Resolution affects how sharp images look. It does not stop users losing their progress.","Correct. Mobile users are often interrupted, so auto-saving and shorter tasks protect their work.","A long video adds time and data use. It makes interruptions more likely, not less harmful."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the accessibility problems","q":"<p>Zara, the creative director at Island Pulse Media, is auditing a draft of the \"Carnival Live TT\" app before launch. Some lines describe accessibility barriers.</p><p>Select every line that describes an ACCESSIBILITY problem.</p>","options":["Each band's photo has a short description for screen readers.","The parade map buttons are images with no text label.","A ticket offer disappears after 5 seconds with no way to pause it.","Light yellow text is placed on a white background.","All videos of the Grand Parade have captions.","The app supports the phone's large text setting.","Users must pinch and rotate with two fingers to open the schedule.","Errors are shown only by turning the field red."],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 6, 7],"why":"Correct. Missing labels, poor contrast, colour-only signals, complex gestures and strict time limits are all accessibility barriers."},{"t":"mc","title":"The beach vendor app (step 1)","q":"<p>The Seven Mile Beach Vendors Association wants an app so visitors can order drinks and snacks from their sunbeds. Jerome asks you to make the key UX decisions.</p><p>Zara's draft uses a stylish light grey text on a white background. The app will be used outdoors in strong sun. What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Switch to high-contrast colours and bold, larger text so the screen is readable in sunlight.","Keep the design; users can turn up their brightness."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Glare makes low-contrast text unreadable outdoors.","Even at full brightness, pale grey on white disappears in Caribbean sun, and full brightness drains the battery."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The beach vendor app (step 2)","q":"<p>Visitors include cruise passengers on roaming plans who avoid using mobile data. What do you do?</p>","options":["Keep the app very light, with a QR code at each sunbed that opens a small menu page, compressed images and the beach bar's free Wi-Fi.","Require a 250 MB app download with video menus."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong. You reduced data use and download size for users with expensive roaming.","A large download on roaming data is costly. Many visitors would not bother."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The beach vendor app (step 3)","q":"<p>Testing shows older visitors and some Spanish-speaking guests struggle with the icons. What is your final change?</p>","options":["Add text labels to icons, larger touch targets, a language switch (English, Spanish, French, Italian) and labels for screen readers.","Remove all text so the design looks cleaner."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. You responded to the user profile and accessibility needs.","Icons alone confuse many users and are unreadable by screen readers."]},{"t":"match","title":"Build a factor, effect, strategy answer","q":"<p>Put the sentences in order to make one well-developed Paper 02 answer about a UX factor for a farmers' app in rural Guyana.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["One factor that affects the user experience is bandwidth and connectivity.","Many farmers in rural Guyana have weak mobile signals and buy small amounts of prepaid data.","As a result, an app that loads large images or needs a constant connection would be slow, costly and often fail.","To address this, the app should use an offline-first design that stores prices and advice on the phone.","It should also compress images and synchronise new data only when a signal is available.","This means farmers can still use the app in the fields without wasting their data."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]}]},{"id":"assess_484","title":"Module 3 · Unit 6 quiz: Tools and techniques to create a mobile application","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 6: Tools and techniques to create a mobile application. 31 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"MVP or version 2? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome, the mobile developer at the Island Pulse Media, has a long wish list for the LoloGo app. He has six weeks to launch. Sort each feature.</p><p>Is this feature a Must have for the MVP, or can it wait for version 2?</p><p><b>Browse each lolo's menu with prices.</b></p>","options":["MVP (Must have)","Version 2 (later)"],"correct":0,"why":"Without a menu, customers cannot order. This is core to the purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MVP or version 2? (2)","q":"<p>Is this feature a Must have for the MVP, or can it wait for version 2?</p><p><b>Place an order for pickup.</b></p>","options":["MVP (Must have)","Version 2 (later)"],"correct":0,"why":"Ordering is the main purpose of the app, so it must be in the MVP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MVP or version 2? (3)","q":"<p>Is this feature a Must have for the MVP, or can it wait for version 2?</p><p><b>A notification when the order is ready.</b></p>","options":["MVP (Must have)","Version 2 (later)"],"correct":0,"why":"This delivers the key benefit: no queuing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MVP or version 2? (4)","q":"<p>Is this feature a Must have for the MVP, or can it wait for version 2?</p><p><b>Loyalty points and badges.</b></p>","options":["MVP (Must have)","Version 2 (later)"],"correct":1,"why":"Nice to have, but the app works without it. Add it once the MVP is proven.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MVP or version 2? (5)","q":"<p>Is this feature a Must have for the MVP, or can it wait for version 2?</p><p><b>Augmented reality view of each dish.</b></p>","options":["Version 2 (later)","MVP (Must have)"],"correct":0,"why":"Impressive but costly, and not needed to order food.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MVP or version 2? (6)","q":"<p>Is this feature a Must have for the MVP, or can it wait for version 2?</p><p><b>Recipe videos from the cooks.</b></p>","options":["MVP (Must have)","Version 2 (later)"],"correct":1,"why":"Adds cultural value but uses data and time. It can come later.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MVP or version 2? (7)","q":"<p>Is this feature a Must have for the MVP, or can it wait for version 2?</p><p><b>Live map tracking of delivery riders.</b></p>","options":["Version 2 (later)","MVP (Must have)"],"correct":0,"why":"The MVP is pickup only. Delivery tracking is a later feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MVP or version 2? (8)","q":"<p>Is this feature a Must have for the MVP, or can it wait for version 2?</p><p><b>Choose which lolo to order from.</b></p>","options":["MVP (Must have)","Version 2 (later)"],"correct":0,"why":"Customers need to pick the vendor before they can order.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"The design journey for LoloGo","q":"<p>Jerome, the mobile developer at Island Pulse Media, is planning the design stage of the LoloGo food-ordering app. Put his steps in the order of the design journey.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Sketch paper screens of Home, Menu and Order, labelled \"Low-Fidelity Wireframe\".","Show the wireframes to three lolo vendors and fix the layout and flow.","Create two mockup variations of the Home screen with real colours, fonts and food photos.","The client chooses one visual direction.","Link the mockups in Figma into a clickable prototype of the ordering flow.","Test the prototype with real users and fix any confusing steps before coding."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Design and prototyping terms","q":"<p>Match each term to its best description.</p>","left":["Low-fidelity wireframe","High-fidelity mockup","User flow","Clickable prototype","Persona","MVP","Annotation"],"right":["Linked screens that respond to taps so users can test the flow","A note on a wireframe explaining what an element does","A detailed static design with real colours, fonts and images","The simplest usable version with only the core features","A but research-based profile of a typical user","A quick sketch of boxes and labels on paper","The path of screens and actions a user takes to complete a task"],"correct":[5,2,6,0,4,3,1]},{"t":"match","title":"Build a Figma clickable prototype","q":"<p>Maya, the web developer at Island Pulse Media, is teaching an intern to turn the LoloGo wireframes into a clickable prototype. Put her steps in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Create phone-sized frames for Home, Menu, Order and Confirmation in the Design tab.","Add the layout to each frame: header, images, text and labelled buttons.","Switch to the Prototype tab.","Drag a connection from the \"Order now\" button to the Menu frame.","Set the interaction to On tap, Navigate to, Smart animate.","Set the Home frame as the flow starting point.","Click Present and tap through the flow to test it."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Native and cross-platform quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Jerome is answering questions from students on a work-experience visit to Island Pulse Media. Choose the best answer.</p><p>Which IDE is the official tool for building native Android apps?</p>","options":["Balsamiq","Android Studio","Xcode"],"correct":1,"why":"Android Studio is Google's official IDE for Android, with the Android SDK and emulator built in.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Native and cross-platform quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>Google's preferred language for new Android apps is...</p>","options":["HTML","Swift","Kotlin"],"correct":2,"why":"Kotlin is preferred; Java is still supported. Swift is for Apple platforms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Native and cross-platform quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>A student has only a Windows laptop. Which tool can she NOT run directly?</p>","options":["MIT App Inventor","Xcode","Android Studio"],"correct":1,"why":"Xcode runs only on macOS. Android Studio runs on Windows; App Inventor runs in a browser.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Native and cross-platform quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>A set of tools, libraries, documentation and emulators for building apps for a platform is called...</p>","options":["an SDK","a persona","a wireframe"],"correct":0,"why":"SDK stands for software development kit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Native and cross-platform quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>A small team wants ONE codebase for both Android and iOS. They should consider...</p>","options":["two separate native teams","the BlackBerry SDK","a cross-platform tool such as Flutter or React Native"],"correct":2,"why":"Cross-platform tools share most code across both platforms, saving time and cost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Native and cross-platform quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Which language is used with SwiftUI in Xcode?</p>","options":["Kotlin","Swift","Dart"],"correct":1,"why":"SwiftUI is Apple's interface framework, written in Swift. Dart is used by Flutter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right builder","q":"<p>Grenada Spice Farmers Cooperative keeps a Google Sheet listing 60 farms, their spices, contact numbers and opening days. The manager has no coding skills and a very small budget, and wants a simple app that buyers can search within a week. Which tool is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Android Studio with Kotlin, because Kotlin is Google's preferred language.","Xcode with Swift, because native apps perform best.","Glide, because it builds an app directly from a spreadsheet with no coding.","Balsamiq, because it is quick and easy."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Kotlin is powerful but requires programming skills the manager does not have, and would take far longer than a week.","Native development needs coding skills, a Mac and much more time. It is excessive for a searchable list.","Correct. The data already exists in a sheet, the manager cannot code and time is short: Glide fits all three.","Balsamiq makes wireframes and simple mock prototypes, not a working app connected to live data."]},{"t":"match","title":"App Inventor parts and jobs","q":"<p>Match each MIT App Inventor part to what it does.</p>","left":["Designer","Blocks editor","Palette","Properties panel","AI Companion","HorizontalArrangement","TinyDB",".aia file"],"right":["The view where you lay out the screen and its components","The exported project source file used for backup or submission","The view where you build the app's logic from snap-together blocks","The list of components you can drag onto the screen","A layout component that places items side by side","A phone app that shows your project live by scanning a QR code","Where you change a component's text, size, colour and font","A non-visible component that saves data on the phone"],"correct":[0,2,3,6,5,4,7,1]},{"t":"ma","title":"Debug the blocks","q":"<p>A Form 6 student built the Johnny Cake Counter, but testers reported problems. Here are some of her blocks and settings.</p><p>Select every line that contains a BUG or a design error.</p>","options":["btnAdd Width and Height set to 20 pixels","Components named Button1, Button2, Label1 and Label2","when btnMinus.Click: set global count to (get global count - 1), with no if block","set lblTotal.Text to join \"Total: US$\" , (get global count × 2)","when btnSpeak.Click: call TextToSpeech1.Speak message lblTotal.Text","when btnAdd.Click: set global count to (get global count - 1)","initialize global count to 0","when btnReset.Click: set global count to 0 but updateDisplay is never called"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 5, 7],"why":"Correct. You found a logic error (subtracting on Add), missing validation, a tiny touch target, a missing display update and poor naming."},{"t":"mc","title":"Getting LoloGo ready to launch (step 1)","q":"<p>The LoloGo prototype is built. Jerome must decide how to test it before Carnival, the busiest season for the lolos. Advise him.</p><p>Jerome has tested LoloGo only on his own new phone over office Wi-Fi. Everything works. What should he do next?</p>","options":["Test on several phones, including an older budget Android, on mobile data and in sunlight, and log every bug.","Publish it to Google Play immediately; it works on his phone."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Real users have different devices and conditions. Testing only on the developer's phone hides problems.","One new phone on fast Wi-Fi is not representative of real users."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Getting LoloGo ready to launch (step 2)","q":"<p>The device tests are fixed. Now, how should he check that LoloGo meets the lolo owners' needs?</p>","options":["Run user acceptance testing: the owners use the app against their agreed requirements and sign off, with usability sessions with a few customers.","Ask a fellow developer to read the code instead."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. UAT confirms the app does what the client agreed, and usability testing shows how real customers cope.","A code review is useful, but it cannot tell you whether the client's needs are met or whether customers find it easy."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Getting LoloGo ready to launch (step 3)","q":"<p>The owners approve. There are three weeks to Carnival. What is the best final step?</p>","options":["Add five new features, such as loyalty points and AR dishes, without further testing.","Run a beta through Google Play internal or closed testing (and TestFlight for iPhone users) with about 30 regular customers, collect feedback, fix the top issues, then launch."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["New, untested features just before the busiest period add risk. Stabilise the MVP first.","Strong. A beta finds real-world problems before the public sees them, and the feedback guides the final refinement."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage or test is this? (1)","q":"<p>The Dominica Trails app team kept a diary of their work. Sort each entry by the stage of development it belongs to.</p><p>Which stage does this diary entry describe?</p><p><b>We wrote a persona: Andre, 35, hiker from Roseau, uses an older Android phone.</b></p>","options":["Wireframing and prototyping","Building","Conceptualisation","Testing and refinement"],"correct":2,"why":"Personas describe target users, part of conceptualisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage or test is this? (2)","q":"<p>Which stage does this diary entry describe?</p><p><b>We agreed the MVP: trail list, offline maps, emergency contacts.</b></p>","options":["Building","Wireframing and prototyping","Conceptualisation","Testing and refinement"],"correct":2,"why":"Defining core features and the MVP is conceptualisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage or test is this? (3)","q":"<p>Which stage does this diary entry describe?</p><p><b>We sketched five screens on paper with annotations.</b></p>","options":["Conceptualisation","Testing and refinement","Building","Wireframing and prototyping"],"correct":3,"why":"Paper sketches are low-fidelity wireframes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage or test is this? (4)","q":"<p>Which stage does this diary entry describe?</p><p><b>We linked the Figma frames so testers could tap through.</b></p>","options":["Wireframing and prototyping","Testing and refinement","Building","Conceptualisation"],"correct":0,"why":"Linking frames creates a clickable prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage or test is this? (5)","q":"<p>Which stage does this diary entry describe?</p><p><b>We dragged a ListView and a Map component into the App Inventor Designer.</b></p>","options":["Conceptualisation","Building","Wireframing and prototyping","Testing and refinement"],"correct":1,"why":"Adding components in App Inventor is building the app.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage or test is this? (6)","q":"<p>Which stage does this diary entry describe?</p><p><b>We wrote Kotlin code in Android Studio for the trail list.</b></p>","options":["Testing and refinement","Building","Conceptualisation","Wireframing and prototyping"],"correct":1,"why":"Writing code in an IDE is building.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage or test is this? (7)","q":"<p>Which stage does this diary entry describe?</p><p><b>The tourism office checked the app against the brief and signed off.</b></p>","options":["Building","Testing and refinement","Conceptualisation","Wireframing and prototyping"],"correct":1,"why":"The client confirming requirements is user acceptance testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage or test is this? (8)","q":"<p>Which stage does this diary entry describe?</p><p><b>Thirty hikers tried a beta and we fixed the top three issues.</b></p>","options":["Wireframing and prototyping","Testing and refinement","Building","Conceptualisation"],"correct":1,"why":"Beta testing followed by fixes is testing and refinement.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_4bi","title":"Module 3 · Unit 7 quiz: Marketing and distributing mobile applications","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 7: Marketing and distributing mobile applications. 22 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Which distribution model? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is advising four clients across the region. Sort each situation into the distribution model it describes.</p><p>Which distribution model is being used?</p><p><b>A Grenada spice tour app is approved by Apple and appears in App Store search results.</b></p>","options":["Enterprise","Web-based / PWA","Direct download","App store"],"correct":3,"why":"Apple reviewed and listed it, so it is distributed through an official app store.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which distribution model? (2)","q":"<p>Which distribution model is being used?</p><p><b>A Barbados hotel emails its housekeeping staff a private app that only company phones can install.</b></p>","options":["Enterprise","Web-based / PWA","App store","Direct download"],"correct":0,"why":"A private app for internal staff, managed by the organisation, is enterprise distribution.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which distribution model? (3)","q":"<p>Which distribution model is being used?</p><p><b>A festival in Trinidad tells visitors to scan a QR code and tap \"Add to Home Screen\".</b></p>","options":["Enterprise","App store","Web-based / PWA","Direct download"],"correct":2,"why":"Adding a site to the home screen from the browser is how a progressive web app is installed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which distribution model? (4)","q":"<p>Which distribution model is being used?</p><p><b>A developer posts an APK file on a website and tells users to allow \"install unknown apps\".</b></p>","options":["Web-based / PWA","Enterprise","Direct download","App store"],"correct":2,"why":"Installing an APK from outside the store is direct download, also called sideloading.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which distribution model? (5)","q":"<p>Which distribution model is being used?</p><p><b>A Barbados food delivery app updates itself automatically every week through Google Play.</b></p>","options":["Enterprise","App store","Web-based / PWA","Direct download"],"correct":1,"why":"Automatic updates handled by Google Play are a feature of store distribution.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which distribution model? (6)","q":"<p>Which distribution model is being used?</p><p><b>Electricity-company field technicians use a meter-reading app pushed to their tablets by the IT department.</b></p>","options":["App store","Enterprise","Web-based / PWA","Direct download"],"correct":1,"why":"An app pushed by IT to staff devices through device management is enterprise distribution.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which distribution model? (7)","q":"<p>Which distribution model is being used?</p><p><b>A Dominica news service works in any phone browser and saves articles for offline reading.</b></p>","options":["Direct download","Web-based / PWA","Enterprise","App store"],"correct":1,"why":"Browser-based access with offline saving describes a web app or PWA.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which distribution model? (8)","q":"<p>Which distribution model is being used?</p><p><b>A beta version of a game is shared as an installation file in a WhatsApp group.</b></p>","options":["Enterprise","App store","Web-based / PWA","Direct download"],"correct":3,"why":"Sharing the installation file directly, outside a store, is direct download.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"The sideloading decision","q":"<p>Grand Anse Taxi Co-op wants its new booking app on members' Android phones quickly. A volunteer suggests posting the APK on a Facebook page so passengers can install it straight away. Which is the STRONGEST reason to distribute through Google Play instead?</p>","options":["APK files cannot be installed on Android phones.","Google Play apps are always free, so passengers save money.","Passengers would have to allow \"install unknown apps\", the file would not be reviewed for malware, and security updates would not arrive automatically.","Facebook does not allow links to be posted."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Incorrect. APK is the Android installation format, and it can be sideloaded once the user changes a setting. The issue is risk, not possibility.","Store apps can be free or paid. Price is set by the developer, not by the distribution model.","Correct. This names three specific risks of sideloading: weakened device security, no review and no automatic updates. These all damage user trust in a booking app that handles personal details.","Links can be posted on Facebook. This does not explain why the store is the better choice."]},{"t":"match","title":"Put the user journey in order","q":"<p>Aaliyah, a student in Linden, finds and keeps using a new Caribbean recipe app called Pepperpot Kitchen. Arrange her journey in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Discover: she sees a TikTok of black cake made with the app.","Test: she watches the demo video and opens the free sample recipes.","Review: she reads the 4.6-star rating and several reviews from Guyanese users.","Purchase: she taps \"Get\" on the free app and later buys a Christmas recipe pack.","Download: the light 25 MB app installs quickly on her data plan.","Update: a new version adds a shopping list and fixes a bug.","Retain: weekly \"Sunday lunch\" notifications keep her opening the app."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the weak ASO","q":"<p>The Belize City Eats app is struggling to get downloads. Here is its Google Play listing plan.</p><p>Select every line that is POOR App Store Optimisation.</p>","options":["First screenshot: the login screen with no caption","Icon: a photo of the whole restaurant menu with small text","A rating request pops up the moment the app is opened for the first time","Keywords in the description: delivery, Belize City, Belmopan, rice and beans, fry jacks","The listing has not been updated for two years","Title: \"DQE App Final Version 3\"","Every one-star review receives a polite reply and a fix in the next update","Short description: \"Order from 40 Belize City restaurants and track your delivery live.\""],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 4, 5],"why":"Sharp eye. Weak titles, cluttered icons, dull screenshots, badly timed rating requests and stale listings all reduce ranking and installs."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the marketing channel","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is launching the HurriReady disaster-preparedness app. Match each channel to the tactic she uses.</p>","left":["Influencer partnership","Press release","Search advertising","Email marketing","Community outreach","Content marketing","App review site"],"right":["A launch-day message to 800 people who signed up on the landing page","A popular Barbadian comedian shows how he packs his hurricane kit using the app","A YouTube tutorial: \"5 things to do before a storm\", using the app","A one-page announcement sent to the national newspapers and local radio stations","A demo stall at a community centre in Oistins with QR posters","An ad appears when someone searches \"hurricane checklist app\"","A Caribbean tech blogger tests the app and publishes a rating"],"correct":[1,3,5,0,4,2,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Launch day for Hairouna Trails (step 1)","q":"<p>Jerome and Keisha at the Island Pulse Media are launching the Hairouna Trails app: a guide to hiking trails, heritage sites and food stops across St. Vincent for residents and cruise visitors. You are the launch manager. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The app works, but nobody outside the team has used it. The client wants it in the stores tomorrow. What do you do first?</p>","options":["Launch tomorrow; bugs can be fixed after the reviews come in.","Run a two-week closed beta with 30 local hikers and tour guides, and fix what they report."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Early one-star reviews are hard to recover from and hurt ASO ranking.","Good. A beta finds real-world bugs and creates your first fans and reviewers."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Launch day for Hairouna Trails (step 2)","q":"<p>The beta went well. Now you prepare the store listing. Which listing do you choose?</p>","options":["Title \"GT App\", a long paragraph of history, and the login screen as the first screenshot.","Title \"Hairouna Trails: St. Vincent Hikes\", keywords such as St. Vincent, hiking, beaches, heritage, and captioned screenshots of the trail map first.","Copy the description of a popular international hiking app word for word."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A vague title and dull first screenshot will not rank or convert.","Strong ASO: a clear title with keywords, and the most useful screen first.","Copying is a policy and copyright problem and ignores what makes your app local."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Launch day for Hairouna Trails (step 3)","q":"<p>You have a small budget. The audience is residents plus cruise visitors arriving at the Kingstown cruise berth. How do you promote the launch?</p>","options":["Spend the whole budget on one international TV advert.","QR posters at the cruise terminal and tourist office, a press release to local newspapers and radio, two local hiking influencers, and an email to beta sign-ups."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Expensive, poorly targeted and impossible to measure for a small local app.","Excellent mix: outreach where visitors actually are, earned media, trusted voices and your warm audience."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Launch day for Hairouna Trails (step 4)","q":"<p>One month later, downloads are good but many users stop opening the app after their first hike. What next?</p>","options":["Add new trails monthly, send a friendly weekend notification, prompt for a rating after a completed hike and reply to reviews.","Send five promotional notifications every day."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["This targets retention, update and review: the later stages of the user journey.","Too many notifications annoy users, who then mute or delete the app."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Distribution and marketing quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Maya and Jerome at the Island Pulse Media face these decisions for clients in Belize and Curaçao. Choose the best answer.</p><p>A Belize client wants one product that works on any phone, needs no store approval and updates instantly. Best choice?</p>","options":["enterprise distribution","a progressive web app","an APK on a website"],"correct":1,"why":"A PWA runs in the browser on any device, avoids store review and updates as soon as the site changes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Distribution and marketing quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>Many people see the store listing but few install it. Which metric shows this problem?</p>","options":["cost per install","retention rate","conversion rate"],"correct":2,"why":"Conversion rate compares listing visitors with installs. Low conversion points to weak screenshots, icon or description.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Distribution and marketing quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>Improving an app's title, keywords and screenshots to rank higher in the store is called...</p>","options":["ASO","SEO","PR"],"correct":0,"why":"App Store Optimisation works inside the app stores; SEO is for web search engines.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Distribution and marketing quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>A Curaçao bank wants an app only its staff can install. Which model?</p>","options":["enterprise distribution","open beta on Google Play","pre-registration"],"correct":0,"why":"Enterprise distribution keeps a private app within one organisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Distribution and marketing quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>Users can sign up on Google Play so the app installs automatically on launch day. This is...</p>","options":["interstitial advertising","pre-registration","sideloading"],"correct":1,"why":"Pre-registration builds a launch-day audience before the app is released.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Distribution and marketing quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Which is an example of \"earned\" rather than paid media?</p>","options":["a sponsored Instagram post","a Google search ad","a newspaper article written after a press release"],"correct":2,"why":"Earned media is coverage you did not pay for directly, such as news stories and independent reviews.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_4ef","title":"Module 3 · Unit 8 quiz: Revenue models for mobile applications","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 8: Revenue models for mobile applications. 20 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Paid, freemium or in-app purchase? (1)","q":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing how different apps charge users. Sort each description.</p><p>Which commercial model does this describe?</p><p><b>Users pay US$4.99 before they can download a Carnival costume design app.</b></p>","options":["Freemium","Paid app","In-app purchase"],"correct":1,"why":"Payment before download, with everything unlocked, is a paid (premium) app.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paid, freemium or in-app purchase? (2)","q":"<p>Which commercial model does this describe?</p><p><b>A Caribbean language app is free, but offline lessons and no ads need \"Plus\".</b></p>","options":["Freemium","Paid app","In-app purchase"],"correct":0,"why":"A free core with a paid upgrade tier is the freemium model.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paid, freemium or in-app purchase? (3)","q":"<p>Which commercial model does this describe?</p><p><b>A domino game sells packs of 500 coins that can be bought again and again.</b></p>","options":["Paid app","Freemium","In-app purchase"],"correct":2,"why":"Coins are consumable virtual goods bought inside the app: in-app purchases.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paid, freemium or in-app purchase? (4)","q":"<p>Which commercial model does this describe?</p><p><b>A recipe app sells a one-off \"Christmas in Jamaica\" content pack.</b></p>","options":["Freemium","In-app purchase","Paid app"],"correct":1,"why":"A content pack bought inside the app is a non-consumable in-app purchase.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paid, freemium or in-app purchase? (5)","q":"<p>Which commercial model does this describe?</p><p><b>A photo editor is free with basic filters; professional tools are in a paid version.</b></p>","options":["In-app purchase","Paid app","Freemium"],"correct":2,"why":"Basic features free, advanced features paid: freemium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paid, freemium or in-app purchase? (6)","q":"<p>Which commercial model does this describe?</p><p><b>A sailing navigation app costs US$9.99 in the store and has no ads or upgrades.</b></p>","options":["Freemium","Paid app","In-app purchase"],"correct":1,"why":"One upfront price with no further charges is a paid app.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paid, freemium or in-app purchase? (7)","q":"<p>Which commercial model does this describe?</p><p><b>A cricket game lets players unlock a new stadium for US$1.99.</b></p>","options":["Paid app","In-app purchase","Freemium"],"correct":1,"why":"Unlocking a feature inside the app with a single payment is an in-app purchase.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the model to the example","q":"<p>Match each revenue term to the app example that illustrates it.</p>","left":["Banner ad","Interstitial ad","Rewarded video","Subscription","Consumable IAP","Freemium","Paid app"],"right":["A Barbados fitness app charges US$5 every month for new workouts","A thin strip advertising a Grand Anse restaurant runs along the bottom of a bus-times app","A free note-taking app with a paid tier for cloud backup and extra storage","A full-screen car advert appears between levels of a Carnival dance game","Buying 100 gems in a game, which can be spent and bought again","A tide-chart app for fishermen costs US$2.99 to download","A player chooses to watch a 15-second ad to earn an extra life"],"correct":[1,3,6,0,4,2,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which business model?","q":"<p>The Caribbean Taxi Tech company built one booking app. It now sells the same app to taxi associations in St. Lucia, Antigua and Dominica, each with its own name, logo and colours, for a setup fee plus a monthly charge. Which model BEST describes this?</p>","options":["In-app advertising","Freemium","Crowdfunding","White-label app with licensing fees"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Nothing in the scenario mentions ads. The income comes from the taxi associations, not advertisers.","Freemium is a free core app with a paid upgrade for end users. Here, businesses pay for a rebranded copy.","Crowdfunding collects many small contributions from the public, usually before a product exists.","Correct. One codebase rebranded for many clients is white-label, and the monthly charge for permission to use it is a licensing fee."]},{"t":"mc","title":"How is it funded? (1)","q":"<p>Identify the community-supported funding model in each situation.</p><p>A Guyanese team raises US$8,000 from 400 backers on a crowdfunding platform before they start coding a farming app.</p>","options":["subscription","crowdfunding","licensing"],"correct":1,"why":"Many small pledges for a specific future project is crowdfunding.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How is it funded? (2)","q":"<p>A foundation gives a Jamaican health-app team money to build a clinic finder, with progress reports required.</p>","options":["donation","in-app purchase","grant"],"correct":2,"why":"Institutional funding for a defined purpose, with conditions and reports, is a grant.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How is it funded? (3)","q":"<p>Readers of Wikipedia see a banner asking them to give whatever they can.</p>","options":["sponsorship","interstitial ad","donation"],"correct":2,"why":"Voluntary gifts from users are donations; this is Wikipedia's main funding source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How is it funded? (4)","q":"<p>A telecoms company funds a free hurricane-alert app and its logo appears on the start screen.</p>","options":["sponsorship","white-label","freemium"],"correct":0,"why":"Funding in return for recognition is sponsorship.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How is it funded? (5)","q":"<p>Signal can avoid collecting users' data for ads mainly because...</p>","options":["it charges a high download price","it shows only banner ads","it is funded by donations and grants through a non-profit"],"correct":2,"why":"Signal's non-profit, donation-and-grant model removes the commercial incentive to track users.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Is this civic app sustainable?","q":"<p>The Grenada Flood Watch team presents its funding plan for a free flood-alert app.</p><p>Select every line that makes the app MORE sustainable in the long term.</p>","options":["Full-screen ads appear before every flood warning","Corporate sponsorship from a local bank, renewed annually","A one-off school prize of US$500 is the only funding","A small annual licence fee from neighbouring islands that adopt the app","A three-year agreement with the national disaster agency to fund server costs","The app will sell users' location data to advertisers","A volunteer community of island developers maintains the open-source code"],"correct":[1, 3, 4, 6],"why":"Good judgement. Long-term agreements, renewed sponsorship, a maintenance community and licensing to other islands all support sustainability without harming users."},{"t":"mc","title":"The monetisation lab: Bim Moves (step 1)","q":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is advising Bim Moves, a new app listing events, beach parties, live music and Crop Over activities across Barbados. The audience is local young people aged 16 to 30 and visitors. Help choose a revenue model.</p><p>The founders want to charge US$6.99 to download the app so they make money from day one. What do you advise?</p>","options":["Agree: a high price shows quality.","Make the core listings free so many people download it; a price would stop most young locals from trying it."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Few people pay for an unknown app, especially a guide they can partly replace with Instagram.","Good. An events guide depends on a large audience, and a price barrier kills discovery."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The monetisation lab: Bim Moves (step 2)","q":"<p>The app will be free. How should it earn money?</p>","options":["Collect and sell users' location history to marketing companies.","Charge event organisers and bars a fee for featured listings, plus a small banner slot sold to local businesses.","Show a full-screen interstitial ad every time a user opens an event."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This is a serious privacy and ethics breach and may break data protection law.","Strong. The businesses that gain customers pay, and local ads are relevant to users.","Constant interruptions drive users away and waste their prepaid data."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The monetisation lab: Bim Moves (step 3)","q":"<p>Some regular users ask for extra features: early ticket alerts and no ads. What do you add?</p>","options":["Remove all free features and force everyone onto a subscription.","An optional \"Bim Moves Plus\" at a low monthly price, with clear terms and easy cancellation."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Removing the free tier loses most of the audience that event organisers are paying to reach.","A freemium tier rewards loyal users without taking anything away from free users."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The monetisation lab: Bim Moves (step 4)","q":"<p>Many young users do not have credit cards. How do you handle payments for Plus?</p>","options":["Support store gift cards and check carrier billing with local operators, and price it for the local market.","Accept international credit cards only; that is the users' problem."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["This improves accessibility and affordability for the real audience.","This excludes a large part of the target audience."]},{"t":"match","title":"Steps to choose a revenue model","q":"<p>Arrange the steps a developer should follow to choose a revenue model for a new Caribbean app.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Define the app's purpose and who benefits from it (users, businesses, the public).","Research the audience: income, payment methods, devices and data habits.","Estimate ongoing costs: store fees, servers, updates, support and marketing.","List possible models (commercial, community-supported, civic) and how competitors earn.","Evaluate each model on user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and sustainability.","Choose and justify a model or hybrid, then test it with beta users.","Monitor revenue and user feedback after launch, and adjust."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]}]},{"id":"assess_4hd","title":"Module 3 · Unit 9 quiz: Factors influencing trends in the mobile ecosystem","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 9: Factors influencing trends in the mobile ecosystem. 21 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the driving factor","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is writing a trends report for clients. Match each factor to the example that best illustrates it.</p>","left":["Commoditisation","Affordability","Accessibility","Adoption","User behaviour","Empowerment","Technology access"],"right":["A blind user in Barbados navigates apps using TalkBack and voice commands","Within a few years almost every business in Castries takes orders on WhatsApp","A Digicel prepaid weekly data bundle lets a student get online without a contract","Fibre and 4G coverage reach rural villages in Belize for the first time","Young people now send voice notes rather than make phone calls","A Jamaican farmer checks market prices on her phone and negotiates a better deal","Dozens of brands sell similar entry-level Android phones, so prices keep falling"],"correct":[6,2,0,1,4,5,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the trend (1)","q":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing client requests from around the region. Identify the trend behind each one.</p><p>A St. Kitts hotel wants guests' phones to unlock room doors and control the air conditioning.</p>","options":["foldable devices","Internet of Things","super app"],"correct":1,"why":"Connected locks and air-conditioning units controlled by phone are IoT devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the trend (2)","q":"<p>A traffic camera at the Demerara Harbour Bridge processes video locally and sends only alerts, not full footage.</p>","options":["edge computing","subscription model","low-code"],"correct":0,"why":"Processing data near its source and sending only results is edge computing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the trend (3)","q":"<p>A bank app adds a chatbot that answers customer questions in plain language at any hour.</p>","options":["PWA","AI integration","wearable technology"],"correct":1,"why":"A conversational assistant is AI built into the app.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the trend (4)","q":"<p>A network upgrade gives near-instant response for cloud gaming and supports thousands of sensors.</p>","options":["digital identity","5G","sideloading"],"correct":1,"why":"Very low latency and capacity for many devices are the key features of 5G.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the trend (5)","q":"<p>Customers at a Georgetown café pay by tapping their phone on the card reader.</p>","options":["enterprise distribution","mobile payments","AR"],"correct":1,"why":"Tap-to-pay with a phone wallet is a mobile payment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Device trend or development trend? (1)","q":"<p>Some trends are about new <b>hardware</b>; others change how apps are <b>built or delivered</b>; others are about <b>experiences</b>. Sort each example.</p><p>What kind of trend is this?</p><p><b>A phone that unfolds into a small tablet.</b></p>","options":["Immersive experience (AR/VR)","New hardware / devices","How apps are built or delivered"],"correct":1,"why":"Foldables are a hardware trend that forces apps to adapt their layouts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Device trend or development trend? (2)","q":"<p>What kind of trend is this?</p><p><b>A smartwatch that tracks heart rate during a Carnival road march.</b></p>","options":["Immersive experience (AR/VR)","New hardware / devices","How apps are built or delivered"],"correct":1,"why":"Wearables are new devices with their own app requirements.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Device trend or development trend? (3)","q":"<p>What kind of trend is this?</p><p><b>A youth group in Curaçao builds its event app in Thunkable with no code.</b></p>","options":["New hardware / devices","How apps are built or delivered","Immersive experience (AR/VR)"],"correct":1,"why":"Low-code/no-code changes how apps are built and who can build them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Device trend or development trend? (4)","q":"<p>What kind of trend is this?</p><p><b>A Dominica news site can be added to the home screen and read offline.</b></p>","options":["How apps are built or delivered","New hardware / devices","Immersive experience (AR/VR)"],"correct":0,"why":"A PWA is a delivery method: web technology that behaves like an app.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Device trend or development trend? (5)","q":"<p>What kind of trend is this?</p><p><b>A camera view shows labels floating over each shop on Frederick Street in Port of Spain.</b></p>","options":["How apps are built or delivered","New hardware / devices","Immersive experience (AR/VR)"],"correct":2,"why":"Digital labels laid over the live camera view are augmented reality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Device trend or development trend? (6)","q":"<p>What kind of trend is this?</p><p><b>A headset lets a buyer in Toronto walk through a villa in Ocho Rios.</b></p>","options":["How apps are built or delivered","New hardware / devices","Immersive experience (AR/VR)"],"correct":2,"why":"A fully virtual environment through a headset is virtual reality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Device trend or development trend? (7)","q":"<p>What kind of trend is this?</p><p><b>Smart earbuds translate a conversation in real time.</b></p>","options":["Immersive experience (AR/VR)","New hardware / devices","How apps are built or delivered"],"correct":1,"why":"Wearable devices increasingly carry AI features, but the trend here is the new hardware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Device trend or development trend? (8)","q":"<p>What kind of trend is this?</p><p><b>A Bahamas agency uses FlutterFlow to build an app for both Android and iOS from one design.</b></p>","options":["Immersive experience (AR/VR)","New hardware / devices","How apps are built or delivered"],"correct":2,"why":"Visual cross-platform builders are part of the low-code trend.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Super app or not?","q":"<p>Which description BEST fits the definition of a super app?</p>","options":["An app that runs extremely fast on 5G networks.","One app in which users chat, pay bills, order food, book rides and use mini-programs from other businesses.","A very popular app with millions of downloads, such as a hit game.","A single-purpose app built with a no-code tool."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Speed relates to network technology, not to the range of services the app offers.","Correct. A super app consolidates many different services, usually tied together by a built-in payment wallet, as WeChat and Grab do.","Popularity alone does not make a super app. It must combine many different services in one place.","A super app is the opposite of single-purpose; the tool used to build it is irrelevant."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Privacy by design check","q":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing the Bayside Clinic appointment app, which will serve patients on both the Dutch and French sides of St. Martin.</p><p>Select every feature that shows GOOD privacy and security practice.</p>","options":["Patient data is shared with an advertising network to show health product ads","It requests access to all contacts \"just in case\"","A clear privacy policy explains what is collected and how to delete an account","The app asks for location only when the user searches for the nearest clinic","Login uses a fingerprint or face ID plus an optional one-time code","Medical notes are encrypted on the device and on the server","Passwords are stored in plain text to make support easier"],"correct":[2, 3, 4, 5],"why":"Correct. Asking only when needed, encryption, strong authentication and a clear privacy policy follow privacy by design and laws such as GDPR."},{"t":"mc","title":"Advising the Ministry: a digital services app (step 1)","q":"<p>Zara and Maya at the Island Pulse Media are advising a government working group in Sint Maarten that wants a mobile app for public services: bill payments, permit applications and appointment booking. Make the best decisions.</p><p>A minister wants the app to require the newest phones so it can use the latest AR and AI features. What do you advise?</p>","options":["Agree: the newest technology will make the island look modern.","First research residents' devices, data costs and digital skills; the app must serve everyone, including older residents."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A public service that excludes people with older phones fails its purpose.","Good. Technology access, affordability and accessibility decide whether a public service is actually used."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advising the Ministry: a digital services app (step 2)","q":"<p>Research shows most residents use mid-range Android phones and prepaid data. How should the service be delivered?</p>","options":["A large app that streams high-definition video guides and only works on 5G.","A light store app plus a PWA version, both working on 4G and saving forms offline until a connection returns."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This ignores current network coverage and data costs.","Strong: it matches real devices and data conditions and widens access."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advising the Ministry: a digital services app (step 3)","q":"<p>The app will handle ID numbers and payments, and some users live on the French side or are EU citizens. What do you build in?</p>","options":["Collect as much data as possible now; security can be added later.","Privacy by design: encryption, 2FA, data minimisation, a clear privacy policy and compliance with local law and GDPR."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Unnecessary data plus weak security invites breaches and legal penalties.","Excellent. Security and data protection are essential for public trust."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advising the Ministry: a digital services app (step 4)","q":"<p>Before launch, how do you check you are building on real trends, not hype?</p>","options":["Copy whatever the most-downloaded app in the US store is doing.","Use GSMA Intelligence and ITU data for regional mobile use, Google Trends for local search interest, and survey residents."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Global store rankings do not reflect Sint Maarten's needs or conditions.","Combining reliable secondary data with primary research is sound trend evaluation."]},{"t":"match","title":"Which research tool?","q":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media needs evidence for several client reports. Match each research need to the best tool.</p>","left":["Google Trends","GSMA Intelligence","data.ai (formerly App Annie)","Sensor Tower","Statista","Google Play Console"],"right":["Free comparison of search interest in \"food delivery\" in Trinidad over five years","A credible regional report on mobile subscribers and network coverage in the Caribbean","Estimates of downloads and revenue for the top travel apps by country","Keyword and ad intelligence to plan a competitor's ASO strategy","A ready-made chart of global smartphone shipments by brand, with a cited source","Day-30 retention for the client's own app"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 01 Mock Papers","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_4nn","title":"Mock Paper 01 · Set 1","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Digital Media Unit 2 · Grade One · Mock Paper 01 · Set 1</b> · 1 hour 30 minutes · 45 items</p><ol><li>Answer <b>ALL</b> items. Each item has four options (A), (B), (C), (D).</li><li>Items 1 to 15 Module 1, 16 to 30 Module 2, 31 to 45 Module 3.</li><li>Submit to see your score, the key and an explanation for every item.</li></ol>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Sol Studio in St. John’s is hired by a clinic to \"make something digital\" to cut long waiting times. Which pre-production step should the team complete FIRST?</p>","options":["Test a clickable prototype with patients","Draw a storyboard of the main screens","Define the problem, purpose and goal of the product","Build a Gantt chart for the production schedule"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Problem definition comes first: until the team knows exactly what challenge it is solving and for whom, every later step is guesswork. The Gantt chart is tempting because planning feels like an early step, but you cannot schedule tasks for a product that has not yet been defined.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Malik is framing a shot of a cruise ship in Scarborough harbour for a Tobago cruise port promo. Which framing BEST applies the rule of thirds?</p>","options":["Zoom in until the ship fills the whole frame","Place the ship exactly in the centre of the frame","Place the horizon along the very bottom edge of the frame","Place the ship on one of the four points where lines dividing the frame into thirds, horizontally and vertically, intersect"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The rule of thirds divides the frame with two horizontal and two vertical lines and places the point of interest on a line or an intersection, which feels more dynamic than central framing. Centring the ship is tempting because it looks \"balanced\", but that is symmetrical composition, not the rule of thirds.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3 (Module 1)","q":"<p>On the traditional red, yellow, blue (RYB) colour wheel, which pair of colours is COMPLEMENTARY?</p>","options":["Blue and green","Blue and orange","Red and yellow","Yellow and yellow-green"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Complementary colours sit directly opposite each other on the colour wheel; blue is opposite orange. Blue and green is tempting because the colours look good together, but they sit next to each other, which makes them analogous, not complementary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4 (Module 1)","q":"<p>In the PayRite utility app, after a user taps \"Pay bill\", the button turns into a spinner and then shows a green tick with \"Payment received\". Which principle of interactive design is MOST clearly applied?</p>","options":["Feedback","Consistency","Perceivability","Learnability"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Feedback means the system tells users what has happened as a result of their action, here the spinner and confirmation message. Consistency is tempting, but it is about using the same layouts, icons and behaviours across screens, not about responding to a specific action.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which statement BEST describes a key motivation for producing indigenous Caribbean digital products such as local animations, games and apps?</p>","options":["To tell Caribbean stories in authentic local voices and preserve cultural identity in a media space dominated by imported content","To avoid having to follow copyright law","To make content that only Caribbean audiences can access","To guarantee lower production costs than imported content"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Indigenous products are motivated by representation, authenticity and cultural preservation, giving Caribbean people content that reflects their lives. Lower cost is tempting, but local production is often more expensive per viewer than buying imported content, so cost is not the defining motivation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Island Pulse Media rents servers, storage and computing power from Amazon Web Services to host its clients' websites. Which type of digital platform is Amazon Web Services in this case?</p>","options":["A social platform","A marketplace","A search engine","An infrastructure platform"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Infrastructure platforms provide the underlying computing resources (servers, storage, networking) that other services are built on. Marketplace is tempting because Amazon also runs a shopping site, but here the company is renting computing resources, not buying goods from sellers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Maya needs a version of the Island Pulse logo with sharp edges and a fully transparent background to place over photos on a website. Which file format is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["PNG","GIF","MP4","JPEG"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. PNG uses lossless compression and supports full alpha transparency, so edges stay crisp and the background can be see-through. GIF is tempting because it also supports transparency, but it is limited to 256 colours and only on or off transparency, which gives jagged edges over photos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Keisha has recorded a podcast episode about Carnival and must keep a master copy for further editing with NO loss of quality. Which audio format should she save the master in?</p>","options":["MP4","MP3","WAV","OGG"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. WAV is an uncompressed format, so it keeps every detail of the recording and is ideal as an editing master. MP3 is tempting because it is the usual format for podcasts, but it is lossy: each export discards data, so it is the right format for distribution, not for the master.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9 (Module 1)","q":"<p>In three-point lighting, which light is placed BEHIND the subject to separate them from the background?</p>","options":["The fill light","The key light","The ambient light","The back light (rim light)"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The back light shines from behind the subject, creating a thin outline of light that lifts them off the background. The fill light is tempting because it is also a supporting light, but it sits in front, opposite the key light, to soften shadows on the face.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10 (Module 1)","q":"<p>In Malik's 2D bouncing ball exercise, the ball flattens when it hits the ground and becomes longer and thinner as it falls fast. Which principle of animation is he applying?</p>","options":["Anticipation","Follow through","Squash and stretch","Staging"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Squash and stretch changes an object's shape to show weight, impact and speed while keeping its volume. Anticipation is tempting because it is also a core principle, but it is a preparatory movement before an action (such as crouching before a jump), not the deformation on impact.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11 (Module 1)","q":"<p>In a 3D polygon mesh created in Blender, what is a VERTEX?</p>","options":["A flat surface enclosed by edges","A single point in 3D space where edges meet","A straight line joining two points","A complete 3D object made of many polygons"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A vertex is the most basic element of a mesh: one point with X, Y and Z coordinates. The straight line option is tempting because vertices and edges are closely linked, but a line between two vertices is an edge, and a flat surface enclosed by edges is a face.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Maya and a freelance designer in Guyana are editing the same Figma file when an internet outage in Roseau stops Maya's work for three hours. Which DISADVANTAGE of digital collaboration does this illustrate?</p>","options":["Slow real-time feedback","Dependence on reliable connectivity","Lack of version control","Limited global reach"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Cloud collaboration tools only work when every member has a stable connection, so outages halt progress. Lack of version control is tempting because work time was lost, but Figma keeps version history; the problem here is access, not tracking changes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13 (Module 1)","q":"<p>To launch a juice bar on Broad Street, Keisha paints chalk footprints along the pavement leading to the shop, ending at a QR code for a free sample. The cost is very low and the idea is unusual. This is an example of</p>","options":["influencer marketing","guerrilla marketing","email marketing","cross-platform marketing"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Guerrilla marketing uses unconventional, low-cost, high-impact ideas in public spaces to surprise people. Cross-platform marketing is tempting because the QR code links to a digital channel, but cross-platform marketing means keeping one consistent message across several channels, which is not the focus here.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A design trend that uses frosted, semi-transparent panels with a blurred background showing through is known as</p>","options":["neumorphism","flat design","skeuomorphism","glassmorphism"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Glassmorphism imitates frosted glass: translucent layers with background blur and subtle borders. Neumorphism is tempting because the names are similar, but it uses soft inner and outer shadows to make elements look pressed into or pushed out of the background, not see-through.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Lolo Run game team raises US$4,000 online from about 300 supporters, each giving a small amount in return for rewards such as early access. Which source of funding is this?</p>","options":["A government grant","Sponsorship","Crowdfunding","Angel investment"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Crowdfunding raises money from many people, each contributing a small amount, usually through a platform such as Kickstarter or GoFundMe. Sponsorship is tempting because supporters receive something back, but sponsorship normally involves one organisation paying in exchange for brand exposure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which e-book format is REFLOWABLE, meaning the text adjusts to fit the screen size and the reader's chosen font size?</p>","options":["ISBN","ePub","DOCX","PDF"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. ePub (electronic publication) is the open standard for reflowable e-books, so text rewraps on phones, tablets and e-readers. PDF is tempting because it is widely used for digital documents, but it is a fixed-layout format: pages look the same everywhere, which makes small screens hard to read.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which network, funded by the United States Department of Defense and first connected in 1969, is regarded as the forerunner of the internet?</p>","options":["ARPANET","Web 2.0","Ethernet","The World Wide Web"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. ARPANET linked research computers using packet switching and is the ancestor of today's internet. The World Wide Web is tempting, but it came about twenty years later (Tim Berners-Lee, 1989 to 1991) and is a system of linked pages that runs on top of the internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Keisha finds a photo of San Pedro Beach licensed CC BY-NC. In which situation may she use it?</p>","options":["In a paid hotel advertisement, with credit given to the photographer","In a school blog post, with credit given to the photographer","In any project, without giving credit","Only after buying the full copyright from the photographer"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. BY means attribution is required and NC means non-commercial use only, so a credited school blog post is allowed. The paid hotel advert is tempting because credit is given, but advertising is commercial use, which the NC condition forbids.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19 (Module 2)","q":"<p>At the start of a website project for a Portsmouth restaurant, what is the MAIN advantage of a low-fidelity paper prototype over a high-fidelity Figma prototype?</p>","options":["It can be handed straight to a developer as finished code","It shows the final colours, fonts and images exactly","It can be clicked through like the real website","It is quick and cheap to change, so the team gets feedback on structure and layout before investing time in detail"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Low-fidelity prototypes are rough by design: they let you test ideas and layout early, when changes cost almost nothing. Being clickable is tempting, but that is a feature of high-fidelity digital prototypes, not paper sketches.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Maya draws a diagram showing every page of the KIN Carnival Village website, with the home page at the top and branches to Events, Food, Tickets and Contact. This diagram is a</p>","options":["concept map","site map","storyboard","mood board"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A site map shows all the pages of a website and how they are organised and linked, usually as a hierarchy from the home page. A concept map is tempting because it also uses boxes and lines, but it links ideas with labelled relationships, not pages of a site.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which of the following is NOT a criterion for web-appropriate content?</p>","options":["Addressing users directly","Making content easy to scan","Using short paragraphs and sentences","Using long, detailed paragraphs with technical jargon to show expertise"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The syllabus asks for short paragraphs, direct address, no needless repetition, no jargon and scannable text, because online readers skim. Addressing users directly might seem informal, but it is an explicit criterion: \"you\" language keeps web readers engaged.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Apart from helping visually impaired users with screen readers, the <code>alt</code> text on an image ALSO supports</p>","options":["faster page loading, because alt text compresses the image","higher image resolution on large screens","copyright protection of the image","machine indexability, because search engines read alt text to understand the image"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Search engines cannot \"see\" images, so they rely on alt text to index them, which improves SEO. Faster loading is tempting, but alt text does not change the image file at all; only resizing and compression reduce load time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Jerome recorded a voice-over in a room with a humming air conditioner. In Audacity, what is the FIRST step of the Noise Reduction process?</p>","options":["Select the whole track and apply Amplify","Export the track as MP3 to remove the noise","Select a few seconds containing only the background hum and choose Get Noise Profile","Apply a fade-in to the start of the track"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Audacity must first learn what the noise sounds like from a section with no speech; only then do you select the whole track and apply the reduction. Amplify is tempting, but it makes the voice AND the hum louder, so the problem gets worse, not better.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24 (Module 2)","q":"<p>What does the attribute <code>target=\"_blank\"</code> do in this HTML?<br><code>&lt;a href=\"https://www.cxc.org\" target=\"_blank\"&gt;CXC&lt;/a&gt;</code></p>","options":["It makes the link text bold","It downloads the linked page as a file","It turns the link into an internal anchor link","It opens the linked page in a new browser tab or window"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. <code>target=\"_blank\"</code> tells the browser to open the destination in a new tab, so the user keeps your page open. The anchor link option is tempting, but internal anchor links use <code>href=\"#id\"</code> to jump within a page and have nothing to do with the target attribute.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25 (Module 2)","q":"<p>In the CSS box model, which order correctly lists the layers from the INSIDE of an element outwards?</p>","options":["Content, margin, border, padding","Content, padding, border, margin","Margin, border, padding, content","Padding, content, border, margin"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Content sits in the centre, padding is the space inside the border, then the border, then the margin outside it that separates the element from its neighbours. Margin, border, padding, content is tempting because it is the correct order reversed, but the question asks from the inside outwards.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Look at this CSS rule:<br><code>.special { color: teal; }</code><br>Which elements will it style?</p>","options":["Only the first element on the page","Only the element with id=\"special\"","Every paragraph on the page","Every element with class=\"special\""],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A full stop before a name makes a class selector, which applies to every element carrying that class. The id option is tempting, but an id selector starts with a hash (<code>#special</code>) and targets one unique element.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A student checks whether a blog post about Hurricane Irma recovery was written by a qualified, identifiable author or organisation. Which evaluation criterion is she applying?</p>","options":["Accuracy","Authority","Accessibility","Usability"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Authority asks who created the content and whether they are qualified and credible. Accuracy is tempting because the two are linked, but accuracy is about whether the facts themselves are correct and can be verified, not who wrote them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Sunset Tours company builds its entire audience on one social media platform. When the platform changes its algorithm, the reach of its posts falls by half overnight. Which LIMITATION of online platforms does this illustrate?</p>","options":["High hosting costs","Weak analytics tools","Platform dependency and lack of control over algorithms","Copyright infringement"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. When a business relies on one platform, the platform's rules and algorithms decide who sees its content, and it can change them at any time. Weak analytics is tempting because the company notices the drop through its statistics, but the cause is the algorithm change, not poor data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Port Pulse tourism board wants ONE content store to feed its website, its mobile app and the digital screens at NMIA arrivals. Which type of CMS is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["A headless CMS","A document CMS","A mobile content CMS","A single-theme web CMS"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A headless CMS separates content (the \"body\") from presentation (the \"head\") and delivers it through an API to any channel. A mobile content CMS is tempting, but it is designed around mobile delivery only, while the board needs web, app and airport screens fed from one source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A website that can be installed on a phone's home screen, sends notifications and works offline, without going through an app store, is called a</p>","options":["native app","Web 1.0 site","progressive web app (PWA)","headless CMS"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A PWA is a website built with modern web technologies so it behaves like an app: installable, offline-capable and able to send notifications. Native app is tempting, but native apps are written for a specific OS and are normally installed through an app store.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31 (Module 3)","q":"<p>In the mobile ecosystem, what is the MAIN role of carriers such as Digicel, Flow and Flow?</p>","options":["Reviewing and approving apps for the app stores","Designing the operating systems that run on phones","Providing the network infrastructure and data connectivity that link devices to content and services","Writing the apps that users download"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Carriers (telcos) build and run the mobile networks, selling voice and data services so that devices can reach content. Designing operating systems is tempting because carriers sell phones, but operating systems are made by platform companies such as Google (Android) and Apple (iOS).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which statement BEST explains the INTERDEPENDENCE of players in the mobile ecosystem?</p>","options":["Each player works independently, so a change in one part has no effect on the others","Only carriers matter, because without them no other player exists","Developers need users and good connectivity for their apps to succeed, while carriers and device makers need useful apps to drive demand for data and devices","Users depend on developers, but developers do not depend on users"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Interdependence means each component relies on the others: content drives demand for devices and data, while infrastructure and users make content worthwhile. The carriers option is tempting because networks are essential, but devices, content and users are equally necessary; no single player stands alone.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Jerome must test the BusSXM Android app on many phone brands, screen sizes and OS versions. Which characteristic of Android causes this extra testing?</p>","options":["The requirement to write all Android apps in Swift","The fact that Android runs only on Google Pixel phones","Its closed ecosystem, controlled entirely by one company","Fragmentation, because many manufacturers ship different devices running different Android versions"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Because Android is open-source and used by many manufacturers, the market is fragmented across devices and versions, so apps must be tested widely. A closed ecosystem is tempting, but that describes iOS, where Apple controls a small range of hardware, which actually reduces testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Smart water meters in a Caribbean town automatically send readings to the utility company over the network, with no person reading them. This is an example of</p>","options":["cross-platform development","the Internet of Things (IoT)","augmented reality","a super app"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. IoT describes everyday physical objects with sensors and network connections that collect and exchange data automatically. Cross-platform development is tempting because the data may reach different systems, but that term refers to building one app that runs on several operating systems.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which statement BEST describes the content consumption versus content production imbalance in the Caribbean mobile ecosystem?</p>","options":["Content produced in the Caribbean cannot be shared on global platforms","Caribbean users produce more content than they consume","Caribbean users consume far more mobile content made outside the region than they produce locally","Caribbean mobile users do not consume digital content"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The region is a heavy consumer of imported apps, streaming and social content, while local production is comparatively small, so most value and cultural influence flows from abroad. The final option is tempting because local creators face challenges, but global platforms are open to Caribbean creators; the gap is in volume, not access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Why are submarine fibre-optic cables critical to small island states such as Barbados?</p>","options":["They supply electricity to mobile phone towers","They carry most international internet traffic, so damage to them can cut or slow connectivity for the whole island","They are used only for landline telephone calls","Satellites carry most traffic, so cables are only a backup"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Most international data travels on undersea cables, so islands depend on them for fast, affordable internet; redundancy (more than one cable) improves resilience. The satellite option is tempting because satellites are used in emergencies, but they carry only a small share of traffic and usually with higher latency.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following is an ECONOMIC benefit of developing mobile apps locally in the Caribbean?</p>","options":["Preserving local languages such as Papiamentu","Sharing local history with young people","Improving hurricane alerts for residents","Creating local jobs and keeping app revenue within the region"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Economic benefits include jobs, entrepreneurship, export potential and keeping income in local hands. Preserving Papiamentu is tempting because it is a real benefit, but it is a cultural benefit, not an economic one; the command word asks you to classify the benefit correctly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The FarmLink app will be used by farmers in rural Dominica where mobile data is slow and expensive. Which design strategy BEST addresses this?</p>","options":["Require a constant internet connection for every feature","Add a large animated splash screen to build the brand","Autoplay high-definition tutorial videos on the home screen","Compress images, cache content for offline use and keep the app lightweight"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Designing for low bandwidth means reducing data use and allowing offline access, so the app works on weak signals and saves users money. The animated splash screen is tempting because branding matters, but it adds file size and loading time, which hurts users on slow connections.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Fishers in Grenada will use a weather app on open boats in bright sunlight, often with wet hands. Which design decision BEST responds to this physical environment?</p>","options":["A dark, low-contrast theme with thin fonts","Many small icons packed onto one screen","High-contrast colours, large text and large touch targets","Long text forms that require typing"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Bright light washes out low-contrast screens, and wet hands make small targets hard to hit, so strong contrast and big buttons improve usability. Dark mode is tempting because it can reduce glare at night, but low contrast and thin fonts are very hard to read in direct sunlight.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which statement BEST describes a WIREFRAME in mobile app development?</p>","options":["The finished code of the app ready for the app store","A basic skeletal layout showing where interface elements will be placed, without final colours or images","A fully interactive version of the app used to test user flows","A marketing video showing the app's main features"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A wireframe is a blueprint of the screen layout: boxes and placeholders for buttons, text and images. The interactive version is tempting because wireframes can be linked later, but a clickable, interactive model is a prototype, which comes after wireframing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41 (Module 3)","q":"<p>To build a NATIVE iOS app, which official development environment and programming language are used?</p>","options":["Visual Studio Code and HTML","Xcode and Swift","Android Studio and Kotlin","MIT App Inventor and code blocks"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Apple's official IDE is Xcode, and Swift is Apple's modern language for iOS apps. Android Studio and Kotlin is tempting because it is also an official native pairing, but it is for Android, not iOS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Before launch, Jerome gives a beta of the BusSLU app to 20 regular riders on the Castries to Gros Islet route to confirm it meets their real needs. This is</p>","options":["user acceptance testing (UAT)","unit testing","debugging","App Store Optimisation"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. UAT is carried out by real target users to confirm the app does what they need before release. Unit testing is tempting because it is also testing, but it is done by developers on small individual pieces of code, not by end users.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43 (Module 3)","q":"<p>App Store Optimisation (ASO) is BEST described as</p>","options":["improving a website's ranking in Google search results","sending a press release to app review websites","paying influencers to post about an app on TikTok","improving an app's title, keywords, description, screenshots and ratings so it ranks higher in app store searches"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. ASO focuses on the app store listing itself so the app is found and downloaded more. Improving Google rankings is tempting because ASO is often called \"SEO for apps\", but SEO targets websites in search engines, while ASO targets app store search.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Bim Eats app is free to download and lets everyone browse menus and reviews, but users pay to unlock premium features such as booking tables with no fees. Which revenue model is this?</p>","options":["Subscription","White-label licensing","Freemium","In-app advertising"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Freemium gives the core app away free and charges for premium features. Subscription is tempting because premium users may pay regularly, but the defining feature here is a free core with paid upgrades; a pure subscription model charges for access itself on a recurring basis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45 (Module 3)","q":"<p>An app that combines messaging, payments, ride-hailing and food delivery in one platform, such as WeChat or Grab, is known as a</p>","options":["super app","cross-platform app","progressive web app","IoT hub"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Super apps consolidate many digital services inside one app, so users rarely need to leave it. Cross-platform app is tempting, but that term describes an app built to run on more than one operating system, not one that bundles many services.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_4tx","title":"Mock Paper 01 · Set 2","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Digital Media Unit 2 · Grade One · Mock Paper 01 · Set 2</b> · 1 hour 30 minutes · 45 items</p><ol><li>Answer <b>ALL</b> items. Each item has four options (A), (B), (C), (D).</li><li>Items 1 to 15 Module 1, 16 to 30 Module 2, 31 to 45 Module 3.</li><li>Submit to see your score, the key and an explanation for every item.</li></ol>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Zara must show a client when scripting, filming, editing and review will happen across six weeks, including which tasks overlap and the key milestones. Which planning tool is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["A mood board","A Gantt chart","A mind map","A storyboard"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A Gantt chart uses horizontal bars on a timeline to show each task's start, duration, overlaps and milestones. A storyboard is tempting because it is a pre-production document, but it plans the visual sequence of shots, not the schedule.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Why should a team test a prototype with target users BEFORE full production of a digital media product?</p>","options":["To replace the need for a written project proposal","To find problems and confirm that the idea meets users' needs while changes are still cheap to make","To secure copyright protection for the idea","To produce the final export files for distribution"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Idea validation tests a concept with real users early, so weaknesses are fixed before money and time are spent on the finished product. Copyright protection is tempting because it also protects work, but copyright is automatic once work is fixed; testing has nothing to do with it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Serenity Spa in Gros Islet wants a calm palette of blue, blue-green and green for its website. Which colour harmony is this?</p>","options":["Complementary","Analogous","Split-complementary","Triadic"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Analogous colours sit next to each other on the colour wheel, giving a calm, unified look. Split-complementary is tempting because it also uses three colours, but it pairs one base colour with the two colours on either side of its complement, which creates far more contrast.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A designer adds black to a pure red to create a deep maroon for a Carnival band costume graphic. The maroon is a</p>","options":["tint","tone","gradient","shade"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A shade is a hue mixed with black, which darkens it. Tone is tempting because it also reduces brightness, but a tone is a hue mixed with grey, and a tint is a hue mixed with white.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Every screen of the Carnival Pass app places the back arrow in the top left corner and uses the same icon style and colours. Which principle of interactive design is this?</p>","options":["Predictability","Consistency","Perceivability","Feedback"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Consistency means using the same layout, controls and visual language throughout, so users do not have to relearn the interface. Predictability is tempting because consistent design helps users predict, but predictability is about users correctly anticipating what an action will do, not about repeated placement and style.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Free tools such as Canva, GIMP and Blender allow a student in Belize to create professional-looking designs without buying expensive software licences. Which TWO driving factors are MOST clearly shown?</p>","options":["Commoditisation and politics","Adoption and politics","Affordability and empowerment","Pervasiveness and adoption"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The tools cost nothing (affordability) and give individuals the power to create and compete (empowerment). Pervasiveness and adoption is tempting because the tools are popular, but the scenario stresses cost and personal capability, not how widespread the technology is.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7 (Module 1)","q":"<p>When critiquing the AUTHENTICITY of a Caribbean animated series, which question is MOST useful?</p>","options":["Do the characters, settings, music and dialogue reflect real Caribbean experiences, and were people from that culture involved in making it?","How many episodes were produced in the first season?","Is each episode longer than imported cartoons?","Was the most expensive animation software used?"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Authenticity is about genuine cultural representation and whether the culture is portrayed from the inside rather than as a stereotype. The software question is tempting because production quality affects impact, but expensive tools do not make content more culturally authentic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Keisha wants a small looping animation of a waving Guyana flag to play inside an email newsletter, which cannot play video. Which format is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["MP4","GIF","PNG","PDF"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. GIF supports simple frame animation and loops automatically, and it displays in almost all email software. MP4 is tempting because it gives better quality and smaller files, but most email clients will not play embedded video, so the animation would not appear.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Malik saves the same JPEG photo of Fort George many times, and blocky artefacts start to appear. Why does this happen?</p>","options":["JPEG is a vector format that cannot store photographs","JPEG stores only 1-bit colour, so detail is lost","JPEG uses lossy compression, discarding some image data every time the file is re-saved","JPEG uses lossless compression, which adds extra pixels on each save"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Lossy compression throws away data the eye is less likely to notice, and each re-save compresses again, so the loss builds up over time. The lossless option is tempting because it sounds technical, but lossless formats such as PNG keep all data, which is exactly why they do not degrade.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Malik is filming an interview on windy Trellis Bay beach in Tortola, beside the airport, with planes passing overhead. Which choice will BEST capture a clear voice?</p>","options":["Turn the recording level to maximum","Rely on the camera's built-in microphone from three metres away","Clip a lavalier microphone close to the speaker's mouth and fit a foam or furry windscreen","Record with no microphone and add subtitles later"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A lavalier sits close to the mouth, so the voice is much louder than the background, and a windscreen blocks wind rumble. Turning the level to maximum is tempting, but it raises the wind and plane noise too and can cause clipping and distortion.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Before a cartoon character leaps over a puddle, she bends her knees and swings her arms back. Which principle of animation is this?</p>","options":["Staging","Squash and stretch","Anticipation","Follow through"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Anticipation is a preparatory movement that signals to the audience that an action is about to happen. Follow through is tempting because it also deals with movement around an action, but it happens AFTER the main action, when parts such as hair or clothes keep moving.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12 (Module 1)","q":"<p>In Adobe Animate, Malik sets the ball's position at frame 1 and frame 24, and the software automatically creates the frames in between. This process is called</p>","options":["rendering","rotoscoping","interpolation (tweening)","onion skinning"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Tweening (from \"in-betweening\") is when software calculates the intermediate frames between two keyframes. Onion skinning is tempting because it is also used when animating between keys, but it only shows faded previous and next frames for reference; it does not generate frames.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which action shows good NETIQUETTE when working on a shared Google Doc for a group SBA?</p>","options":["Deleting sections you disagree with before the group meets","Typing comments in all capital letters so they stand out","Sharing the document link publicly without asking the group","Using suggestion mode and comments to propose changes instead of deleting a teammate's work"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Netiquette means respectful, professional conduct online; suggestions and comments let teammates see and discuss changes. Capital letters is tempting because it seems to add emphasis, but online it reads as shouting and is considered rude.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Keisha runs an Instagram campaign for a cashew jam brand and wants to know whether it is ENGAGING the audience. Which metric is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["The cost of the camera used for the photos","The number of posts the team published","Engagement rate (likes, comments, shares and saves relative to reach)","The number of followers Keisha has on her personal account"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Engagement rate measures how people interact with the content compared with how many saw it, which is a key performance indicator for social campaigns. The number of posts is tempting because it is easy to count, but it measures effort, not audience response.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which section of a project proposal gives a brief overview of the WHOLE project, so busy funders can quickly decide whether to read further?</p>","options":["The executive summary","The methodology","The budget","The problem statement"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The executive summary comes first and condenses the problem, solution, costs and outcomes into a short overview. The problem statement is tempting because it also appears early, but it explains only the need the project addresses, not the whole plan.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16 (Module 2)","q":"<p>An author publishes her novel set in San Pedro as an e-book on Kobo and Google Play Books, and also as a paperback. She learns that each format needs its own unique 13-digit identifier. This identifier is the</p>","options":["ISBN","metadata","ePub","CMS"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The International Standard Book Number uniquely identifies each edition and format of a book, so e-book and paperback get different ISBNs. Metadata is tempting because it also describes the book, but metadata is a set of descriptive fields (title, author, keywords), not one unique number.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which stage of the web is MOST associated with user-generated content, social networking and two-way interaction?</p>","options":["Web 2.0","Web 3.0","Web 1.0","ARPANET"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Web 2.0 turned users from readers into contributors through blogs, wikis, social media and video sharing. Web 3.0 is tempting because it is the newest, but it is associated with decentralisation, blockchain and the semantic web rather than the rise of social media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Writing a podcast script differs from writing a blog post MAINLY because</p>","options":["listeners cannot scan back, so the script needs conversational language, short sentences, signposting and repetition of key points","a podcast script must include headings and hyperlinks","a podcast script relies on alt text for accessibility","a podcast script must always be longer than a blog post"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Audio is linear: listeners hear each word once, so writers signpost (\"first\", \"next\") and repeat key ideas. Headings and hyperlinks is tempting because they help readers, but they are techniques for scannable text on a screen, not for the ear.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which of these actions is PLAGIARISM?</p>","options":["Quoting a short passage from an article and naming the source","Using an image released under a CC0 public domain dedication","Copying paragraphs from a newspaper article into your blog and presenting them as your own words","Linking to an article so readers can read it on the original site"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Plagiarism is presenting someone else's work or ideas as your own without credit. Quoting a short passage is tempting because it uses another person's words, but attribution makes it legitimate quotation, not plagiarism.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Keisha draws a diagram with \"Carnival\" in the centre, linked by labelled phrases such as \"includes\" to J'ouvert, the Parade and the Village, and \"features\" to soca and calypso. This is a</p>","options":["concept map","wireframe","site map","hierarchy map"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A concept map links ideas using labelled connecting phrases to show relationships. A hierarchy map is tempting because both use connected boxes, but a hierarchy map shows levels of pages or content from top to bottom, not labelled relationships between ideas.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which document is used BEFORE writing begins to define the target group, the tone of voice and a checklist of content to include?</p>","options":["A CSS style sheet","A privacy policy","A site map","A creative brief"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A creative brief sets out the purpose, audience, tone and content requirements so everyone works to the same plan. A site map is tempting because it is also a planning document, but it maps the structure of pages, not the audience and voice of the content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22 (Module 2)","q":"<p>For a product photo of a nutmeg bottle, Malik wants the bottle sharp and the background softly blurred. Which camera setting BEST achieves this shallow depth of field?</p>","options":["A very high ISO setting","A very fast shutter speed with no other change","A wide aperture, such as f/1.8","A narrow aperture, such as f/16"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A wide aperture (low f-number) produces a shallow depth of field, isolating the subject from the background. f/16 is tempting because a bigger number sounds stronger, but a narrow aperture keeps more of the scene in focus, which is the opposite of what Malik wants.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23 (Module 2)","q":"<p>On a tours website, the line \"Home &gt; Tours &gt; Pigeon Island\" near the top of the page is an example of</p>","options":["breadcrumbs","pagination","a footer","a hamburger menu"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Breadcrumbs show where the user is in the site's hierarchy and let them jump back to higher levels. A hamburger menu is tempting because it is also a navigation feature, but it is a hidden menu opened from a three-line icon, not a location trail.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24 (Module 2)","q":"<p>What will this HTML display in a browser?<br><code>&lt;ol&gt;&lt;li&gt;Rice&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Peas&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;</code></p>","options":["A table with two cells","Two headings","A bulleted list with two items","A numbered list with two items"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> creates an ordered list, which browsers number by default, and each <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> is a list item. A bulleted list is tempting, but that is produced by <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> (unordered list).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Look at this CSS rule:<br><code>#menu { background-color: navy; }</code><br>Which element(s) will it style?</p>","options":["The one element with id=\"menu\"","Every element with class=\"menu\"","Every link on the page","Every nav element on the page"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The hash symbol makes an id selector, which targets the single element carrying that id. The class option is tempting, but class selectors begin with a full stop (<code>.menu</code>) and can style many elements.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Why do web designers often set font sizes in <code>rem</code> rather than fixed <code>px</code> values?</p>","options":["rem is used only for printed pages","rem sizes scale relative to the root font size, so text respects users' browser settings and supports accessibility","rem is required to set image dpi","rem sizes look exactly the same on every device regardless of settings"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. One rem equals the root (html) font size, so if a user enlarges their default text, the whole layout scales with it. The \"exactly the same\" option is tempting, but that describes fixed pixel sizing, which ignores user preferences.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A free quiz app about Caribbean history asks for access to users' contacts and location, although it does not need them. Which evaluation criterion is MOST relevant?</p>","options":["Usability","Authority","Privacy and security risks","Accuracy"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Unnecessary permissions put users' personal data at risk, so this is a privacy and security concern. Accuracy is tempting because the app is about history, but the issue here is how the app treats user data, not whether its facts are correct.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28 (Module 2)","q":"<p>When writing a Wikipedia article about Emancipation Day in Barbados, which standard must contributors follow?</p>","options":["Their personal opinions about the celebration","Promotional language encouraging people to attend events","A neutral point of view, supported by reliable, verifiable sources","Original research that has not been published elsewhere"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Wikipedia requires neutrality and citations to reliable published sources, because articles are written collaboratively for a global audience. Original research is tempting because it sounds valuable, but Wikipedia does not accept unpublished findings that readers cannot verify.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which sequence shows a typical content WORKFLOW in a content management system?</p>","options":["Archive, create, publish, review, approve","Publish, create, review, approve, archive","Create, publish, approve, review, archive","Create, review, approve, publish, archive"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Content is drafted, checked by an editor, formally approved, published and eventually archived when out of date. Create, publish, approve, review is tempting because it starts correctly, but publishing before review and approval risks putting errors live.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The rise of subscription newsletters (for example on Substack) helps writers MAINLY by</p>","options":["guaranteeing that every newsletter goes viral","providing free hosting for large video files","giving them a direct relationship with readers and recurring income that does not depend on social media algorithms","removing the need to write regularly"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Newsletters land directly in readers' inboxes and paid subscriptions give predictable income, reducing reliance on platforms. Going viral is tempting because newsletters can be shared, but nothing guarantees viral reach; the strength of newsletters is loyal, direct audiences.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A national telecommunications regulator that sets policy, allocates spectrum and licenses carriers is part of which component of the mobile ecosystem?</p>","options":["Content","Infrastructure","The enabling environment","Devices"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The enabling environment includes policy, regulation, regulators and developer support that shape how the ecosystem operates. Infrastructure is tempting because spectrum is used by networks, but the regulator does not build networks; it sets the rules that carriers follow.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which statement correctly describes iOS compared with Android?</p>","options":["Apple controls the hardware, the operating system and App Store review, giving tight security and a consistent design, but less customisation","Its source code is open, so any manufacturer can install it on their phones","It runs on Samsung and Huawei phones as well as iPhones","Apps can be published with no review process"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. iOS is a closed ecosystem: Apple makes the devices and the OS and reviews every app. The open source option is tempting, but it describes Android, whose open-source base allows many manufacturers to use it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33 (Module 3)","q":"<p>HarmonyOS is a mobile operating system alongside Android and iOS. It was developed by</p>","options":["Samsung","Google","Huawei","Apple"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. HarmonyOS is Huawei's operating system for phones, wearables and smart devices. Samsung is tempting because it is also a major device maker, but Samsung phones mainly run Android (with Samsung's One UI on top).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The start-up Island Bites has ONE small team and a tight deadline, but needs its app on both Android and iOS. Which approach is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Build two separate native apps in Xcode and Android Studio","Use a cross-platform tool such as Flutter or React Native to build both versions from one codebase","Publish the menu as a PDF instead of an app","Release on iOS only and ignore Android users"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Cross-platform tools let one team write one codebase for both platforms, saving time and money. Two native apps is tempting because native apps can perform best, but it doubles the work, which a small team with a tight deadline cannot afford.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35 (Module 3)","q":"<p>How does mobile technology MOST strengthen the connection between Caribbean islands and their diaspora?</p>","options":["It makes international travel unnecessary for all business","It stops people from migrating abroad","Messaging, video calls, remittance apps and streaming of local radio let families abroad stay in touch and send money home","It removes the need for international submarine cables"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Mobile tools keep diaspora communities emotionally, culturally and economically linked to home, including through remittances. Removing cables is tempting because connectivity matters, but mobile services actually depend on submarine cables to carry international traffic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which is a key reason why 5G rollout may be slower in small islands and rural Caribbean areas than in large cities abroad?</p>","options":["High infrastructure costs compared with small populations make the return on investment lower for carriers","5G phones cannot be sold in the Caribbean","Caribbean users have no demand for mobile data","5G technology is banned throughout the region"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Building towers and fibre backhaul is expensive, and small markets mean fewer paying customers per tower, so carriers roll out more slowly. No demand is tempting as a simple explanation, but mobile data demand in the region is high; the barrier is economics, not interest.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37 (Module 3)","q":"<p>An app that teaches Kwéyòl phrases and folk stories to young people in St. Lucia MAINLY provides which benefit?</p>","options":["Cultural preservation and social linkage","Export revenue from foreign markets","Faster emergency response","Improved public transport"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Apps about language, history and stories help preserve culture and connect generations. Export revenue is tempting because any app might earn money, but the main purpose here is cultural, not economic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Why might a locally built bus app serve Trinidad and Tobago riders better than a global maps app?</p>","options":["Local apps never need internet access","Global apps are always more expensive to download","Local developers can include informal stops, local landmarks and route knowledge that global datasets often miss","Global maps apps cannot run on smartphones in the Caribbean"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Locally relevant apps reflect how services really work in the community, such as buses that stop at landmarks rather than fixed timetabled stops. The cost option is tempting, but major global maps apps are free; their weakness is missing local detail, not price.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Users complain that the TrackFit app drains their battery because it checks GPS location every second, even in the background. Which factor and fix BEST match?</p>","options":["Device performance and battery life: reduce location update frequency and limit background tasks","User profile: translate the app into Dutch","Screen size: increase the font size","Bandwidth: compress the images"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Constant GPS polling uses processing power and drains the battery, so updating less often and pausing background work fixes it. Compressing images is tempting because it improves performance in general, but it saves data, not GPS-related battery use.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40 (Module 3)","q":"<p>MIT App Inventor allows beginners to build Android apps by</p>","options":["typing commands into a terminal only","designing in Blender and exporting to the Play Store","snapping together visual code blocks in a web browser","writing Swift code in Xcode"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. MIT App Inventor is a no-code, low-code tool: logic is built from drag-and-drop blocks, and the interface is designed in the browser. Swift in Xcode is tempting because it is a real app development route, but it is professional coding for iOS, not block-based building.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which sequence shows the correct order for developing a mobile app?</p>","options":["Wireframe, build, conceptualise, prototype, test","Build, wireframe, conceptualise, test, prototype","Conceptualise, wireframe, prototype, build, test","Prototype, conceptualise, test, wireframe, build"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. You first define the purpose and users, sketch layouts, create a clickable model, then code and test. Wireframe, build, conceptualise is tempting because wireframing is early, but you cannot sketch screens before deciding what the app is for.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42 (Module 3)","q":"<p>In the app user journey, a person reading star ratings and comments before deciding to install an app is at which stage?</p>","options":["Download","Review","Discover","Retain"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The journey runs discover, test, review, purchase, download and retain; reading others' ratings and comments is the review stage. Discover is tempting because the person is still deciding, but discovery is when they first become aware of the app.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A mobile game gives players three extra lives if they choose to watch a 30-second advert. Which revenue model is this?</p>","options":["In-app advertising using rewarded video","In-app advertising using banner ads","A subscription model","In-app advertising using interstitial ads"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Rewarded video ads are optional and give users something in return for watching. Interstitial ads are tempting because they are also full-screen, but they appear automatically between screens, and the user gets no reward.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The ReadyBVI hurricane app is free, shows no adverts and is funded by a government grant and a local NGO. Which category of app is this?</p>","options":["A subscription app","A freemium app","A civic, social good app funded by grants and sponsorship","A white-label licensed app"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Civic and charity apps serve the public good, such as disaster preparedness, and are funded by grants, sponsors or donations rather than users. Freemium is tempting because the app is free, but freemium relies on selling premium features, which ReadyBVI does not do.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45 (Module 3)","q":"<p>An app made in Sint Maarten is used by residents of the French side, Saint-Martin, which is part of the European Union. Which regulation MOST affects how it collects and stores their personal data?</p>","options":["The General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)","Creative Commons licensing","The ISBN standard","Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG)"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. GDPR governs how personal data of people in the EU is collected, stored and used, requiring clear consent and data protection. WCAG is tempting because it is also a standard that apps should follow, but it covers accessibility, not data privacy.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_507","title":"Mock Paper 01 · Set 3","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Digital Media Unit 2 · Grade One · Mock Paper 01 · Set 3</b> · 1 hour 30 minutes · 45 items</p><ol><li>Answer <b>ALL</b> items. Each item has four options (A), (B), (C), (D).</li><li>Items 1 to 15 Module 1, 16 to 30 Module 2, 31 to 45 Module 3.</li><li>Submit to see your score, the key and an explanation for every item.</li></ol>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Zara asks the team to take an existing tourist map and ask: what can we Substitute, Combine, Adapt, Modify, Put to another use, Eliminate or Reverse? Which brainstorming technique is this?</p>","options":["Rapid ideation","Mood boarding","SCAMPER","Mind mapping"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. SCAMPER is a checklist of seven prompts used to generate new ideas from an existing product. Mind mapping is tempting because it is also a brainstorming technique, but it branches ideas out from a central topic rather than applying set prompts to a product.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2 (Module 1)","q":"<p>For a Carnival campaign, Keisha collects photos, colour swatches, fabric textures and font samples on one board to set the overall visual feel. This is a</p>","options":["wireframe","mood board","style guide","storyboard"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A mood board is an inspirational collage that communicates the look and feel of a project early in planning. A style guide is tempting because it also deals with colours and fonts, but it sets fixed rules (exact colour codes, logo use, type sizes) once the design is decided.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A poster for the Island Rhythm festival uses orange, green and violet, three colours spaced evenly around the colour wheel. Which colour harmony is this?</p>","options":["Split-complementary","Tetradic","Analogous","Triadic"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A triadic scheme uses three colours equally spaced (about 120 degrees apart) on the wheel. Tetradic is tempting because it is also an evenly spaced scheme, but it uses four colours, forming two complementary pairs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A designer divides a poster of Fort Charlotte into a large square and a smaller rectangle, then repeats the division to form a spiral, placing the fort where the spiral tightens. Which rule of composition is she using?</p>","options":["Symmetrical balance around a central axis","The golden ratio (golden rectangle), approximately 1 to 1.618","The rule of thirds, dividing the frame into nine equal parts","Repetition of identical shapes"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The golden rectangle has sides in the ratio of about 1 to 1.618; repeatedly cutting off squares produces the golden spiral, and the point of interest sits where it tightens. The rule of thirds is tempting because it also guides subject placement, but it uses a simple grid of equal thirds, not a ratio-based spiral.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Maya is choosing text and background colours for a long article on the Island Pulse website. Which combination will give the BEST legibility on screen?</p>","options":["Yellow text on a white background","Dark navy text on an off-white background","Light grey text on a white background","Red text on a green background"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Strong value contrast (dark against light) makes text easy to read for long periods and for users with low vision. Red on green is tempting because they are complementary colours with high hue contrast, but they have similar values, vibrate on screen and are hard to separate for people with colour blindness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6 (Module 1)","q":"<p>In the DOM Tours app, the \"Book now\" button is pale grey on a light grey background, and testers do not notice it. Which principle of interactive design has been ignored?</p>","options":["Perceivability","Learnability","Consistency","Predictability"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Perceivability means users must be able to see, hear or otherwise notice interface elements; a low-contrast button fails this. Learnability is tempting because users struggle, but learnability is about how quickly users understand how to use something once they find it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following is an example of POLICY support for local Caribbean digital creators?</p>","options":["Fans sharing a local creator's video with friends","A creator buying a new camera with personal savings","A government offering tax incentives or grants for local film and animation production","A viewer leaving a positive comment on a local game"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Policy support comes from governments and institutions through laws, incentives, quotas or funding programmes. Fans sharing videos is tempting because it genuinely helps creators, but it is community support, not policy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Maya wants a YouTube video of the Carnival parade to play inside the school website WITHOUT uploading the large video file to the website's server. What is the BEST method?</p>","options":["Take a screenshot of the video and post the image","Paste YouTube's embed code (an iframe) into the web page","Download the MP4 and email it to every visitor","Convert the video into a GIF and upload that instead"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Embedding displays content hosted elsewhere inside your page, so YouTube streams the video and your server stores nothing. Converting to GIF is tempting because GIFs move, but a long video as a GIF would be a huge file with no sound and poor quality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Malik is exporting a short promo for Instagram Reels and TikTok, which display full-screen vertical video on phones. Which aspect ratio should he choose?</p>","options":["16:9","4:3","9:16","1:1"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. 9:16 is vertical video that fills a phone screen held upright, which suits Reels and TikTok. 16:9 is tempting because it is the standard for YouTube and television, but it is horizontal, so it would appear small with large empty bars on a vertical feed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10 (Module 1)","q":"<p>In Blender's Graph Editor, the curve for a ball's movement is flat at the start and end and steep in the middle. What does this show?</p>","options":["Ease in and ease out (slow in and slow out): the ball starts slowly, speeds up, then slows down","Linear motion: the ball moves at the same speed throughout","Anticipation: the ball moves backwards first","Squash and stretch: the ball changes shape"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A flat section means little change in value per frame (slow), and a steep section means rapid change (fast), which is the principle of slow in and slow out. Linear motion is tempting, but a linear interpolation would be a straight diagonal line with no flattening at the ends.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11 (Module 1)","q":"<p>An animator first draws the key poses of a dance move, then goes back to fill in the drawings between them. Which approach is this?</p>","options":["Pose-to-pose","Straight-ahead action","Staging","Follow through"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Pose-to-pose plans the important key poses first and adds in-betweens afterwards, giving control over timing. Straight-ahead action is tempting because it is the other main method, but there the animator draws each frame in order from start to finish without planned key poses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A group member accidentally deletes a section of the shared SBA report in Google Docs. Which feature of cloud collaboration tools will BEST let the group recover it?</p>","options":["Version history (version control)","Real-time chat","Offline mode","Link sharing"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Version history stores earlier versions of a file, so the group can restore the deleted section. Real-time chat is tempting because it helps the team communicate about the problem, but it cannot bring back lost text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Sol Studio campaign uses the same slogan, colours and hashtag on TikTok, Instagram, its email newsletter and posters at Douglas–Charles Airport. This is an example of</p>","options":["cross-platform marketing","guerrilla marketing","viral marketing","influencer marketing"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Cross-platform marketing keeps one consistent message and identity across many channels so the brand is recognised everywhere. Viral marketing is tempting because a hashtag can spread, but viral marketing is about designing content that people share widely on their own.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Zara wants to see which visual and interactive design styles leading designers are publishing this month. Which research tools are MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Mailchimp and Constant Contact","Google Trends and Google Analytics","Behance and Dribbble","Trello and Slack"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Behance and Dribbble are showcases where designers publish current work, so they reveal emerging styles directly. Google Trends is tempting because it tracks trends, but it shows search interest over time, not examples of design work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A wealthy Barbadian businessperson invests her own money in a student animation start-up in exchange for a share of ownership and offers mentoring. This source of funding is</p>","options":["sponsorship","crowdfunding","angel investment","a government grant"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. An angel investor is an individual who invests personal funds in an early-stage venture in return for equity, often adding advice. Sponsorship is tempting because money comes from one source, but sponsors pay for brand exposure, not a share of the company.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Why is a Massively Multiplayer Online Game (MMOG) considered a form of digital publishing?</p>","options":["It must be printed and sold in shops before it can be played","It is always published as an ePub file","Its content, such as worlds, events and expansions, is distributed and updated online to very large audiences at the same time","It can only be played offline on one computer"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Publishing means making content available to an audience; MMOGs publish and continually update shared online worlds for thousands of players. The ePub option is tempting because ePub is a digital publishing format, but it is for e-books, not games.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The title, author, description, keywords and categories an author enters on Amazon Kindle Direct Publishing so readers can find her book are called</p>","options":["the ISBN","metadata","DRM","the ePub"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Metadata is data about data: descriptive information that helps stores and search engines classify and surface the book. ISBN is tempting because it is also entered during publishing, but it is a single unique identifying number, not the descriptive fields.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which statement correctly distinguishes the internet from the World Wide Web?</p>","options":["The World Wide Web is the cables and routers that carry data","The internet is the global network of connected computers; the World Wide Web is a system of linked web pages accessed over it","They are two names for exactly the same thing","The World Wide Web was created before the internet"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The internet is the infrastructure; the Web, created by Tim Berners-Lee around 1989 to 1991, is one service that runs on it, alongside email and others. \"The same thing\" is tempting because people use the words interchangeably, but in an exam you must distinguish them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Under copyright law, which of the following is protected AUTOMATICALLY as soon as it is created and fixed in a form?</p>","options":["An idea for a story that has not been written down","A common everyday phrase","An original song recorded by a Trinidadian soca artist","The date of Emancipation Day"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Copyright protects original creative works once they are fixed (recorded, written, saved); no registration is needed. The story idea is tempting because it is original, but copyright protects the expression of ideas, not ideas themselves.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which pair of content management systems are BOTH open-source?</p>","options":["Wix and Squarespace","WordPress and Drupal","Wix and Joomla","Squarespace and Drupal"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. WordPress, Drupal and Joomla are open-source: their code is free to use, modify and self-host. Wix and Joomla is tempting because Joomla is open-source, but Wix is proprietary, owned and hosted by one company.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Maya's wireframe for a tours website makes the main heading largest, the \"Book a tour\" button bold and prominent, and the small print smallest. Which design principle guides the user's eye from most to least important?</p>","options":["Texture","Visual hierarchy","Proximity","Repetition"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Visual hierarchy uses size, weight, colour and position to show the order of importance. Proximity is tempting because it is also a layout principle, but it is about grouping related items close together, not ranking their importance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Optimising a restaurant's web content for VOICE SEARCH mainly means</p>","options":["writing only in technical jargon","repeating the same keyword as many times as possible","removing all text and using only images","using natural, question-style phrases with short, clear answers, such as \"Where can I buy johnny cakes in Kingston?\""],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. People speak to voice assistants in full questions, so content that mirrors natural speech and gives concise answers ranks better. Keyword repetition is tempting because keywords matter for SEO, but \"keyword stuffing\" is penalised by search engines and sounds unnatural.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which licence allows anyone to use, change and sell a photo with NO conditions at all, not even giving credit?</p>","options":["CC BY-NC","CC0 (public domain dedication)","All rights reserved","CC BY"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. CC0 waives all rights, placing the work as close to the public domain as possible. CC BY is tempting because it is very open, but it still requires attribution to the creator.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The hero image for the Coral Cove Marina website is 6000 by 4000 pixels and 12 MB. What is the BEST way to prepare it for the web?</p>","options":["Upload the original file so it looks as sharp as possible","Convert it to an uncompressed BMP file","Save it as a full-size PNG to avoid any loss of quality","Resize it to the display width (about 1920 pixels), compress it as a web JPEG or WebP, and add alt text"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Web images should match their display size and be compressed, so pages load quickly on mobile data; alt text adds accessibility and SEO. A full-size PNG is tempting because it is lossless, but for a large photograph it would produce an even bigger file and a slow page.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25 (Module 2)","q":"<p>In a podcast about Belize food, Keisha wants background music to sit quietly under her voice. What is the BEST technique?</p>","options":["Speed up the music so it finishes sooner","Put the music on a separate track and lower its volume (gain) under the speech","Mix the music and voice onto the same track before editing","Normalise the music to full volume"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Working with multiple tracks lets you adjust each element independently; lowering music under speech (ducking) keeps the voice clear. Mixing everything onto one track is tempting because it seems simpler, but then you can no longer change the music level without affecting the voice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26 (Module 2)","q":"<p>What happens if the image in this HTML fails to load?<br><code>&lt;img src=\"san-pedro.jpg\" alt=\"Plane landing over San Pedro beach\"&gt;</code></p>","options":["The page refuses to load at all","A link to San Pedro beach opens in a new tab","The browser downloads san-pedro.jpg from Google instead","The text \"Plane landing over San Pedro beach\" is shown in its place, and screen readers read it aloud"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The alt attribute provides alternative text for when the image cannot be displayed and for screen reader users. The new tab option is tempting because the tag contains text, but <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> displays an image; only an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag creates a link.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27 (Module 2)","q":"<p>What does this CSS rule do?<br><code>a:hover { color: orange; }</code></p>","options":["It makes all visited links orange permanently","It changes a link's text colour to orange while the mouse pointer is over it","It turns links orange only after they are clicked and released","It makes every link orange at all times"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. <code>:hover</code> is a pseudo-class that applies a style only while the pointer rests on the element, giving visual feedback. The visited option is tempting because it is also a link state, but visited links are styled with <code>a:visited</code>.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which statement correctly distinguishes a DOMAIN NAME from web HOSTING?</p>","options":["They are the same service with two names","A domain name stores the website's images and videos","A domain name is the address users type to find a site; hosting is the server space that stores the site's files","Hosting is the address users type; the domain is the server"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. In website infrastructure, the domain (for example islandpulse.sx) points to the hosting server where files live. Swapping the two is tempting because they are usually bought together, but the domain is the address and the host is the storage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29 (Module 2)","q":"<p>During a tropical storm watch, a student compares a viral social media post about wind speeds with the official bulletin from the national meteorological service. Which evaluation criterion is she applying?</p>","options":["Audience engagement","Accuracy","Accessibility","Usability"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Checking facts against a reliable, official source tests accuracy. Audience engagement is tempting because the post is viral, but high engagement says nothing about whether the information is correct.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30 (Module 2)","q":"<p>What is the MAIN advantage of a self-hosted WordPress.org site over a free hosted blog on WordPress.com or Blogger?</p>","options":["It costs nothing at all to run","Full control over themes, plugins, data, monetisation and your own domain","It needs no maintenance or updates","Security is handled entirely by the platform"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Self-hosting gives you ownership and freedom to customise and earn as you choose. \"Costs nothing\" is tempting because WordPress software is free, but you must pay for hosting and a domain, and you also take on maintenance and security yourself.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31 (Module 3)","q":"<p>In the mobile ecosystem, apps, music, news, videos and games are examples of which component?</p>","options":["Content","The enabling environment","Infrastructure","Devices"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Content is what users consume and create through their devices, and it drives demand across the ecosystem. Infrastructure is tempting because content travels over networks, but infrastructure refers to towers, cables and data centres, not the material itself.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The StormWatch alert app fails to reach many elderly residents because their older phones cannot install it. Which relationship in the mobile ecosystem does this BEST illustrate?</p>","options":["Device capability limits which content users can access, so developers must design for the devices people actually own","Carriers decide which apps users are allowed to install","Regulators design the apps that users download","Content has no connection to the devices users own"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. This shows interdependence: content is only useful if it runs on users' devices, so developers must consider older hardware and OS versions. The carriers option is tempting because carriers sell phones, but here the barrier is the phone's capability, not the network provider.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Jerome is designing a smartwatch app for runners on the Palisadoes road in Kingston. Which design consideration is MOST important for this wearable device?</p>","options":["Show short, glanceable information with minimal input because the screen is very small","Rely on a full on-screen keyboard for all controls","Use the same detailed layout as the tablet version","Include long forms for runners to type their notes"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Wearables have tiny screens and are used on the move, so information must be readable at a glance with very few taps. Reusing the tablet layout is tempting because it saves design time, but it would be unreadable and unusable on a watch face.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Using 5G networks with edge computing improves augmented reality apps MAINLY by</p>","options":["removing the need for any internet connection","making every smartphone cheaper to buy","processing data closer to the user, reducing latency so digital overlays respond almost instantly","making phone screens physically larger"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Edge computing moves processing from distant data centres to servers near the user, and with 5G this cuts delay, which AR needs to feel real. Removing the need for internet is tempting, but edge computing still depends on the network; it just shortens the distance data travels.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A student needs reliable statistics on mobile internet use across the Caribbean for her SBA report. Which source is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["A personal travel blog","A star rating in the app store","A recent ITU or GSMA report","An anonymous social media post"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The ITU (International Telecommunication Union) and GSMA publish researched, regularly updated data on mobile and internet use. The travel blog is tempting because it may mention phone use in the islands, but it is personal opinion, not authoritative statistics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Why are SMS text alerts still valuable in disaster management, even where smartphones are common?</p>","options":["SMS alerts work even when the phone is switched off","SMS uses satellites and needs no carrier network","SMS messages can carry high-definition video","SMS works on basic phones and often gets through on weak or congested networks when mobile data fails"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. During hurricanes such as Irma, data networks may be overloaded or damaged, but short SMS messages need very little bandwidth and reach any handset. The satellite option is tempting because it sounds resilient, but standard SMS runs on the carrier's mobile network.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A Trinidad and Tobago app for managing steelpan band bookings is later licensed to bands in Miami and London. Which benefit of local app development does this MOST clearly show?</p>","options":["Export potential, earning income from outside the region","Improved emergency services","Cultural preservation within Trinidad and Tobago","Reduced data usage for local users"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Selling or licensing a Caribbean-made product abroad is export: it brings foreign income and shows regional entrepreneurship. Cultural preservation is tempting because steelpan is a cultural art form, but the scenario focuses on selling the app overseas, which is an economic benefit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The AgriHelp app is for older farmers in rural Guyana, many with limited literacy and digital skills. Which design approach BEST suits this user profile?</p>","options":["Small buttons to fit more features on screen","Dense text menus with many options on each screen","Clear icons, voice prompts, simple language and large buttons","Technical terms in English only"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Designing for the user profile means matching the app to users' age, literacy, skills and language. Fitting more features on screen is tempting because it seems to add value, but crowded small buttons confuse users with limited digital skills.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Adding text labels to icons so that a screen reader can announce \"Search\" and \"Menu\" to a blind user is an example of designing for</p>","options":["bandwidth","accessibility and inclusive design","processing power","portability"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Accessibility ensures people with disabilities can use the app, and screen readers need text labels on icons to work. Portability is tempting because phones are used on the go, but portability is about using the device while moving, not about disability access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40 (Module 3)","q":"<p>What is the MAIN purpose of a clickable Figma prototype in mobile app development?</p>","options":["To serve as the final app submitted to the app store","To automatically generate Kotlin code for the app","To test user flows and navigation with real users before investing time in coding","To improve the app's ranking in app store searches"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Clickable prototypes simulate the app's screens and links so problems in the user flow are found early. Submitting it to the app store is tempting because it looks realistic, but a prototype has no working code or data behind it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which is the official integrated development environment (IDE) for building native Android apps, and which language does Google recommend?</p>","options":["Android Studio and Kotlin","Xcode and Swift","Blender and Python","Thunkable and code blocks"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Android Studio is Google's official IDE, and Kotlin is its preferred language (Java is still supported). Thunkable is tempting because it can build Android apps, but it is a no-code, low-code builder, not the official native IDE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Jerome's app crashes with an error message he does not understand. Which resources are MOST appropriate for solving it?</p>","options":["Stack Overflow and the official Android developer documentation","Dribbble and Behance","Google Trends and Sensor Tower","Mailchimp and Constant Contact"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Online developer communities and official documentation explain errors and show tested solutions. Google Trends and Sensor Tower are tempting because they are research tools, but they track search and app market trends, not coding problems.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A hospital in Barbados wants an internal app for its staff only, NOT available to the public. Which distribution model is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["The public Google Play Store","Enterprise distribution","Direct download from a public website","A public web-based app"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Enterprise distribution delivers apps privately to an organisation's own devices and staff. Direct download is tempting because it avoids the app store, but a public website download could be accessed by anyone, which the hospital does not want.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The developer Kezia builds one food ordering app and sells customised versions, each with its own name and branding, to restaurants in Barbados, St. Kitts and Antigua. Which revenue model is this?</p>","options":["In-app purchases","Subscription","White-label licensing","Freemium"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A white-label app is built once and licensed to clients who rebrand it as their own. Subscription is tempting because restaurants may pay yearly, but the defining feature is reselling one rebrandable product to many businesses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Open-source and non-profit services such as the Signal messaging app and Wikipedia are funded MAINLY through</p>","options":["compulsory paid subscriptions","donations, grants and crowdfunding","banner adverts on every screen","selling users' personal data"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Community-supported models rely on voluntary donations, grants and sponsorship, which lets them stay free and avoid ads. Banner adverts are tempting because many free apps use them, but Signal and Wikipedia deliberately avoid ads to protect user privacy and independence.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 02 Practical Tasks","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_509","title":"Module 1: Paper 02 practice: design and animation for a Caribbean campaign","desc":"<p>Module 1: Visual and Interactive Design. Paper 02-style task.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Paper 02 practice: design and animation for a Caribbean campaign","q":"<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p>\n<p><b>Reef Guardians SLU</b> is a youth conservation group in Gros Islet, St. Lucia. It has asked Island Pulse Media to promote a beach clean-up and turtle-nesting awareness day at Grande Anse beach. The audience is students aged 13 to 19 and residents; the day falls during the busy Carnival season. Zara reviews the group's first draft materials:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a poster with red text on a bright green background, six different fonts, and a small turtle photo placed in the exact centre, surrounded by logos with almost no empty space;</li>\n<li>a 10-second animated turtle for social media that moves across the screen at exactly the same speed throughout, with no keyframes except the first and last, and a bouncing beach ball that never changes shape;</li>\n<li>a plan to post the poster once on Facebook, with no other channels and no way of measuring success.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The group has a small budget and a deadline of three weeks.</p><p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Identify <b>TWO</b> weaknesses in the draft poster. For each, name the design element or principle involved and recommend an improvement. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Recommend a colour harmony scheme for the campaign. Justify your choice for the audience and explain how tints or shades could be used. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain how <b>keyframing</b>, <b>timing and spacing</b>, and <b>squash and stretch</b> could improve the turtle and beach ball animation. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Outline a cross-platform promotional campaign for the clean-up day. Describe <b>TWO</b> strategies and state <b>ONE</b> KPI that would measure the success of each. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking criteria</h4><ul><li><b>Poster critique and improvements</b> (4 marks): You identified two weaknesses with precise design terms and gave practical fixes.</li><li><b>Colour harmony recommendation</b> (3 marks): Your scheme is named, specific and justified for the audience, with a clear use of tints or shades.</li><li><b>Animation principles applied</b> (4 marks): You applied all three principles accurately to the turtle and the ball.</li><li><b>Cross-platform campaign and KPIs</b> (4 marks): Your two strategies suit the audience and each has a measurable KPI.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> One weakness is <b>poor colour contrast</b>. Red text on a bright green background is hard to read, vibrates visually and is almost invisible to viewers with red-green colour blindness, so the key message is lost. I recommend dark navy text on a pale sand-coloured background, which gives strong contrast and suits a beach theme. A second weakness is <b>clutter and a weak point of interest</b>. Six fonts destroy consistency and visual hierarchy, and the small turtle in the exact centre, crowded by logos, has no <b>negative space</b> around it, so the eye has nowhere to land. I recommend two fonts only (one bold display font for the headline and one clean sans-serif for details), enlarging the turtle and placing it on a <b>rule of thirds</b> intersection, and grouping the logos in a strip at the bottom with white space around the headline.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> I recommend an <b>analogous scheme</b> of teal, turquoise and sea green with a small <b>complementary accent</b> of coral orange for the date and call to action. The analogous blues and greens link directly to the ocean and nature, feel calm and trustworthy for a conservation message, and the coral accent draws students' eyes to the action. Light <b>tints</b> of turquoise can form the background so the text stands out, while darker <b>shades</b> of teal can be used for headlines and the logo strip to create hierarchy.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <b>Keyframing</b>: the turtle has only a start and end keyframe, so it slides like a cut-out. Extra keyframes for each flipper stroke and a slight rise and fall of the shell would make the swim believable. <b>Timing and spacing</b>: the constant speed looks mechanical. Placing drawings closer together at the start and end (ease in and ease out) and further apart in the middle would make the turtle accelerate and slow naturally; for the ball, wide spacing near the ground and close spacing at the top of each arc shows gravity. <b>Squash and stretch</b>: the beach ball should squash (flatten) on impact with the sand and stretch (lengthen) as it falls and rebounds, while keeping the same volume, so it feels light and bouncy rather than rigid.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The campaign will use one hashtag, <b>#ReefGuardiansSXM</b>, and the same turtle mascot and colours on every channel for <b>cross-platform consistency</b>. <b>Strategy 1: a TikTok and Instagram Reels challenge</b> in which students post a 15-second clip picking up litter with the hashtag, encouraged by a local student influencer. KPI: number of shares and hashtag uses before the event. <b>Strategy 2: a low-cost guerrilla stunt</b>: chalk turtle tracks leading from the Gros Islet bus stop to a QR code that opens a Google Form sign-up, also shared in school WhatsApp groups. KPI: number of registrations through the QR code, compared with attendance on the day.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_50b","title":"Module 2: Paper 02 practice: planning and fixing a Caribbean tour website","desc":"<p>Module 2: Web and Digital Publishing. Paper 02-style task.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Paper 02 practice: planning and fixing a Caribbean tour website","q":"<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p>\n<p><b>Booby Island Ferry Tours</b> runs boat trips from Frigate Bay, St. Kitts, to Booby Island. The owner, Ms. Richardson, wants a website that cruise visitors and residents can use on their phones to see tours, prices, a photo gallery, reviews and a way to book. Maya at the Island Pulse Media inspects the first draft page, written by a relative. Part of the code is shown below.</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;h1&gt;Booby Island Ferry Tours&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;img src=\"ferry.jpg\"&gt;\n&lt;p style=\"color:#cccccc\"&gt;Daily trips from Frigate Bay to Booby Island.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"https://book.example.com\"&gt;Book now&lt;/a&gt;\n\n/* style.css */\nimg { width: 1600px; }</code></pre>\n<p>Maya also notes that the page has no title tag or meta description, the photo is a 9 MB file, and the owner has no coding skills but wants to post new photos and prices herself every week.</p><p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Draw or describe a site map for the Booby Island Ferry Tours website with at least <b>FIVE</b> pages, showing the hierarchy. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Identify <b>THREE</b> errors or weaknesses in the code shown and write corrected HTML or CSS for each. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Explain <b>TWO</b> ways to improve the page's search engine optimisation and machine indexability. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Recommend a content management system or publishing platform for Ms. Richardson. Justify your choice with <b>TWO</b> benefits and state <b>ONE</b> limitation. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking criteria</h4><ul><li><b>Site map and hierarchy</b> (3 marks): Your site map has a clear home page, five or more pages and a visible hierarchy.</li><li><b>HTML and CSS corrections</b> (5 marks): You found three real errors and wrote accurate corrected code.</li><li><b>SEO and machine indexability</b> (3 marks): You explained two SEO improvements and why they help search engines.</li><li><b>CMS recommendation</b> (4 marks): Your CMS choice is justified with two relevant benefits and a realistic limitation.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> Site map:<br><b>Home</b> (level 1)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; <b>Tours and Prices</b> (level 2) &gt; Booby Island Day Trip, Snorkel Trip, Sunset Cruise (level 3)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; <b>Photo Gallery</b> (level 2)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; <b>Reviews</b> (level 2)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; <b>About Us</b> (level 2)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; <b>Book and Contact</b> (level 2), with directions and a Google Map.<br>Every level 2 page is reachable from the main menu, and each tour page links to Book and Contact.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Error 1: the image has no alt text</b>, so screen reader users and search engines cannot tell what it shows. Corrected: <code>&lt;img src=\"ferry.jpg\" alt=\"Booby Island Ferry Tours boat arriving at Booby Island\"&gt;</code><br><b>Error 2: the CSS fixes the image at 1600px wide</b>, so on a phone it overflows the screen and forces sideways scrolling. Corrected: <code>img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }</code> The 9 MB photo should also be resized to about 1600px and compressed as a JPEG of under 300 KB so it loads quickly on mobile data.<br><b>Error 3: the paragraph colour #cccccc on a white background has very low contrast</b> and fails WCAG, especially in bright Caribbean sunlight. Corrected: <code>&lt;p style=\"color:#222222\"&gt;Daily trips from Frigate Bay to Booby Island.&lt;/p&gt;</code>, or better, move the colour into style.css: <code>p { color: #222222; }</code><br>The booking link goes to an external site, so it should open in a new tab to keep visitors on the tour site: <code>&lt;a href=\"https://book.example.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"&gt;Book now&lt;/a&gt;</code></p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> First, add a descriptive <b>title tag</b> such as <code>&lt;title&gt;Booby Island Ferry Tours | Frigate Bay, St. Kitts&lt;/title&gt;</code>, because search engines display it as the headline in results and use its keywords to rank the page. Second, add a <b>meta description</b> in the head, for example \"Daily ferry and snorkel trips to Booby Island from Frigate Bay, St. Kitts. Book online.\", because it gives search engines a summary to index and encourages visitors to click. Adding alt text and compressing the image also help, as Google favours accessible, fast-loading pages.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> I recommend <b>Wix</b>, a proprietary cloud-hosted CMS. <b>Benefit 1:</b> it has a drag-and-drop editor and dashboard, so Ms. Richardson can upload new photos and change prices every week without any coding. <b>Benefit 2:</b> its templates are mobile-responsive and it offers built-in SEO settings and booking tools, which suits cruise visitors searching on their phones. <b>Limitation:</b> it charges a monthly fee for a custom domain and removes adverts only on paid plans, and because it is proprietary she has less control over the code and cannot easily move the site to another host later.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_50d","title":"Module 3: Paper 02 practice: a Caribbean mobile app concept","desc":"<p>Module 3: Mobile App Development. Paper 02-style task.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Paper 02 practice: a Caribbean mobile app concept","q":"<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p>\n<p>St. Lucia's public transport relies largely on privately operated minibuses that follow routes between Castries, Gros Islet, Rodney Bay, Babonneau and Vieux Fort. There is no official live timetable, so students, workers, older residents and cruise visitors often wait without knowing when a bus will come. Jerome and Keisha at the Island Pulse Media want to create <b>BusLink SLU</b>, a mobile app to solve this problem. They note that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>many users have older or mid-range Android phones, and data signals are weak in some hillside areas;</li>\n<li>users often check the app outdoors in strong sunlight, while carrying bags or holding a child;</li>\n<li>cruise visitors may speak English, Spanish, German or French, and some older residents have limited digital skills;</li>\n<li>minibus drivers and owners are willing to share their location if it brings them more passengers;</li>\n<li>the team has a small budget and wants the app to be sustainable after launch.</li>\n</ul><p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Describe the app concept for BusLink GEO, stating its purpose, its target users and <b>THREE</b> core features. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain <b>TWO</b> factors from the scenario that impact the mobile user experience, and describe a design strategy to address each. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Recommend a suitable revenue model for BusLink GEO. Justify your choice and explain why <b>ONE</b> other model would be less suitable. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Describe how the team should distribute and market the app, including <b>ONE</b> App Store Optimisation technique and <b>ONE</b> pre-launch activity. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking criteria</h4><ul><li><b>App concept</b> (3 marks): Your concept has a clear purpose, defined users and three specific features.</li><li><b>Mobile UX factors and strategies</b> (4 marks): You linked two scenario factors to specific, practical design strategies.</li><li><b>Revenue model</b> (4 marks): Your revenue model is justified for this audience and you rejected an alternative with a clear reason.</li><li><b>Distribution and marketing</b> (4 marks): Your plan covers distribution, a specific ASO technique, a pre-launch activity and targeted promotion.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>BusLink SLU</b> will solve the problem of uncertain minibus waiting times in St. Lucia by showing where buses are and when they are likely to arrive. Its <b>target users</b> are students, workers and older residents who rely on minibuses daily, and cruise visitors exploring the island. Its <b>three core features</b> are: (1) a <b>live map</b> showing minibuses on each route using drivers' GPS locations, with estimated arrival times at each stop; (2) a <b>route finder</b> where users type or tap a destination, such as Rodney Bay or Soufrière, to see which route to take and the usual fare; and (3) <b>arrival alerts</b> that notify users when their bus is about five minutes away, available in English, Spanish, German and French.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One factor is <b>bandwidth and data usage</b>. Signals are weak in some hillside areas and many users have limited data, so a heavy app would load slowly or fail. The strategy is to <b>cache route maps and stop lists for offline use</b>, keep images small, and send only tiny location updates, so the app still shows routes with poor coverage. A second factor is the <b>physical environment and portability</b>. Users check the app outdoors in strong sunlight while carrying bags or a child. The strategy is a <b>high-contrast theme</b> with large text, and <b>large touch targets</b> (about 48dp) placed in the thumb zone so the main actions can be done one-handed. Simple icons and a language selector on first launch would also support older residents and visitors with different digital skills.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> I recommend a <b>free app funded by advertising and business partnerships</b>, combined with a small monthly fee from minibus owners for a 'verified bus' listing. Keeping the app free removes the barrier for students and visitors, which grows the number of users; that large local audience makes it attractive for businesses such as restaurants along the routes to buy small, relevant banner ads, and owners pay because the app brings them more passengers. This makes the app sustainable after launch. A <b>paid upfront model</b> would be less suitable, because students and short-stay cruise visitors are unlikely to pay before trying an app they may use only a few times, so downloads would stay low and the live map would be less useful.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The app should be distributed mainly through <b>Google Play</b>, since many users have Android phones, with an iOS version or a progressive web app so visitors with iPhones can also use it. For <b>ASO</b>, the store title should be 'BusLink SLU: St. Lucia Bus Times' with keywords such as 'Castries bus' and 'St. Lucia minibus', plus clear screenshots of the live map. Before launch, the team should run a <b>beta test</b> (user acceptance testing) with fifty student commuters and ten drivers for two weeks to fix bugs and gather early ratings. Marketing should include <b>QR code posters</b> at bus stops and at the Castries cruise port, short TikTok and Instagram videos showing a student catching the bus on time, and a press release to local news media.</p>"}]}]}];
